const QUIZ_SERIES = [{"id":"pyq-2026-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2026 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"During the trial, the witness’s deposition was EQUIVOCAL.","o":["ambiguous","evasive","explicit","guarded"],"a":2,"e":"Equivocal means ambiguous or open to more than one interpretation. Explicit means clear and definite, hence the opposite.","c":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the word that is OPPOSITE IN MEANING to the underlined word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"EQUIVOCAL"},{"n":2,"q":"The professor is known for his characteristic RETICENCE.","o":["communicativeness","reserve","restraint","diffidence"],"a":0,"e":"Reticence means reserve or unwillingness to speak. Communicativeness is its opposite.","t":"RETICENCE"},{"n":3,"q":"Observers noted the officials’ increasingly CAPRICIOUS approach to administrative reforms.","o":["erratic","whimsical","ceremonial","steadfast"],"a":3,"e":"Capricious means unpredictable or changeable. Steadfast means firm and consistent.","t":"CAPRICIOUS"},{"n":4,"q":"The novel presents an IDEALISTIC view of the ongoing political reform.","o":["visionary","utopian","pragmatic","romantic"],"a":2,"e":"Idealistic is based on ideals; pragmatic means practical and realistic.","t":"IDEALISTIC"},{"n":5,"q":"The speaker’s tone became increasingly CAUSTIC during the debate.","o":["sarcastic","conciliatory","acerbic","acrimonious"],"a":1,"e":"Caustic means harsh or sarcastic. Conciliatory means intended to calm or reconcile.","t":"CAUSTIC"},{"n":6,"q":"The note drew attention to the FALLACIOUS assumptions underlying the proposals.","o":["veracious","misleading","procedural","erroneous"],"a":0,"e":"Fallacious means false or based on mistaken reasoning. Veracious means truthful or accurate.","t":"FALLACIOUS"},{"n":7,"q":"The activist was SCEPTICAL with regard to the antecedents of the newly discovered documents.","o":["restrained","credulous","circumlocutory","guarded"],"a":1,"e":"Sceptical means doubtful or questioning. Credulous means too ready to believe.","t":"SCEPTICAL"},{"n":8,"q":"The panel found the conclusion somewhat SPECULATIVE.","o":["conjectural","hypothetical","substantiated","interpretive"],"a":2,"e":"Speculative means based on conjecture rather than evidence. Substantiated means supported by evidence.","t":"SPECULATIVE"},{"n":9,"q":"After the mishap, the mood was SOMBRE among the survivors.","o":["shady","dingy","restrained","buoyant"],"a":3,"e":"Sombre means gloomy or serious. Buoyant means cheerful and optimistic.","t":"SOMBRE"},{"n":10,"q":"He is a PROFESSIONAL artist.","o":["apprentice","dilettante","amateur","aspirant"],"a":2,"e":"A professional practises an occupation as a career; an amateur does it without professional status.","t":"PROFESSIONAL"},{"n":11,"q":"The controversial policy received the committee’s IMPRIMATUR.","o":["criticism","approval","rejection","appraisal"],"a":1,"e":"Imprimatur means official approval or authorization.","c":"Directions :Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the word that is NEAREST IN MEANING to the underlined word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"IMPRIMATUR"},{"n":12,"q":"The archaeologist was fascinated by the ANNULAR design of the ancient monument.","o":["circular","vertical","triangular","funicular"],"a":0,"e":"Annular means ring-shaped or circular.","t":"ANNULAR"},{"n":13,"q":"The nobility is not known for its MUNIFICENCE.","o":["reluctance","valour","generosity","graciousness"],"a":2,"e":"Munificence means great generosity or liberality.","t":"MUNIFICENCE"},{"n":14,"q":"The goods kept in the storage facility were declared to be CONTRABAND.","o":["prohibited","taxable","foreign","adulterated"],"a":0,"e":"Contraband refers to goods whose possession, import or export is prohibited.","t":"CONTRABAND"},{"n":15,"q":"The judge ruled on the EGREGIOUS violations of human rights by the authorities.","o":["subtle","flagrant","inadvertent","concealed"],"a":1,"e":"Egregious means outstandingly bad or shocking; flagrant is the closest synonym.","t":"EGREGIOUS"},{"n":16,"q":"Ipso facto","o":["After the fact","In fact","By the fact itself","The primary factor"],"a":2,"e":"Ipso facto literally means “by the fact itself.”","c":"Directions: Given below are some foreign phrases followed by four alternatives to each. Select the most appropriate alternative which provides the CORRECT MEANING OF THE FOREIGN PHRASE used in English and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"Ipso facto"},{"n":17,"q":"Via media","o":["Through newspapers","Through diplomacy","In secrecy","A middle way"],"a":3,"e":"Via media means a middle course or compromise between two extremes.","t":"Via media"},{"n":18,"q":"Quid pro quo","o":["The existing state of affairs","Therefore or consequently","Something given in exchange for something else","Done for public good without compensation"],"a":2,"e":"Quid pro quo means one thing given or received in return for another.","t":"Quid pro quo"},{"n":19,"q":"A posteriori","o":["At the very beginning","Based on reasoning from known facts or empirical evidence","Military campaign leading to swift victory","A person with a sociable and expansive lifestyle"],"a":1,"e":"A posteriori refers to knowledge derived from experience or observation.","t":"A posteriori"},{"n":20,"q":"Modus operandi","o":["The most appropriate word","Method of operating","The violation of a rule","Successful operation"],"a":1,"e":"Modus operandi means a particular method or manner of doing something.","t":"Modus operandi"},{"n":21,"q":"‘Burn your bridges’","o":["Ending one’s life","To make a decision you cannot change","To ignore danger","To settle an argument"],"a":1,"e":"Burn your bridges means to take an action that makes returning to an earlier position impossible.","c":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternatives to each. Select the most appropriate alternative which provides the CORRECT MEANING OF THE IDIOM/PHRASE and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"‘Burn your bridges’"},{"n":22,"q":"‘Make no bones about something’","o":["To do your own thing","To make no money","To not try to hide one’s feelings","To not come right to the point"],"a":2,"e":"Make no bones about something means to speak openly and directly about it.","t":"‘Make no bones about something’"},{"n":23,"q":"‘A blot on the landscape’","o":["A painting that has turned out wrong","A bald patch in a meadow","A black sheep in an office","Something ugly that spoils the appearance of a place"],"a":3,"e":"A blot on the landscape is an unattractive feature that spoils an area.","t":"‘A blot on the landscape’"},{"n":24,"q":"‘A bone of contention’","o":["Source of agreement","Point of long standing dispute","A hidden agenda","Legal obligation"],"a":1,"e":"A bone of contention is a subject that causes disagreement or dispute.","t":"‘A bone of contention’"},{"n":25,"q":"‘Pour oil on troubled waters’","o":["To mastermind a trick or deception","To produce something that is required as if by magic","To exacerbate a festering situation","To say or do something to stop an argument or calm down a situation"],"a":3,"e":"Pour oil on troubled waters means to calm a quarrel or tense situation.","t":"‘Pour oil on troubled waters’"},{"n":26,"q":"Despite the heavy rain, the match continued as scheduled.","o":["Conjunction","Adverb","Noun","Preposition"],"a":3,"e":"Despite introduces the noun phrase “the heavy rain”; therefore, it functions as a preposition.","c":"Directions :Each of the following sentences has an underlined word. Read the sentence carefully and identify which word class the underlined word belongs to. Indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"Despite"},{"n":27,"q":"He gave both candidates an equal opportunity to present their case.","o":["Determiner","Pronoun","Adverb","Adjective"],"a":0,"e":"Both comes before and determines the noun “candidates.”","t":"both"},{"n":28,"q":"The old woman sat on the bench, knitting in silence.","o":["Verb","Adjective","Adverb","Noun"],"a":3,"e":"Silence is the object of the preposition “in” and functions as a noun.","t":"silence"},{"n":29,"q":"The injured soldier crawled forward in spite of debilitating pain.","o":["Noun","Adverb","Adjective","Preposition"],"a":1,"e":"Forward modifies the verb “crawled” by indicating direction.","t":"forward"},{"n":30,"q":"The results were beyond everyone’s expectation.","o":["Preposition","Conjunction","Adjective","Adverb"],"a":0,"e":"Beyond precedes the noun phrase “everyone’s expectation” and functions as a preposition.","t":"beyond"},{"n":31,"q":"The findings were\nP : that had long been accepted\nQ : because they fundamentally challenged the assumptions\nR : met with considerable resistance at first\nS : by the mainstream scientific community\nThe proper sequencing should be :","o":["RQPS","RSPQ","PQSR","PSQR"],"a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: The findings were met with considerable resistance at first because they fundamentally challenged the assumptions that had long been accepted by the mainstream scientific community.","c":"Directions :Reorder the PQRS set to create the most appropriate meaningful sentence. Indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":32,"q":"It is often argued that\nP : are not the result of innate talent alone\nQ : but rather the product of\nR : extraordinary achievements\nS : sustained effort and deliberate practice\nThe proper sequencing should be :","o":["SPQR","PSQR","RPQS","QRSP"],"a":2,"e":"Correct sentence: It is often argued that extraordinary achievements are not the result of innate talent alone but rather the product of sustained effort and deliberate practice."},{"n":33,"q":"The historian noted that\nP : had been systematically erased\nQ : the memory of certain communities\nR : and replaced with a sanitized narrative\nS : from the official records of the colonial period\nThe proper sequencing should be :","o":["PQRS","QPSR","RQSP","QSPR"],"a":1,"e":"Correct sentence: The historian noted that the memory of certain communities had been systematically erased from the official records of the colonial period and replaced with a sanitized narrative."},{"n":34,"q":"Critics have long maintained that\nP : literature’s true power lies in its\nQ : assumptions and not in the mere\nR : ability to disturb comfortable\nS : reproduction of social reality\nThe proper sequencing should be :","o":["PQRS","PRQS","SPQR","QRSP"],"a":1,"e":"Correct sentence: Critics have long maintained that literature’s true power lies in its ability to disturb comfortable assumptions and not in the mere reproduction of social reality."},{"n":35,"q":"The movie\nP : it was hardly possible\nQ : were so humorous that\nR : to keep a straight face\nS : had many scenes which\nThe proper sequencing should be :","o":["PRSQ","SPRQ","QPRS","SQPR"],"a":3,"e":"Correct sentence: The movie had many scenes which were so humorous that it was hardly possible to keep a straight face."},{"n":36,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["She insisted that he apologises immediately.","She insisted that he apologise immediately.","She insisted that he may apologise immediately.","She insisted that he apologised immediately."],"a":1,"e":"After insisted that, the subjunctive form is used: “he apologise”, not apologises/apologised.","c":"Directions :The following items have four sentences, one of which is grammatically correct. Select the MOST APPROPRIATE CORRECT SENTENCE and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":37,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["The police has arrested the accused last night.","The police have arrested the accused last night.","The police arrested the accused last night.","The police had arrested the accused last night."],"a":2,"e":"With a definite past-time expression such as last night, the simple past is appropriate: The police arrested..."},{"n":38,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["The teacher made the students to rewrite the essay.","The teacher has the students to rewrite the essay.","The teacher got the students rewriting the essay.","The teacher had the students rewrite the essay."],"a":3,"e":"The causative structure is have + object + bare infinitive: had the students rewrite."},{"n":39,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["I shall base my decision on how much free time can I manage.","I shall base my decision about how much free time I can manage.","I shall base my decision on how much free time I can manage.","I shall base my decision to how much free time I can manage."],"a":2,"e":"The correct expression is base my decision on, followed by normal statement word order: how much free time I can manage."},{"n":40,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["Do not panic because a situation is under control.","Do not panic because situation under control.","Do not panic however a situation is under control.","Do not panic because the situation is under control."],"a":3,"e":"The sentence is grammatically complete and correctly uses the situation: Do not panic because the situation is under control."},{"n":41,"q":"Identify the part of the sentence that has an error.","o":["He is a honest man","who has served","this institution for twenty years","No error"],"a":0,"e":"Honest begins with a vowel sound, so the correct expression is “an honest man.”","c":"Directions: A sentence is given in three parts (a), (b) and (c). Find out which part of the sentence has an error. If there is no error, mark your option (d). Indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":42,"q":"Identify the part of the sentence that has an error.","o":["The jury has delivered","its verdict after","long and careful deliberations","No error"],"a":3,"e":"The sentence is grammatically acceptable: The jury has delivered its verdict after long and careful deliberations."},{"n":43,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nI. Active Voice: These grapes taste sour.\nPassive Voice: These grapes are sour when they are tasted.\nII. Active Voice: Forget the past.\nPassive Voice: Let the past be forgotten.\nIdentify the pair(s) wherein the Passive Voice is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":2,"e":"According to the official key, both pairs are accepted as correct passive constructions."},{"n":44,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nI. Active Voice: They asked me my name.\nPassive Voice: My name was asked by them.\nII. Active Voice: They found him guilty.\nPassive Voice: He found them guilty.\nIdentify the pair(s) wherein the Passive Voice is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"e":"According to the official key, Pair I is accepted; Pair II does not correctly transform the active sentence into passive voice."},{"n":45,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nI. Direct Speech: He said, “Bravo! You have written the best answer.”\nIndirect Speech: He applauded him saying that he had written the best answer.\nII. Direct Speech: “The sun rises in the East,” said the teacher.\nIndirect Speech: The teacher said that the sun rose in the East.\nIdentify the pair(s) wherein the Indirect Speech is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"e":"Pair I correctly reports the exclamation of praise. In Pair II, a universal truth should remain in the present tense: the sun rises in the East."},{"n":46,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nI. Direct Speech: He said to him, “Wait here until I return.”\nIndirect Speech: He ordered him to wait here until he returned.\nII. Direct Speech: He said, “Alas! I am undone.”\nIndirect Speech: He exclaimed sadly that he was undone.\nIdentify the pair(s) wherein the Indirect Speech is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":1,"e":"Pair I should use there instead of here in reported speech; Pair II is correctly transformed."},{"n":47,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nI. Group of words: One who studies handwriting\nOne-word substitution: Graphologist\nII. Group of words: One who draws maps\nOne-word substitution: Cartographer\nIII. Group of words: One who studies flags\nOne-word substitution: Vexillologist\nIdentify the pairs wherein the one word substitution is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"A graphologist studies handwriting, a cartographer draws maps, and a vexillologist studies flags."},{"n":48,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nI. Group of words: One who knows everything\nOne-word substitution: Omnipotent\nII. Group of words: One who is all powerful\nOne-word substitution: Omniscient\nIII. Group of words: One who abandons religious faith\nOne-word substitution: Apostate\nIdentify the pair(s) wherein the one word substitution is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I only","II only","III only","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"One who knows everything is omniscient; one who is all-powerful is omnipotent. Apostate correctly means one who abandons religious faith."},{"n":49,"q":"The car tended to ______ every time the rains decided to ______.","o":["act out; come in","act up; come down","act out; come down","act up; come in"],"a":1,"e":"Act up means malfunction; rain comes down when it falls heavily.","c":"Directions: Fill in the blanks with the most appropriate phrasal verb and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":50,"q":"He was eager to ______ after having resigned his position, but was advised to ______ his next move given the prevailing market situation.","o":["move on; hold on","move off; hold off","move on; hold off","move off; hold on"],"a":2,"e":"Move on means proceed to a new stage; hold off means postpone or delay."}],"source":"Corrected Word Source – NDA/NA English 2026 II"},{"id":"pyq-2026-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2026 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The ‘Jataka Tales’ present moral narratives in the form of ALLEGORIES.","o":["aphorisms","adages","enjambments","parables"],"a":3,"e":"Allegory is a symbolic narrative conveying a moral or spiritual idea. Parables are short moral stories with the same purpose.","c":"Directions: Choose the word that is NEAREST IN MEANING to the highlighted word or expression.","t":"ALLEGORIES"},{"n":2,"q":"He was upbraided for appearing in court in a state of INEBRIATION.","o":["ululation","intoxication","confabulation","sublimation"],"a":1,"e":"Inebriation means drunkenness. Intoxication is its direct synonym.","t":"INEBRIATION"},{"n":3,"q":"The OBSTREPEROUS behavior of the students inside the classroom drew the attention of the Principal.","o":["compliant","obnoxious","inconspicuous","disorderly"],"a":3,"e":"Obstreperous means noisy and difficult to control. Disorderly is the closest meaning.","t":"OBSTREPEROUS"},{"n":4,"q":"These were based primarily in Himachal Pradesh, where the treks were as GRUELLING as the terrain.","o":["rigorous","egregious","unctuous","ominous"],"a":0,"e":"Gruelling describes something extremely demanding. Rigorous best conveys this idea.","t":"GRUELLING"},{"n":5,"q":"I think they would wish for some more ADORNMENT around the school campus for the special function.","o":["subsummation","decoration","coloration","inculcation"],"a":1,"e":"Adornment means beautification by adding ornaments. Decoration is the closest synonym.","t":"ADORNMENT"},{"n":6,"q":"The travel company tried to identify destinations that were OFF THE BEATEN TRACK.","o":["untenanted","unfrequented","unattended","untoward"],"a":1,"e":"Off the beaten track refers to places rarely visited. Hence, unfrequented.","t":"OFF THE BEATEN TRACK"},{"n":7,"q":"Women gather in the community park on a regular basis to CHANT prayers for the benefit of all.","o":["confound","inculcate","indite","recite"],"a":3,"e":"Chant means to repeat rhythmically. Recite is the nearest equivalent.","t":"CHANT"},{"n":8,"q":"The HR Manager was able to UTILIZE the specific skills of each member of the team assembled for the project.","o":["harness","harbor","harangue","herald"],"a":0,"e":"Utilize means to use effectively. Harness best matches this meaning.","t":"UTILIZE"},{"n":9,"q":"The oppressive pollution was noticed to have ABATED after the unseasonal rainfall.","o":["mitigated","enhanced","discontinued","suppressed"],"a":0,"e":"Abated means reduced in intensity. Mitigated is the closest synonym.","t":"ABATED"},{"n":10,"q":"Often, the context and structure of an AMBIGUOUS statement is difficult to discern.","o":["absolutistic","explicit","cryptic","solipsistic"],"a":2,"e":"Ambiguous means unclear or capable of more than one meaning. Cryptic is the best match.","t":"AMBIGUOUS"},{"n":11,"q":"She had a RUDDY, open face and an oddly guileless manner.","o":["cacophonous","cadaverous","nauseous","bumptious"],"a":1,"e":"Ruddy means healthy and reddish. Cadaverous is its opposite.","c":"Directions: Choose the word that is OPPOSITE IN MEANING to the highlighted word or expression.","t":"RUDDY"},{"n":12,"q":"He used to be known among his colleagues for his RIGHTEOUSNESS.","o":["iniquity","ingenuity","absorptivity","incredulity"],"a":0,"e":"Righteousness means moral goodness. Iniquity means wickedness.","t":"RIGHTEOUSNESS"},{"n":13,"q":"He found the musical rendition by the septuagenarian maestro to be LISTLESS.","o":["wearying","unedifying","unengaging","scintillating"],"a":3,"e":"Listless means dull and lifeless. Scintillating means lively and brilliant.","t":"LISTLESS"},{"n":14,"q":"The official accused of espionage was found to be DISINGENUOUS.","o":["scrupulous","duplicitous","mendacious","perfidious"],"a":0,"e":"Disingenuous means dishonest. Scrupulous means honest and morally upright.","t":"DISINGENUOUS"},{"n":15,"q":"The young are easily EXCITABLE when presented with strong emotional triggers.","o":["composed","agitated","aggrieved","alarmed"],"a":0,"e":"Excitable means easily agitated. Composed means calm.","t":"EXCITABLE"},{"n":16,"q":"Our professor was PERPLEXED by the answers we wrote in the examination.","o":["misled","enlightened","confused","befuddled"],"a":1,"e":"Perplexed means confused. Enlightened means having clear understanding.","t":"PERPLEXED"},{"n":17,"q":"Effective governance seeks to OPTIMIZE public resource allocation.","o":["upgrade","rationalize","maximize","degrade"],"a":3,"e":"Optimize means improve. Degrade means make worse.","t":"OPTIMIZE"},{"n":18,"q":"The student’s striking AUDACIOUSNESS disturbed long-standing social hierarchies on campus.","o":["boldness","fearlessness","timidness","insightfulness"],"a":2,"e":"Audaciousness means boldness. Timidness is its opposite.","t":"AUDACIOUSNESS"},{"n":19,"q":"There are four CONCURRENT handloom fairs around the city.","o":["consecutive","distributive","aberrative","abstensive"],"a":0,"e":"Concurrent means happening together. Consecutive means one after another.","t":"CONCURRENT"},{"n":20,"q":"LUCIDITY is a desired quality in effective communication.","o":["Clarity","Felicity","Facility","Ambiguity"],"a":3,"e":"Lucidity means clarity. Ambiguity means lack of clarity.","t":"LUCIDITY"},{"n":21,"q":"‘Nuts and bolts’","o":["To complete the first step in a process","To get down to practical details","To receive harm or punishment for one’s actions","To irritate people and make them go away"],"a":1,"e":"Nuts and bolts means the practical details of a task.","c":"Directions: Choose the alternative that best expresses the meaning of the highlighted idiom or phrase.","t":"Nuts and bolts"},{"n":22,"q":"‘Hat in hand’","o":["With humility","Easy to use","Available for anyone","To marry someone"],"a":0,"e":"Hat in hand means with humility.","t":"Hat in hand"},{"n":23,"q":"‘Straight from the horse’s mouth’","o":["Ready and eager to engage in an activity","Significantly disturbing business","Information from reliable and authoritative source","Sudden feelings of fear and embarrassment"],"a":2,"e":"Straight from the horse's mouth means information from a reliable source.","t":"Straight from the horse’s mouth"},{"n":24,"q":"‘Bite off more than you can chew’","o":["To suddenly wrap out of conversation","To try to do more than you can manage","To do something in the easiest or cheapest way","To exaggerate something wildly"],"a":1,"e":"Bite off more than you can chew means attempt more than you can manage.","t":"Bite off more than you can chew"},{"n":25,"q":"‘A piece of cake’","o":["Something difficult to extract from the original","Something very difficult to understand","Something that appears very inviting","Something very easy to accomplish"],"a":3,"e":"A piece of cake means something very easy.","t":"A piece of cake"},{"n":26,"q":"He claimed his half share of the booty.","o":["Adverb","Adjective","Noun","Determiner"],"a":1,"e":"Half qualifies the noun share, so it is an adjective.","c":"Directions: Identify the correct part of speech or word class of the highlighted word/expression.","t":"half"},{"n":27,"q":"Swimming is a healthy exercise.","o":["Verb","Gerund","Adjective","Adverb"],"a":1,"e":"Swimming functions as the subject; hence it is a gerund.","t":"Swimming"},{"n":28,"q":"I have told you now and again that you must not read such trash.","o":["Conjunction","Preposition","Noun","Adverb"],"a":3,"e":"Now and again modifies the verb by showing frequency.","t":"now and again"},{"n":29,"q":"He succeeded by dint of perseverance and sheer hard work.","o":["Conjunctival phrase","Adjectival phrase","Prepositional phrase","Adverbial phrase"],"a":2,"e":"By dint of is a fixed prepositional phrase meaning because of.","t":"by dint of"},{"n":30,"q":"I need you to return those books I lent you last month.","o":["Modal","Determiner","Noun","Adjective\nComplete the sentence by choosing the correct alternative"],"a":1,"e":"In the sentence, those is determiner.","t":"those"},{"n":31,"q":"I will not pay for the goods","o":["till he sends me the bill again","until he sends me the bill again","if he sends me the bill again","unless he sends me the bill again"],"a":3,"e":"Unless introduces a negative condition (if not).","c":"Directions: Complete the sentence by choosing the correct alternative."},{"n":32,"q":"I did not buy it","o":["because I did not like the look of it","as far as I did not like the look of it","anyway I did not like the look of it","if I did not like the look of it"],"a":0,"e":"Because correctly expresses the cause."},{"n":33,"q":"I sent her a bouquet of orchids","o":["for which she was absolutely delighted","with which she was absolutely delighted","of which she was absolutely delighted","on which she was absolutely delighted"],"a":1,"e":"The correct collocation is delighted with."},{"n":34,"q":"You must stay inside","o":["where it starts raining","when it starts raining","because it starts raining","till it starts raining"],"a":1,"e":"When correctly introduces the time clause."},{"n":35,"q":"We must wait","o":["until you see the light turn green","till you see the green light","because you can see the green light","unless you see the red signal\nSelect the correct sentence"],"a":0,"e":"Until expresses continuation up to a particular time."},{"n":36,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["Although it was raining, we had the picnic.","Although it was raining, still we had the picnic.","Although it was raining, yet we had the picnic.","Although it was raining, even then we had the picnic."],"a":0,"e":"Although itself shows contrast; adding still/yet is unnecessary.","c":"Directions: Select the grammatically correct sentence."},{"n":37,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["When I would arrive, I will call you.","When I shall arrive, I will call you.","When I will arrive, I will call you.","When I arrive, I will call you."],"a":3,"e":"After when, the simple present is used for future time clauses."},{"n":38,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["Who among you has the answer?","Whom among you has the answer?","Whom amongst you has the answer?","Who amid you has the answer?"],"a":0,"e":"Who is correct because it is the subject of the clause."},{"n":39,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["No sooner than he reached the station the train left.","No sooner had he reached the station than the train left.","No sooner he had reached the station than the train left.","No sooner had he reached the station then the train left."],"a":1,"e":"The correct pair is No sooner...than."},{"n":40,"q":"Select the grammatically correct sentence.","o":["Hardly had I arrived at the airport than the plane departed.","Scarcely had I arrived at the airport when the plane departed.","Hardly I had arrived at the airport when the plane departed.","Scarcely had I arrived at the airport then the plane departed.\nSpotting Errors"],"a":1,"e":"The correct pair is Scarcely...when."},{"n":41,"q":"Identify the part containing an error.","o":["The project staff are","working on the weekend","so as not to delay","the project any further."],"a":1,"e":"Error in the preposition/time expression.","c":"Directions: Each sentence is divided into four parts. Identify the part that contains an error."},{"n":42,"q":"Identify the part containing an error.","o":["When I saw Arnab","he said he","had taken his","driving test yesterday."],"a":3,"e":"With reported speech, yesterday changes to the previous day."},{"n":43,"q":"Identify the part containing an error.","o":["Of all the players","it was Ramesh","who plan his tactics","with utmost care."],"a":2,"e":"Error in subject–verb agreement; use plans."},{"n":44,"q":"Identify the part containing an error.","o":["Many people in India are","still not aware","of India’s indigenous fabrics","that reflects the rich heritage of craftsmanship."],"a":3,"e":"A plural subject takes reflect, not reflects."},{"n":45,"q":"Identify the part containing an error.","o":["Meaningful ecological","effects of the handloom","sector can be optimized","through recycling practices."],"a":1,"e":"Use affects (verb), not effects (noun)."},{"n":46,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nActive Voice — Passive Voice\nI. They laughed at his warnings and objected to all his proposals.\n→ His warnings were laughed at and his proposals were objected to.\nII. Do not insult the weak.\n→ You are advised to not insult the weak.\nIdentify the pair(s) wherein the Passive Voice is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":0,"e":"Only Statement I is in the correct passive voice."},{"n":47,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nDirect Speech — Indirect Speech\nI. The policeman said to us, “Where are you going?”\n→ The policeman asked where were we going.\nII. “Call the first witness,” said the Judge.\n→ The Judge said them to call the witness first.\nIdentify the pair(s) wherein the Indirect Speech is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"a":3,"e":"Both reported speech conversions are incorrect."},{"n":48,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nIdiom/Phrase — Meaning\nI. Have one foot in the grave → To be in the last stage of life\nII. Get your feet wet → Establish yourself securely in an establishment\nIII. Follow suit → Correctly conform to another’s action\nIdentify the pair(s) wherein the meaning is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I only","III only","I and III","II and III"],"a":2,"e":"Statements I and III are correct."},{"n":49,"q":"Read the following pairs:\nGroup of words — One-word substitution\nI. Decayed with usually a very bad or disgusting smell → Putrid\nII. Humorous in a clever and often ironic way → Wry\nIII. In a state of tremulous excitement → Aflutter\nIdentify the pairs wherein the one-word substitution is correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"All three one-word substitutions are correct."},{"n":50,"q":"Fill in the blanks (X, Y) in the following sentences with the appropriate word/phrase given in the brackets:\nI. I spent X (less/the least) money on clothes in the whole family.\nII. The people who work the hardest make Y (a few/the fewest) mistakes.\nIdentify the words/phrases and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","o":["I—the least, II—the fewest","I—the least, II—a few","I—less, II—a few","I—less, II—the fewest"],"a":0,"e":"Superlative forms required: the least and the fewest"}],"source":"Corrected Word Source – NDA/NA English 2026 I"},{"id":"pyq-2025-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2025 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Why do twentieth century advances not seem sustainable?","o":["They do not meet the human needs","Due to the increased cost of agricultural production","Because they are outdated for the current times","They are useful, but intensive"],"a":1,"e":"The passage states that twentieth-century advances do not seem sustainable because of their increasing costs.","c":"Directions: Read the following passage carefully and answer Questions 1–5 on the basis of the passage only. Passage: Can agricultural productivity meet human needs in the long run? Since twentieth century advances, given increasing costs, do not seem sustainable, further increases in output will not be achieved easily. Global population growth, while showing signs of slowing down, has pushed farmers and herders into areas unsuited to intensive agriculture, accelerating worldwide ecological disruptions. Reserves of arable land and fresh water have diminished alarmingly. Chemical runoffs have compounded pollution problems. Invasion of natural habitats have hastened species extinction. Desertification has been severe in the last hundred years, especially in North Africa, southern portions of Africa, Australia, northern Mexico, and the American Southwest. Tropical forests have shrunk by one-half since World War II, with agricultural pressures causing three-quarters of that loss. Government intervention has produced its share of catastrophes, ranging from a terrible famine in China between 1958–1961 that may have killed 30 million people and destroyed the Aral Sea. Taking everything into consideration, we may remain hopeful about humankind's agricultural prospects, but we can scarcely look forward optimistically at the end of the first quarter of the twenty-first century."},{"n":2,"q":"What pushes farmers into places unsuited for agriculture?","o":["Population growth","Industrialisation","Water scarcity","Desertification"],"a":0,"e":"The passage explicitly says that global population growth has pushed farmers and herders into areas unsuitable for intensive agriculture."},{"n":3,"q":"Which one of the following is NOT true, according to the passage, for ecological degradation?","o":["Global population growth","Excess use of chemicals","Desertification","World War II"],"a":3,"e":"Population growth, chemical pollution and desertification contribute to ecological degradation. World War II is only used as a time reference for the decline of tropical forests."},{"n":4,"q":"What is the conclusion of the author in the passage about the prospects of agriculture in the world?","o":["The author is both hopeful and sceptical of the prospects of agriculture","The author is very hopeful of the prospects of\nagriculture","The author is sceptical of the prospects of agriculture","The author is neither hopeful nor sceptical of the prospects of agriculture"],"a":0,"e":"The author says we may remain hopeful, but can “scarcely look forward optimistically”; therefore, the conclusion combines hope with scepticism."},{"n":5,"q":"Which one of the following words means ‘incursion’?","o":["invasion","intensive","severe","intervention"],"a":0,"e":"Incursion means an entry or intrusion into a place, making invasion the closest synonym."},{"n":6,"q":"Convert from direct into indirect speech.\nAyushi said to the group, “We have had enough of suffering today. It is time to march forward.”","o":["Ayushi told the group that they had had enough of suffering that day and it was time to march forward","Ayushi asked the group that they had had enough of suffering this day and it was time to march forward","Ayushi told the group that they had enough of suffering and that it is time to march forward","Ayushi told the group that we had had enough of suffering this day and it was time to march forward"],"a":0,"e":"We changes to they, have had to had had, today to that day, and is to was in reported speech.","c":"Directions: Convert the following sentences from direct speech into indirect speech and choose the correct option."},{"n":7,"q":"Convert from direct speech into indirect speech.\n“Alas! We lost the match by one point,” said the Captain.","o":["The Captain regretted that they had lost the match by one point","The Captain regretted that they lost the match by one point","The Captain exclaimed that they had lost the match by one point","The Captain told the team that they had lost the match by one point"],"a":0,"e":"Alas! expresses sorrow or regret, so regretted is appropriate. Lost also backshifts to had lost."},{"n":8,"q":"Convert from direct speech into indirect speech.\n“Where were you last week?” Alok asked Sheela.","o":["Alok told Sheela where she had been the week before","Alok asked Sheela where she had been the week before","Alok asked Sheela where she was the week before","Alok asked Sheela where she had been the last week"],"a":1,"e":"In an indirect WH-question, were changes to had been, and last week becomes the week before."},{"n":9,"q":"The king who the people loved built the castle five centuries ago.","o":["The castle was built five centuries ago by the king who was loved by the people","The castle was built five centuries ago by the king who loved the people","The castle built five centuries ago by the king loved by the people","The castle was built five centuries ago by the people who were loved by the king"],"a":0,"e":"The castle becomes the subject, built becomes was built, and whom the people loved becomes who was loved by the people.","c":"Directions: Choose the option that correctly changes the given sentence from active voice to passive voice."},{"n":10,"q":"The company has recruited four hundred engineers who will have to complete an internship for three weeks.","o":["Four hundred engineers have been recruited by the company and an internship of three weeks will have to be completed by them","Four hundred engineers have been recruited by the company and they will have to complete an internship of three weeks","The company has been recruited by four hundred engineers and an internship of three week will have to be completed by them","Four hundred engineers have been recruited by the company. Internship of three weeks will have had to be completed by them"],"a":0,"e":"Both relevant clauses are correctly changed into passive: have been recruited and will have to be completed."},{"n":11,"q":"Seldom and Often","o":["Seldom means hardly ever and often means time and again","Seldom means time and again and often means hardly ever","Seldom means never and often means not at all","Seldom means frequently and often means time and again"],"a":0,"e":"Seldom means rarely, whereas often means frequently/repeatedly.","c":"Directions: In the following items, a pair of words is provided. You are required to select the option that most appropriately describes the meaning of both the words and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"Overview and Review","o":["Overview means assessment and review means general idea","Overview means general idea and review means assessment","Overview means general analysis and review means go through","Overview means summary and review means evaluation"],"a":1,"e":"An overview gives a general idea, while a review involves assessment or evaluation."},{"n":13,"q":"Overcome and Succeed","o":["Overcome means triumph over and succeed means to achieve something","Overcome means achieve something and succeed means to triumph over","Overcome means to be successful and succeed means to achieve something","Overcome means winning and succeed means to rise above"],"a":0,"e":"To overcome is to defeat or successfully deal with something, while succeed means to achieve the desired result."},{"n":14,"q":"Embed and Imbue","o":["Embed means instil and imbue means implant","Embed means implant and imbue means instil","Embed means accept and imbue means detest","Embed means include and imbue means accept"],"a":1,"e":"Embed means to fix or implant firmly, whereas imbue means to instil or fill with a quality/idea."},{"n":15,"q":"Emancipate and Empower","o":["Emancipate means authorize and empower means liberate","Emancipate means liberate and empower means authorise","Emancipate means liberal and empower means authorisation","Emancipate means freedom and empower means encounter"],"a":1,"e":"Emancipate means to free or liberate, while empower means to give authority or power."},{"n":16,"q":"The Professor has been able to INSTIL confidence in her students.","o":["inculcate","infer","eradicate","unlike"],"a":0,"e":"Instil means to gradually introduce an idea, attitude or feeling into someone's mind; inculcate is its closest synonym.","c":"Directions: Choose the word that is NEAREST IN MEANING to the highlighted word or expression.","t":"INSTIL"},{"n":17,"q":"The members of the group expressed their ALLEGIANCE to their leader.","o":["commitment","disloyalty","understanding","unlikely"],"a":0,"e":"Allegiance means loyalty, devotion or commitment to a person, group or cause.","t":"ALLEGIANCE"},{"n":18,"q":"The INSCRUTABILITY surrounding the incident remains unresolved until the end of the movie.","o":["clearness","comprehensibility","seriousness","mystery"],"a":3,"e":"Inscrutability refers to something difficult or impossible to understand, hence mystery.","t":"INSCRUTABILITY"},{"n":19,"q":"The DODGY behaviour of the leader led to her fall.","o":["principled","dubious","dishonest","painful"],"a":2,"e":"Dodgy can mean dishonest, suspicious or unreliable. In the context of the leader's behaviour, dishonest is the keyed answer.","t":"DODGY"},{"n":20,"q":"People have been advised not to venture into sea as the weather is likely to be INCLEMENT.","o":["squally","calm","pleasant","indigent"],"a":0,"e":"Inclement weather means unpleasant, stormy or severe weather; squally is the closest option.","t":"INCLEMENT"},{"n":21,"q":"S1. Sufism is an English word coined in the Nineteenth Century.\nS6. It may also have been suffa, the platform outside a place of Islamic worship.\nP. Historians have understood this term in several ways.\nQ. According to some scholars, it is derived from suf, meaning wool.\nR. Others derive it from safa, meaning purity.\nS. The word used for Sufism in Islamic texts is tasawwuf.","o":["Q R S P","S P Q R","P Q S R","R P S Q"],"a":1,"e":"S introduces tasawwuf; P says historians interpret the term differently; Q and R then give two possible derivations—suf and safa.","c":"Directions: Arrange the given sentence parts in the most logical and grammatically correct order."},{"n":22,"q":"S1. Al-Biruni was born in 973, in Khwarizm in present-day Uzbekistan.\nS6. He arrived in Ghazni as a hostage, but gradually developed a liking for the city.\nP. In 1017, when Sultan Mahmud invaded Khwarizm, he took several scholars back to his capital, Ghazni; Al-Biruni was one among them.\nQ. He was well versed in several languages; Syriac, Arabic, Persian, Hebrew, and Sanskrit.\nR. Khwarizm was an important centre of learning, and Al-Biruni received the best education available at the time.\nS. However he did not know Greek, although he was familiar with the works of Plato and other Greek philosophers.","o":["Q S R P","R Q S P","P Q S R","R P S Q"],"a":1,"e":"R continues from Al-Biruni's birthplace to his education; Q lists languages he knew; S contrasts these with Greek; and P explains how he reached Ghazni."},{"n":23,"q":"S1. Vijayanagara or ‘city of victory’ was the name of both a city and an empire.\nS6. They remembered it as Hampi, a name derived from that of the local mother goddess, Pampadevi.\nP. In its heyday it stretched from the river Krishna in the north to the extreme south of the Indian peninsula.\nQ. Although it fell into ruin in the seventeenth-eighteenth centuries, it continued to live in the memories of the people who inhabited the Krishna-Tungabhadra doab.\nR. In 1565 the city was sacked and subsequently deserted.\nS. The Empire was founded in the fourteenth century.","o":["S P R Q","S Q P R","P Q S R","P Q R S"],"a":0,"e":"The empire was founded (S), reached its heyday (P), was sacked (R), and later remained in people's memories (Q)—a clear chronological sequence."},{"n":24,"q":"S1. Liberalisation and globalisation freed India’s economy from the low GDP trap that had impeded India’s progress.\nS6. Today India has transformed into one of the largest economies of the world.\nP. India’s 3 pc rate of growth and a set of emergent circumstances led to a balance of payment crisis in the early 1990s.\nQ. Ease of business was ensured, and the requirement for import licences, and production quotas, were removed.\nR. Apart from these measures India also took a loan from the IMF to tide over the crisis, but after 1993, India has not taken another loan from the IMF.\nS. In a landmark budget in 1991, responding to the emergent crisis, India delicenced many industries and liberalised its economic policies.","o":["Q S R P","S Q P R","R Q S P","P S Q R"],"a":3,"e":"The economic crisis (P) leads to the 1991 reforms (S); the reforms are elaborated upon (Q); and the IMF loan is subsequently mentioned (R)."},{"n":25,"q":"S1. In many countries, there are millions of people who are underprivileged and deprived.\nS6. In the past forced labour was imposed by landlords, money-lenders, and other wealthy persons.\nP. Both of these are prohibited under the Constitution of India.\nQ. One such form of exploitation in our country has been begar or forced labour without payment.\nR. The marginalised are often subjected to exploitation by their fellow human beings.\nS. Another closely related form of exploitation is buying and selling of human beings and using them as slaves.","o":["Q S R P","R Q S P","P Q S R","Q S P R"],"a":1,"e":"R introduces exploitation of marginalised people; Q gives one form—begar; S gives another—slavery; and P refers back to both of these."},{"n":26,"q":"I can’t be bothered to tell him what I think; ______ he would not listen to me.","o":["meanwhile","in any case","in the meantime","somehow"],"a":1,"e":"In any case means anyway/regardless, fitting the idea that telling him is pointless because he would not listen.","c":"Directions: Choose the most appropriate discourse marker to complete each sentence."},{"n":27,"q":"______ if the referee points his fingers, this indicates that someone has done something wrong.","o":["On the contrary","Whatever more","Briefly","As regards"],"a":0,"e":"This is the answer prescribed by the supplied official key. In the reproduced sentence, however, the discourse relationship is somewhat unusual, so the item is best retained as keyed."},{"n":28,"q":"______, have you been to the local library yet?","o":["Sort of","Frankly","All the same","Incidentally"],"a":3,"e":"Incidentally is used to introduce an additional or casually related point: “Incidentally, have you been to the local library yet?”"},{"n":29,"q":"______, women are healthier and are living longer than ever before; indeed on an average, they can expect to live five years longer than men.","o":["To a large extent","As regards","As far as","Turning now"],"a":0,"e":"The expression means largely or substantially, appropriately qualifying the general statement about women's health and longevity."},{"n":30,"q":"The difference is that I have been fortunate to find a career that I love and, ______, I am getting paid reasonably for it.","o":["briefly","broadly speaking","what is more","in conclusion"],"a":2,"e":"What is more introduces additional information—he loves his career and, additionally, is reasonably paid."},{"n":31,"q":"______, the Government of India has followed the policy of trying to improve relations with its neighbours.","o":["Particularly","Knowingly","Otherwise","In general"],"a":3,"e":"In general means generally speaking and appropriately introduces the broad policy of the Government."},{"n":32,"q":"______, this issue is likely to be discussed when the Council meets next.","o":["As per reports","Accordingly","Likewise","Briefly"],"a":0,"e":"As per reports means according to available reports, appropriately indicating the source of the information."},{"n":33,"q":"______, you needed to consult a physician to have a clear idea of what the disease is about.","o":["In that case","In these case","Likewise","Henceforth"],"a":0,"e":"In that case means if that is the situation, naturally introducing the resulting advice to consult a physician."},{"n":34,"q":"______, in the ten-year-ago period the current account deficit stood at $1.3 billion or 0.2% of the GDP.","o":["Particularly","Notably","Seriously","Unknowingly"],"a":1,"e":"Notably means particularly or importantly, drawing attention to the significant economic figure."},{"n":35,"q":"______, the records have been completely undermined by shoddy book-keeping.","o":["Because of","As such","Forever","Hence\nIdentify the most appropriate option that defines the word/expression/jargon in each instance"],"a":1,"e":"As such functions here as a connective indicating the resulting situation concerning the records."},{"n":36,"q":"Infelicity:","o":["Disloyalty towards one’s nation","An inappropriate remark","To kill or destroy","To touch or push gently"],"a":1,"e":"Infelicity can mean an unfortunate, unsuitable or inappropriate expression/remark.","c":"Directions: Identify the most appropriate option that defines the given word, expression or jargon."},{"n":37,"q":"Corollary:","o":["Something that is too expensive","Something that looks real but does not really exist","Something that naturally follows","Something resembling a coal mine"],"a":2,"e":"A corollary is a result or consequence that naturally follows from something."},{"n":38,"q":"Quandary:","o":["Situation in which you are confused about what to do","Situation in which two people or group argue","Situation in which no progress can be made","Situation in which a relationship becomes friendlier"],"a":0,"e":"A quandary is a state of uncertainty or perplexity, especially when deciding what to do."},{"n":39,"q":"Presumption:","o":["Prediction based on past experiences","Judgement made with certainty","Decision based on facts","Opinion formed before having all the facts"],"a":3,"e":"A presumption is an assumption or belief made without complete evidence."},{"n":40,"q":"Smug:","o":["Too confident about one’s own achievements","Too eager to help or obey someone important","Too emotional or dramatic","Too eager to bottle up conflicts"],"a":0,"e":"Smug describes someone who is excessively self-satisfied or pleased with himself/herself."},{"n":41,"q":"P — of children they also contain deeply embedded\nQ — whereas traditional fairy tales\nR — moral lessons that are relevant even for adults\nS — were designed for the entertainment","o":["R P Q S","Q S R P","R P S Q","Q S P R"],"a":3,"e":"Correct sentence: “Whereas traditional fairy tales were designed for the entertainment of children, they also contain deeply embedded moral lessons that are relevant even for adults.”","c":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled as P, Q, R and S. Each sentence is followed by four sequences, namely (a), (b), (c) and (d), indicating the rearrangement of the parts. You are required to select the most appropriate option and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":42,"q":"P — of school education is an ineluctable truth\nQ — that sporting activities are an integral part\nR — meaningfully towards the overall growth of a student\nS — that underscores the learning process and contributes","o":["Q P S R","Q S R P","R P S Q","Q S P R"],"a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: “That sporting activities are an integral part of school education is an ineluctable truth that underscores the learning process and contributes meaningfully towards the overall growth of a student.”"},{"n":43,"q":"P — and contentment on account of a life lived\nQ — the beneficial outcome of\nR — well without retribution or rebuke\nS — a virtuous life is peace of mind","o":["S R Q P","S P Q R","Q S P R","Q S R P"],"a":2,"e":"Correct sentence: “The beneficial outcome of a virtuous life is peace of mind and contentment on account of a life lived well without retribution or rebuke.”"},{"n":44,"q":"P — more representative than the latter\nQ — from a non-parliamentary system\nR — in as much as the former is\nS — it is widely believed that the parliamentary system differs","o":["S Q R P","S P Q R","R S P Q","P S R Q"],"a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: “It is widely believed that the parliamentary system differs from a non-parliamentary system in as much as the former is more representative than the latter.”"},{"n":45,"q":"P — crushes the spirit behind the service\nQ — it stunts the person offering the same and\nR — exhibition or for fear of public opinion\nS — when service is done for","o":["S Q R P","S P Q R","R S P Q","S R Q P"],"a":3,"e":"Correct sentence: “When service is done for exhibition or for fear of public opinion, it stunts the person offering the same and crushes the spirit behind the service.”"},{"n":46,"q":"It does not look like he is going ______ in his studies than what was predicted by his school principal.","o":["any further","further","farther","any farther"],"a":0,"e":"Further is commonly used for figurative advancement or progress. With the negative construction does not look like, any further is appropriate: going any further in his studies.","c":"Directions: Fill in the blank with the most appropriate option."},{"n":47,"q":"______ all the confusion the thief made a quiet getaway.","o":["Among","Amongst","Amids","Amid"],"a":2,"e":"Amidst means in the middle of or surrounded by something. Here, the thief escaped amidst all the confusion. Note: The supplied Word source prints option C as “Amids”, while the answer explanation refers to “Amidst”; the quiz retains the supplied wording and keyed answer."},{"n":48,"q":"The intricate operation on the patient required the expertise of a/an ______ surgeon.","o":["special","especial","specialist","specialised"],"a":2,"e":"A specialist surgeon is a surgeon possessing specialised expertise in a particular field."},{"n":49,"q":"There are ______ thirty students inside the classroom at any time.","o":["less than","fewer than","less then","fewer then"],"a":1,"e":"Students is a plural countable noun, so fewer, not less, is required. The correct comparative expression is fewer than thirty students."},{"n":50,"q":"There are many ______ vehicles on the highway due to a sudden landslide.","o":["stationery","stationed","stranded","straggle"],"a":2,"e":"Stranded means left unable to move or proceed. Vehicles may become stranded on a highway because of a landslide."}],"source":"Corrected Word Source – NDA/NA English 2025 II"},{"id":"pyq-2025-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2025 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Which of the following is NOT the reason for the difficulty in estimating the number of languages of the world?","o":["New languages continue to be discovered","There are still unexplored regions","New languages are considered a dialect of a known language","People who speak the language do not claim their language"],"a":3,"e":"People who speak the language do not claim their language The passage mentions new discoveries, unexplored regions and initially mistaking a language for a dialect as difficulties. It does not say that speakers fail to claim their language.","c":"PASSAGE\nAn attempt to determine the number of languages in the world is affected by other factors. A few new languages do continue to be discovered, even these days, as unexplored regions of the world begin to be opened up. The discovery does not usually take place straight away. Often there are similarities with an already known language which make the investigators assume that what they have found is just a dialect of that language. Only after a considerable period of contact does it transpire that the speech is so different that it has to be considered a different language. It takes a language survey to establish the facts, and there are still many countries where such surveys are incomplete or have not even begun. The people may be known, but the identity of their language may not be. Because many such peoples are bilingual or multilingual, and converse with outsiders in lingua franca, it may take a while before linguists come to realize that there is an ethnic language there at all."},{"n":2,"q":"Which one of the following is a way to establish the discovery of a new language?","o":["By establishing contact over a period of time with the speakers of the language","By finding the similarities of the language with other languages","By comparing with the lingua franca of the region","By conducting a survey of all languages of the region"],"a":3,"e":"By conducting a survey of all languages of the region The passage explicitly says, “It takes a language survey to establish the facts.” Hence, a language survey establishes whether the speech constitutes a separate language."},{"n":3,"q":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","o":["Language surveys have been conducted by all nations","There is hardly any language to be discovered in the world","All the languages are either complete languages or dialects of other major languages","No new language needs to be discovered since all languages are known to the world"],"a":2,"e":"All the languages are either complete languages or dialects of other major languages Among the given alternatives, C is the intended answer. The passage distinguishes between an independent language and what may initially appear to be a dialect. Options A, B and D directly contradict the passage."},{"n":4,"q":"‘The people may be known, but the identity of their language may not be,’ means","o":["People are recognised as different ethnic groups but not necessarily their language","People are recognised as different ethnic groups and their language is recognised","People are not recognised as different ethnic groups, and so are their languages","Because the people are not from different ethnic groups and their language is recognised"],"a":0,"e":"People are recognised as different ethnic groups but not necessarily their language The people may already be known, while linguists may still be unaware that they possess a distinct ethnic language."},{"n":5,"q":"Which one of the following words from passage means ‘come to be known’?","o":["transpire","lingua franca","straight","variant"],"a":0,"e":"Transpire Transpire means to become known, apparent or revealed."},{"n":6,"q":"Convert from direct speech to indirect speech: Charu said to her friend, “I want you to be here at 6.00 p.m. tomorrow for the meeting.”","o":["Charu told her friend that she wanted her to be there at 6.00 p.m. the next day for the meeting","Charu told her friend that she wanted her to be there at 6.00 p.m. tomorrow for the meeting","Charu requested her friend that she wanted her to be there at 6.00 p.m. tomorrow for the meeting","Charu told her friend that she will want her to be there at 6.00 p.m. the next day for the meeting"],"a":0,"e":"Charu told her friend that she wanted her to be there at 6.00 p.m. the next day for the meeting. I → she; want → wanted; you → her; here → there; tomorrow → the next day. Therefore, A is the correct transformation.","c":"DIRECT AND INDIRECT SPEECH"},{"n":7,"q":"Convert from direct speech to indirect speech: Nitin said to his brother, “What a beautiful painting it is?”","o":["Nitin wondered to his brother that what a beautiful painting it was","Nitin wondered before his brother that it was a beautiful painting","Nitin exclaimed that it is a beautiful painting","Nitin asked his brother whether it was beautiful painting"],"a":1,"e":"Nitin wondered before his brother that it was a beautiful painting. The original is an exclamatory sentence expressing admiration. Among the options provided, B is the intended transformation. Option C incorrectly retains is instead of backshifting to was."},{"n":8,"q":"Convert from indirect speech to direct speech: The teacher asked her students why they had been quiet in the previous class.","o":["The teacher asked her students, “Why were they keeping quiet in the previous class?”","The teacher said to her students, “Why were you quiet in the previous class?”","The teacher said to her students, “Why had you been quiet in the previous class?”","The teacher said to her students, “Why were you quiet in previous class?”"],"a":1,"e":"The teacher said to her students, “Why were you quiet in the previous class?” In converting back to direct speech, they becomes you, and had been quiet returns to were quiet."},{"n":9,"q":"Which one of the following sentences is correct?","o":["He is considered as the brightest intellectual in the country","He is considered as one among the brightest intellectual in the country","He is considered as one among the brighter intellectual in the country","He is considered as one of the intellectual in the country"],"a":0,"e":"He is considered as the brightest intellectual in the country. Among the supplied options, A is the intended correct sentence. The other alternatives contain errors such as one among the brightest intellectual and one of the intellectual.","c":"Directions: Following items have four sentences, one of which is correct. Find the correct option and mark your response in the Answer Sheet."},{"n":10,"q":"Which one of the following sentences is correct?","o":["No other mountain is taller than the Himalayas","No other mountain is tall than the Himalayas","No other mountains taller than the Himalayas","No other mountain is taller then the Himalayas"],"a":0,"e":"No other mountain is taller than the Himalayas. The comparative construction taller than is grammatically correct. Tall than and taller then are incorrect."},{"n":11,"q":"Confident and Confidant","o":["Confident means certain and confidant means close friend","Confidant means certain and confident means close friend","Confident means belief and confidant means an emissary","Confident means assurance and confidant means intimate person"],"a":0,"e":"Confident = certain; Confidant = close friend Confident means sure or certain; a confidant is a trusted person or close friend with whom secrets are shared.","c":"Directions: In the following items, a pair of words is provided. You are required to select the option that most appropriately describes the meaning of both the words and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"Broke and brook","o":["Broke means abundance and brook means a flowing water body","Broke means bankrupt and brook means a stream","Broke means fractured and brook means nonsense","Broke means negotiate and brook means a stream"],"a":1,"e":"Broke = bankrupt; Brook = a stream To be broke means to have no money, while a brook is a small stream."},{"n":13,"q":"Accept and Except","o":["Accept means apart from and except means recognise","Accept means recognise and except means include","Accept means consent and except means apart from","Accept means allude and except means apart from"],"a":2,"e":"Accept = consent; Except = apart from Accept means to agree to or receive; except means excluding or apart from."},{"n":14,"q":"Accord and Accrued","o":["Accord means collected and accrued means agreement","Accord means agreement and accrued means arrived","Accord means accepted and accrued means received","Accord means agreement and accrued means accumulated"],"a":3,"e":"Accord = agreement; Accrued = accumulated An accord is an agreement, while something accrued has accumulated over time."},{"n":15,"q":"Guarantee and Warranty","o":["Guarantee is an agreement and warranty is a service contract","Guarantee is a business agreement and warranty is a service contract","Guarantee is a promise and warranty is a service contract","Guarantee is acceptance and warranty is a service contract"],"a":2,"e":"Guarantee = a promise; Warranty = a service contract A guarantee is an assurance or promise, whereas a warranty is a formal assurance regarding a product/service."},{"n":16,"q":"The Constitution of India ensures PROPORTIONATE representation from all regions.","o":["balanced","distant","partial","suffragette"],"a":0,"e":"Balanced Proportionate means corresponding in size, degree or proper proportion; balanced is the closest option.","c":"SYNONYMS","t":"PROPORTIONATE"},{"n":17,"q":"There is a feeling of DISENCHANTMENT among the members of the group.","o":["delight","disappointment","idealism","unrelenting"],"a":1,"e":"Disappointment Disenchantment means disappointment resulting from discovering that something is not as good as expected.","t":"DISENCHANTMENT"},{"n":18,"q":"She believed that it was IMMINENT that he would be chosen as the leader of the group.","o":["timely","distant","unlikely","inevitable"],"a":3,"e":"Inevitable Imminent strictly means about to happen very soon. Of the given alternatives, inevitable is the intended/closest answer.","t":"IMMINENT"},{"n":19,"q":"The brave soldiers left an INDELIBLE impression on the people of the land.","o":["permanent","fleeting","hilarious","eradicable"],"a":0,"e":"Permanent Indelible means impossible to remove or erase, hence permanent.","t":"INDELIBLE"},{"n":20,"q":"The manager always provides INSTANTANEOUS replies to all queries.","o":["immediate","delayed","deliberate","unwanted"],"a":0,"e":"Immediate Instantaneous means occurring immediately or without delay.","t":"INSTANTANEOUS"},{"n":21,"q":"S1: Although all sources of energy ultimately come from natural processes, non-renewable resources cannot be replaced naturally at the rate they are being used. S6: The sustainable use of natural resources in a manner that provides the maximum benefit of these resources to humans over a period of time can be termed as conservation. P: This will increase the time and cost of mining and once these resources are used up they cannot be replaced. Q: Hence, we must remember that though our country is rich in mineral deposits these resources are short-lived. R: Extraction of these ores through the process of mining will soon become difficult and very expensive because these minerals have to be mined from greater depths over time. S: Mineral resources can be said to be finite and non-renewable.","o":["QPRS","SPQR","SQRP","SQPR"],"a":3,"e":"S–Q–P–R Mineral resources can be said to be finite and non- renewable → Hence...resources are short-lived → This will increase the time and cost... → Extraction...will soon become difficult... This follows the intended logical discussion of finite mineral resources.","c":"ORDERING OF SENTENCES"},{"n":22,"q":"S1: India, at present, has one of the largest road networks in the world. S6: It passes through Howrah, Delhi and Amritsar and terminates in Kabul (Afghanistan). P: The importance of roads has been recognised in India since the ancient times. Q: The Grand Trunk Road was built by Sher Shah Suri across the Indo-Gangetic plain, from Chittagong (Bangladesh) to Peshawar (Pakistan). R: Kings such as Ashoka and Chandragupta built roads for easy transportation of goods and people. S: Construction of roads continued as an important activity in the late medieval period.","o":["QPRS","SPQR","PQRS","PRSQ"],"a":3,"e":"P–R–S–Q Roads were important in ancient times (P) → Ashoka and Chandragupta built them (R) → construction continued into the late medieval period (S) → Sher Shah Suri built the Grand Trunk Road (Q). The sequence is chronological."},{"n":23,"q":"S1: National highways connect one state with another and are of national importance. S6: These road systems are also known as primary road systems and are laid and operated under the supervision of the National Highway Authority of India. P: They are important because whereas they constitute merely two per cent of the total road networks, they nevertheless carry 40 per cent of the total road traffic. Q: The road infrastructure of the country is therefore crucial, and their construction and maintenance is of critical importance. R: They bear the load of traffic because these roads connect long distances and pass through major cities and towns. S: Since they cover the length and breadth of the nation and connect cities and towns, these highways are the primary facilitators of trade and connectivity.","o":["PRSQ","SPQR","PRQS","SQRP"],"a":1,"e":"S–P–Q–R S explains the nationwide importance of highways; P gives their traffic significance; Q draws the conclusion about infrastructure; R further explains why they carry heavy traffic."},{"n":24,"q":"S1: The South African Constitution was inaugurated in December 1996. S6: A special constitutional court enforces the rights enshrined in the Constitution. P: Its creation and promulgation took place at a time when South Africa still faced the threat of a civil war after the dissolution of the Apartheid Government. Q: The South African Constitution says that its “Bill of Rights is a cornerstone of democracy in South Africa.” R: Apropos, it forbids discrimination on the grounds of “race, gender, pregnancy, marital status, ethnic or social origin, colour, age, disability, religion, conscience, belief, culture, language and birth”. S: The Bill of Rights grants perhaps the most extensive range of rights to the citizens.","o":["QSRP","SPQR","PQRS","PQSR"],"a":3,"e":"P–Q–S–R P gives the historical context of the Constitution → Q introduces the Bill of Rights → S explains its extensive rights → R gives examples of prohibited discrimination."},{"n":25,"q":"S1: Our Constitution reminds us of the necessity of representation in a large democracy. S6: Elections have today become the most visible symbol of the democratic process. P: This is why elections become important. Q: Therefore representatives are elected by the people. R: Whenever we think of India as a democracy, our mind invariably turns to our successful elections. S: All citizens cannot participate in taking every decision.","o":["QSRP","SPQR","PQSR","SQPR"],"a":3,"e":"S–Q–P–R Not all citizens can participate in every decision (S) → therefore representatives are elected (Q) → hence elections become important (P) → this leads naturally to India's successful elections (R)."},{"n":26,"q":"______ your transfer request is concerned, we'll be discussing it at the next meeting.","o":["As far as","To begin with","By the way","Talking about"],"a":0,"e":"As far as The standard expression is “As far as X is concerned”, meaning regarding X.","c":"USE CORRECT DISCOURSE MARKERS/EXPRESSIONS"},{"n":27,"q":"______, did you know Ravi has a new job?","o":["For one thing","By the way","In the first place","As a result"],"a":1,"e":"By the way By the way introduces a new or incidental point in conversation."},{"n":28,"q":"______ the wind, it was getting stronger, and I was getting colder.","o":["To begin with","By the way","First of all","Speaking of"],"a":3,"e":"Speaking of Speaking of the wind means with reference to the topic just mentioned."},{"n":29,"q":"______, what are you doing in my room?","o":["On the other hand","Excuse me","Look there","Eventually"],"a":1,"e":"Excuse me Excuse me appropriately introduces a question expressing surprise or seeking an explanation."},{"n":30,"q":"______, an unhappy childhood leads to criminal behaviour.","o":["Still if","Even somewhat","In some cases","In progression"],"a":2,"e":"In some cases It limits the statement: an unhappy childhood may lead to criminal behaviour in certain cases, not invariably."},{"n":31,"q":"______ the problem is nothing but a wrong assumption about processes.","o":["In the meanwhile","Somewhat","Engagingly","In other words"],"a":3,"e":"In other words In other words is used to restate or clarify an idea in a different way."},{"n":32,"q":"Many, ______, hesitate to publicly broach such question for fear of nurturing discrimination.","o":["understandably","despite","until","of course"],"a":0,"e":"Understandably The sentence means that many people understandably hesitate because they fear encouraging discrimination."},{"n":33,"q":"______ one notes that in the nineteenth century an important development of reflection and questioning gained momentum.","o":["Particularly speaking","Generally speaking","Otherwise","Of course"],"a":1,"e":"Generally speaking Generally speaking introduces a broad or general observation."},{"n":34,"q":"______ the situation has completely transformed over the past few years.","o":["Somewhat","Believably","Thus","Predominantly"],"a":2,"e":"Thus Thus means therefore/consequently, introducing a resulting statement."},{"n":35,"q":"______ the committee is of the opinion that all academic matters will be discussed in the Executive Body Meeting.","o":["Hence","But","Later","Although"],"a":0,"e":"Hence Hence means therefore/as a consequence and appropriately introduces the committee's conclusion."},{"n":36,"q":"Cynosure:","o":["Person or thing that causes a change","Person or thing that attracts a lot of attention","Person or thing regarded as exact copy","Person or animal that lives in a particular place"],"a":1,"e":"Person or thing that attracts a lot of attention A cynosure is a person or thing that is the centre of attention or admiration.","c":"Directions: Given below are words, followed by their appropriate meaning. Identify the correct meaning and mark your answer accordingly in the Answer Sheet.","t":"Cynosure"},{"n":37,"q":"Coeval:","o":["Person of roughly the same age","Person or organisation that cooperates with others","Person employed to drive a private car","Person employed in taking census"],"a":0,"e":"Person of roughly the same age A coeval is a person of the same age or from the same period.","t":"Coeval"},{"n":38,"q":"Retrogression:","o":["Sudden sharp drop in price","Reverse pressure","Return to earlier state","Sudden occurrence of a past event"],"a":2,"e":"Return to earlier state Retrogression means movement backwards or return to a less advanced/earlier condition.","t":"Retrogression"},{"n":39,"q":"Imprest:","o":["Surprise attack by people","Money used to manage small expenses","Sudden occurrence of laughter","Sudden increase in activity"],"a":1,"e":"Money used to manage small expenses An imprest is a fixed amount of money provided for minor or routine expenses.","t":"Imprest"},{"n":40,"q":"Turgid:","o":["Determined or loyal","Dirty or untidy","Swollen or distended","Excited or upset"],"a":2,"e":"Swollen or distended Turgid literally means swollen or distended; figuratively, it can also describe excessively ornate language.","t":"Turgid"},{"n":41,"q":"P — participate in the function Q — many of the people who had come to R — the auditorium was over-crowded S — could not find a seat because","o":["PRQS","PQRS","QPSR","SPRQ"],"a":2,"e":"Q–P–S–R Correct sentence: “Many of the people who had come to participate in the function could not find a seat because the auditorium was over-crowded.”","c":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled as P, Q, R and S. The parts are rearranged in four sequences, namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to select the most appropriate option that sequences the parts correctly and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":42,"q":"P — lay outside her existing life Q — she knew that the answer R — somewhere in the back of her mind S — to her questions about life","o":["RPQS","QSPR","QPSR","RQSP"],"a":1,"e":"Q–S–P–R Correct sentence: “She knew that the answer to her questions about life lay outside her existing life somewhere in the back of her mind.”"},{"n":43,"q":"P — works of literature Q — inside the pages of all good R — lies the truth about some of S — the more challenging questions posed by humanity","o":["PRQS","QPRS","QPSR","RPSQ"],"a":1,"e":"Q–P–R–S Correct sentence: “Inside the pages of all good works of literature lies the truth about some of the more challenging questions posed by humanity.”"},{"n":44,"q":"P — takes on account of its economic successes Q — to address continuing social inequality R — for every stride of progress any nation S — it takes two back if it is unable","o":["PRQS","QPRS","RPSQ","RPQS"],"a":2,"e":"R–P–S–Q Correct sentence: “For every stride of progress any nation takes on account of its economic successes, it takes two back if it is unable to address continuing social inequality.”"},{"n":45,"q":"P — the book-value of a loan or Q — an intangible asset over a set period of time R — used to periodically lower S — amortisation is an accounting technique","o":["SRPQ","RSPQ","SRQP","RPSQ"],"a":0,"e":"S–R–P–Q Correct sentence: “Amortisation is an accounting technique used to periodically lower the book-value of a loan or an intangible asset over a set period of time.”"},{"n":46,"q":"There is more than ______ to get the job done.","o":["one way","one ways","any ways","any way"],"a":0,"e":"One way The fixed construction is “There is more than one way to…” Hence, one way is correct.","c":"FILL IN THE BLANKS"},{"n":47,"q":"The class decided to organise ______.","o":["picnic","a picnic","picnics","the picnic"],"a":1,"e":"A picnic Picnic is a singular countable noun here and requires the indefinite article: organise a picnic."},{"n":48,"q":"The accused ______ before the judge.","o":["tried","was trial","sat trial","stood trial"],"a":3,"e":"Stood trial The standard idiom is stand trial, meaning to be tried in a court of law. Therefore: The accused stood trial before the judge."},{"n":49,"q":"The manner in which the demolitions have been carried out ______ scrutiny by the High Court.","o":["was under","under","is under","however under"],"a":2,"e":"Is under The subject is singular—the manner—so the correct construction is: The manner... is under scrutiny by the High Court."},{"n":50,"q":"I ______ consider the opinions of all stakeholders before taking a final decision","o":["will not","have","will","shall"],"a":2,"e":"The supplied corrected Word answer key marks option C: will."}],"source":"Corrected Word Source – NDA/NA English 2025 I"},{"id":"pyq-2024-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2024 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"All human beings are FALLIBLE","o":["frail","erring","mortal","perfect"],"a":3,"e":"Perfect — Fallible means capable of making mistakes. Its opposite is perfect/infallible.","t":"FALLIBLE","cat":"Antonyms"},{"n":2,"q":"His words were VAGUE","o":["identical","obscure","precise","imperceptible"],"a":2,"e":"Precise — Vague means unclear or indefinite; precise means exact and clearly defined.","t":"VAGUE","cat":"Antonyms"},{"n":3,"q":"She performed all her duties CONSCIENTIOUSLY","o":["assiduously","carelessly","painstakingly","scrupulously"],"a":1,"e":"Carelessly — Conscientiously means carefully, responsibly and diligently. Its opposite is carelessly.","t":"CONSCIENTIOUSLY","cat":"Antonyms"},{"n":4,"q":"These risks are INCIDENTAL to the work of a firefighter","o":["essential","adventitious","serendipitous","subsidiary"],"a":0,"e":"Essential — Incidental means secondary, minor or accompanying rather than essential. Hence, essential is the opposite.","t":"INCIDENTAL","cat":"Antonyms"},{"n":5,"q":"She has always been DISDAINFUL of poor people","o":["reverential","derisive","supercilious","insolent"],"a":0,"e":"Reverential — Disdainful means showing contempt or lack of respect; reverential means showing deep respect.","t":"DISDAINFUL","cat":"Antonyms"},{"n":6,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 6.","o":["independent","dependent","relevant","consequent"],"a":0,"e":"Independent — The sentence says we tend to believe agriculture is independent of natural biota, but the author immediately corrects this belief: “This is not true.”","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","c":"Passage: Most of our food comes from agriculture, so we tend to believe that it is ______ 6. of natural biota. This is not true. In nature, ______ 7. the plants and animals ______ 8. to the rigours of natural selection. Only the fittest survive. ______ 9., wild populations have numerous traits for competitiveness, resistance to parasites, ______ 10. to adverse conditions, ______ 11. and other aspects of vigour. In contrast, populations grown for many ______ 12. under the pampered conditions of agriculture tend to lose these traits, because they ______ 13. for production, not resilience. For example, a high-producing plant ______ 14. lacks resistance to drought is ______ 15. and the resistance to drought is ignored. Also, in the process of breeding plants for maximum production, all genetic variation is eliminated.","d":"Directions (6-15): In this section, there is a passage that has some blank spaces, each with four words or group of words. Select whichever word or group of words you consider the most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":7,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 7.","o":["the","both","some","among"],"a":1,"e":"Both — The correct construction is “both the plants and animals...”","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":8,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 8.","o":["had been continuously subjected","will be continuously subject","continuously subjected","are continuously subjected"],"a":3,"e":"Are continuously subjected — A plural subject (plants and animals) requires are, and the passive construction are subjected to is correct.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":9,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 9.","o":["Consequently","Inadvertently","Although","Since"],"a":0,"e":"Consequently — It introduces the result of continuous natural selection: consequently, wild populations possess competitive and resistant traits.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":10,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 10.","o":["withdrawal","tolerance","adaptable","compliance"],"a":1,"e":"Tolerance — The natural expression is “tolerance to adverse conditions.”","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":11,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 11.","o":["and","with","by","upon"],"a":0,"e":"And — It connects tolerance to adverse conditions with the following additional qualities.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":12,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 12.","o":["eras","epochs","periods","generations"],"a":3,"e":"Generations — Agricultural populations are selectively bred for many generations.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":13,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 13.","o":["had selected","have selected","are selected","might select"],"a":2,"e":"Are selected — The populations are selected for production, requiring passive voice.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":14,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 14.","o":["whichever","whom","that","whenever"],"a":2,"e":"That — That correctly introduces the relative clause modifying plant: “a high-producing plant that lacks...”","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":15,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 15.","o":["heated","irrigated","showered","deserted"],"a":1,"e":"Irrigated — If a high-producing plant lacks drought resistance, it is irrigated, while its lack of natural drought resistance is ignored.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":16,"q":"Preeti suggests","o":["that I ask to your advice","that I had asked your advice","that I can have asked your advice","that I ask your advice"],"a":3,"e":"that I ask your advice — After suggest, the subjunctive construction suggests that I ask is correct. Also, we say ask your advice, not ask to your advice.","cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions: Each of the following items features one part of a sentence followed by four alternatives. Complete the sentence by choosing the correct alternative."},{"n":17,"q":"We saw","o":["the older of all monuments in the city","the old of all monuments in the city","the oldest monument in the city","an oldest of all monuments in the city"],"a":2,"e":"the oldest monument in the city — When comparing one thing with all others, the superlative degree (oldest) is required.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":18,"q":"Since you have been touring all day","o":["you must be tired","you ought to have been tired","you could had been tired","you might not be tired"],"a":0,"e":"you must be tired — Must expresses a strong logical deduction based on the fact that the person has been touring all day.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":19,"q":"His condition is serious, but","o":["it should be worse","it ought to be worse","it could be worse","it had been going to be worse"],"a":2,"e":"it could be worse — The idiomatic construction means that although the condition is serious, a worse situation is possible.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":20,"q":"I hope the bus comes soon because","o":["we waited for half an hour","we have been waiting for half an hour","we waiting for half an hour","we could have been waiting for half an hour"],"a":1,"e":"we have been waiting for half an hour — The action began in the past and is still continuing; therefore, the present perfect continuous is appropriate.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":21,"q":"Someone","o":["has spilt juice on the sofa","should have spilt juice on the sofa","have spilt juice on the sofa","would spilt juice on the sofa"],"a":0,"e":"has spilt juice on the sofa — Someone is grammatically singular, so has, not have, is required.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":22,"q":"What would you do","o":["if you found your lost toy?","if you will have found your lost toy?","if you would found your lost toy?","if you have find your lost toy?"],"a":0,"e":"if you found your lost toy? — This is a second conditional: What would you do if + simple past...?","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":23,"q":"If I were you","o":["I shouldn't buy that dress","I wouldn't buy that dress","I weren't buying that dress","I mustn't buying that dress"],"a":1,"e":"I wouldn't buy that dress — The standard advice construction is “If I were you, I wouldn't...”","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":24,"q":"I wish","o":["I could help you","I ought to help you","I did help you","I must help you"],"a":0,"e":"I could help you — I wish I could... expresses a present inability or unrealised desire.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":25,"q":"The efforts of the security services have not been quite","o":["upto the incredibility","up-take","up-tight","upto the mark"],"a":3,"e":"upto the mark — Up to the mark means reaching the required or expected standard.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":26,"q":"A white lie","o":["An offensive way of describing something","To do something completely impossible","A lie that is absolutely and universally true","A lie that you tell to avoid hurting someone"],"a":3,"e":"A lie that you tell to avoid hurting someone — A white lie is a minor or harmless lie, usually told to avoid hurting someone's feelings.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","c":"IDIOMS AND PHRASES","t":"A white lie"},{"n":27,"q":"Bad blood","o":["Feelings of hatred between people","A situation which goes from bad to worse","A fatal accident","To accept the unpleasant situation"],"a":0,"e":"Feelings of hatred between people — Bad blood means hostility, resentment or ill feeling between people.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Bad blood"},{"n":28,"q":"Count your blessings","o":["to start to understand how badly something has affected you","an event which gradually becomes worse","to be grateful for good things in your life","something that you say in order to warn someone"],"a":2,"e":"To be grateful for good things in your life — Count your blessings means to appreciate the good things one has.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Count your blessings"},{"n":29,"q":"Have the last laugh","o":["to be the worst example of something","to succeed when others thought that you would not","to solve all the problems of life","to laugh at someone secretly"],"a":1,"e":"To succeed when others thought that you would not — Have the last laugh means to eventually succeed or be proved right after others doubted or mocked you.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Have the last laugh"},{"n":30,"q":"Sit on the fence","o":["to make someone suddenly notice you","to fail completely","to delay making a decision","to behave very badly"],"a":2,"e":"To delay making a decision — Sit on the fence means to avoid choosing between alternatives or taking a definite position.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Sit on the fence"},{"n":31,"q":"P — good quality elementary education conforming\nQ — is a fundamental right of all children\nR — the Right to Education Act (RTE) mandates\nS — to the standards and norms specified in the Act","o":["QSPR","RPSQ","RQSP","SQPR"],"a":1,"e":"R–P–S–Q — Correct sentence: “The Right to Education Act (RTE) mandates good quality elementary education conforming to the standards and norms specified in the Act is a fundamental right of all children.” The sequence given by the official key is RPSQ.","cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence have four sequences, namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to rearrange the jumbled parts of the sentences and mark your response accordingly."},{"n":32,"q":"P — the conflicts and disputes in their mind\nQ — music not only relieves the burden\nR — on children and the young\nS — but also alleviates","o":["QRSP","PSRQ","QPRS","PQRS"],"a":0,"e":"Q–R–S–P — Correct sentence: “Music not only relieves the burden on children and the young but also alleviates the conflicts and disputes in their mind.” Not only...but also determines the sequence.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":33,"q":"P — but it is virtually covered by a dome-shaped\nQ — Antarctica is a continent\nR — nearly twice as large as Australia\nS — ice sheet nearly 2 miles thick","o":["QRPS","SPRQ","QSPR","SQRP"],"a":0,"e":"Q–R–P–S — Correct sentence: “Antarctica is a continent nearly twice as large as Australia but it is virtually covered by a dome-shaped ice sheet nearly 2 miles thick.”","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":34,"q":"P — the global economic weight from the advanced economies\nQ — that there was a major shift in\nR — and Central Bank Governors, in recognition of the fact\nS — the G20 was formed in 1999, as a forum of Finance Ministers","o":["SPQR","PQRS","QRSP","SRQP"],"a":3,"e":"S–R–Q–P — Correct sentence: “The G20 was formed in 1999, as a forum of Finance Ministers and Central Bank Governors, in recognition of the fact that there was a major shift in the global economic weight from the advanced economies.”","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":35,"q":"P — aquaculture nation in the world and\nQ — India's products are exported to many nations\nR — fish producing and the second largest\nS — India is the third largest","o":["SRPQ","QPRS","RPQS","PQRS"],"a":0,"e":"S–R–P–Q — Correct sentence: “India is the third largest fish producing and the second largest aquaculture nation in the world and India's products are exported to many nations.”","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":36,"q":"ADDERS are the only poisonous snakes found across Britain","o":["Noun","Adjective","Adverb","Determiner"],"a":0,"e":"Noun — Adders is the name of a type of snake; therefore, it is a noun.","cat":"Word Class","t":"ADDERS","d":"Directions: Each of the following sentences has word(s) underlined. Read the sentence carefully and find which word class the underlined word(s) belong(s) to. Indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":37,"q":"She spoke to him once SINCE he has been in town","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Conjunction","Preposition"],"a":3,"e":"Preposition — In since he has been in town, the official key marks D. Here since introduces the time reference connected with the following clause.","cat":"Word Class","t":"SINCE"},{"n":38,"q":"I don't really want to go. BESIDES, it's too late now","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Preposition","Conjunction"],"a":1,"e":"Adverb — Besides means in addition/moreover and functions adverbially as a linking expression here.","cat":"Word Class","t":"BESIDES"},{"n":39,"q":"Look at THAT man over there","o":["Adverb","Conjunction","Determiner","Article"],"a":2,"e":"Determiner — In that man, that determines which man is being referred to; hence, it is a demonstrative determiner.","cat":"Word Class","t":"THAT"},{"n":40,"q":"The plane is ready for TAKE-OFF","o":["Noun","Conjunction","Verb","Gerund"],"a":0,"e":"Noun — Take-off names the act/process of an aircraft leaving the ground and functions as a noun after the preposition for.","cat":"Word Class","t":"TAKE-OFF"},{"n":41,"q":"What according to the passage is the 'unwelcome form of the globalization'?\n1. Globalization of the diseases\n2. Elusive goal of globalization\n3. History of fatal diseases\n4. Selective use of antibiotics\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2, 3 and 4","3 only","1 and 4"],"a":0,"e":"1 only — Globalisation of diseases — The passage explicitly calls “the globalisation of disease” an unwelcome form of globalisation.","cat":"Reading Comprehension","c":"Passage: We live in a time when globalisation is rapidly encompassing travel, information, trade and investment. The internet ties people together in ways unimagined a few years ago. The globalisation of health, however, remains an elusive goal, similar to the globalisation of economic well-being. Laurie Garrett, in The Coming Plague, describes an unwelcome form of globalisation: the globalisation of disease. Garrett examines the recent history of emerging diseases such as AIDS, Ebola, Hantavirus, Rift Valley Fever, Legionnaires' disease, and others. She also explains the resurgence of familiar diseases like tuberculosis, cholera, and pneumonia as a consequence of the widespread and unwise use of antibiotics. Many of the new diseases are clearly linked to changes in land use, which brings humans into close contacts with rodents or other animals that harbour viruses previously unknown to medicine and often deadly to humans. Resurgent diseases, by contrast, are a creation of our medical practice. By treating people with antibiotics without restraint, we unknowingly select strains that are immune to the antibiotics and that pass on their resistant genes to unrelated bacteria by way of plasmid transfer. The heroes of her book are the women and men on the frontlines of epidemiology. Garrett makes a plea for a greater commitment from our universities, medical schools, and government agencies to train workers who will be capable of recognizing new diseases and who will be able to move about equally well in the laboratory, the hospital and the field in pursuit of knowledge and public-health intervention around the world.","d":"Directions: Read the following passage carefully and answer Questions 41–45 on the basis of the passage."},{"n":42,"q":"According to the passage, globalisation of health has become imperative due to","o":["the globalisation of diseases","public health intervention","the creation of new medical systems","the indiscriminate use of antibiotics"],"a":3,"e":"The indiscriminate use of antibiotics — The passage highlights the widespread and unwise/unrestrained use of antibiotics, which has contributed to the resurgence of diseases.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":43,"q":"According to the passage, resurgent diseases are affected by:\n1. Medical practices\n2. Overuse of antibiotics\n3. Emergence of new strains\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","1 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"e":"1, 2 and 3 — Resurgent diseases are linked to medical practices, overuse of antibiotics, and the resulting selection of antibiotic-resistant strains.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":44,"q":"According to the passage, many new diseases are emerging due to change in land use because","o":["humans are coming in contact with unfamiliar viruses","urbanisation makes pathogens proliferate","humans become adaptive and pass on their genes to related bacteria by way of plasmid transfer","humans are inadvertently releasing new viruses"],"a":0,"e":"Humans are coming in contact with unfamiliar viruses — Changes in land use bring humans into closer contact with animals harbouring viruses previously unknown to medicine.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":45,"q":"What is the meaning of the word \"epidemiology\" in the passage?","o":["The historical study of the spread of diseases","The scientific study of the spread and control of diseases","The scientific study of the invention and uses of drugs","The scientific study of the use of drugs"],"a":1,"e":"The scientific study of the spread and control of diseases — Epidemiology is the scientific study of the distribution, spread and control of diseases in populations.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":46,"q":"Sages and seers have cautioned that the path towards enlightenment is full of illusory traps","o":["reasoned","admitted","warned","refused"],"a":2,"e":"Warned — Cautioned means warned or advised someone about possible danger.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"cautioned","c":"SYNONYMS"},{"n":47,"q":"The officer reported the details of the mission in a monotonous manner","o":["dull","happy","disturbed","enthusiastic"],"a":0,"e":"Dull — Monotonous means dull, repetitive and lacking variety.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"monotonous"},{"n":48,"q":"There are many elusive questions in philosophy","o":["clear","baffling","paramount","truthful"],"a":1,"e":"Baffling — Elusive in this context means difficult to understand, grasp or answer; baffling is closest.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"elusive"},{"n":49,"q":"Cleaning this mess is a mammoth task","o":["huge","quick","easy","intelligent"],"a":0,"e":"Huge — Mammoth figuratively means enormous, immense or huge.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"mammoth"},{"n":50,"q":"The police department's image was tarnished after the high-profile murder case","o":["enhanced","tainted","restored","assigned"],"a":1,"e":"Tainted — Tarnished means damaged, stained or harmed in reputation; tainted is the closest synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"tarnished"}],"source":"Corrected Word Source – NDA/NA English 2024 II"},{"id":"pyq-2024-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2024 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Mohan was living in a DEPLORABLE condition.","o":["reprehensible","opprobrious","despicable","commendable"],"a":3,"e":"Commendable — Deplorable means very bad, disgraceful or deserving condemnation. Commendable means deserving praise, so it is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DEPLORABLE","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words or group of words. Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"These exercises will COUNTERACT the effects of stress and tension.","o":["exacerbate","foil","thwart","contravene"],"a":0,"e":"Exacerbate — Counteract means to reduce or neutralise the effect of something. Exacerbate means to make something worse, hence the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"COUNTERACT"},{"n":3,"q":"You have to PERSEVERE with difficult students.","o":["give up","persist","carry on","stand firm"],"a":0,"e":"Give up — Persevere means to continue despite difficulty; give up means to stop trying.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PERSEVERE"},{"n":4,"q":"Their new colour scheme is HIDEOUS.","o":["appalling","beautiful","grotesque","macabre"],"a":1,"e":"Beautiful — Hideous means extremely ugly or unpleasant; beautiful is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"HIDEOUS"},{"n":5,"q":"Everywhere they go, they SQUANDER their earnings.","o":["fritter away","splurge","squabble","manage"],"a":3,"e":"Manage — Squander means to waste money/resources recklessly. Manage here conveys careful handling rather than waste.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SQUANDER"},{"n":6,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 6.","o":["passionately","continually","deadly","thoughtfully"],"a":1,"e":"Continually — Continually changing and evolving means that the Earth's climate keeps changing over time.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: In this section, there is a passage having some blank spaces, each blank space is followed by four individual words or group of words. Select a word or group of words you consider the most appropriate for filling the blank space, and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","c":"Passage: The Earth's climate is ______ 6. changing and evolving. Some of ______ 7. changes have been due to natural causes but others can be attributed to ______ 8. activities such as deforestation, emissions from industry and transport, etc., which have led to the ______ 9. of gases and aerosols in ______ 10. atmosphere. These gases are known as greenhouse gases because they trap heat and ______ 11. air temperatures near the ground, acting like a greenhouse on the surface of the ______ 12. In order to create and strengthen the scientific and analytical capacity ______ 13. of climate change in the country, different studies under Climate Change Action Programme ______ 14. Many important bilateral and multilateral meetings and negotiations are held ______ 15. regular intervals."},{"n":7,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 7.","o":["these","those","this","that"],"a":0,"e":"These — The demonstrative these correctly refers back to the changes just mentioned.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":8,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 8.","o":["practical","geological","human","animal"],"a":2,"e":"Human — Deforestation and emissions from industry and transport are human activities.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":9,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 9.","o":["assimilation","absolution","collection","accumulation"],"a":3,"e":"Accumulation — Greenhouse gases accumulate in the atmosphere; accumulation means gradual build-up.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":10,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 10.","o":["an","a","the","no article"],"a":2,"e":"The — The standard expression is “in the atmosphere.”","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":11,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 11.","o":["raise","hike","rise","balance"],"a":0,"e":"Raise — Raise is transitive: greenhouse gases raise air temperatures. Rise would not take an object.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":12,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 12.","o":["crust","core","mantle","planet"],"a":3,"e":"Planet — A greenhouse acts on the surface of the planet.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":13,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 13.","o":["for assessment","of","in","to"],"a":0,"e":"For assessment — The natural construction is “capacity for assessment of climate change.”","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":14,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 14.","o":["are developed","are initiated","have developed","have initiated"],"a":1,"e":"Are initiated — Studies under the programme are initiated for the stated purpose.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":15,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 15.","o":["with","at","in","on"],"a":1,"e":"At — The fixed expression is “at regular intervals.”","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":16,"q":"He paused","o":["with a few moments.","for a few moments.","by a few moments.","into a few moments."],"a":1,"e":"for a few moments — We use for to indicate a duration of time: He paused for a few moments.","cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions: Each of the following items features one part of a sentence, followed by four alternatives. Complete the sentence by choosing the correct alternative and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":17,"q":"Late at night, our car","o":["broke down on the highway.","broke up on the highway.","broke into the highway.","broke out on the highway."],"a":0,"e":"broke down on the highway — Break down means to stop functioning, especially of a vehicle or machine.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":18,"q":"All the countries signed the treaty","o":["beneath Germany and France.","except Germany and France.","among Germany and France.","during Germany and France."],"a":1,"e":"except Germany and France — Except means excluding. Thus, all countries other than Germany and France signed.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":19,"q":"I reminded her","o":["to turn off the lights at night.","to call off the lights at night.","to drop off the lights at night.","to break off the lights at night."],"a":0,"e":"to turn off the lights at night — Turn off means to switch off an electrical light/device.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":20,"q":"They told us about the woman","o":["whom lived next door.","which lived next door.","who lived next door.","whose lived next door."],"a":2,"e":"who lived next door — Who is the appropriate relative pronoun for a person when it functions as the subject.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":21,"q":"I'll see you","o":["at the end of February.","on the end of February.","along the end of February.","for the end of February."],"a":0,"e":"at the end of February — The standard expression is at the end of a period.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":22,"q":"I dislike films","o":["who have sad endings.","that have sad endings.","whose have sad endings.","whom have sad endings."],"a":1,"e":"that have sad endings — That correctly introduces the relative clause modifying the non-human antecedent films.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":23,"q":"The thieves denied","o":["having stealing the money.","stealing the money.","had stolen the money.","having to steal the money."],"a":1,"e":"stealing the money — Deny is followed by a gerund (-ing form): denied stealing.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":24,"q":"Did you","o":["drop out to see Sunita on your way home?","drop in to see Sunita on your way home?","drop behind to see Sunita on your way home?","drop away to see Sunita on your way home?"],"a":1,"e":"drop in to see Sunita — Drop in means to visit someone informally or casually.","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":25,"q":"Would you like to","o":["meeting her?","meet her?","met her?","meet with her?"],"a":1,"e":"meet her? — Would you like to is followed by the base form of the verb: Would you like to meet her?","cat":"Grammar & Usage"},{"n":26,"q":"Be bad news","o":["feelings of hate","someone who behaves in a dishonest way","someone who is considered undesirable","to be ill"],"a":2,"e":"Someone who is considered undesirable — Be bad news describes a person or thing considered troublesome, unpleasant or undesirable.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Be bad news","d":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternative meanings to each. Select the most appropriate response from the options (a), (b), (c) or (d), and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":27,"q":"Back to the drawing board","o":["to draw comparisons","to be a member of planning group","to start planning again because the previous plan has failed","to be fast at understanding a situation"],"a":2,"e":"To start planning again because the previous plan has failed — Back to the drawing board means starting again after an unsuccessful attempt.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Back to the drawing board"},{"n":28,"q":"Be in the eye of the storm","o":["to look at something quickly","to fail miserably at a sport","to see something that you do not want to see","to be in the midst of a controversy"],"a":3,"e":"To be in the midst of a controversy — Be in the eye of the storm means being at the centre of a difficult or controversial situation.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Be in the eye of the storm"},{"n":29,"q":"Life in the fast lane","o":["to lead a selfless life","to have a happy life without complaints","life without money or comforts","an exciting and eventful lifestyle"],"a":3,"e":"An exciting and eventful lifestyle — Life in the fast lane refers to an exciting, busy and often high-pressure lifestyle.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Life in the fast lane"},{"n":30,"q":"To pass the buck","o":["to collect money from a group of people","to shift the responsibility of something to someone","to have a short conversation with someone","to have the credentials for a particular job"],"a":1,"e":"To shift the responsibility of something to someone — Pass the buck means to transfer responsibility or blame to another person.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"To pass the buck"},{"n":31,"q":"P — include basic freedoms\nQ — the fundamental rights are\nR — guaranteed to the individual\nS — basic rights and","o":["QSPR","QRSP","SQRP","RQSP"],"a":0,"e":"Q–S–P–R — Correct sentence: “The fundamental rights are basic rights and include basic freedoms guaranteed to the individual.”","cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled as P, Q, R and S. Rearrange the jumbled parts of the sentence into sequence and select the correct sequence as (a), (b), (c) and (d) given below and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":32,"q":"P — for many millennia\nQ — been in the making\nR — of a historical process that has\nS — the Indian nation is the product","o":["SRQP","SQPR","PRSQ","RSQP"],"a":0,"e":"S–R–Q–P — Correct sentence: “The Indian nation is the product of a historical process that has been in the making for many millennia.”","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":33,"q":"P — an epoch that was imbued\nQ — for its people the start of\nR — with a new vision\nS — India's Independence represented","o":["SQPR","QRPS","SPQR","RQSP"],"a":0,"e":"S–Q–P–R — Correct sentence: “India's Independence represented for its people the start of an epoch that was imbued with a new vision.”","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":34,"q":"P — science and mathematics exhibition\nQ — for a discussion on the forthcoming\nR — to be present before the Principal\nS — all students have been instructed","o":["SQPR","RPQS","SRQP","QPRS"],"a":2,"e":"S–R–Q–P — Correct sentence: “All students have been instructed to be present before the Principal for a discussion on the forthcoming science and mathematics exhibition.”","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":35,"q":"P — two bowlers and two batsmen respectively\nQ — to the new team\nR — with the selection of another player\nS — the team now has","o":["QPRS","SQRP","RQSP","RPSQ"],"a":2,"e":"R–Q–S–P — Correct sentence: “With the selection of another player to the new team, the team now has two bowlers and two batsmen respectively.”","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":36,"q":"You are most welcome to visit my humble ABODE.","o":["Noun","Adjective","Adverb","Participle"],"a":0,"e":"Noun — Abode means a home or place of residence. It names a place/thing and is therefore a noun.","cat":"Word Class","t":"ABODE","d":"Directions: Each of the following sentences has an underlined word. Read the sentence carefully and find which word class the underlined word belongs to. Indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":37,"q":"The situation is BEYOND our control.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Conjunction","Preposition"],"a":3,"e":"Preposition — Beyond is followed by the noun phrase “our control” and shows a relationship; therefore, it functions as a preposition.","cat":"Word Class","t":"BEYOND"},{"n":38,"q":"I didn't tell him ANYTHING except that I needed the money.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Preposition","Conjunction"],"a":3,"e":"Conjunction — According to the supplied answer key, the tested underlined expression is classified as a conjunction in the given construction.","cat":"Word Class","t":"ANYTHING"},{"n":39,"q":"Her dry-cleaned my coat NICELY.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Relative pronoun","Conjunction"],"a":1,"e":"Adverb — Nicely modifies the action and tells us how it was done; therefore, it is an adverb.","cat":"Word Class","t":"NICELY"},{"n":40,"q":"Would you like to buy THIS new item?","o":["Determiner","Conjunction","Interjection","Adjective"],"a":0,"e":"Determiner — This comes before and specifies the noun phrase “new item”, so it functions as a demonstrative determiner.","cat":"Word Class","t":"THIS"},{"n":41,"q":"Which one of the following does not belong to the biotic community?","o":["Water","Human","Protozoan","Bush"],"a":0,"e":"Water — A biotic community consists of living organisms such as plants, animals and microbes. Water is non-living and therefore an abiotic component.","cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions: Read the following passage carefully and answer the items based on it. You are required to select your answers based solely on the contents of the passage and the opinion of the author.","c":"Passage: The grouping or assemblage of plants, animals and microbes we observe when we study a natural forest, a grassland, a pond, a coral reef or some other undisturbed area, is referred to as the area's biotic or biotic community. The plant portion of the biotic community includes all vegetation, from large trees down through to microscopic algae. Likewise, the animal portion includes everything from large mammals, birds, reptiles and amphibians through to earthworm, tiny insects and mites. Microbes encompass a large array of microscopic bacteria, fungi and protozoans. Thus, the biotic community comprises a plant community, an animal community and a microbial community.\n\nThe particular kind of biotic community found in a given area is, in large part, determined by abiotic factors such as the amount of water or moisture present, the temperature, the salinity or the type of soil in the area. These abiotic factors both support and limit the particular community. For example, a relative lack of available moisture prevents the growth of most species of plants, but supports certain species, such as cacti; these kinds of areas are deserts. Land with plenty of available moisture and a suitable temperature supports forests. The presence of water is the major factor that sustains aquatic communities.\n\nThe first step in investigating a biotic community may be simply to catalogue all the species present. Species are the different kinds of plants, animals and microbes in the community. A given species includes all those individuals which have a strong similarity in appearance to one another and which are distinct in appearance from other such groups. Each species in a biotic community is represented by a certain population — that is, by a certain number of individuals that make up the interbreeding, reproducing group."},{"n":42,"q":"The nature of the biotic community largely depends on:\n1. Biotic components\n2. Abiotic components\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","Both 1 and 2","Neither 1 nor 2"],"a":1,"e":"2 only — The passage explicitly says that the particular kind of biotic community in an area is largely determined by abiotic factors such as water, temperature, salinity and soil.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":43,"q":"Which of the following is not an abiotic factor?","o":["Temperature","Humidity","Algae","Moisture"],"a":2,"e":"Algae — Temperature, humidity/moisture are non-living environmental factors. Algae are living organisms, so they are not abiotic.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":44,"q":"In the passage, the word “aquatic community” refers to:","o":["Biotic community inhabiting the land","Biotic community found in water","Biotic community found in the air","Assemblage of rain and moisture"],"a":1,"e":"Biotic community found in water — Aquatic means related to or living in water; therefore, an aquatic community is a biotic community found in water.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":45,"q":"Species are defined on the basis of:","o":["Similarities only","Differences only","Both similarities and differences","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"Both similarities and differences — The passage defines a species as individuals having a strong similarity to one another while being distinct in appearance from other groups.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":46,"q":"The king's son was BANISHED due to the political rivalry.","o":["deported","called back","accepted","notified"],"a":0,"e":"Deported — Banished means sent away or expelled from a place/country, usually as punishment. Deported is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"BANISHED","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words/group of words. Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":47,"q":"He gave a DAZZLING performance at the opera.","o":["brilliant","dismal","pathetic","puerile"],"a":0,"e":"Brilliant — Dazzling means extremely impressive, brilliant or outstanding in this context.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DAZZLING"},{"n":48,"q":"She REBUFFED all suggestions from her friends.","o":["took","rejected","criticized","granted"],"a":1,"e":"Rejected — Rebuffed means rejected or refused abruptly.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"REBUFFED"},{"n":49,"q":"He DISOWNED his royal legacy and became a commoner.","o":["accepted","abandoned","allowed","questioned"],"a":1,"e":"Abandoned — Disowned means renounced, rejected or refused to acknowledge. Abandoned is the closest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DISOWNED"},{"n":50,"q":"The public was SCEPTICAL about the new government policy.","o":["hopeful","anxious","doubtful","expectant"],"a":2,"e":"Doubtful — Sceptical means having doubts or not being easily convinced.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"SCEPTICAL"}]},{"id":"pyq-2023-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2023 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"As the Southwest monsoon ______ through most of July, flash floods caused by the waters of the Narmada hit Jabalpur town.","o":["lashed","flourished","occupied","dried"],"a":0,"e":"Lashed — Lash is used for rain, wind or storms striking violently or forcefully. Thus, ‘the monsoon lashed through most of July’ is appropriate.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: In this section, each of the following sentences has a blank space and is followed by four words. Select the word you consider most appropriate to fill in the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"Given the scale of the challenges that ______ us today, our company proposes more viable plans for sustainability.","o":["supports","conflict","confronts","convince"],"a":2,"e":"Confronts — Confront means to face a difficult problem or challenge. Hence, ‘the challenges that confront us today’.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":3,"q":"It is a really tough course; I am just worried that I won't be able to go the ______.","o":["way","distance","far","route"],"a":1,"e":"Distance — ‘Go the distance’ is an idiom meaning to continue until something difficult has been successfully completed.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":4,"q":"The ______ of the advancement made in agricultural productivity in India has been remarkable.","o":["extent","context","distance","syntax"],"a":0,"e":"Extent — Extent means the degree, scale or magnitude of something: ‘the extent of the advancement’.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":5,"q":"This essay ______ and examines various systems for water appropriation and agricultural practices adapted by society in the arid conditions of Rajasthan.","o":["confuses","enhances","controls","explores"],"a":3,"e":"Explores — An essay may explore and examine a subject, meaning it studies it in detail.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":6,"q":"India has been able to successfully ______ its population through family planning programmes.","o":["sterilize","stabilise","stratify","establish"],"a":1,"e":"Stabilise — ‘Stabilise the population’ means to prevent it from increasing or decreasing rapidly, fitting the context of family-planning programmes.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":7,"q":"Adolescence is the ______ wherein a child enters adulthood.","o":["threshold","position","condition","period"],"a":0,"e":"Threshold — Threshold figuratively means the point of entry or beginning of a new stage. Adolescence is the threshold of adulthood.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":8,"q":"The British who ruled India for 200 years were here to ______ India's natural resources.","o":["spoil","implore","exploit","drill"],"a":2,"e":"Exploit — ‘Exploit natural resources’ means to use them, especially extensively or unfairly, for one's own advantage.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":9,"q":"Endangered species have a fifty per cent or greater probability of ______ within five years or two generations, whichever is longer.","o":["distinction","extinction","complication","completion"],"a":1,"e":"Extinction — Extinction means the complete disappearance of a species.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":10,"q":"The first law of thermodynamics states that whereas energy can be ______ from one form into another, it cannot be created or destroyed.","o":["reduced","induced","performed","transformed"],"a":3,"e":"Transformed — According to the first law of thermodynamics, energy can be transformed from one form into another, but cannot be created or destroyed.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":11,"q":"Bread, butter and / jam common breakfast items / that are consumed by many households. / No error","o":["Bread, butter and","jam common breakfast items","that are consumed by many households","No error"],"a":1,"e":"A finite verb is missing. It should be: ‘Bread, butter and jam are common breakfast items...’","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter, i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":12,"q":"Ten thousand dollars is / the prize money being offered / by the game show hosts. / No error","o":["Ten thousand dollars is","the prize money being offered","by the game show hosts","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — ‘Ten thousand dollars’ represents one amount of money, so the singular verb ‘is’ is correct.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":13,"q":"Three hundred and sixty five / create up the number of days / in a calendar year. / No error","o":["Three hundred and sixty five","create up the number of days","in a calendar year","No error"],"a":1,"e":"The correct expression is ‘make up the number of days’, not ‘create up’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":14,"q":"The process of removing / wool from the body of / an animal is called sharing. / No error","o":["The process of removing","wool from the body of","an animal is called sharing","No error"],"a":2,"e":"The process of removing wool is called ‘shearing’, not ‘sharing’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":15,"q":"Many people came / from faraway places to prayed / at the temple on the auspicious day. / No error","o":["Many people came","from faraway places to prayed","at the temple on the auspicious day","No error"],"a":1,"e":"After ‘to’, the base form is required: ‘to pray’, not ‘to prayed’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":16,"q":"After reading foreword of / the book on environmental issues in India, / I decided to meet its author. / No error","o":["After reading foreword of","the book on environmental issues in India","I decided to meet its author","No error"],"a":0,"e":"‘Foreword’ is a singular countable noun here and requires the article: ‘After reading the foreword of...’","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":17,"q":"The guidance from the mentors / and teachers facilitated the / smooth conduct of elections to the Students' council. / No error","o":["The guidance from the mentors","and teachers facilitated the","smooth conduct of elections to the Students' council","No error"],"a":2,"e":"According to the supplied official key, the error lies in part C.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":18,"q":"The Principal of our school / felicitated the students in / the prize distribution ceremony. / No error","o":["The Principal of our school","felicitated the students in","the prize distribution ceremony","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — The supplied official key marks the sentence as having no error.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":19,"q":"If you were to / come to Chennai to settle down / it will be the best possible decision. / No error","o":["If you were to","come to Chennai to settle down","it will be the best possible decision","No error"],"a":2,"e":"‘If you were to...’ presents a hypothetical condition; therefore, ‘would’ is appropriate: ‘it would be the best possible decision’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":20,"q":"They had promised to delivered / the bags before mid-March / to enable us to inaugurate the shop. / No error","o":["They had promised to delivered","the bags before mid-March","to enable us to inaugurate the shop","No error"],"a":0,"e":"‘To’ must be followed by the base verb: ‘promised to deliver’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":21,"q":"He is always CAPTIOUS.","o":["worried","passionate","faultfinding","jovial"],"a":2,"e":"Faultfinding — Captious means inclined to find fault or raise petty objections.","cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"CAPTIOUS"},{"n":22,"q":"Geomorphologists ASCERTAIN several wonderful facts about our earth.","o":["verify","invert","increase","omit"],"a":0,"e":"Verify — Ascertain means to find out or establish something with certainty; verify is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ASCERTAIN"},{"n":23,"q":"The Colonel ordered the soldiers to meet him in the COMMISSARY.","o":["office","field","playground","canteen"],"a":3,"e":"Canteen — A commissary can refer to a place where food and provisions are supplied, making canteen the closest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"COMMISSARY"},{"n":24,"q":"The doctor DISMEMBERED the patient's finger first and then cured the ailment.","o":["stitched","dissected","plastered","splintered"],"a":1,"e":"Dissected — Dismember means to cut or separate a body/limb into parts; dissected is the closest option provided.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DISMEMBERED"},{"n":25,"q":"Mr. Kumar works as a BURSAR in the sports academy.","o":["secretary","accountant","coach","trainer"],"a":1,"e":"Accountant — A bursar is an official responsible for financial affairs/accounts, especially in an educational institution.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"BURSAR"},{"n":26,"q":"His intentions are INSIDIOUS.","o":["pious","inadvertent","deceitful","naive"],"a":2,"e":"Deceitful — Insidious means treacherous, crafty or harmful in a subtle way; deceitful is closest here.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"INSIDIOUS"},{"n":27,"q":"Respect for other human beings is INTRINSIC to the teachings of all religions.","o":["inconsistent","peripheral","inherent","existential"],"a":2,"e":"Inherent — Intrinsic means belonging naturally or essentially to something; hence inherent.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"INTRINSIC"},{"n":28,"q":"He completed the ONEROUS task with relative ease.","o":["smooth","complex","risky","wearying"],"a":3,"e":"Wearying — Onerous means burdensome, difficult or requiring considerable effort; wearying is closest.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ONEROUS"},{"n":29,"q":"Alfred was a POTENTATE during the Middle Ages.","o":["Monarch","lenient","liberal","democrat"],"a":0,"e":"Monarch — A potentate is a powerful ruler or sovereign, hence monarch.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"POTENTATE"},{"n":30,"q":"I abandoned the task due to INDOLENCE.","o":["difficulty","laziness","ugliness","helplessness"],"a":1,"e":"Laziness — Indolence means habitual laziness or avoidance of activity.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"INDOLENCE"},{"n":31,"q":"‘Principal’ and ‘Principle’","o":["‘Principal’ means belief system, and ‘principle’ means head of an educational institution.","‘Principal’ means head of an educational institution, and ‘principle’ means original sum on which interest is charged.","‘Principle’ means moral rule, and ‘principal’ means head of an educational institution.","‘Principal’ means original sum on which interest is charged, and ‘principle’ means head of an educational institution."],"a":2,"e":"Principle = a moral rule or fundamental belief; Principal = head of an educational institution.","cat":"Confusing Words","d":"Directions: In the following questions a pair of similar sounding words is provided. You are required to select the option that most appropriately describes the meaning of both the words and mark your response in the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":32,"q":"‘Plane’ and ‘Plain’","o":["‘Plane’ means a flat surface, and ‘plain’ means something that flies.","‘Plane’ means the flat surface, and ‘plain’ means not elaborate.","‘Plane’ means something that flies, and ‘plain’ means flat surface.","‘Plane’ means not elaborate, and ‘plain’ means flat surface."],"a":1,"e":"Plane = a flat surface; Plain = simple or not elaborate.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":33,"q":"‘Pail’ and ‘Pale’","o":["‘Pale’ means inferior or unimpressive, and ‘pail’ means faded.","‘Pale’ means faded or light in shade, and ‘pail’ means inferior or unimpressive.","‘Pale’ means faded or light in shade, and ‘pail’ means a bucket.","‘Pale’ means a bucket, and ‘pail’ means inferior or unimpressive."],"a":2,"e":"Pale = faded or light in colour; Pail = a bucket.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":34,"q":"‘Metal’ and ‘Mettle’","o":["‘Metal’ is a hard shiny material that is a good conductor, and ‘mettle’ means malleable metal.","‘Metal’ is a hard shiny material that is a good conductor, and ‘mettle’ means metallic substance.","‘Metal’ means mental resilience, and ‘mettle’ is a hard shiny material that is a good conductor.","‘Metal’ is a hard shiny material that is a good conductor, and ‘mettle’ means mental resilience."],"a":3,"e":"Metal = a hard, usually shiny material and good conductor; Mettle = courage, spirit or mental resilience.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":35,"q":"‘Hail’ and ‘Hale’","o":["‘Hail’ means pellets of frozen rain, and ‘hale’ means wind storm.","‘Hail’ means pellets of frozen rain, and ‘hale’ means strong and healthy.","‘Hail’ means pellets of frozen rain, and ‘hale’ means to greet with enthusiastic approval.","‘Hail’ means strong and healthy, and ‘hale’ means pellets of frozen rain."],"a":1,"e":"Hail = pellets of frozen rain; Hale = strong and healthy.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":36,"q":"‘Conscious’ and ‘Conscience’","o":["‘Conscious’ means sense of right and wrong, and ‘conscience’ means awareness about something.","‘Conscious’ means alert, and ‘conscience’ means presence of mind.","‘Conscious’ means intent, and ‘conscience’ means unprincipled action.","‘Conscious’ means aware, and ‘conscience’ means sense of right and wrong."],"a":3,"e":"Conscious = aware; Conscience = one's moral sense of right and wrong.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":37,"q":"‘Dissent’ and ‘Descent’","o":["‘Dissent’ means having ideas different from general beliefs or official position, and ‘descent’ means movement towards a lower place.","‘Dissent’ means having ideas different from general beliefs or official position, and ‘descent’ means being of acceptable standard.","‘Dissent’ means being of acceptable standard, and ‘descent’ means movement towards a lower place.","‘Dissent’ means being of acceptable standard, and ‘descent’ means ideas different from general beliefs or official position."],"a":0,"e":"Dissent = disagreement with accepted/official opinion; Descent = downward movement.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":38,"q":"‘Devise’ and ‘Device’","o":["‘Device’ means to determine, and ‘devise’ means a piece of equipment meant for accomplishing a specific task.","‘Device’ means finding a new way to do something, and ‘devise’ means to determine.","‘Device’ means a piece of equipment meant for accomplishing a specific task, and ‘devise’ means finding a new way to do something.","‘Device’ means finding a new way to do something, and ‘devise’ means a piece of equipment meant for accomplishing a specific task."],"a":2,"e":"Device = equipment designed for a particular purpose; Devise = to invent or formulate a new way or plan.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":39,"q":"‘Discrete’ and ‘Discreet’","o":["‘Discrete’ means separate and distinct categories of objects, and ‘discreet’ means misuse of authority.","‘Discrete’ means separate and distinct categories of objects, and ‘discreet’ means maintaining confidentiality.","‘Discrete’ means being careful in what one says or does, and ‘discreet’ means separate and distinct categories of objects.","‘Discrete’ means misuse of authority and ‘discreet’ means being careful in what one says or does."],"a":1,"e":"Discrete = separate and distinct; Discreet = careful, tactful and able to maintain confidentiality.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":40,"q":"‘Eminent’ and ‘Imminent’","o":["‘Eminent’ means important and famous, and ‘imminent’ means something about to happen.","‘Eminent’ means something unpleasant about to happen, and ‘imminent’ means important and famous person.","‘Eminent’ means important and famous person, and ‘imminent’ means inherent or existing within.","‘Eminent’ means inherent or existing within, and ‘imminent’ means something about to happen."],"a":0,"e":"Eminent = distinguished, important or famous; Imminent = about to happen very soon.","cat":"Confusing Words"},{"n":41,"q":"Many metals are MALLEABLE in nature and property.","o":["hard","strong","tender","weak"],"a":0,"e":"Hard — Malleable means capable of being shaped, bent or moulded. Hard is the intended opposite among the options.","cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"MALLEABLE"},{"n":42,"q":"To NEGLECT a warning is to invite danger.","o":["disregard","ignore","regard","overlook"],"a":2,"e":"Regard — Neglect means to disregard or fail to pay proper attention to; regard means to pay attention to or consider.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"NEGLECT"},{"n":43,"q":"His OSTENTATIOUS behaviour surprised everyone.","o":["tawdry","modest","gaudy","apparent"],"a":1,"e":"Modest — Ostentatious means showy and designed to attract attention; modest is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"OSTENTATIOUS"},{"n":44,"q":"The experience of Covid Pandemic was EXCRUCIATING for much of the population.","o":["gnawing","gruelling","harrowing","mild"],"a":3,"e":"Mild — Excruciating means extremely painful or intense; mild is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"EXCRUCIATING"},{"n":45,"q":"A trip to the hills was REFRESHING.","o":["conniving","wearying","revivifying","rejuvenating"],"a":1,"e":"Wearying — Refreshing means reviving or restoring energy; wearying means tiring and is therefore its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"REFRESHING"},{"n":46,"q":"Why, according to the writer, can't people be motivated to use a resource prudently?\n1. They feel that others may overuse the resource\n2. It is possible to substitute the resource\n3. Abundance of the resource availability\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","o":["1 only","2 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":3,"e":"1, 2 and 3 — The passage gives all three conditions discouraging prudent resource use: others may deplete the resource, substitution may be possible, and a vast resource catchment makes local degradation seem insignificant.","cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions: Carefully read the given passage and answer the questions that follow. You are required to select your answers solely based on the contents of the passage and the opinion of the author only.","c":"Passage: People by and large are concerned with and pursue self-interest. They are unlikely to be motivated to use renewable resources in a prudent and sustainable fashion. They are unlikely to be motivated under the following conditions: if their resource catchments are vast, so that degradation of any particular portion affects them very little; or if they have open before them possibilities of substitution as any one resource element is depleted; or if their control over the resource base is tenuous, so that others may, at any time, deplete a resource they value, even if they use it in a restrained fashion. Indeed, exhaustive use is highly likely when any one of these three conditions obtains. It is only when people perceive their resource catchments as limited, possibilities of substitution of exhausted resource elements as not readily feasible, and their own control over the resource base as secure, will they be motivated to use the resources in a prudent fashion. People, rooted in a locality, and retaining control over their resource base are most likely to fulfill all three prerequisites for sustainable resource use; and therefore to behave in ways conducive to the conservation of biodiversity within their localities."},{"n":47,"q":"When do people use resources exhaustively?","o":["When the substitution of resource is facilitated by NGOs","When the resource is about to expand","When the resources use has little impact","When they desire desperately for sustainable development"],"a":2,"e":"When the resource use has little impact — The passage states that exhaustive use is likely when degradation of a particular portion affects people very little, among other conditions.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":48,"q":"The self-interest of people affects the use of renewable resources","o":["positively","erratically","affirmatively","indifferently"],"a":1,"e":"Erratically — The passage says people generally pursue self-interest and are unlikely to use renewable resources prudently and sustainably under certain conditions. Among the given choices, erratically is the keyed description.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":49,"q":"People rooted in a locality","o":["cause harm to available resources","generate resources","believe in conservation and reproduction of resources","consume resources freely"],"a":2,"e":"Believe in conservation and reproduction of resources — People rooted in a locality and retaining control over their resource base are most likely to practise sustainable resource use and biodiversity conservation.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":50,"q":"Which among the following is closest in meaning with the word ‘deplete’?","o":["exhaust","complete","replete","desperate"],"a":0,"e":"Exhaust — Deplete means to use up, greatly reduce or exhaust the supply of something.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"}]},{"id":"pyq-2023-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2023 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Over long periods of time, / layers of sediments builds up / to a height of a few kilometers. / No Error","o":["Over long periods of time","layers of sediments builds up","to a height of a few kilometers","No Error"],"a":1,"e":"‘Layers’ is plural, so the verb must be ‘build up’, not ‘builds up’.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part. Indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":2,"q":"When a gas is / cooled down it turns into a liquid / from a process called condensation. / No Error","o":["When a gas is","cooled down it turns into a liquid","from a process called condensation","No Error"],"a":2,"e":"The correct preposition is ‘through/by a process’, not ‘from a process’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":3,"q":"If you want / to know the news / you can read a newspaper. / No Error","o":["If you want","to know the news","you can read a newspaper","No Error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — The sentence ‘If you want to know the news, you can read a newspaper’ is grammatically correct.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":4,"q":"Columbus made his / first voyage from Europe to America / on 1492. / No Error","o":["Columbus made his","first voyage from Europe to America","on 1492","No Error"],"a":2,"e":"We use ‘in’ with years: ‘in 1492’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":5,"q":"Whenever the sky is / clear, you can see / the stars in the night. / No Error","o":["Whenever the sky is","clear, you can see","the stars in the night","No Error"],"a":2,"e":"The idiomatic expression is ‘at night’, not ‘in the night’, in this general sense.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":6,"q":"I'm not working tomorrow, / so I don't had to / get up early. / No Error","o":["I'm not working tomorrow","so I don't had to","get up early","No Error"],"a":1,"e":"After ‘don't’, the base form ‘have’ is required: ‘I don't have to get up early’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":7,"q":"She didn't / tell anybody / about her plans. / No Error","o":["She didn't","tell anybody","about her plans","No Error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — ‘She didn't tell anybody about her plans’ is grammatically correct.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":8,"q":"She wouldn't have / has an accident, / if she had driven carefully. / No Error","o":["She wouldn't have","has an accident","if she had driven carefully","No Error"],"a":1,"e":"The third conditional requires ‘wouldn't have + past participle’: ‘wouldn't have had an accident’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":9,"q":"I will watch film / if I finish the work / in time. / No Error","o":["I will watch film","if I finish the work","in time","No Error"],"a":0,"e":"‘Film’ is a singular countable noun and requires an article: ‘I will watch a film’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":10,"q":"In 1989, the government / did an about-face and Iran restored / it's family planning program. / No Error","o":["In 1989, the government","did an about-face and Iran restored","it's family planning program","No Error"],"a":2,"e":"‘It's’ means ‘it is/it has’. The possessive form required here is ‘its’.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":11,"q":"During the pandemic the INDIGENT people had to suffer a lot.","o":["very poor","opulent","solvent","prosperous"],"a":0,"e":"Very poor — Indigent means poor or needy; lacking sufficient money.","cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four options, (a), (b), (c) and (d). Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"INDIGENT"},{"n":12,"q":"She had no idea what made him angry in one minute and JOVIAL the next.","o":["aggrieved","melancholic","doleful","mirthful"],"a":3,"e":"Mirthful — Jovial means cheerful, friendly and good-humoured; mirthful is closest.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"JOVIAL"},{"n":13,"q":"It is sheer LUNACY to drive a car in this frosty weather.","o":["prudence","normalcy","insanity","sanity"],"a":2,"e":"Insanity — Lunacy means madness or extremely foolish behaviour.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"LUNACY"},{"n":14,"q":"Operating on a child with cancer needs METICULOUS planning and teamwork.","o":["strong","long","playful","scrupulous"],"a":3,"e":"Scrupulous — Meticulous means extremely careful and attentive to details; scrupulous is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"METICULOUS"},{"n":15,"q":"The thrill of over-speeding the vehicle can be EXHILARATING, but it is important not to take the consequences lightly.","o":["humdrum","dreary","exciting","agitating"],"a":2,"e":"Exciting — Exhilarating means making one feel very happy, excited or invigorated.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"EXHILARATING"},{"n":16,"q":"The REDEMPTION will now depend on his new strategy of inclusiveness.","o":["retrieval","forfeiture","corporation","desecration"],"a":0,"e":"Retrieval — Redemption can mean recovery, restoration or regaining something. Of the given choices, retrieval is nearest.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"REDEMPTION"},{"n":17,"q":"Only three candidates are now in CONTENTION for the title.","o":["involved in dispute","in agreement with each other","chance of winning","amiable to each other"],"a":2,"e":"Chance of winning — To be in contention for a title means to have a realistic chance of winning it.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"CONTENTION"},{"n":18,"q":"All my fishing PARAPHERNALIA is in the car.","o":["boxes","accessories","fuel","food"],"a":1,"e":"Accessories — Paraphernalia means equipment, accessories or miscellaneous items associated with a particular activity.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"PARAPHERNALIA"},{"n":19,"q":"The public watched in ASTONISHMENT as he took a sudden jump from the bridge.","o":["anticipation","hurriedly","wonderment","calmness"],"a":2,"e":"Wonderment — Astonishment means great surprise or amazement; wonderment is closest.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ASTONISHMENT"},{"n":20,"q":"Drinking INORDINATE amount of liquor is not good for health.","o":["temperate","exorbitant","moderate","regular"],"a":1,"e":"Exorbitant — Inordinate means excessive or unusually large; exorbitant is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"INORDINATE"},{"n":21,"q":"P: because I feel\nQ: you remember it better\nR: when you write something,\nS: I make at least three drafts of a song","o":["SPRQ","RQPS","QRSP","PRSQ"],"a":0,"e":"S–P–R–Q — Correct sentence: ‘I make at least three drafts of a song because I feel when you write something, you remember it better.’","cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence are four sequences namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to re-arrange the jumbled parts of the sentence and mark your response accordingly."},{"n":22,"q":"P: for hours in the shop\nQ: a gunman who held\nR: a hostage\nS: demanded ransom of ₹ 10 lakh","o":["SPRQ","RQPS","QRPS","PRSQ"],"a":2,"e":"Q–R–P–S — Correct sentence: ‘A gunman who held a hostage for hours in the shop demanded ransom of ₹10 lakh.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":23,"q":"P: living in Russia\nQ: country immediately\nR: Ukraine has urged its citizens\nS: to leave the","o":["SPRQ","RPSQ","RSQP","PRSQ"],"a":1,"e":"R–P–S–Q — Correct sentence: ‘Ukraine has urged its citizens living in Russia to leave the country immediately.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":24,"q":"P: ripping roofs off houses and raising fears of\nQ: coast of Madagascar in the early hours,\nR: cyclone Emnati crashed into the southeastern\nS: flooding and food shortages in the region","o":["SPRQ","RPSQ","RQPS","PRSQ"],"a":2,"e":"R–Q–P–S — Correct sentence: ‘Cyclone Emnati crashed into the southeastern coast of Madagascar in the early hours, ripping roofs off houses and raising fears of flooding and food shortages in the region.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":25,"q":"P: ecology is protected and aquatic life thrives\nQ: we need to utilize the resources of water\nR: for different purposes while\nS: ensuring that its natural","o":["SPRQ","RQPS","QRSP","PRSQ"],"a":2,"e":"Q–R–S–P — Correct sentence: ‘We need to utilize the resources of water for different purposes while ensuring that its natural ecology is protected and aquatic life thrives.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":26,"q":"P: very difficult\nQ: the poor visibility\nR: made the movement of traffic\nS: due to fog","o":["SPRQ","RQPS","QRPS","QSRP"],"a":3,"e":"Q–S–R–P — Correct sentence: ‘The poor visibility due to fog made the movement of traffic very difficult.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":27,"q":"P: avenues for the patients to indict\nQ: but rarely is it the other way round\nR: there are multiple legal\nS: the doctors","o":["SPRQ","RPSQ","QRPS","QSRP"],"a":1,"e":"R–P–S–Q — Correct sentence: ‘There are multiple legal avenues for the patients to indict the doctors, but rarely is it the other way round.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":28,"q":"P: flight, and each person\nQ: her own journey\nR: life is a solo\nS: makes his or","o":["SPRQ","RPQS","RPSQ","PQRS"],"a":2,"e":"R–P–S–Q — Correct sentence: ‘Life is a solo flight, and each person makes his or her own journey.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":29,"q":"P: made it evolve\nQ: technologies and equipment has\nR: into a rare centre of excellence\nS: adoption of the latest","o":["PQRS","RPQS","SQPR","QSRP"],"a":2,"e":"S–Q–P–R — Correct sentence: ‘Adoption of the latest technologies and equipment has made it evolve into a rare centre of excellence.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":30,"q":"P: please mark the same\nQ: be made in response to this notice\nR: if you have any complaint to\nS: to the concerned authority","o":["PQRS","RPQS","SQPR","RQPS"],"a":3,"e":"R–Q–P–S — Correct sentence: ‘If you have any complaint to be made in response to this notice, please mark the same to the concerned authority.’","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":31,"q":"The parental support can FORTIFY a child's learning.","o":["weaken","encourage","brace","strengthen"],"a":0,"e":"Weaken — Fortify means to strengthen; its opposite is weaken.","cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four options, (a), (b), (c) and (d). Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"FORTIFY"},{"n":32,"q":"Now we can have another DAZZLING thought, as an outcome of my brilliant research!","o":["splendid","murky","dazzling","fulgent"],"a":1,"e":"Murky — Dazzling means very bright or brilliant; murky means dark, gloomy or unclear.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DAZZLING"},{"n":33,"q":"The group held some CLANDESTINE meetings to resolve the matter.","o":["secret","covert","sneaky","public"],"a":3,"e":"Public — Clandestine means secret or concealed; public is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CLANDESTINE"},{"n":34,"q":"In the winters, the countryside view is BLEAK and the house is drafty.","o":["cheerless","verdant","desolate","bare"],"a":1,"e":"Verdant — Bleak here suggests bare, dreary and lacking vegetation; verdant means green and flourishing.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"BLEAK"},{"n":35,"q":"Amid COMMOTION the leader and his supporters remained passive.","o":["quietude","uproar","tempest","stir"],"a":0,"e":"Quietude — Commotion means noisy disturbance or confusion; quietude means calmness or tranquillity.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"COMMOTION"},{"n":36,"q":"He had the AUDACITY to blame him.","o":["boldness","vigour","temerity","politeness"],"a":3,"e":"Politeness — Audacity means boldness or impudent daring. Among the options, politeness provides the intended contrast.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"AUDACITY"},{"n":37,"q":"They are making plans for the ABATEMENT of the nuisance within a specified time.","o":["subsidence","ebbing","accumulation","mitigation"],"a":2,"e":"Accumulation — Abatement means reduction or decrease; accumulation means increase or build-up.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ABATEMENT"},{"n":38,"q":"There is EXPONENTIAL growth in the development of the scientific researches in the recent few decades.","o":["aggressive","rampant","meager","augmented"],"a":2,"e":"Meager — Exponential growth means very rapid/increasing growth; meager means deficient or very small.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"EXPONENTIAL"},{"n":39,"q":"He was known for his SAGACITY.","o":["prudence","wisdom","ignorance","sapience"],"a":2,"e":"Ignorance — Sagacity means wisdom and good judgement; ignorance is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SAGACITY"},{"n":40,"q":"The river Ganges CULMINATES in the Bay of Bengal.","o":["concludes","flows","originates","merges"],"a":2,"e":"Originates — Culminates means ends or reaches its final point. Originates means begins, hence the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CULMINATES"},{"n":41,"q":"The lion's share","o":["A portion of something","The largest and the best part of something","An important decision","An aggressive statement"],"a":1,"e":"The lion's share means the largest portion or greatest part.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternative meanings for each. Choose the most appropriate answer from among the options (a), (b), (c) or (d).","t":"The lion's share"},{"n":42,"q":"Spill the beans","o":["Reveal a secret","Forced to leave belongings","Share the news","Take offence at someone's rude behaviour"],"a":0,"e":"Spill the beans means to disclose secret information.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Spill the beans"},{"n":43,"q":"Cock someone's goose","o":["To assist others","Hypnotize other's mind","To spoil other's plans","Inviting misfortune"],"a":2,"e":"Cock someone's goose means to ruin or spoil someone's plans or chances.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Cock someone's goose"},{"n":44,"q":"Disappear into thin air","o":["Lost forever","Become forgetful","Become uncaring","Disappear suddenly"],"a":3,"e":"Disappear into thin air means to vanish suddenly and completely.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Disappear into thin air"},{"n":45,"q":"Put the screws on","o":["Forcing someone to do what you want","Never letting go of things","Keep the past alive","Using someone's trick for own benefit"],"a":0,"e":"Put the screws on someone means to exert strong pressure on someone to make them act as desired.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Put the screws on"},{"n":46,"q":"Sit on the fence","o":["Avoid meeting someone","Waiting patiently","Avoid taking sides","Hiding from someone"],"a":2,"e":"Sit on the fence means to remain neutral or avoid making a definite choice.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Sit on the fence"},{"n":47,"q":"Be as sharp as a tack","o":["A talkative person","A shrewd person","A clumsy person","A clever person"],"a":[1,3],"e":"The supplied official key accepts both B and D. ‘As sharp as a tack’ means very intelligent, quick-witted or perceptive. Both ‘shrewd’ and ‘clever’ can convey this meaning.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Be as sharp as a tack"},{"n":48,"q":"From pillar to post","o":["Upside down","Keep moving from one place to another","Constructing a huge building","Jumping from the top of the mountain"],"a":1,"e":"From pillar to post means to move repeatedly from one place/person to another, usually unsuccessfully or inconveniently.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"From pillar to post"},{"n":49,"q":"Heath Robinson","o":["Very complicated system or machine for doing a simple task","Treating everyone as enemy, when they are not","Sleeping all day, doing nothing","Very lazy person, slow in action"],"a":0,"e":"Heath Robinson describes an absurdly complicated or ingenious contraption designed to perform a simple function.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"Heath Robinson"},{"n":50,"q":"As thick as thieves","o":["Dumb person","Good for nothing","Someone who uses everyone for personal gain","Very close friends who are looked upon with suspicion"],"a":3,"e":"As thick as thieves means very close, intimate or friendly, often sharing secrets.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"As thick as thieves"}]},{"id":"pyq-2022-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2022 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Human CRAVING for comfort leads to anxiety.","o":["like","dislike","discomfort","complacency"],"a":1,"e":"Dislike — Craving means a strong desire or longing for something. Its opposite is dislike.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CRAVING","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words (a), (b), (c) and (d). Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"His writings on the subject are OBSCURE.","o":["murky","vague","obvious","uncertain"],"a":2,"e":"Obvious — Obscure means unclear, unknown or uncertain. Obvious, meaning clear and easily understood, is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"OBSCURE"},{"n":3,"q":"The achievements of the team have been MONUMENTAL in the last ten years.","o":["enormous","memorable","striking","unimpressive"],"a":3,"e":"Unimpressive — Monumental means very great, important or impressive. Hence, unimpressive is the antonym.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"MONUMENTAL"},{"n":4,"q":"His FRAIL appearance was considered a merit.","o":["weak","delicate","sturdy","big"],"a":2,"e":"Sturdy — Frail means weak and delicate. Sturdy means strong and robust, so it is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"FRAIL"},{"n":5,"q":"The novel depicts the HAPLESS status of certain sections of society.","o":["wretched","miserable","rural","fortunate"],"a":3,"e":"Fortunate — Hapless means unfortunate or unlucky. Therefore, fortunate is its antonym.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"HAPLESS"},{"n":6,"q":"Her ideas have always been ECCENTRIC.","o":["special","strange","normal","local"],"a":2,"e":"Normal — Eccentric means unconventional, unusual or strange. Normal expresses the opposite idea.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ECCENTRIC"},{"n":7,"q":"The land appears to be PARCHED.","o":["dry","desiccated","wet","scorched"],"a":2,"e":"Wet — Parched means extremely dry or dried out by heat. Its opposite is wet.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PARCHED"},{"n":8,"q":"The newly made road was SMOOTH.","o":["rutted","dangerous","winding","silken"],"a":0,"e":"Rutted — Smooth means having an even and regular surface. Rutted means having deep tracks or grooves, making it the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SMOOTH"},{"n":9,"q":"PLENITUDE is one major cause for environmental degradation.","o":["abundance","insufficiency","ample","plethora"],"a":1,"e":"Insufficiency — Plenitude means abundance or plenty. Its opposite is insufficiency, meaning shortage or inadequacy.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PLENITUDE"},{"n":10,"q":"The scholar quoted IRREFUTABLE evidence for her thesis.","o":["overwhelming","undeniable","certain","questionable"],"a":3,"e":"Questionable — Irrefutable means impossible to deny or disprove. Questionable conveys the opposite sense.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"IRREFUTABLE"},{"n":11,"q":"Ravi told his friend that he had no intention to MALIGN him.","o":["benign","slander","praise","greet"],"a":1,"e":"Slander — Malign means to speak harmful or defamatory things about someone; slander is the closest synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MALIGN","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words/group of words (a), (b), (c) and (d). Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"Sheela appeared to be VEXED.","o":["clear","calm","displeased","uncomplicated"],"a":2,"e":"Displeased — Vexed means annoyed, frustrated or worried. Displeased is the nearest meaning.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"VEXED"},{"n":13,"q":"Do not hold any PRECONCEPTION about people.","o":["notion","experiment","great idea","conceit"],"a":0,"e":"Notion — Preconception means an idea or opinion formed beforehand, often without adequate evidence. Notion is the closest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"PRECONCEPTION"},{"n":14,"q":"All MORTAL elements are impermanent.","o":["temporal","immortal","never-ending","divinely"],"a":0,"e":"Temporal — According to the supplied key, “mortal” is taken in the sense of relating to temporal or earthly existence; therefore, “temporal” is the keyed synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MORTAL"},{"n":15,"q":"The ITINERARY of the group's visit to the hills was given to everyone.","o":["invitation","information","schedule","names"],"a":2,"e":"Schedule — An itinerary is a planned route or schedule of a journey. Hence, schedule is correct.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ITINERARY"},{"n":16,"q":"The effort was to HARNESS the power of nature.","o":["abuse","benefit","apply","control"],"a":3,"e":"Control — Harness means to control and make effective use of something, particularly power or natural resources.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"HARNESS"},{"n":17,"q":"The FICKLE nature of humans is at times the cause of great innovation.","o":["vacillating","consistent","strong","wily"],"a":0,"e":"Vacillating — Fickle describes someone who changes opinions, loyalties or feelings frequently. Vacillating is the closest synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"FICKLE"},{"n":18,"q":"The flood situation during the monsoon this year was CALAMITOUS.","o":["abundance","catastrophic","encouraging","welcome"],"a":1,"e":"Catastrophic — Calamitous means disastrous or involving great damage or suffering. Catastrophic is synonymous.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"CALAMITOUS"},{"n":19,"q":"He was appointed as the HONORARY member of the team.","o":["paid","invited","accepted","unpaid"],"a":3,"e":"Unpaid — Honorary describes a position/title often conferred as an honour without the usual payment or duties. Hence, unpaid is the keyed answer.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"HONORARY"},{"n":20,"q":"The church decided to EXCOMMUNICATE him for his actions.","o":["admit","reward","expel","promote"],"a":2,"e":"Expel — Excommunicate means to officially exclude someone from participation in the sacraments or membership of a church. Expel is the nearest general synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"EXCOMMUNICATE"},{"n":21,"q":"P: to be successful Q: what it is R: as a human being S: have you ever considered The correct sequence should be:","o":["SQPR","RQPS","QRSP","PRSQ"],"a":0,"e":"S–Q–P–R — Correct sentence: “Have you ever considered what it is to be successful as a human being?” The interrogative construction begins with Have you, followed by the remaining parts in their logical sequence.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence are four sequences namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to rearrange the jumbled parts of the sentence and mark your response accordingly."},{"n":22,"q":"P: there is in all instance a single, Q: surrender to the ideas that R: authoritative truth to be discovered and defended S: many people today are willing to The correct sequence should be:","o":["RPSQ","SPRQ","RPQS","SQPR"],"a":3,"e":"S–Q–P–R — The sentence begins with the subject many people, followed by what they are willing to accept/surrender to, and then explains the idea of a single authoritative truth.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":23,"q":"P: for yourself the implication of death, Q: if you do not reflect and understand R: from one preacher to another S: you will go endlessly The correct sequence should be:","o":["SRPQ","QPSR","RSPQ","QRSP"],"a":1,"e":"Q–P–S–R — The conditional clause begins “If you do not understand...”, followed by its object, and then its consequence: “you will go endlessly from one preacher to another.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":24,"q":"P: a way of understanding the world, Q: a language represents R: and relating them to one another S: differentiating between things The correct sequence should be:","o":["PSRQ","SRPQ","RSPQ","QPSR"],"a":3,"e":"Q–P–S–R — A language forms the subject, followed by the different ways in which it represents and helps us understand and differentiate the world.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":25,"q":"P: for India's future Q: mathematics and mathematical thinking R: will be very important S: it is recognized that The correct sequence should be:","o":["QRPS","RPSQ","QRSP","SQRP"],"a":3,"e":"S–Q–R–P — The sentence logically begins “It is recognized that...”, followed by the proposition concerning the importance of mathematical thinking for India's future.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":26,"q":"P: in agriculture and veterinary sciences through Q: will be enhanced sharply R: programmes integrated with general education S: the preparation of professionals The correct sequence should be:","o":["SRPQ","RQPS","SPRQ","PQRS"],"a":2,"e":"S–P–R–Q — The preparation of professionals provides the subject; the remaining parts explain how such preparation will be enhanced sharply through programmes integrated with general education.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":27,"q":"P: and should shift the way Q: are certainly very important, R: we think about politics S: the issues raised by feminists and multiculturalists The correct sequence should be:","o":["RPQS","RSPQ","SPQR","SQPR"],"a":3,"e":"S–Q–P–R — The issues raised by feminists connects naturally with “are certainly very important”, followed by their effect on the way politics is understood.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":28,"q":"P: by which the genetic material of a plant is altered, Q: pests and enhance its nutritional value R: perhaps to make it more resistant to S: genetic modification (GM) is the science The correct sequence should be:","o":["QRPS","SPRQ","RSPQ","RPQS"],"a":1,"e":"S–P–R–Q — The sentence defines Genetic Modification and then explains it as the science through which genetic material is altered to make plants more resistant to pests.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":29,"q":"P: a much wider array of perspectives Q: the editors of the present work R: the history of social reforms from S: argue the need to understand The correct sequence should be:","o":["QSRP","QPRS","RSQP","SQRP"],"a":0,"e":"Q–S–R–P — The editors of the present work provide the subject for argue, followed by their argument that social reform history should be understood from a wider range of perspectives.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":30,"q":"P: for which she fought till the end Q: as a champion of women's rights, R: Pandita Ramabai was truly remarkable S: as a pioneer in women's education and The correct sequence should be:","o":["SRQP","QPRS","RSQP","PQRS"],"a":2,"e":"R–S–Q–P — The sentence begins with Pandita Ramabai, identifies her pioneering contribution to women's education and rights, and then completes the related thought.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":31,"q":"Our greatest glory is / not in never falling but in / raising every time we fall. / No error","o":["Our greatest glory is","not in never falling but in","raising every time we fall.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"The sentence requires parallel/correct verb usage. The supplied explanation identifies “raising” as incorrect in the context; the appropriate form is “rising.”","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part. Indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter, i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":32,"q":"Rahim was one of that / selected for / the award. / No error","o":["Rahim was one of that","selected for","the award.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"The correct construction is “Rahim was one of those...” Those is required because the reference is plural; that is singular.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":33,"q":"Sujini has met her old friend / Guna before she shifted / to Hyderabad two years ago. / No error","o":["Sujini has met her old friend","Guna before she shifted","to Hyderabad two years ago.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"When one past event precedes another, the earlier action can take the past perfect and the subsequent action the simple past. The corrected form given is “Sujini had met...”","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":34,"q":"This monument happens / to be one of the old monument / in the city. / No error","o":["This monument happens","to be one of the old monument","in the city.","No error"],"a":1,"e":"When one monument is compared with all other monuments in the city, the superlative degree is appropriate: “the oldest monument in the city.”","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":35,"q":"Amit told to Divya / that he would come / with a reward for her. / No error","o":["Amit told to Divya","that he would come","with a reward for her.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"The correct construction is “Amit told Divya...”, not told to Divya. The verb tell takes a direct object without to.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":36,"q":"If you have asked me / for the truth I would have told / you at that time. / No error","o":["If you have asked me","for the truth I would have told","you at that time.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"The appropriate conditional construction is “If you had asked me for the truth...” The past perfect is required in the if-clause.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":37,"q":"If I will be the millionaire / I would eradicate / poverty from the country. / No error","o":["If I will be the millionaire","I would eradicate","poverty from the country.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"For an unreal or hypothetical situation, standard English uses “If I were...” rather than “If I will be...”. Correct: “If I were a millionaire...”","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":38,"q":"A bird in hand / is better than / two in the bush. / No error","o":["A bird in hand","is better than","two in the bush.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — The sentence is grammatically correct as given; therefore, the answer is No Error.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":39,"q":"The opportunity to attain foundational literacy / and pursue a honest livelihood / must be viewed as basic right of every citizen. / No error","o":["The opportunity to attain foundational literacy","and pursue a honest livelihood","must be viewed as basic right of every citizen.","No error"],"a":1,"e":"The correct phrase is “an honest livelihood”, because honest begins with a vowel sound—the h is silent.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":40,"q":"Though there is progress / for different directions, / why is there no brotherhood? / No error","o":["Though there is progress","for different directions,","why is there no brotherhood?","No error"],"a":1,"e":"The appropriate preposition is “in”: “progress in different directions.”","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":41,"q":"A queer fish","o":["A strange person","A good person","An unlucky person","A lucky person"],"a":0,"e":"A strange person — A queer fish refers to a person whose behaviour seems strange, peculiar or unusual.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternative meanings for each. Choose the most appropriate answer from among the options (a), (b), (c) and (d)."},{"n":42,"q":"Eat like a bird","o":["Eat very quickly","Eat a lot","Eat continuously","Eat very little"],"a":3,"e":"Eat very little — Eat like a bird means to eat only a very small amount of food.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":43,"q":"Lock horns","o":["Be like a bull","Argue about something","Agree with someone","Think about the horns"],"a":1,"e":"Argue about something — Lock horns means to engage in conflict, disagreement or argument.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":44,"q":"Early bird","o":["Low flying","Latest","Ancient avian","First arrival"],"a":3,"e":"First arrival — An early bird is someone who arrives, rises or acts earlier than others or before the expected time.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":45,"q":"Be in the pink","o":["To be very healthy","To be very colourful","To be very sad","To be very rich"],"a":0,"e":"Healthy — In the pink means in excellent health or in very good condition.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":46,"q":"Be in the running","o":["In a very bad state to speak","In a powerful position","In a good position to win","In a losing position"],"a":2,"e":"In a good position to win — Be in the running means to have a realistic chance of succeeding or winning a competition/election.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":47,"q":"A sea change","o":["Change in the sea tides","A complete change","Change like sea","Changed sea with pollution"],"a":1,"e":"A profound/notable transformation — A sea change means a complete, profound or striking transformation.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":48,"q":"A pearl of wisdom","o":["An important event in one's life","An important person of wisdom","An important piece of advice","An important thing in one's life"],"a":2,"e":"An important piece of advice — A pearl of wisdom means a wise saying, valuable insight or useful piece of advice.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":49,"q":"Be in seventh heaven","o":["To be extremely sorry","To be extremely wise","To be extremely mad","To be extremely happy"],"a":3,"e":"Extremely happy — Be in the seventh heaven means to be in a state of extreme happiness or bliss.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":50,"q":"Be in the red","o":["To owe money to a bank","To get money from one's family","To be in danger of being caught","To be in favour of needy people"],"a":0,"e":"Owe money to a bank — Be in the red means to be in debt or to owe more money than one has available.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"}]},{"id":"pyq-2022-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2022 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"A company of five thousand soldiers, / having marched tirelessly for over five days, / have just moved into their cantonment. / No error","o":["A company of five thousand soldiers,","having marched tirelessly for over five days,","have just moved into their cantonment.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"have just moved into their cantonment — The subject is “A company”, which is singular. Therefore, have should be has. Correct: A company of five thousand soldiers has just moved into its cantonment.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to determine whether there is any error in any underlined part and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter, i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":2,"q":"Every person who believes in principles / must stand up to fight / for their convictions. / No error","o":["Every person who believes in principles","must stand up to fight","for their convictions.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"for their convictions — Every person is treated as singular in the traditional agreement tested here. Hence, his/her conviction(s) is expected instead of their convictions.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":3,"q":"The Olympic Games reflects / the highest spirit of / human endeavour and achievement. / No error","o":["The Olympic Games reflects","the highest spirit of","human endeavour and achievement.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"The Olympic Games reflects — The Olympic Games takes a plural verb. Correct: The Olympic Games reflect the highest spirit of human endeavour and achievement.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":4,"q":"The principal of the school / stressed the need for discipline / amongst the students. / No error","o":["The principal of the school","stressed the need for discipline","amongst the students.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — The sentence is grammatically acceptable. Amongst is a correct variant of among.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":5,"q":"Failure is the stepping stone to success; / however, successive failures are not / successive stepping stones to success. / No error","o":["Failure is the stepping stone to success;","however, successive failures are not","successive stepping stones to success.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — The sentence is grammatically correct. Successive appropriately means following one after another.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":6,"q":"India's strengths are / its diversity of culture and / the spirit of tolerance in it's people. / No error","o":["India's strengths are","its diversity of culture and","the spirit of tolerance in it's people.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"the spirit of tolerance in it's people — It's means it is/it has. The possessive form is its. Correct: the spirit of tolerance in its people.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":7,"q":"Once considered ninth planet of the solar system, / Pluto is today listed as the / largest dwarf planet of the solar system. / No error","o":["Once considered ninth planet of the solar system,","Pluto is today listed as the","largest dwarf planet of the solar system.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"Once considered ninth planet of the solar system — A definite article is required. Correct: Once considered the ninth planet of the solar system...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":8,"q":"The greatest glory in life / is to be able to realize ones dreams and ambitions / without trampling on those of others. / No error","o":["The greatest glory in life","is to be able to realize ones dreams and ambitions","without trampling on those of others.","No error"],"a":1,"e":"is to be able to realize ones dreams and ambitions — The possessive pronoun requires an apostrophe: one's, not ones. Correct: to realize one's dreams and ambitions.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":9,"q":"To be able to manage one's / anger is a reflection of an individual's / psychological maturity. / No error","o":["To be able to manage one's","anger is a reflection of an individual's","psychological maturity.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — One's anger and an individual's psychological maturity are grammatically correct.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":10,"q":"A honest mistake / is no more than that; / just an honest mistake. / No error","o":["A honest mistake","is no more than that;","just an honest mistake.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"A honest mistake — Honest begins with a vowel sound because h is silent. Therefore, an is required. Correct: An honest mistake...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":11,"q":"Counting your chickens","o":["confident of success","greedily accumulating wealth","being careful about spending money","getting scared because of danger"],"a":0,"e":"Confident of success — Counting your chickens refers to assuming or expecting success before it has actually happened, from the expression count your chickens before they hatch.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternative meanings to each. Choose the most appropriate answer from among the options (a), (b), (c) and (d)."},{"n":12,"q":"The icing on the cake","o":["baked food that is delicate and delicious with a topping","extra benefit over and above an already good deal","getting what you asked for","more than what is needed"],"a":1,"e":"Extra benefit over and above an already good deal — The icing on the cake means an additional advantage that makes an already good situation even better.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":13,"q":"A stitch in time","o":["tailoring one's efforts efficiently","making an effort to succeed","inability to take timely decision","timely action that prevents a negative outcome"],"a":3,"e":"Timely action that prevents a negative outcome — A stitch in time refers to taking prompt action to prevent a problem from becoming worse.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":14,"q":"At sixes and sevens","o":["on top of the situation","state of denial","state of total confusion","well-planned and prepared"],"a":2,"e":"State of total confusion — At sixes and sevens means in a confused, disorganised or disorderly state.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":15,"q":"Talking twenty to the dozen","o":["talking hurriedly and rapidly","talking too much","talking without making sense","talking out of turn"],"a":0,"e":"Talking hurriedly and rapidly — Talking nineteen/twenty to the dozen means talking very quickly and continuously.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":16,"q":"Under the weather","o":["walking in the rain","controlled by the elements","browbeaten by the heat","unwell"],"a":3,"e":"Unwell — Under the weather means feeling ill or slightly unwell.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":17,"q":"To sit on the fence","o":["to act promptly","to be lazy","to be undecided","to sit without doing anything"],"a":2,"e":"To be undecided — Sit on the fence means to avoid making a decision or choosing between two sides.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":18,"q":"Once in a blue moon","o":["romantic moment","occasionally","rarely","often"],"a":2,"e":"Rarely — Once in a blue moon means very rarely or almost never.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":19,"q":"Through thick and thin","o":["in genuine friendship","through the best of everything","among people both fat and slim","through difficult times"],"a":3,"e":"Through difficult times — Through thick and thin means through all circumstances, particularly difficulties and hardships.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":20,"q":"Like chalk and cheese","o":["very different from each other","without any blemish","pure white in colour","very like each other"],"a":0,"e":"Very different from each other — Like chalk and cheese describes two people or things that are completely different.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":21,"q":"P: but perhaps the best way is to agree Q: there are many ways of dealing R: with them without excessive argumentation S: with intransigent customers The correct sequence should be:","o":["QPRS","QSPR","PSQR","RSQP"],"a":1,"e":"Q–S–P–R — Correct sentence: “There are many ways of dealing with intransigent customers, but perhaps the best way is to agree with them without excessive argumentation.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence are four sequences namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to rearrange the jumbled parts of the sentence and mark your response accordingly."},{"n":22,"q":"P: in the years following Q: India achieved remarkable economic development R: liberalization in the year 1991 S: the landmark reforms inaugurated via The correct sequence should be:","o":["PQRS","PRSQ","SPRQ","QPSR"],"a":3,"e":"Q–P–S–R — Correct sentence: “India achieved remarkable economic development in the years following the landmark reforms inaugurated via liberalization in the year 1991.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":23,"q":"P: was the victory secured by the women's hockey team Q: in the arena of sport, perhaps R: against Australia in the Tokyo Olympics S: the greatest moment in Indian Olympic history The correct sequence should be:","o":["SPQR","RPQS","QSPR","QRPS"],"a":2,"e":"Q–S–P–R — Correct sentence: “In the arena of sport, perhaps the greatest moment in Indian Olympic history was the victory secured by the women's hockey team against Australia in the Tokyo Olympics.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":24,"q":"P: in terms of GDP and GNP, Q: for gauging the success of a nation R: which are the two major determinants S: economic progress is determined The correct sequence should be:","o":["SQRP","RPQS","SPRQ","QRPS"],"a":2,"e":"S–P–R–Q — Correct sentence: “Economic progress is determined in terms of GDP and GNP, which are the two major determinants for gauging the success of a nation.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":25,"q":"P: with the aplomb of a stage artist, Q: that they are not men of straw R: they can twist and shake their wobbly heads S: mesmerizing the gullible into believing The correct sequence should be:","o":["PRQS","RPSQ","SPQR","QRPS"],"a":1,"e":"R–P–S–Q — Correct sentence: “They can twist and shake their wobbly heads with the aplomb of a stage artist, mesmerizing the gullible into believing that they are not men of straw.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":26,"q":"P: of its rain-soaked verdure Q: and on the other there are R: on the one hand there is the immense beauty S: the artifacts of its visible modernity The correct sequence should be:","o":["PRQS","QRSP","SPQR","RPQS"],"a":3,"e":"R–P–Q–S — Correct sentence: “On the one hand there is the immense beauty of its rain-soaked verdure, and on the other there are the artifacts of its visible modernity.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":27,"q":"P: are often rooted Q: the causes R: of extreme poverty S: in the inequalities of social systems The correct sequence should be:","o":["QRPS","QRSP","SRQP","SRPQ"],"a":0,"e":"Q–R–P–S — Correct sentence: “The causes of extreme poverty are often rooted in the inequalities of social systems.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":28,"q":"P: a person by the way Q: he behaves and R: you must judge S: not by the way he looks The correct sequence should be:","o":["RPQS","QRSP","RSQP","QPSR"],"a":0,"e":"R–P–Q–S — Correct sentence: “You must judge a person by the way he behaves and not by the way he looks.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":29,"q":"P: to the greatest number of people Q: that causes the least discomfort R: whenever in doubt S: always opt for the option The correct sequence should be:","o":["RQPS","QRSP","RSQP","QPSR"],"a":2,"e":"R–S–Q–P — Correct sentence: “Whenever in doubt, always opt for the option that causes the least discomfort to the greatest number of people.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":30,"q":"P: brought by bad times Q: against the despondency R: our greatest defence S: faith and belief are The correct sequence should be:","o":["RQPS","SRQP","RSQP","QPRS"],"a":1,"e":"S–R–Q–P — Correct sentence: “Faith and belief are our greatest defence against the despondency brought by bad times.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":31,"q":"He is essentially a CRUDE person.","o":["coarse","refined","eager","balanced"],"a":1,"e":"Refined — Crude means rough, coarse or lacking sophistication. Refined is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CRUDE","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words (a), (b), (c) and (d). Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":32,"q":"His CONFIDENCE is high.","o":["diffidence","eagerness","steadfastness","endurance"],"a":0,"e":"Diffidence — Confidence means self- assurance, whereas diffidence means lack of confidence or excessive shyness.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CONFIDENCE"},{"n":33,"q":"His INTEGRITY is noticed.","o":["skilfulness","ability","dependability","dishonesty"],"a":3,"e":"Dishonesty — Integrity means honesty and strong moral principles. Its direct opposite is dishonesty.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"INTEGRITY"},{"n":34,"q":"She is a BENEVOLENT individual.","o":["clever","muddled","malevolent","ambivalent"],"a":2,"e":"Malevolent — Benevolent means kind and well-meaning; malevolent means wishing harm or evil to others.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"BENEVOLENT"},{"n":35,"q":"His SARTORIAL manner is judged.","o":["unstylish","uncompromising","common","crude"],"a":0,"e":"Unstylish — Sartorial relates to dress, clothing and style of dressing. In the given context, unstylish provides the intended opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SARTORIAL"},{"n":36,"q":"This piece of art is AUTHENTIC.","o":["genuine","expensive","rare","fake"],"a":3,"e":"Fake — Authentic means genuine or real; fake is its direct opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"AUTHENTIC"},{"n":37,"q":"The DELUGE affected the population.","o":["cloudburst","drought","deforestation","drizzle"],"a":1,"e":"Drought — Deluge means a severe flood or an enormous amount of water. Drought, a prolonged absence of rainfall, is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DELUGE"},{"n":38,"q":"His DEDICATION is known to all.","o":["clarity","hostility","apathy","anger"],"a":2,"e":"Apathy — Dedication means strong commitment and devotion. Apathy means lack of interest or enthusiasm.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DEDICATION"},{"n":39,"q":"His PERSPICACITY was remarkable.","o":["smartness","dullness","dedication","deviousness"],"a":1,"e":"Dullness — Perspicacity means sharpness of understanding or mental insight. Dullness is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PERSPICACITY"},{"n":40,"q":"He has a PENCHANT for spicy food.","o":["tendency","affinity","fear","dislike"],"a":3,"e":"Dislike — Penchant means a strong liking or inclination for something. Its opposite is dislike.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PENCHANT"},{"n":41,"q":"After a good meal, it is important to pay a COMPLIMENT to the chef.","o":["tip","praise","admonish","revile"],"a":1,"e":"Praise — A compliment is an expression of praise, admiration or approval.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"COMPLIMENT","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words (a), (b), (c) and (d). Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":42,"q":"His work is LAUDABLE.","o":["praiseworthy","laughable","bold","loud"],"a":0,"e":"Praiseworthy — Laudable means deserving praise or commendation.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"LAUDABLE"},{"n":43,"q":"Raj is a COMPETENT carpenter.","o":["capable","exceptional","inept","clumsy"],"a":0,"e":"Capable — Competent means having the necessary ability or skill to do something successfully; capable is the closest synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"COMPETENT"},{"n":44,"q":"He is DILIGENT in submitting assignments.","o":["dilly-dallying","procrastinating","intelligent","conscientious"],"a":3,"e":"Conscientious — Diligent means careful, hardworking and persistent in one's duties. Conscientious is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DILIGENT"},{"n":45,"q":"He appears to be RETICENT.","o":["expansive","jolly","silent","withdrawn"],"a":3,"e":"Withdrawn — Reticent means reserved, restrained or unwilling to speak freely. Withdrawn is the closest option in the given context.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"RETICENT"},{"n":46,"q":"He is always JOVIAL.","o":["cheerful","callous","garrulous","credulous"],"a":0,"e":"Cheerful — Jovial means cheerful, friendly and good-humoured.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"JOVIAL"},{"n":47,"q":"Mohan remains MOROSE these days.","o":["introspective","generous","chirpy","sullen"],"a":3,"e":"Sullen — Morose means gloomy, ill- tempered or sullen.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MOROSE"},{"n":48,"q":"He was a RELUCTANT learner.","o":["enthusiastic","eager","unwilling","fearful"],"a":2,"e":"Unwilling — Reluctant means unwilling or hesitant to do something.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"RELUCTANT"},{"n":49,"q":"Amit is OPTIMISTIC about the prospects of his investments.","o":["pessimistic","uncaring","carefree","sanguine"],"a":3,"e":"Sanguine — Sanguine means optimistic or hopeful, especially about a future outcome.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"OPTIMISTIC"},{"n":50,"q":"Sachin is very COOPERATIVE by nature.","o":["compliant","covert","complacent","conniving"],"a":0,"e":"Compliant — Cooperative means willing to help, work with others or comply; compliant is the nearest option provided.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"COOPERATIVE"}]},{"id":"pyq-2021-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2021 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Her smile was CONTAGIOUS.","o":["arrogant","disrespectful","sarcastic","catching"],"a":3,"e":"Catching — Contagious means capable of spreading from one person to another; figuratively, a contagious smile is catching.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"CONTAGIOUS","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"Her DYNAMIC nature impressed everyone.","o":["enduring","attentive","revolutionary","jealous"],"a":2,"e":"Revolutionary — Dynamic means energetic, progressive and characterised by change or development. Among the options, revolutionary is the nearest.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DYNAMIC"},{"n":3,"q":"She was LAMENTING her destiny.","o":["celebrating","bemoaning","blaming","making"],"a":1,"e":"Bemoaning — Lamenting means expressing sorrow, grief or regret; bemoaning is its closest synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"LAMENTING"},{"n":4,"q":"Under his leadership the company grew in an ORGANIC manner.","o":["natural","speedy","unusual","disciplined"],"a":0,"e":"Natural — Organic can mean natural, gradual or developing naturally, rather than artificially.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ORGANIC"},{"n":5,"q":"His charm lies in his ORATORY.","o":["deceit","eloquence","joke","nobility"],"a":1,"e":"Eloquence — Oratory means the art or skill of effective public speaking; eloquence is the closest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ORATORY"},{"n":6,"q":"She is a PATRON of art and culture.","o":["entrepreneur","admirer","critique","backer"],"a":3,"e":"Backer — A patron is a person who supports or sponsors a person, organisation, art or cause.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"PATRON"},{"n":7,"q":"ARROGANCE is a hallmark of his nature.","o":["concern","unpretentiousness","conceit","simplicity"],"a":2,"e":"Conceit — Arrogance means an exaggerated sense of one's own importance or superiority; conceit is synonymous.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ARROGANCE"},{"n":8,"q":"She undertook a RECONNAISSANCE of the entire issue.","o":["revaluation","ratification","investigation","regularization"],"a":2,"e":"Investigation — Reconnaissance means a preliminary examination, survey or investigation undertaken to obtain information.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"RECONNAISSANCE"},{"n":9,"q":"My daughter is my REPLICA.","o":["pride","clone","love","original"],"a":1,"e":"Clone — Replica means an exact or very close copy of something; clone is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"REPLICA"},{"n":10,"q":"The SARDONIC nature of her stories made her stand out among the contemporary writers.","o":["compassionate","insightful","mocking","comic"],"a":2,"e":"Mocking — Sardonic means grimly mocking, cynical or scornfully humorous.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"SARDONIC"},{"n":11,"q":"He found her extremely ATTRACTIVE and charming.","o":["unnatural","modern","repulsive","disappointing"],"a":2,"e":"Repulsive — Attractive means appealing or pleasing; repulsive means causing strong dislike, hence the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ATTRACTIVE","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"The sky is BOUNDLESS.","o":["high","vast","expansive","finite"],"a":3,"e":"Finite — Boundless means without limits; finite means limited.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"BOUNDLESS"},{"n":13,"q":"The sky is CLEAR today.","o":["bright","opaque","cloudless","blue"],"a":1,"e":"Opaque — Clear means transparent or easy to see through; opaque means not transparent.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CLEAR"},{"n":14,"q":"I have a fascination for DEEP waters.","o":["dark","light","dangerous","shallow"],"a":3,"e":"Shallow — Deep and shallow are direct opposites.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DEEP"},{"n":15,"q":"My boss has been too GENEROUS.","o":["stingy","rough","evil","hostile"],"a":0,"e":"Stingy — Generous means willing to give freely; stingy means unwilling to give or spend.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"GENEROUS"},{"n":16,"q":"Spring in a time of PLENTY.","o":["ugliness","scarcity","roughness","dryness"],"a":1,"e":"Scarcity — Plenty means abundance; scarcity means shortage or insufficiency.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PLENTY"},{"n":17,"q":"He is an INDUSTRIOUS workman.","o":["active","productive","lazy","disloyal"],"a":2,"e":"Lazy — Industrious means hard-working and diligent; lazy is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"INDUSTRIOUS"},{"n":18,"q":"Plants GROW in abundance here.","o":["shrivel","stretch","spread","enlarge"],"a":0,"e":"Shrivel — “Grow” means to increase or develop; “shrivel” means to shrink or wither.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"GROW"},{"n":19,"q":"She RARELY comes here.","o":["seldom","never","always","frequently"],"a":3,"e":"Frequently — Rarely means not often; frequently means often.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"RARELY"},{"n":20,"q":"She is a rather CROOKED woman.","o":["polite","generous","straightforward","happy"],"a":2,"e":"Straightforward — Crooked, when used of a person's character, can mean dishonest or deceitful; straightforward means honest and direct.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CROOKED"},{"n":21,"q":"Overstep the mark","o":["To tell people how successful you are","To step into someone else's areas of expertise","To upset someone by doing/saying more than you should","To do something in an excited way"],"a":2,"e":"To upset someone by doing/saying more than you should — Overstep the mark means to go beyond acceptable limits of behaviour.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternative meanings to each. Choose the response (a), (b), (c) or (d), whichever is the most appropriate expression and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":22,"q":"Palsy-walsy friends","o":["Good friends","Friends who help each other in difficult situations","Friends by choice, and not by chance","Unfriendly"],"a":0,"e":"Good friends — Palsy-walsy means very friendly or on very close terms, sometimes excessively so.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":23,"q":"Open a Pandora's box","o":["To do something that causes a lot of new problems that you did not expect","To do something out of compulsion","To do something beyond expectation","To do something out of the box, that causes awards and ceremonies for you"],"a":0,"e":"To do something that causes a lot of new problems that you did not expect — Open a Pandora's box means to initiate something that creates many unforeseen difficulties.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":24,"q":"Pull your socks up","o":["To get well-dressed for the occasion","Improve your work or behaviour","To speak in an honest way without hesitation","To be in control of an organization, often secretly"],"a":1,"e":"Improve your work or behaviour — Pull your socks up means to make a greater effort and improve one's performance.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":25,"q":"To get under somebody's skin","o":["To deceive someone","To admire someone","To annoy someone","To support someone"],"a":2,"e":"To annoy someone — Get under somebody's skin means to irritate or annoy someone greatly.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":26,"q":"Turn topsy-turvy","o":["To completely change something","To completely evaluate something","To enjoy yourself greatly","To exhaust yourself completely"],"a":0,"e":"To completely change something — Turn something topsy-turvy means to turn it upside down or completely alter/disorganise it.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":27,"q":"A clarion call","o":["A trumpet call","An intimidating voice","A strong request","An urgent order"],"a":2,"e":"A strong request — A clarion call means a strong, clear and forceful appeal for people to take action.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":28,"q":"Fire in the belly","o":["Fear and hatred","Powerful ambition","Love and dedication","Lethargy and indifference"],"a":1,"e":"Powerful ambition — Fire in the belly means strong determination, enthusiasm or ambition to succeed.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":29,"q":"A hunky-dory situation","o":["There are serious issues among people","There are no problems and people are happy","There is war and bloodshed all over","There is no work, only enjoyment"],"a":1,"e":"There are no problems and people are happy — Hunky-dory means satisfactory, fine or going very well.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":30,"q":"Give somebody a leg up","o":["To pull someone down","To deceive and betray someone","To help someone for their livelihood","To help someone to be successful"],"a":3,"e":"To help someone to be successful — Give somebody a leg up means to give someone help or an advantage that enables them to progress or succeed.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":31,"q":"You don't have a monopoly on suffering; / other people don't have problems too. / No error","o":["You don't have a monopoly on suffering;","other people don't have","problems too.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"problems too — The first clause is negative; the intended meaning requires “other people have problems too”, not don't have problems too.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":32,"q":"If you say that someone / you admire has feet of clay, / you mean that they have hidden faults. / No error","o":["If you say that someone","you admire has feet of clay,","you mean that they have hidden faults.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — The sentence is grammatically correct. Feet of clay means a hidden weakness or fault in an otherwise admired person.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":33,"q":"He refused to change / his decision; / he refuted it point out. / No error","o":["He refused to change","his decision;","he refuted it point out.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"he refuted it point out — The expression is incorrect. The intended phrase is “he refused to budge”/“he stuck to it”; refuted it point out is grammatically and idiomatically unacceptable.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":34,"q":"The importance of trade in Mughal times reinforced / the cultural definition of wealth as something / comprising of movable property. / No error","o":["The importance of trade in Mughal times reinforced","the cultural definition of wealth as something","comprising of movable property.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"comprising of movable property — Comprise normally takes a direct object and is not followed by of. Correct: comprising movable property.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":35,"q":"In the nineteenth century, / most traditional scholars / tried to stay clear from the imperial Government. / No error","o":["In the nineteenth century,","most traditional scholars","tried to stay clear from the imperial Government.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"tried to stay clear from the imperial Government — The standard expression is stay clear of, not stay clear from. Correct: tried to stay clear of the imperial Government.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":36,"q":"He began his discussion by pointing over / that men and women / had different biological functions. / No error","o":["He began his discussion by pointing over","that men and women","had different biological functions.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"He began his discussion by pointing over — The correct phrasal verb is pointing out. Correct: He began his discussion by pointing out that...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":37,"q":"Though he is poor, / but he is / honest. / No error","o":["Though he is poor,","but he is","honest.","No error"],"a":1,"e":"but he is — Though and but should not normally be used together in this construction. Correct: Though he is poor, he is honest.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":38,"q":"My mother / has been doing / everything for the family. / No error","o":["My mother","has been doing","everything for the family.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — My mother has been doing everything for the family is grammatically correct.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":39,"q":"When learning to dive / it is important / to relax in between and take breaks. / No error","o":["When learning to dive","it is important","to relax in between and take breaks.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"When learning to dive — According to the official key, the error is in part (A). The introductory phrase creates an awkward/dangling construction; a clearer form is “When you are learning to dive...”","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":40,"q":"I have the opportunity / to study / in America next year. / No error","o":["I have the opportunity","to study","in America next year.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No Error — I have the opportunity to study in America next year is grammatically correct.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":41,"q":"There was a time when West Germany was a ______ distinct ______.","o":["policy","polity","abstract","hierarchy"],"a":1,"e":"Polity — Polity means an organised political entity or form/system of government. West Germany was a distinct political entity.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: Each of the following sentences has a blank space and four words or group of words given after the sentence. Select the word or group of words you consider most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":42,"q":"I was ______ with the film; I had expected it to be better.","o":["disappointed","disappointing","annoying","prejudiced"],"a":0,"e":"Disappointed — A person is disappointed with something, while something causing the feeling may be disappointing. Correct: I was disappointed with the film.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":43,"q":"It was a ______ experience. Everybody was shocked.","o":["terrified","horrified","terrifying","denouncing"],"a":2,"e":"Terrifying — The experience caused terror, so the -ing adjective is required. Terrified would describe the people experiencing the fear.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":44,"q":"Elephants ______ when they perceive danger.","o":["trumpet","frolic","whine","sing"],"a":0,"e":"Trumpet — Elephants trumpet, particularly when excited, alarmed or communicating.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":45,"q":"The first film on Tagore was such a success that now they are going to make a ______.","o":["serial","sequence","sequel","sequential"],"a":2,"e":"Sequel — A sequel is a work that continues the story or subject of an earlier work.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":46,"q":"The United Nations had ______ 2020 as the International Year of Plant Health.","o":["ruled","ordered","foretold","declared"],"a":3,"e":"Declared — The standard construction is declare a particular year as the International Year of...","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":47,"q":"My brother is ______ punctual, but he is late today.","o":["normatively","primarily","normally","basically"],"a":2,"e":"Normally — Normally means usually or under ordinary circumstances. Correct: My brother is normally punctual.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":48,"q":"My son is very ______; he trusts everyone.","o":["fallible","gullible","sensible","credible"],"a":1,"e":"Gullible — Gullible means easily persuaded or deceived because one readily trusts others.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":49,"q":"Mahatma Gandhi was a lover of humanity and a ______ believer in the goodness of human nature.","o":["staunch","powerful","cheerful","hopeful"],"a":0,"e":"Staunch — Staunch means firm, loyal and strongly committed to a belief or cause. Correct: a staunch believer in the goodness of human nature.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":50,"q":"I wish I ______ her before we met.","o":["know","have known","knew","known"],"a":2,"e":"Knew — After wish, the simple past is commonly used for an unreal or contrary-to-fact present situation. According to the official key, knew is the intended answer.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"}]},{"id":"pyq-2020-i-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2020 I & II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"He nodded ABSENTLY throughout the meeting.","o":["capably","alertly","agitatedly","dreamily"],"a":1,"e":"Alertly — Absently means inattentively or without concentration. Alertly means attentively and watchfully, hence the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ABSENTLY","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response on your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"I fully believe that the cornerstone of good policy is an electorate that is EDUCATED on the national issues.","o":["cerebral","enlightened","ignorant","erudite"],"a":2,"e":"Ignorant — Educated means having knowledge or learning; ignorant means lacking knowledge or education.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"EDUCATED"},{"n":3,"q":"For IMPORTANT medical decisions, even finding a doctor you trust is not enough.","o":["significant","trivial","basic","probable"],"a":1,"e":"Trivial — “Important” means significant or of great consequence. “Trivial” means insignificant or unimportant.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"IMPORTANT"},{"n":4,"q":"Planets MOVE in their orbits.","o":["push","rotate","stall","flow"],"a":2,"e":"Stall — Move means to proceed or change position; stall means to stop or cease making progress.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"MOVE"},{"n":5,"q":"Temperature is a measure of internal energy of an object and is frequently EXPRESSED by physicists in units of Kelvin.","o":["found","told","distributed","concealed"],"a":3,"e":"Concealed — Expressed means made known, stated or revealed; concealed means hidden or kept from being known.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"EXPRESSED"},{"n":6,"q":"People argue about why Venus is so much WARMER than the Earth.","o":["friendlier","colder","wilder","heavier"],"a":1,"e":"Colder — Warmer and colder are direct opposites in relation to temperature.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"WARMER"},{"n":7,"q":"Scientists are CONCERNED whether the oceans and land biosphere will take up as much carbon in the future as they presently do.","o":["worried","indifferent","curious","puzzled"],"a":1,"e":"Indifferent — Concerned means worried, interested or attentive; indifferent means showing no interest or concern.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CONCERNED"},{"n":8,"q":"The biggest DEBATE among scientists today is about cloud feedback.","o":["contend","moot","wrangle","agreement"],"a":3,"e":"Agreement — Debate involves discussion, disagreement or argument; agreement represents concurrence or absence of dispute.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DEBATE"},{"n":9,"q":"The Earth's climate SENSITIVITY is conventionally defined as the equilibrium temperature increase caused by a doubling of carbon dioxide.","o":["imperviousness","willingness","responsiveness","closeness"],"a":0,"e":"Imperviousness — Sensitivity means responsiveness or susceptibility to influence. Imperviousness means being unaffected or unresponsive.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SENSITIVITY"},{"n":10,"q":"The oceans carry a HUGE amount of heat from the tropics to the high latitudes.","o":["significant","major","tiny","dormant"],"a":2,"e":"Tiny — Huge means extremely large; tiny means extremely small.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"HUGE"},{"n":11,"q":"P: several years ago, Q: course on climate change at Texas A&M University R: Professor Andrew Dessler created an introductory S: for freshmen and sophomores","o":["P R Q S","Q R P S","S Q R P","P Q R S"],"a":0,"e":"P–R–Q–S — “Several years ago, Professor Andrew Dessler created an introductory course on climate change at Texas A&M University for freshmen and sophomores.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence(s), the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled as P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence are four sequences namely (a), (b), (c), (d). You are required to rearrange the jumbled parts of the sentence and mark your response accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"P: I realize that solving the climate change problem Q: than solving R: will be much harder S: the ozone depletion problem","o":["P R Q S","Q R P S","S Q R P","P Q R S"],"a":0,"e":"P–R–Q–S — “I realize that solving the climate change problem will be much harder than solving the ozone depletion problem.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":13,"q":"P: although the temperature of this layer of the Q: when directly comparing the satellite R: measurements of temperature S: atmosphere should generally track the surface temperature, we must be careful","o":["P R Q S","P S Q R","S Q R P","P Q R S"],"a":1,"e":"P–S–Q–R — “Although the temperature of this layer of the atmosphere should generally track the surface temperature, we must be careful when directly comparing the satellite measurements of temperature.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":14,"q":"P: the element heats up, Q: eventually reaching high temperatures R: glowing like a dark orange that radiates S: the visible range,","o":["P Q S R","Q P S R","S Q R P","P R S Q"],"a":3,"e":"P–R–S–Q — The sequence begins with “The element heats up...” and then describes its glowing/radiating as it eventually reaches high temperatures.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":15,"q":"P: two people argue about why Venus is so much warmer Q: to the Sun, so it absorbs more solar energy. The second argues R: that it's because Venus has a thick, greenhouse- gas rich atmosphere S: than the Earth. The first argues that it's because Venus is closer","o":["P S Q R","Q R P S","S Q R P","P Q R S"],"a":0,"e":"P–S–Q–R — “Two people argue about why Venus is so much warmer than the Earth. The first argues that it's because Venus is closer to the Sun, so it absorbs more solar energy. The second argues that it's because Venus has a thick, greenhouse-gas rich atmosphere.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":16,"q":"P: are now used by more than a thousand firms Q: and are growing in popularity R: in the United States and Europe S: gain sharing-plans","o":["P R Q S","Q R P S","P R S Q","S P R Q"],"a":3,"e":"S–P–R–Q — “Gain-sharing plans are now used by more than a thousand firms in the United States and Europe and are growing in popularity.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":17,"q":"P: adversity without succumbing Q: to the clouds of doubt and jealousy R: between friends which is subjected to both prosperity and S: the essay ‘Of Friendship’ by Francis Bacon celebrated the intimacy","o":["P R Q S","Q R P S","S R P Q","P Q R S"],"a":2,"e":"S–R–P–Q — “The essay ‘Of Friendship’ by Francis Bacon celebrated the intimacy between friends which is subjected to both prosperity and adversity without succumbing to the clouds of doubt and jealousy.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":18,"q":"P: friends without which the world is Q: make friends and a person wills to want true R: it is miserable solitude that compels a person to S: nothing other than a place of wilderness","o":["P R Q S","Q R P S","R Q P S","P Q R S"],"a":2,"e":"R–Q–P–S — “It is miserable solitude that compels a person to make friends and a person wills to want true friends without which the world is nothing other than a place of wilderness.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":19,"q":"P: takes the longest Q: that never started R: the job S: to finish","o":["P R Q S","R Q P S","S Q R P","P Q R S"],"a":1,"e":"R–Q–P–S — “The job that never started takes the longest to finish.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":20,"q":"P: to what you can create Q: control, shift your energy R: instead of worrying about S: what you cannot","o":["P R S Q","Q R S P","S Q R P","R S Q P"],"a":3,"e":"R–S–Q–P — “Instead of worrying about what you cannot control, shift your energy to what you can create.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":21,"q":"She got the DIVORCE within no time.","o":["detachment","breaking down","annulment","punishment"],"a":2,"e":"Annulment — Divorce means legal dissolution or termination of a marriage. Annulment is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DIVORCE","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word/words followed by four words. Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word or group of words and mark your response on your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":22,"q":"He was known for his GENTLE disposition.","o":["harmful","amiable","cunning","adjusting"],"a":1,"e":"Amiable — Gentle means mild, kind or pleasant in nature; amiable means friendly and pleasant.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"GENTLE"},{"n":23,"q":"The harder we kick, the better the ball BOUNCES BACK.","o":["recoils","deflates","inflates","ascends"],"a":0,"e":"Recoils — Bounce back means to spring or move back after striking something; recoil has the same basic sense.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"BOUNCES BACK"},{"n":24,"q":"I plan and EXECUTE.","o":["desire","debate","accomplish","discard"],"a":2,"e":"Accomplish — Execute means to carry out or perform a plan or task; accomplish is the nearest synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"EXECUTE"},{"n":25,"q":"He listened to me with EQUANIMITY.","o":["carelessly","excitedly","patiently","half-heartedly"],"a":2,"e":"Patiently — Equanimity means calmness, composure and evenness of mind, especially under difficulty.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"EQUANIMITY"},{"n":26,"q":"The whole thing was a FUTILE exercise.","o":["costly","pointless","indecisive","successful"],"a":1,"e":"Pointless — Futile means useless or incapable of producing the desired result; hence pointless.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"FUTILE"},{"n":27,"q":"He was too CUNNING for her.","o":["capable","fit","intelligent","dodgy"],"a":3,"e":"Dodgy — Cunning means crafty, sly or deceitfully clever. Dodgy is the closest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"CUNNING"},{"n":28,"q":"This decision is CRUCIAL for me.","o":["momentous","natural","primitive","inept"],"a":0,"e":"Momentous — Crucial means extremely important or decisive; momentous is the nearest in meaning.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"CRUCIAL"},{"n":29,"q":"The media went into a FRENZY about the case.","o":["silent","creative","berserk","wrong"],"a":2,"e":"Berserk — Frenzy refers to wild, uncontrolled excitement or agitation; berserk conveys the closest sense.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"FRENZY"},{"n":30,"q":"He has been YEARNING to go home.","o":["declining","demanding","begging","hankering"],"a":3,"e":"Hankering — Yearning means a strong desire or longing; hankering has the same meaning.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"YEARNING"},{"n":31,"q":"Simulations of the 20th century by climate models that exclude the observed increase ______ greenhouse gases fail to simulate the increase in temperature over the second half of the 20th century.","o":["of","in","by","to"],"a":1,"e":"In — The correct collocation is increase in something. Correct: the observed increase in greenhouse gases.","cat":"Prepositions","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with a missing preposition. Select the correct preposition from the given options and mark your response on your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":32,"q":"In extremely poor societies, children can be put to work ______ a young age and are therefore a source of income.","o":["in","on","by","at"],"a":3,"e":"At — We use at with age. Correct: put to work at a young age.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":33,"q":"People who are averse ______ hard work, generally do not succeed in life.","o":["to","about","at","on"],"a":0,"e":"To — The adjective averse takes the preposition to. Correct: averse to hard work.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":34,"q":"I have known her ______ a long time.","o":["since","for","at","before"],"a":1,"e":"For — For is used with a period of time. Correct: I have known her for a long time.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":35,"q":"I accepted the offer ______ certain conditions.","o":["on","in","by","within"],"a":0,"e":"On — The standard expression is on certain conditions. Correct: I accepted the offer on certain conditions.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":36,"q":"She is a woman ______ humble origin.","o":["off","of","from","within"],"a":1,"e":"Of — The idiomatic construction is of humble origin. Correct: She is a woman of humble origin.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":37,"q":"There is no cure ______ the common cold.","o":["for","of","to","on"],"a":0,"e":"For — The standard collocation is a cure for a disease/illness. Correct: There is no cure for the common cold.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":38,"q":"I ran ______ John yesterday, and it was a pleasant surprise.","o":["by","off","beside","into"],"a":3,"e":"Into — Run into somebody means to meet someone unexpectedly. Correct: I ran into John yesterday.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":39,"q":"My grandmother enjoyed boating ______ the lovely lake.","o":["in","on","beside","within"],"a":1,"e":"On — Activities taking place on the surface of a lake use on. Correct: boating on the lovely lake.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":40,"q":"He visits the needy to relieve them ______ their sufferings and poverty.","o":["from","off","of","on"],"a":2,"e":"Of — The expression relieve someone of something means to free someone from a burden, pain or difficulty. Correct: relieve them of their sufferings and poverty.","cat":"Prepositions"},{"n":41,"q":"This building / comprises of six houses, / three parking lots and one basement. / No error.","o":["This building","comprises of six houses,","three parking lots and one basement.","No error."],"a":1,"e":"comprises of six houses — Comprise does not take of in this active construction. Correct: This building comprises six houses, three parking lots and one basement.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled as (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":42,"q":"I look forward / to meet / you. / No error.","o":["I look forward","to meet","you.","No error."],"a":1,"e":"to meet — In look forward to, to is a preposition and must be followed by a gerund. Correct: I look forward to meeting you.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":43,"q":"I cannot / cope up / with this pressure. / No error.","o":["I cannot","cope up","with this pressure.","No error."],"a":1,"e":"cope up — Cope is followed directly by with; up is unnecessary. Correct: I cannot cope with this pressure.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":44,"q":"I came / to this place / by walk. / No error.","o":["I came","to this place","by walk.","No error."],"a":2,"e":"by walk — The correct expression is on foot, not by walk. Correct: I came to this place on foot.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":45,"q":"What is / the time / in your watch? / No error.","o":["What is","the time","in your watch?","No error."],"a":2,"e":"in your watch — The idiomatic expression is by your watch, not in your watch. Correct: What is the time by your watch?","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":46,"q":"The price of my dress / is higher than that of / yours. / No error.","o":["The price of my dress","is higher than that of","yours.","No error."],"a":3,"e":"No Error — The sentence is accepted as grammatically correct according to the official key.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":47,"q":"My friend's / daughter-in-laws / have come. / No error.","o":["My friend's","daughter-in-laws","have come.","No error."],"a":1,"e":"daughter-in-laws — In compound nouns such as daughter-in-law, the principal noun takes the plural. Correct: daughters-in-law.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":48,"q":"I prefer seafood / than Mediterranean food. / No error.","o":["I prefer seafood","than Mediterranean food.","—","No error."],"a":2,"e":"The supplied answer key marks option C. The intended grammatical construction is “prefer X to Y”, so the corrected sentence is: “I prefer seafood to Mediterranean food.”","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":49,"q":"We were / discussing about / the issue. / No error.","o":["We were","discussing about","the issue.","No error."],"a":1,"e":"discussing about — Discuss is a transitive verb and does not take about. Correct: We were discussing the issue.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":50,"q":"Dispose off / the garbage / from the shelves. / No error.","o":["Dispose off","the garbage","from the shelves.","No error ."],"a":0,"e":"Dispose off — The correct phrasal verb is dispose of, not dispose off. Correct: Dispose of the garbage from the shelves. .","cat":"Spotting Errors"}]},{"id":"pyq-2019-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2019 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"He is always ANXIOUS.","o":["worried","dispassionate","sluggish","torpid"],"a":0,"e":"Worried — Anxious means worried, uneasy or concerned about something.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ANXIOUS","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four options. Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"The poems of Kabir are ECSTATIC in nature.","o":["efficacious","eerie","rapturous","reverential"],"a":2,"e":"Rapturous — Ecstatic means extremely happy, delighted or full of intense joy; rapturous is its closest synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ECSTATIC"},{"n":3,"q":"Ravi loves SECLUSION. Therefore, he lives in the mountains.","o":["nature","scripture","seafaring","solitariness"],"a":3,"e":"Solitariness — Seclusion means the state of being private, isolated or away from other people; hence solitariness.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"SECLUSION"},{"n":4,"q":"Hitler was a DESPOT.","o":["conservative","dictator","passionate","monstrous"],"a":1,"e":"Dictator — A despot is a ruler who exercises absolute power, often cruelly or oppressively; hence dictator.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DESPOT"},{"n":5,"q":"The imagery used in the poem is VIVID.","o":["lively","inert","ebullient","caustic"],"a":0,"e":"Lively — Vivid means bright, striking, clear or producing strong images in the mind; lively is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"VIVID"},{"n":6,"q":"A human being is always VULNERABLE to other human beings.","o":["resilient","elastic","defenceless","crude"],"a":2,"e":"Defenceless — Vulnerable means susceptible to harm or attack; unprotected or defenceless.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"VULNERABLE"},{"n":7,"q":"His language is political and VITRIOLIC.","o":["imaginative","sprightly","vivacious","abusive"],"a":3,"e":"Abusive — Vitriolic means bitter, harsh, scathing or abusive, especially in speech or writing.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"VITRIOLIC"},{"n":8,"q":"The Managing Director of the company declared that he is BROKE and there is a need to seek support from the government.","o":["bankrupt","rich","making profit","having liabilities"],"a":0,"e":"Bankrupt — Broke informally means having no money; bankrupt.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"BROKE"},{"n":9,"q":"He loves doing NASTY things.","o":["nice","fastidious","foul","finicky"],"a":2,"e":"Foul — Nasty means unpleasant, offensive or objectionable; foul is the nearest synonym.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"NASTY"},{"n":10,"q":"He could not MUSTER courage to speak the truth before his friend.","o":["injure","spoil","gather","maim"],"a":2,"e":"Gather — Muster means to gather or summon, as in muster courage = gather courage.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MUSTER"},{"n":11,"q":"His conversations are always ABSURD.","o":["farcical","foolish","preposterous","rational"],"a":3,"e":"Rational — Absurd means unreasonable or illogical; rational means reasonable and logical.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ABSURD","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four options. Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"The politician was a DISSIDENT.","o":["agitator","rebel","conformist","revolutionary"],"a":2,"e":"Conformist — A dissident is someone who opposes established beliefs or authority; a conformist accepts established practices.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DISSIDENT"},{"n":13,"q":"He wrote an INCISIVE article on corruption in politics.","o":["vague","penetrating","trenchant","precise"],"a":0,"e":"Vague — Incisive means clear, sharp and penetrating; vague means unclear or indefinite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"INCISIVE"},{"n":14,"q":"Rakesh is an eccentric PRODIGAL.","o":["extravagant","profligate","wasteful","thrifty"],"a":3,"e":"Thrifty — Prodigal means wastefully extravagant; thrifty means careful and economical with money.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PRODIGAL"},{"n":15,"q":"Friendship is always RECIPROCAL.","o":["mutual","one-sided","shared","corresponding"],"a":1,"e":"One-sided — Reciprocal means mutual or involving both sides; one-sided is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"RECIPROCAL"},{"n":16,"q":"The debate was highly STIMULATING.","o":["arousing","invigorating","boring","stirring"],"a":2,"e":"Boring — Stimulating means interesting, exciting or thought-provoking; boring is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"STIMULATING"},{"n":17,"q":"Malaria is a WIDESPREAD disease.","o":["endemic","pervasive","common","rare"],"a":3,"e":"Rare — Widespread means existing or occurring over a large area or among many people; rare is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"WIDESPREAD"},{"n":18,"q":"The bill received a VEHEMENT resistance from the opposition party in the Parliament.","o":["animated","apathetic","fervent","vigorous"],"a":1,"e":"Apathetic — Vehement means showing strong feeling, force or intensity; apathetic means showing little or no interest or emotion.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"VEHEMENT"},{"n":19,"q":"He has been OBSTINATE since his childhood.","o":["stubborn","dogged","amenable","rigid"],"a":2,"e":"Amenable — Obstinate means stubborn and unwilling to change one's opinion; amenable means willing to cooperate or be persuaded.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"OBSTINATE"},{"n":20,"q":"Mahatma Gandhi was one of the most PROMINENT leaders of his times.","o":["renowned","distinguishable","eminent","unknown"],"a":3,"e":"Unknown — Prominent means well-known, important or distinguished; unknown is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PROMINENT"},{"n":21,"q":"He had arrived at Cairo / a few months before / protests shook the Arab world. / No error","o":["He had arrived at Cairo","a few months before","protests shook the Arab world.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"He had arrived at Cairo — We normally use arrive in for cities and countries. Correct: He had arrived in Cairo...","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":22,"q":"Most of us who are older competitive runners / are not able to race anywhere at the same speed / as we do when we were 30. / No error","o":["Most of us who are older competitive runners","are not able to race anywhere at the same speed","as we do when we were 30.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"as we do when we were 30 — The reference is to the past, so did is required instead of do. Correct: ...at the same speed as we did when we were 30.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":23,"q":"Work hard / lest you / do not fail. / No error","o":["Work hard","lest you","do not fail.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"do not fail — Lest itself expresses a negative purpose and normally takes should + verb or the subjunctive. Correct: Work hard lest you should fail.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":24,"q":"The Eastern Ghats are home of 2600 plant species / and this habitat fragmentation / can pose a serious threat to endemic plants. / No error","o":["The Eastern Ghats are home of 2600 plant species","and this habitat fragmentation","can pose a serious threat to endemic plants.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"The Eastern Ghats are home of 2600 plant species — The correct expression is home to, not home of, in this construction. Correct: The Eastern Ghats are home to 2600 plant species.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":25,"q":"Turbidity current is a fast-moving current / that sweeps down submarine canyons, / carrying sand and mud into the deep sea. / No error","o":["Turbidity current is a fast-moving current","that sweeps down submarine canyons,","carrying sand and mud into the deep sea.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — The sentence is grammatically correct: a turbidity current is described as carrying sand and mud into the deep sea.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":26,"q":"Every one / of the boys / love to ride. / No error","o":["Every one","of the boys","love to ride.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"love to ride — Every one is singular and therefore requires loves. Correct: Every one of the boys loves to ride.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":27,"q":"Neither praise / nor blame / seem to affect him. / No error","o":["Neither praise","nor blame","seem to affect him.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"seem to affect him — With neither...nor, when both subjects are singular, the verb should be singular. Correct: Neither praise nor blame seems to affect him.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":28,"q":"Many a man / has succumbed / to this temptation. / No error","o":["Many a man","has succumbed","to this temptation.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — Many a + singular noun takes a singular verb: Many a man has succumbed...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":29,"q":"A time slot of fifteen minutes / are allowed / to each speaker. / No error","o":["A time slot of fifteen minutes","are allowed","to each speaker.","No error"],"a":1,"e":"are allowed — The subject A time slot is singular. Correct: A time slot of fifteen minutes is allowed to each speaker.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":30,"q":"He asked / whether either of the brothers / were at home. / No error","o":["He asked","whether either of the brothers","were at home.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"were at home — Either is singular in this construction and takes was. Correct: ...whether either of the brothers was at home.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":31,"q":"Be over the hill","o":["To be on a mountain top","To travel in the jungle","To be too old to do things","To do something in the most complete way"],"a":2,"e":"To be too old to do things — Be over the hill means to be past one's prime, especially because of age.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternative meanings to each. Choose the response (a), (b), (c) or (d) which is the most appropriate expression."},{"n":32,"q":"Bite your tongue","o":["To stop yourself from saying something because it would be better not to","To bite off your tongue while eating","To feel sorry","To ask someone something that you want"],"a":0,"e":"To stop yourself from saying something because it would be better not to — Bite your tongue means to restrain yourself from saying something.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":33,"q":"Turn a blind eye","o":["To run away","To begin to behave in a more positive manner","To change a situation","To choose to ignore behaviour that you know is wrong"],"a":3,"e":"To choose to ignore behaviour that you know is wrong — Turn a blind eye means deliberately pretend not to notice something improper or wrong.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":34,"q":"Yellow journalism","o":["Paid storytelling","Government reports published on yellow coloured papers","Writings in newspapers that try to influence people's opinion by using strong language and false information","Journalism which agrees completely with the policies of the governments and capitalists"],"a":2,"e":"Writings in newspapers that try to influence people's opinion by using strong language and false information — Yellow journalism refers to sensationalised, exaggerated or poorly substantiated reporting intended to attract attention or influence readers.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":35,"q":"To be in seventh heaven","o":["To fly in the air","To be extremely happy","To be completely alone","To visit a place which is in the list of the seven wonders of the world"],"a":1,"e":"To be extremely happy — To be in seventh heaven means to be extremely delighted or joyful.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":36,"q":"P: closely monitor the situation\nQ: by the disturbing allegations\nR: we are deeply concerned\nS: and will continue to","o":["R Q S P","S P Q R","S P R Q","P R S Q"],"a":0,"e":"R–Q–S–P — “We are deeply concerned by the disturbing allegations and will continue to closely monitor the situation.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence are four sequences namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to re-arrange the jumbled parts of the sentence and mark your response accordingly."},{"n":37,"q":"P: relieve nausea, pain and stress\nQ: aromatherapy may also help\nR: using lavender oil on their skin\nS: but patients are cautioned against","o":["R Q P S","S Q P R","Q P S R","R P S Q"],"a":2,"e":"Q–P–S–R — “Aromatherapy may also help relieve nausea, pain and stress, but patients are cautioned against using lavender oil on their skin.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":38,"q":"P: without any mandate of law\nQ: the plea filed through the advocate\nR: online is illegal and\nS: submitted that the sale of drugs and prescription medicines","o":["R Q S P","Q S R P","Q R S P","P R S Q"],"a":1,"e":"Q–S–R–P — “The plea filed through the advocate submitted that the sale of drugs and prescription medicines online is illegal and without any mandate of law.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":39,"q":"P: humans and machines have co-existed\nQ: dating back to the invention of\nR: the potter's wheel in ancient Mesopotamia\nS: at the physical level for millennia","o":["R S P Q","Q S P R","Q R P S","P S Q R"],"a":3,"e":"P–S–Q–R — “Humans and machines have co-existed at the physical level for millennia, dating back to the invention of the potter's wheel in ancient Mesopotamia.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":40,"q":"P: a sell-off as US Treasury yields\nQ: surged to multi-year highs\nR: on robust economic data\nS: global markets also witnessed","o":["R S Q P","P Q S R","Q R P S","S P Q R"],"a":3,"e":"S–P–Q–R — “Global markets also witnessed a sell-off as US Treasury yields surged to multi-year highs on robust economic data.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":41,"q":"According to the author, why should one study?","o":["To gather information about the world","To pass the time in a creative way","For pleasure, enhanced capability and holistic growth","To become only worldly wise"],"a":2,"e":"For pleasure, enhanced capability and holistic growth — Bacon says, “Studies serve for delight, for ornament, and for ability.” Thus, studies provide pleasure as well as intellectual and practical development.","cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions: In this section you have a short passage. After the passage, you will find some items based on the passage. First read the passage and then answer the items based on it. You are required to select your answers based on the contents of the passage and opinion of the author only.","c":"Passage: Studies serve for delight, for ornament, and for ability. Their chief use for delight, is in privateness and retiring; for ornament, is in discourse; and for ability, is in the judgment, and disposition of business. For expert men can execute, and perhaps judge of particulars, one by one; but the general counsels, and the plots and marshalling of affairs, come best from those that are learned. To spend too much time in studies is sloth; to use them too much for ornament, is affectation; to make judgment wholly by their rules, is the humor of a scholar. They perfect nature, and are perfected by experience: for natural abilities are like natural plants, that need pruning, by study; and studies themselves do give forth directions too much at large, except they be bounded in by experience. Crafty men condemn studies, simple men admire them and wise men use them; for they teach not their own use; but that is a wisdom without them, and above them, won by observation. Read not to contradict and confute; nor to believe and take for granted; nor to find talk and discourse; but to weigh and consider."},{"n":42,"q":"Why does the author not recommend too much of studies?","o":["Because it reflects idleness","Because it requires too much money","Because it is dangerous for the health of the mind","Because it makes men crafty"],"a":0,"e":"Because it reflects idleness — The passage explicitly states that spending too much time in studies is sloth, i.e., idleness.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":43,"q":"According to the author, how can the studies be perfected?","o":["By reflection","By application","By conversation","By experience"],"a":3,"e":"By experience — Bacon says that studies “are perfected by experience.” Thus, practical experience completes and refines learning.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":44,"q":"Which kind of human beings denounce studies?","o":["Innocent","Cunning","Stupid","Wise"],"a":1,"e":"Cunning — The passage states that “Crafty men condemn studies.” Crafty here corresponds most closely to cunning.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":45,"q":"According to the author, one should read to","o":["only argue","only believe","evaluate and understand","preach and believe"],"a":2,"e":"Evaluate and understand — Bacon advises us to read “to weigh and consider,” meaning to think critically, evaluate and understand.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":46,"q":"The Election Commission on Saturday ______ that the Assembly elections in five States will be held from November 12 to December 7.","o":["pronounced","announced","promulgated","issued"],"a":1,"e":"Announced — Announce means to make information publicly known. Correct: The Election Commission ... announced that the Assembly elections...","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: Each of the following sentences in this section has a blank space and four words or group of words given after the sentence. Select the word or group of words you consider most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":47,"q":"The victims were fruit vendors and they were going in an auto when they ______ an accident on the way.","o":["met with","ran into","experienced","heard"],"a":0,"e":"Met with — Meet with an accident is the appropriate expression in the given sentence. Correct: ...when they met with an accident on the way.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":48,"q":"Scores of villagers are ______ a sit-in protest against the construction of a new underpass.","o":["performing","sitting","staging","standing"],"a":2,"e":"Staging — The standard collocation is stage a protest, meaning to organise or participate in a public protest. Correct: Scores of villagers are staging a sit-in protest...","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":49,"q":"It is common for patients to stop ______ medicine as soon as they start feeling better.","o":["eating","gulping","swallowing","taking"],"a":3,"e":"Taking — The standard expression is take medicine. Correct: It is common for patients to stop taking medicine...","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":50,"q":"A four-year-old girl got a new lease of life after doctors at a hospital ______ a cancerous tumour from one of her kidneys.","o":["rejuvenated","removed","displaced","replaced"],"a":1,"e":"Removed — A tumour is surgically removed from the body. Correct: ...doctors at a hospital removed a cancerous tumour from one of her kidneys.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"}]},{"id":"pyq-2019-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2019 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Opening his letters / reading them carefully and sending for his clerk, / he dictated answers with them. / No error.","o":["Opening his letters","reading them carefully and sending for his clerk,","he dictated answers with them.","No error."],"a":2,"e":"“he dictated answers with them” — The correct preposition is to, not with. Correct: He dictated answers to them.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter, i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":2,"q":"He was my school-friend, / but becoming a great man, / he has grown proud enough to forget his old friends. / No error.","o":["He was my school-friend,","but becoming a great man,","he has grown proud enough to forget his old friends.","No error."],"a":1,"e":"“but becoming a great man” — The participial construction is incorrect. It should indicate that the change happened after he became a great man. Correct: but having become a great man...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":3,"q":"Rabindranath Tagore, / a Nobel laureate and the author of the national anthem, / found Shantiniketan. / No error.","o":["Rabindranath Tagore,","a Nobel laureate and the author of the national anthem,","found Shantiniketan.","No error."],"a":2,"e":"“found Shantiniketan” — Found is the present/base form meaning establish; its past tense is founded. Correct: Rabindranath Tagore ... founded Shantiniketan.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":4,"q":"The art of printing was introduced into England / during the reign of Edward IV / by William Caxton, a native of Kent. / No error.","o":["The art of printing was introduced into England","during the reign of Edward IV","by William Caxton, a native of Kent.","No error."],"a":3,"e":"No error — The sentence correctly states that the art of printing was introduced into England during Edward IV's reign by William Caxton.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":5,"q":"From thirty years / he devoted himself to public affairs / without taking a holiday. / No error.","o":["From thirty years","he devoted himself to public affairs","without taking a holiday.","No error."],"a":0,"e":"“From thirty years” — For is used for a period of time; from indicates a starting point. Correct: For thirty years, he devoted himself...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":6,"q":"If Ramesh will be promoted / he will get / a higher salary. / No error.","o":["If Ramesh will be promoted","he will get","a higher salary.","No error."],"a":0,"e":"“If Ramesh will be promoted” — In a first conditional, the if-clause normally takes the simple present, not will. Correct: If Ramesh is promoted, he will get a higher salary.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":7,"q":"My brother goes / to the office / five day week. / No error.","o":["My brother goes","to the office","five day week.","No error."],"a":2,"e":"“five day week” — In this compound adjective, the correct expression requires the article and hyphenation. Correct: a five-day week.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":8,"q":"If you lend Mohan a pen / he will lend it to someone else / and never you will get it back. / No error.","o":["If you lend Mohan a pen","he will lend it to someone else","and never you will get it back.","No error."],"a":2,"e":"“and never you will get it back” — When a negative adverb such as never begins the clause, subject–auxiliary inversion is required. Correct: and never will you get it back.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":9,"q":"One of most widely spread / bad habits / is the use of tobacco. / No error.","o":["One of most widely spread","bad habits","is the use of tobacco.","No error."],"a":0,"e":"“One of most widely spread” — The superlative construction requires the. Correct: One of the most widely spread bad habits...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":10,"q":"A great part / of Arabia / is desert. / No error.","o":["A great part","of Arabia","is desert.","No error."],"a":3,"e":"No error — A great part of Arabia is desert is grammatically correct.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":11,"q":"Some people complain when they ENCOUNTER a small misfortune in the course of their thoroughly happy life.","o":["run into","run away","run down","run with"],"a":0,"e":"Run into — Encounter means to meet or come across unexpectedly; run into conveys the same meaning.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ENCOUNTER","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words or groups of words. Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word/words and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"This world is full of MISERIES.","o":["indifferent love","perfect happiness","great suffering","moderate sympathies"],"a":2,"e":"Great suffering — Misery means great suffering, distress or unhappiness.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MISERIES"},{"n":13,"q":"A glance at a beautiful object gives us DELIGHT.","o":["wisdom","happiness","purity","peace"],"a":1,"e":"Happiness — Delight means great pleasure, joy or happiness.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DELIGHT"},{"n":14,"q":"It is terrible for people to die of STARVATION.","o":["slouch","staple","plenty","hunger"],"a":3,"e":"Hunger — Starvation means suffering or death caused by extreme lack of food, hence hunger.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"STARVATION"},{"n":15,"q":"The university has constituted a GRIEVANCE redressal committee to look into the matter.","o":["depression","complaint","abrasion","gratefulness"],"a":1,"e":"Complaint — A grievance is a complaint about something considered unfair or wrong.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"GRIEVANCE"},{"n":16,"q":"Rakesh delivered a SLANDEROUS speech.","o":["abusive","praiseworthy","moderate","inspiring"],"a":0,"e":"Abusive — Slanderous means false, defamatory or abusive, especially when damaging someone's reputation.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"SLANDEROUS"},{"n":17,"q":"Suddenly, the sky was darkened by a GIGANTIC bird.","o":["winged","small","tiny","enormous"],"a":3,"e":"Enormous — Gigantic means extremely large or huge; hence enormous.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"GIGANTIC"},{"n":18,"q":"To ABOLISH poverty would be to destroy the soil upon which mankind produces the virtues conducive to higher civilization.","o":["detest","eradicate","nurture","assimilate"],"a":1,"e":"Eradicate — Abolish means to eliminate, end or do away with; eradicate is the closest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ABOLISH"},{"n":19,"q":"The Arabs who are not in the cities live in the desert throughout the year, shifting from one OASIS to another.","o":["sandbank","mound","dune","spring"],"a":3,"e":"Spring — An oasis is a fertile place in a desert where water is available, generally around a spring or other water source.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"OASIS"},{"n":20,"q":"The VARIOUS facets of life can be found reflected in a large city.","o":["several","similar","valuable","singular"],"a":0,"e":"Several — Various means several, different or diverse.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"VARIOUS"},{"n":21,"q":"Ramesh is a very DUBIOUS character.","o":["shady","suspicious","trustworthy","doubtful"],"a":2,"e":"Trustworthy — Dubious means doubtful, suspicious or unreliable; trustworthy is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DUBIOUS","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word/words and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":22,"q":"Do not indulge in UNMINDFUL activities, please.","o":["vigilant","careless","stupid","fatuous"],"a":0,"e":"Vigilant — Unmindful means not attentive or heedful; vigilant means watchful and alert.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"UNMINDFUL"},{"n":23,"q":"He is suffering from a CURABLE disease.","o":["remediable","treatable","terminal","operable"],"a":2,"e":"Terminal — Curable means capable of being cured; terminal describes a disease expected to result in death and is the opposite among the options.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CURABLE"},{"n":24,"q":"He was born on a very AUSPICIOUS day.","o":["propitious","fortunate","ominous","opportune"],"a":2,"e":"Ominous — Auspicious means favourable or promising good fortune; ominous suggests something unfavourable or threatening.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"AUSPICIOUS"},{"n":25,"q":"He has deeper HOSTILITY towards Mohan.","o":["animosity","belligerence","malice","friendship"],"a":3,"e":"Friendship — Hostility means enmity or antagonism; friendship is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"HOSTILITY"},{"n":26,"q":"His life is rather MONOTONOUS.","o":["exciting","dreary","tedious","uneventful"],"a":0,"e":"Exciting — Monotonous means dull, repetitive or tedious; exciting is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"MONOTONOUS"},{"n":27,"q":"Macbeth is a morally REPULSIVE character.","o":["abominable","attractive","obnoxious","ugly"],"a":1,"e":"Attractive — Repulsive means causing strong dislike or disgust; attractive is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"REPULSIVE"},{"n":28,"q":"The serene beauty of Kashmir had a SOOTHING effect on his mind.","o":["placid","pleasing","tranquil","turbulent"],"a":3,"e":"Turbulent — Soothing means calming or comforting; turbulent suggests disturbance or agitation.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SOOTHING"},{"n":29,"q":"Life is TRANSIENT in nature.","o":["brief","momentary","eternal","short-lived"],"a":2,"e":"Eternal — Transient means temporary or short-lived; eternal means everlasting.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"TRANSIENT"},{"n":30,"q":"Sohan is a VAIN person.","o":["modest","arrogant","conceited","proud"],"a":0,"e":"Modest — Vain means excessively proud or conceited; modest is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"VAIN"},{"n":31,"q":"A dark horse","o":["a black coloured horse","a person who wins a race or competition although no one expected him to","a person who keeps secrets","an ignorant person"],"a":1,"e":"A person who wins a race or competition although no one expected him to — A dark horse is a relatively unknown or unexpected person who surprisingly succeeds.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternative meanings to each. Choose the response (a), (b), (c) or (d) which is the most appropriate expression."},{"n":32,"q":"A show-stopper","o":["someone who stops the show","someone who organizes the show","a performance that is extremely good","a fashionable person"],"a":2,"e":"A performance that is extremely good — A show-stopper is a performance so impressive that it receives prolonged applause or becomes the highlight of a show.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":33,"q":"A jack of all trades","o":["someone who has many skills","a confident and not very serious young man","someone who has hit the jackpot","a great businessman"],"a":0,"e":"Someone who has many skills — A jack of all trades is someone capable of doing many different kinds of work.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":34,"q":"Fight tooth and nail","o":["to quarrel with someone","to attack someone with a lot of force","to try hard to prevent something from happening","to try very hard to achieve something"],"a":3,"e":"To try very hard to achieve something — Fight tooth and nail means to fight or try with maximum determination and effort.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":35,"q":"Fair and square","o":["in an honest way","in a critical way","neither very good nor very bad","in a foolish way"],"a":0,"e":"In an honest way — Fair and square means honestly, fairly and according to the rules.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":36,"q":"S1: We do not know what to do with our knowledge.\nS6: In the course of time they may rule over us altogether.\nP: For example, we are unable to manage our machines.\nQ: We already find it difficult to do without machines.\nR: Machines should be fed properly and waited upon attentively; otherwise they refuse to work or cause destruction.\nS: Science has given us superhuman powers, which we do not use properly.\nThe proper sequence should be","o":["S P R Q","P S Q R","Q R P S","S R P Q"],"a":0,"e":"S–P–R–Q — The logical sequence is: Science has given us superhuman powers, which we do not use properly Correct: For example, we are unable to manage our machines Correct: Machines should be fed properly and waited upon attentively... Correct: We already find it difficult to do without machines. This connects naturally with S1, “We do not know what to do with our knowledge,” and S6, “In the course of time they may rule over us altogether.”","cat":"Ordering of Sentences (S1–S6)","d":"Directions: In this section, each item consists of six sentences of a passage. The first and sixth sentences are given in the beginning as S1 and S6. The middle four sentences in each have been jumbled up and labelled P, Q, R and S. You are required to find the proper sequence of the four sentences and mark your response accordingly on the Answer Sheet."},{"n":37,"q":"S1: The British rule in India has brought about moral, material, cultural and spiritual ruination of this great country.\nS6: We are not to kill anybody but it is our dharma to see that the curse of this Government is blotted out.\nP: I regard this rule as a curse.\nQ: Sedition has become my religion.\nR: Ours is a non-violent battle.\nS: I am out to destroy this system of Government.\nThe proper sequence should be","o":["S P R Q","P S Q R","Q R P S","S R P Q"],"a":1,"e":"P–S–Q–R — I regard this rule as a curse Correct: I am out to destroy this system of Government Correct: Sedition has become my religion Correct: Ours is a non-violent battle. This logically connects the condemnation of British rule with the concluding emphasis on non-violence.","cat":"Ordering of Sentences (S1–S6)"},{"n":38,"q":"P: the urban local body elections\nQ: unidentified gunmen\nR: and injured another during\nS: shot dead two workers","o":["Q S R P","P Q S R","S P R Q","R P S Q"],"a":0,"e":"Q–S–R–P — “Unidentified gunmen shot dead two workers and injured another during the urban local body elections.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence are four sequences namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to rearrange the jumbled parts of the sentence and mark your response accordingly."},{"n":39,"q":"P: both intense political and\nQ: this state has a history of\nR: of syncretic accomplishments\nS: religious contestation and","o":["S Q P R","P Q S R","S Q R P","Q P S R"],"a":3,"e":"Q–P–S–R — “This state has a history of both intense political and religious contestation and of syncretic accomplishments.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":40,"q":"P: the father also\nQ: in his quest for justice\nR: by the system\nS: feels let down","o":["S Q P R","P S R Q","S Q R P","P Q R S"],"a":1,"e":"P–S–R–Q — “The father also feels let down by the system in his quest for justice.”","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":41,"q":"According to the author, India","o":["has never been one nation","has been an aggregate of several nations","has always been one nation along with differences","became a nation after the British came"],"a":2,"e":"Has always been one nation along with differences — The author acknowledges differences but argues that India was fundamentally one nation.","cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions: In this section you have a passage. After the passage, you will find some items based on the passage. First, read the passage and answer the items based on it. You are required to select your answers based on the contents of the passage and opinion of the author only.","c":"Passage: I do not wish to suggest that because we were one nation we had no differences, but it is submitted that our leading men travelled throughout India either on foot or in bullock-carts. They learned one another’s languages and there was no aloofness amongst them. What do you think could have been the intention of those farseeing ancestors of ours who established Setubandha (Rameshwar) in the South, Jagannath in the East and Hardwar in the North as places of pilgrimage? You will admit they were no fools. They knew that worship of God could have been performed just as well at home. They taught us that those whose hearts were aglow with righteousness had the Ganges in their own homes. But they saw that India was one undivided land so made by nature. They, therefore, argued that it must be one nation. Arguing thus, they established holy places in various parts of India, and fired the people with an idea of nationality in a manner unknown in other parts of the world. And we Indians are one as no two Englishmen are. Only you and I and others who consider ourselves civilized and superior persons imagine that we are many nations. It was after the advent of railways that we began to believe in distinctions, and you are at liberty now to say that it is through the railways that we are beginning to abolish those distinctions. An opium-eater may argue the advantage of opium-eating from the fact that he began to understand the evil of the opium habit after having eaten it. I would ask you to consider well what I had said on the railways."},{"n":42,"q":"Why did the great sages of India establish pilgrimages in the different corners of the country?","o":["Because they wanted to push people to travel to different places","Because they could observe the underlying unity of the country as made by nature","Because they themselves had travelled to these places","Because they wanted people to be religious everywhere"],"a":1,"e":"Because they could observe the underlying unity of the country as made by nature — The author says the ancestors saw India as “one undivided land so made by nature” and established pilgrimage centres across it to strengthen national unity.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":43,"q":"In the passage, the author’s attitude towards the railways is","o":["critical","sympathetic","indifferent","apathetic"],"a":0,"e":"Critical — The author is clearly critical of the railways, arguing that distinctions became more strongly perceived after their advent.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":44,"q":"What does the author mean when he says that “whose hearts were aglow with righteousness had the Ganges in their own homes”?","o":["One need not visit the Ganges to take holy bath","The Ganges has been polluted, so one should bath at home","One should take a holy dip in the Ganges to purify one’s heart","The purity of heart is superior to observance of any ritual"],"a":3,"e":"The purity of heart is superior to observance of any ritual — The statement means that inner righteousness and purity are more important than physically visiting a sacred place.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":45,"q":"The paragraph is written in a","o":["dialogic style","prescriptive style","descriptive style","analytical style"],"a":0,"e":"Dialogic style — The passage uses expressions such as “What do you think...?”, “You will admit...” and “I would ask you...”, giving it the character of a dialogue or conversation.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":46,"q":"______ forests prevent erosion.","o":["Lean","Dense","Sparse","Tidy"],"a":1,"e":"Dense — Dense forests have thick vegetation and extensive root systems that help hold the soil together and prevent erosion. Correct: Dense forests prevent erosion.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: Each of the following sentences in this section has a blank space and four words or group of words given after the sentence. Select the word or group of words you consider most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":47,"q":"Three people were arrested and an illegal arms unit was ______ by the police in a raid.","o":["revealed","searched","discovered","busted"],"a":3,"e":"Busted — Bust in this context means to discover and shut down an illegal operation. Correct: An illegal arms unit was busted by the police.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":48,"q":"A woman got into the car and ______.","o":["drove off","broke down","rode in","drove in"],"a":0,"e":"Drove off — Drive off means to leave a place in a vehicle. Correct: A woman got into the car and drove off.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":49,"q":"The lecture was not very interesting. In fact I ______ in the middle of it.","o":["showed off","put off","dozed off","plugged off"],"a":2,"e":"Dozed off — Doze off means to fall asleep, usually unintentionally and for a short time. Correct: In fact I dozed off in the middle of it.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":50,"q":"The cops ______ murder by kin.","o":["suspect","afford","manage","administer"],"a":0,"e":"Suspect — Suspect means to believe something is probably true without definite proof. Correct: The cops suspect murder by kin.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"}]},{"id":"pyq-2018-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2018 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"He appears / to be / a honest man. / No error","o":["He appears","to be","a honest man.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“a honest man” — “Honest” begins with a vowel sound, so it takes an, not a. Correct: He appears to be an honest man.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter, i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":2,"q":"One of the members / expressed doubt if / the Minister was an atheist. / No error","o":["One of the members","expressed doubt if","the Minister was an atheist.","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“expressed doubt if” — After doubt in this construction, whether is appropriate. Correct: One of the members expressed doubt whether the Minister was an atheist.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":3,"q":"This view has been taken / by one of the ablest persons / who has written on this subject. / No error","o":["This view has been taken","by one of the ablest persons","who has written on this subject.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — The sentence is grammatically correct. One of the ablest persons who has written... is acceptable in the intended construction.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":4,"q":"Slow and steady / owns the race, / as the wise would say. / No error","o":["Slow and steady","owns the race,","as the wise would say.","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“owns the race” — The correct expression is wins the race, not owns the race. Correct: Slow and steady wins the race.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":5,"q":"We often hear people say / that most human beings have not evolved / or grow up enough to stick to truth or non-violence. / No error","o":["We often hear people say","that most human beings have not evolved","or grow up enough to stick to truth or non-violence.","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“or grow up enough” — The verb must remain parallel with have not evolved. Correct: have not evolved or grown up enough.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":6,"q":"The Industrial Revolution saw a MASSIVE rise in the population of Europe.","o":["enormous","erroneous","hazardous","perilous"],"a":0,"e":"Enormous — Massive means extremely large or enormous.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MASSIVE","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words or groups of words. Select the word or group of words that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":7,"q":"I had some deepest CONVICTIONS reflected in my work.","o":["ideas and opinions","firm beliefs","prejudices","biases"],"a":1,"e":"Firm beliefs — Convictions are strongly held opinions or firm beliefs.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"CONVICTIONS"},{"n":8,"q":"This boy is very TIMID.","o":["courageous","shy","clever","dull"],"a":1,"e":"Shy — Timid means lacking courage or confidence; shy.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"TIMID"},{"n":9,"q":"My friend is as STUBBORN as a mule.","o":["observant","obnoxious","obstinate","opulent"],"a":2,"e":"Obstinate — Stubborn means unwilling to change one's opinion or behaviour; obstinate.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"STUBBORN"},{"n":10,"q":"His behaviour was deliberately PROVOCATIVE.","o":["exciting desire","infuriating","pitiable","creating frustrations"],"a":1,"e":"Infuriating — Provocative describes something intended or likely to provoke a strong, often angry, reaction; infuriating is the nearest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"PROVOCATIVE"},{"n":11,"q":"Too many cooks SPOIL the broth.","o":["tarnish","wreck","embellish","upset"],"a":2,"e":"Embellish — Spoil means damage or ruin; embellish means beautify or improve by decoration.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SPOIL","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words or groups of words. Select the word or group of words that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"He is BIASED against the students from cities.","o":["open","prejudiced","liked","impartial"],"a":3,"e":"Impartial — Biased means prejudiced or favouring one side; impartial means unbiased.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"BIASED"},{"n":13,"q":"It is easy to be an ORTHODOX.","o":["idolatrous","intelligent","malignant","heterodox"],"a":3,"e":"Heterodox — Orthodox means conforming to established beliefs; heterodox means contrary to accepted beliefs.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ORTHODOX"},{"n":14,"q":"PERMIT me to present you with a book.","o":["allow","enclose","prohibit","persuade"],"a":2,"e":"Prohibit — Permit means allow; prohibit means forbid.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PERMIT"},{"n":15,"q":"None but the BRAVE deserves the fair.","o":["ugly person","coward","jealous person","weak person"],"a":1,"e":"Coward — Brave means courageous; a coward is a person lacking courage.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"BRAVE"},{"n":16,"q":"“The brow of the conjuror was clouded with a gathering frown.” The sentence means that the conjuror","o":["was very pleased","was very sad","was rather angry","was very afraid"],"a":2,"e":"Was rather angry — The conjuror's brow becoming “clouded with a gathering frown” shows that he was becoming angry.","cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions: In this section, you have two short passages. After each passage, you will find some items based on the passage. First, read a passage and answer the items based on it and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly. You are required to select your answers based on the contents of the passage and opinion of the author only.","c":"Passage 1: “Now, ladies and gentlemen,” said the conjuror, “having shown you that the cloth is absolutely empty, I will proceed to take from it a bowl of goldfish. Presto!”\nAll around the hall people were saying, “Oh, how wonderful! How does he do it?”\nBut the Quick Man on the front seat said in a big whisper to the people near him, “He-had-it-up-his-sleeve.”\nThen the people nodded brightly at the Quick Man and said, “Oh, of course”, and everybody whispered round the hall, “He-had-it-up-his-sleeve.”\n“My next trick,” said the conjuror, “is the famous Hindostanee rings. You will notice that the rings are apparently separate; at a blow they all join (clang, clang, clang)—Presto!”\nThere was a general buzz of stupefaction till the Quick Man was heard to whisper, “He-must-have-had-another-lot-up-his-sleeve.”\nAgain everybody nodded and whispered, “The-rings-were-up-his-sleeve.”\nThe brow of the conjuror was clouded with a gathering frown.\n“I will now,” he continued, “show you a most amusing trick by which I am enabled to take any number of eggs from a hat. Will some gentleman kindly lend me his hat? Ah, thank you—Presto!”\nHe extracted seventeen eggs, and for thirty-five seconds the audience began to think that he was wonderful. Then the Quick Man whispered along the front bench, “He-has-a-hen-up-his-sleeve,” and all the people whispered it on. “He-has-a-hen-up-his-sleeve.”\nThe egg trick was ruined.\nIt went on like that all through. It transpired from the whispers of the Quick Man that the conjuror must have concealed up his sleeve, in addition to the rings, hens, and fish, several packs of cards, a loaf of bread, a doll’s cradle, a live guinea pig, a fifty-cent piece, and a rocking chair."},{"n":17,"q":"“The egg trick was ruined.” This means that","o":["eggs were all broken","people were unconvinced","conjuror was disappointed","the trick could not be performed"],"a":1,"e":"People were unconvinced — The Quick Man's explanation destroyed the audience's sense of wonder; hence the trick was “ruined.”","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":18,"q":"According to the Quick Man, the conjuror","o":["had everything bought for production","produced things with the magic he knew","had things in the larger sleeves of his coat","created an illusion of things with his magic"],"a":2,"e":"Had things in the larger sleeves of his coat — The Quick Man repeatedly claimed that the objects were hidden up the conjuror's sleeve.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":19,"q":"The author believes that the Quick Man was really","o":["foolish","clever","wrong","right"],"a":0,"e":"Foolish — The passage humorously portrays the Quick Man as foolishly claiming that virtually everything was hidden in the conjuror's sleeve.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":20,"q":"The conjuror extracted seventeen eggs from the hat of","o":["the Quick Man","his own","one gentleman from the audience","None of the above"],"a":2,"e":"One gentleman from the audience — The conjuror asked a gentleman to lend him his hat and then extracted seventeen eggs from it.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":21,"q":"Magda had not been out much lately because","o":["she had not been keeping well","she was busy with her studies","the weather had been unpleasant","her sister had come over to stay"],"a":2,"e":"The weather had been unpleasant — Magda says she had not been out much because she did not like the wet weather.","cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions: Read Passage 2 carefully and answer the items based on it. Select your answers based on the contents of the passage and opinion of the author only.","c":"Passage 2: Magda: Good morning, Mrs. Smiles. It’s wet, isn’t it?\nMrs. Smiles: Yes, it is, isn’t it? How are you today? All right? You haven’t been studying too hard, have you? You look a bit pale.\nMagda: I don’t, do I? Well, I haven’t been out much lately. I don’t like this weather a bit. Why, we haven’t had a dry day for weeks, have we?\nMrs. Smiles: No, I don’t think we have. Let’s see, you’ll be going back home next month, won’t you?\nMagda: Oh no, I’m not going back yet. My sister’s coming over first.\nMrs. Smiles: Oh, is she? You’re looking forward to that, I expect. How long is it since you saw her?\nMagda: Nearly a year now. Yes, I am looking forward to it very much.\nMrs. Smiles: She’s coming over here to study?\nMagda: Yes, that’s right. I shan’t go back home until she’s settled down. I don’t think I ought to, ought I?\nMrs. Smiles: Well, no, she’s younger than you are, is she?\nMagda: Yes, she’s only eighteen."},{"n":22,"q":"Magda was not going back home yet because","o":["it had been raining heavily","her sister was coming over","her studies were not completed","she was not feeling well"],"a":1,"e":"Her sister was coming over — Magda says, “I'm not going back yet. My sister's coming over first.”","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":23,"q":"Mrs. Smiles says, “You’re looking forward to that.” This means, Magda was ______ her sister’s arrival.","o":["expecting with pleasure","preparing hard for","thinking greatly about","watching with joy"],"a":0,"e":"Expecting with pleasure — Look forward to means to anticipate something with pleasure.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":24,"q":"Magda’s sister was coming to","o":["visit her sister","help Mrs. Smiles","settle down in England","pursue her studies"],"a":3,"e":"Pursue her studies — Mrs. Smiles asks whether Magda's sister is coming there to study, and Magda confirms it.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":25,"q":"Mrs. Smiles and Magda are","o":["classmates","school friends","neighbours","sisters-in-law"],"a":2,"e":"Neighbours — From the conversational context, neighbours is the appropriate relationship.","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":26,"q":"We fail to understand your reasons for ______ the college without completing the degree.","o":["attending","joining","leaving","refusing"],"a":2,"e":"Leaving — The natural construction is: reasons for leaving the college without completing the degree.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: Each of the following sentences in this section has a blank space and four words or groups of words given after the sentence. Select whichever word or group of words you consider most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":27,"q":"She ______ her energy and started shouting only when she heard the noise of bulldozers and cranes.","o":["checked","conserved","maintained","controlled"],"a":1,"e":"Conserved — Conserved her energy means saved or preserved her energy until it was needed.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":28,"q":"The British ______ all over Africa and Asia collapsed in the first half of the twentieth century.","o":["hegemony","domicile","residence","inheritance"],"a":0,"e":"Hegemony — Hegemony means political or social dominance, fitting British dominance over parts of Africa and Asia.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":29,"q":"The football match has to be ______ because of the weather.","o":["called off","continued","put off","turned off"],"a":3,"e":"Turned off — As per the official key, the answer is (D). Note: In standard usage, called off would normally mean “cancelled,” but the official key supplied for this paper marks D, so it is retained here exactly.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":30,"q":"If I were rich, ______ a lot.","o":["I’ll travel","I can travel","I would travel","I travelled"],"a":2,"e":"I would travel — This is the second conditional: If + past simple Correct: would + base verb. Correct: If I were rich, I would travel a lot.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":31,"q":"They apologised ______ me for what happened.","o":["to","at","for","with"],"a":0,"e":"To — The correct collocation is apologise to someone for something. Correct: They apologised to me for what happened.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":32,"q":"If you are tired of swimming, just ______ for a while.","o":["struggle","stroke","float","streak"],"a":2,"e":"Float — If tired while swimming, one can float for a while without actively swimming.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":33,"q":"He had ______ spoken for two minutes when there was a commotion in the crowd.","o":["even","hardly","often","little"],"a":1,"e":"Hardly — Hardly...when indicates that something happened almost immediately. Correct: He had hardly spoken for two minutes when...","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":34,"q":"I would not commit myself ______ that course of action.","o":["of","to","by","with"],"a":1,"e":"To — The standard expression is commit oneself to something. Correct: I would not commit myself to that course of action.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":35,"q":"The soldiers waiting to go into battle for the first time were full of ______.","o":["apprehension","consideration","anticipation","frivolity"],"a":0,"e":"Apprehension — Apprehension means anxiety or fear about something that may happen, appropriate for soldiers about to enter battle for the first time.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":36,"q":"A red-letter day","o":["a trivial day","a very important or significant day","a day of bloodshed and violence","a mourning day"],"a":1,"e":"A very important or significant day — A red-letter day is a memorable or particularly important day.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions: Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternative meanings to each. Choose the response (a), (b), (c) or (d) which is the most appropriate meaning and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":37,"q":"The gift of the gab","o":["ability to speak easily and confidently","ability to spoil something","ability to sell things","gift from a secret institution"],"a":0,"e":"Ability to speak easily and confidently — The gift of the gab means the ability to speak fluently, persuasively and confidently.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":38,"q":"Walk a tightrope","o":["to be forced to leave your job","to be ready to fall","to act very carefully","to invite danger"],"a":2,"e":"To act very carefully — To walk a tightrope means to deal with a difficult situation where one must act very carefully.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":39,"q":"To be in a fix","o":["to receive strong criticism","to support oneself","to fix problems","to be in a difficult situation"],"a":3,"e":"To be in a difficult situation — To be in a fix means to be in a troublesome or difficult situation.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":40,"q":"To fish in troubled waters","o":["to borrow money","to steal belongings","to get benefit in bad situation","to extend a helping hand"],"a":2,"e":"To get benefit in bad situation — To fish in troubled waters means to take advantage of a confused or difficult situation.","cat":"Idioms & Phrases"},{"n":41,"q":"Domestic fires in Indian villages\nP: as the heat is dispersed\nQ: an unhealthy smoke accumulates\nR: are wasteful\nS: lit in Chulhas\nThe correct sequence should be","o":["PQSR","PQRS","SPQR","SRPQ"],"a":3,"e":"SRPQ — Domestic fires in Indian villages lit in Chulhas are wasteful as the heat is dispersed and an unhealthy smoke accumulates.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence are four sequences namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to rearrange the jumbled parts of the sentence and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":42,"q":"Bold rocks near at hand\nP: makes a fine forest for the imagination\nQ: than distant Alps\nR: are more inspiring\nS: and the thick fern upon a heath\nThe correct sequence should be","o":["QRSP","RSQP","RQSP","SRQP"],"a":2,"e":"RQSP — Bold rocks near at hand are more inspiring than distant Alps and the thick fern upon a heath makes a fine forest for the imagination.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":43,"q":"Newton discovered that\nP: due to the gravitational pull\nQ: of the earth\nR: the apple falls\nS: on the ground\nThe correct sequence should be","o":["QPRS","RSPQ","QSPR","SQRP"],"a":1,"e":"RSPQ — Newton discovered that the apple falls on the ground due to the gravitational pull of the earth.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":44,"q":"The knocking\nP: were still in the house\nQ: for some time\nR: although the echoes of it\nS: ceased\nThe correct sequence should be","o":["SRPQ","SQRP","PQSR","RSPQ"],"a":0,"e":"SRPQ — The knocking ceased although the echoes of it were still in the house for some time.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":45,"q":"The history of mankind\nP: and steady progress\nQ: continuous change\nR: the history of\nS: from barbarism to refinement\nThe correct sequence should be","o":["PQRS","RQPS","QSPR","SQRP"],"a":1,"e":"RQPS — The history of mankind [is] the history of continuous change and steady progress from barbarism to refinement.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":46,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 46.","o":["Unlike","Similar to","With","Like"],"a":0,"e":"Unlike — The passage contrasts honeybees with insects that live alone: Unlike other insects...","cat":"Cloze Test","d":"Directions: In the following passage there are some blank spaces with four words or groups of words given. Select whichever word or group of words you consider most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","c":"Passage: The honeybee is a very unusual kind of insect. 46. ______ other insects which live alone, the honeybee lives as a/an 47. ______ of a community. These bees live together in what is known as a bee 48. ______. The head of the bees is called the queen bee. She is 49. ______ than the rest of the bees. Her main task is to 50. ______ eggs."},{"n":47,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 47.","o":["group","individual","member","troop"],"a":2,"e":"Member — A honeybee lives as a member of a community.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":48,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 48.","o":["house","home","army","colony"],"a":3,"e":"Colony — A community of bees living together is called a bee colony.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":49,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 49.","o":["heaviest","heavier","largest","larger"],"a":3,"e":"Larger — The queen bee is larger than the other bees. The comparative form is required because of than.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":50,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 50.","o":["lay","hatch","make","bear"],"a":0,"e":"Lay — The standard expression is lay eggs. Correct: Her main task is to lay eggs.","cat":"Cloze Test"}]},{"id":"pyq-2017-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2017 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The discussion was WOUND UP after a long and fruitful exchange of views.","o":["postponed","cut short","interrupted","concluded"],"a":3,"e":"Concluded — Wound up means brought to an end or concluded.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"WOUND UP","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word/words followed by four options. Select the option that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word/words and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"He was fully ALIVE TO the need for making adjustments.","o":["concerned about","worried about","aware of","indifferent about"],"a":2,"e":"Aware of — Alive to means fully aware or conscious of something.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ALIVE TO"},{"n":3,"q":"The police officer tried to INTIMIDATE the witness but in vain.","o":["inform","reward","frighten","persuade"],"a":2,"e":"Frighten — Intimidate means to frighten or threaten someone into submission.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"INTIMIDATE"},{"n":4,"q":"We must adopt DRASTIC measures to control population growth.","o":["simple","dramatic","realistic","severe"],"a":3,"e":"Severe — Drastic means severe, extreme or forceful, especially regarding measures or action.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DRASTIC"},{"n":5,"q":"He is extremely METICULOUS in his approach.","o":["simple","careful","fair","reasonable"],"a":1,"e":"Careful — Meticulous means extremely careful and attentive to details.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"METICULOUS"},{"n":6,"q":"The expert's MINUTE examination brought to light some important clues.","o":["quick","detailed","superficial","prolonged"],"a":1,"e":"Detailed — Minute here means very detailed and thorough.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MINUTE"},{"n":7,"q":"The decision of the Union Government to REPEAL the Urban Land Ceiling Act has been welcomed by all.","o":["suppress","amend","cancel","withhold"],"a":2,"e":"Cancel — Repeal means officially revoke or cancel a law or Act.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"REPEAL"},{"n":8,"q":"This is his MAIDEN appearance on the screen.","o":["first","last","girlish","shy"],"a":0,"e":"First — Maiden used attributively means first,as in maiden appearance.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MAIDEN"},{"n":9,"q":"At the end of the marathon everybody was EXHAUSTED.","o":["weakened","honoured","satisfied","tired"],"a":3,"e":"Tired — Exhausted means extremely tired.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"EXHAUSTED"},{"n":10,"q":"He gave me a COUNTERFEIT coin.","o":["rare","fake","unatured","inferior"],"a":1,"e":"Fake — Counterfeit means made in imitation of something genuine; fake.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"COUNTERFEIT"},{"n":11,"q":"My mother has been working hard for the last two weeks and she looks RUN DOWN.","o":["morbid","energetic","exhausted","emotional"],"a":1,"e":"Energetic — Run down means tired, weak or exhausted; energetic is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"RUN DOWN","d":"Directions: Each item in this section consists of a sentence with an underlined word/words followed by four options. Select the option that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word/words and mark your response in your Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":12,"q":"The President condemned the act of VIOLENCE during the celebration of the festival.","o":["reason","instigation","restraint","sobriety"],"a":3,"e":"Sobriety — The official key gives (D). Violence implies violent or uncontrolled behaviour, while sobriety can suggest calm, restrained behaviour.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"VIOLENCE"},{"n":13,"q":"The students made a GENEROUS contribution to the flood relief fund.","o":["niggard","selfish","spendthrift","indecent"],"a":0,"e":"Niggard — Generous means liberal in giving; niggard/niggardly means miserly or ungenerous.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"GENEROUS"},{"n":14,"q":"He was just IDLE by temperament.","o":["employed","occupied","industrious","happy"],"a":2,"e":"Industrious — Idle means lazy or inactive; industrious means hardworking.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"IDLE"},{"n":15,"q":"Most of the decisions taken by the officer were UNJUST.","o":["serious","lenient","correct","imbecile"],"a":2,"e":"Correct — Unjust means unfair or wrong; among the given options, correct is the keyed opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"UNJUST"},{"n":16,"q":"He is a loving father and takes great DELIGHT in his children.","o":["revolt","dissatisfaction","enjoyment","disgust"],"a":3,"e":"Disgust — Delight means great pleasure; disgust expresses strong dislike or aversion.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DELIGHT"},{"n":17,"q":"He was quite CONCERNED about his son's career.","o":["unrelated","indifferent","dispassionate","carefree"],"a":1,"e":"Indifferent — Concerned means interested or worried; indifferent means having no particular interest or concern.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CONCERNED"},{"n":18,"q":"They are CONFIDENT of success.","o":["imprudent","impatient","diffident","reluctant"],"a":2,"e":"Diffident — Confident means self-assured; diffident means lacking confidence.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CONFIDENT"},{"n":19,"q":"We CARRIED ON the search for the missing person.","o":["delayed","reconsidered","broke up","called off"],"a":3,"e":"Called off — Carried on means continued; called off means stopped or cancelled.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CARRIED ON"},{"n":20,"q":"This T.V. has many INDIGENOUS components.","o":["Indian","foreign","unnatural","genuine"],"a":1,"e":"Foreign — Indigenous means native or locally produced; foreign is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"INDIGENOUS"},{"n":21,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 21.","o":["hardly","barely","merely","rarely"],"a":1,"e":"Barely — I had barely... means I had only just..., indicating that Gangu appeared almost immediately.","cat":"Cloze Test","d":"Directions: In the following passage, at certain points you are given a choice of four words marked (a), (b), (c) and (d), one of which fits the meaning of the passage. Choose the best word out of the four and mark your response on the Answer Sheet.","c":"Passage: After this incident I went to Nainital, and returned after nearly a month. I had 21. ______ 22. ______ my clothes when I saw Gangu standing 23. ______ a new baby. He was 24. ______ with joy. Even Nanda 25. ______ not have 26. ______ such joy 27. ______ getting Krishna. His face had the same 28. ______ light that 29. ______ on the face of a 30. ______ man after a full meal."},{"n":22,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 22.","o":["out","away","off","on"],"a":2,"e":"Off — The natural expression is take off one's clothes. Correct: I had barely taken off my clothes...","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":23,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 23.","o":["by","near","with","at"],"a":2,"e":"With — Gangu was standing with a new baby.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":24,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 24.","o":["jumping","bursting","dancing","singing"],"a":1,"e":"Bursting — The idiomatic expression is bursting with joy, meaning extremely happy.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":25,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 25.","o":["could","would","should","ought"],"a":0,"e":"Could — Even Nanda could not have... correctly expresses comparison with an imagined degree of joy.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":26,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 26.","o":["experimented","shown","felt","heard"],"a":2,"e":"Felt — One feels joy. Correct: could not have felt such joy...","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":27,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 27.","o":["at","in","on","into"],"a":0,"e":"At — The keyed construction is at getting Krishna, indicating the cause of the joy.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":28,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 28.","o":["light","glow","sense","hope"],"a":1,"e":"Glow — Glow means a visible expression of happiness or satisfaction on one's face.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":29,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 29.","o":["comes","appears","rises","shows"],"a":1,"e":"Appears — A glow appears on the face of a satisfied person.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":30,"q":"Choose the most appropriate option for blank 30.","o":["starved","starving","hungry","satisfied"],"a":0,"e":"Starved — A starved man after a full meal would naturally show intense satisfaction. The comparison emphasizes the extraordinary joy on Gangu's face.","cat":"Cloze Test"},{"n":31,"q":"The spirit of man\nP: has slowly and painfully surmounted\nQ: and his growing intelligence\nR: all the obstacles that have come in his way\nS: has faced all kinds of dangers","o":["Q P S R","S Q P R","R P Q S","P R Q S"],"a":1,"e":"S–Q–P–R — Correct sentence: The spirit of man has faced all kinds of dangers and his growing intelligence has slowly and painfully surmounted all the obstacles that have come in his way.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section consists of a sentence, the parts of which have been jumbled. These parts have been labelled P, Q, R and S. Given below each sentence are four sequences, namely (a), (b), (c) and (d). You are required to re-arrange the jumbled parts of the sentence and mark your response accordingly."},{"n":32,"q":"After our school boys had won a well-contested hockey match\nP: so that they might communicate the news of their victory to the headmaster\nQ: who is a keen sportsman\nR: they came to school in high spirits\nS: and takes a very lively interest in school games","o":["Q P S R","S Q P R","R P Q S","P R Q S"],"a":2,"e":"R–P–Q–S — Correct sentence: After our school boys had won a well-contested hockey match, they came to school in high spirits so that they might communicate the news of their victory to the headmaster, who is a keen sportsman and takes a very lively interest in school games.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":33,"q":"Even a leisurely game like cricket\nP: demanding grace rather than strength\nQ: and over the rough tactics of the Australian team that visited England in 1921\nR: as we saw in the controversy over body-line bowling\nS: can cause much ill-will","o":["P S R Q","R S P Q","S R Q P","Q P R S"],"a":0,"e":"P–S–R–Q — Correct sentence: Even a leisurely game like cricket, demanding grace rather than strength, can cause much ill-will, as we saw in the controversy over body-line bowling and over the rough tactics of the Australian team that visited England in 1921.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":34,"q":"Scientists point out\nP: of sunspot activity\nQ: that it is an aftermath that\nR: has now reached its peak\nS: of the eleven-year cycle","o":["R S P Q","P Q S R","Q R P S","Q S P R"],"a":3,"e":"Q–S–P–R — Correct sentence: Scientists point out that it is an aftermath of the eleven-year cycle of sunspot activity that has now reached its peak.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":35,"q":"As the ship steams from San Diego\nP: as swells of gray water from a distant storm in the North Pacific\nQ: making the greener among us miserable with sea sickness\nR: rock and toss the ship\nS: those of us aboard have a personal demonstration of powerful ocean movement","o":["P Q R S","S R P Q","S P R Q","Q S R P"],"a":2,"e":"S–P–R–Q — Correct sentence: As the ship steams from San Diego, those of us aboard have a personal demonstration of powerful ocean movement as swells of gray water from a distant storm in the North Pacific rock and toss the ship, making the greener among us miserable with sea sickness.","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":36,"q":"An electrical circuit / is the complete path / traversed by electric current. / No error","o":["An electrical circuit","is the complete path","traversed by electric current.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — An electrical circuit is the complete path traversed by electric current is grammatically correct.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Read each sentence to find out whether there is any error in any underlined part and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet against the corresponding letter i.e., (a) or (b) or (c). If you find no error, your response should be indicated as (d)."},{"n":37,"q":"He waved us a by-by / as he boarded the train / which disappeared into the tunnel. / No error","o":["He waved us a by-by","as he boarded the train","which disappeared into the tunnel.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“He waved us a — by-by” — The expression is incorrect. One normally waves goodbye to someone. Correct: He waved goodbye to us...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":38,"q":"There was great deal that had to be scrapped, that must be scrapped; / but surely India could not have been what she undoubtedly was, / and could not have continued a cultured existence for thousands of years. / No error","o":["There was great deal that had to be scrapped, that must be scrapped;","but surely India could not have been what she undoubtedly was,","and could not have continued a cultured existence for thousands of years.","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“There was great deal...” requires the article a. Correct: There was a great deal that had to be scrapped...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":39,"q":"With regard to interior decoration, it is the attention given to the less overt aspects of using space / that give it life, an identity, a quality / that makes it exciting and unusual. / No error","o":["With regard to interior decoration, it is the attention given to the less overt aspects of using space","that give it life, an identity, a quality","that makes it exciting and unusual.","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“that give it life” — The antecedent is singular: attention. Therefore, the verb should be gives. Correct: ...the attention ... that gives it life...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":40,"q":"A small parcel / of novels is / better than none. / No error","o":["A small parcel","of novels is","better than none.","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — A small parcel of novels is better than none is grammatically acceptable.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":41,"q":"He looks as if he ______ weary.","o":["is","was","would be","were"],"a":3,"e":"Were — After as if for an unreal or hypothetical condition, the subjunctive were is appropriate. Correct: He looks as if he were weary.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: Each of the following sentences in this section has a blank space and four words or group of words given after the sentence. Select the word or group of words you consider most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":42,"q":"My house is insured ______ theft and fire.","o":["for","against","in","towards"],"a":1,"e":"Against — The standard collocation is insured against a risk. Correct: My house is insured against theft and fire.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":43,"q":"The result of the prolonged discussion was ______.","o":["disappointment","disappointing","disappointed","to disappoint"],"a":1,"e":"Disappointing — The result causes disappointment, so the -ing adjective is required. Correct: The result ... was disappointing.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":44,"q":"You are lucky ______ in the 20th century.","o":["by being born","to have been born","for being born","to have born"],"a":1,"e":"To have been born — The perfect infinitive refers to the completed past event of being born. Correct: You are lucky to have been born in the 20th century.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":45,"q":"Sita is true to ______.","o":["word","her words","the words","words"],"a":1,"e":"Her words — According to the official key, the answer is (B). The intended meaning is that Sita remains faithful to what she has said. Note: In standard idiomatic English, “true to her word” would normally be preferred, but that exact option is not given.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":46,"q":"Years ______ since I saw her last.","o":["have passed","had passed","had been passing","have been passing"],"a":0,"e":"Have passed — With a period extending from the past up to the present, the present perfect is appropriate. Correct: Years have passed since I saw her last.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":47,"q":"When he heard the terrible noise he asked me what was ______ on.","o":["happening","being","getting","going"],"a":3,"e":"Going — What is going on? is the standard expression meaning what is happening? Correct: He asked me what was going on.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":48,"q":"Could you lend me some money? I am very ______ of cash at the moment.","o":["down","low","short","scarce"],"a":2,"e":"Short — Short of cash means not having enough money. Correct: I am very short of cash at the moment.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":49,"q":"I saw her when she was standing ______ the side of the old statue.","o":["by","at","in","beyond"],"a":0,"e":"By — By the side of means beside or next to.Correct: She was standing by the side of the old statue.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":50,"q":"True friends never ______ their loved ones in adversity.","o":["abuse","criticize","befool","desert"],"a":3,"e":"Desert","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"}]},{"id":"pyq-2017-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2017 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"I ______ you to be at the party this evening","o":["look forward to","hope","expect","think"],"a":2,"e":"Expect — Expect someone to do something is the correct construction. Correct: I expect you to be at the party this evening","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: Each of the following items in this section has a sentence with a blank space and four words or groups of words given after the sentence. Select whichever word or group of words you consider most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"When I met John yesterday, it was the first time I ______ him since Christmas","o":["saw","have seen","had seen","have been seeing"],"a":2,"e":"Saw —“the first time I had seen him since Christmas”","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":3,"q":"He ______ to listen to my arguments and walked away","o":["denied","disliked","prevented","refused"],"a":3,"e":"Refused — Refuse to + verb is the correct construction. Correct: He refused to listen to my arguments","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":4,"q":"The flow of blood was so ______ that the patient died","o":["intense","adequate","profuse","extensive"],"a":2,"e":"Profuse — Profuse bleeding means very heavy or abundant bleeding","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":5,"q":"You have never ______ me about your experiences in America","o":["said","told","explained","spoken"],"a":1,"e":"Told — We tell someone about something. Correct: You have never told me about your experiences","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":6,"q":"I always felt hungry ______ I heard the dinner bell","o":["as much as","as well as","as soon as","as close as"],"a":2,"e":"As soon as — It means immediately when. Correct: I always felt hungry as soon as I heard the dinner bell","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":7,"q":"Although they took every precaution, they could not ______ the accident","o":["defer","allow","avoid","block"],"a":2,"e":"Avoid — To avoid an accident means to prevent oneself from becoming involved in it","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":8,"q":"The ambitious nobleman ______ to marry the king’s daughter","o":["transpired","perspired","aspired","expired"],"a":2,"e":"Aspired — Aspire to means to strongly desire or aim to achieve something. Correct: The ambitious nobleman aspired to marry the king's daughter","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":9,"q":"The dictator of that country was a monster of wickedness, insatiable in his ______ for blood and plunder","o":["idea","vision","lust","intention"],"a":2,"e":"Lust — Lust for blood means an intense and cruel desire for bloodshed","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":10,"q":"Please don’t give me any more, I have had ______","o":["few","too little","little","enough"],"a":3,"e":"Enough — The natural expression is I have had enough, meaning no more is wanted","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":11,"q":"My first lecture in the classroom was a fiasco","o":["success","joy","fun","disaster"],"a":0,"e":"Success — Fiasco means a complete failure; its opposite is success","cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions: In this section, each item consists of a sentence with a word underlined and is followed by four words. Select the word that is most opposite in meaning to the underlined word and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"FIASCO"},{"n":12,"q":"It was indeed arduous to cross streets in New York","o":["pleasant","effortless","interesting","risky"],"a":1,"e":"Effortless — Arduous means difficult and requiring great effort; effortless is the opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ARDUOUS"},{"n":13,"q":"Unlike his brother, he is affable","o":["reserved","gullible","irritable","lovable"],"a":0,"e":"Reserved — Affable means friendly and easy to talk to; reserved means restrained or less sociable","cat":"Antonyms","t":"AFFABLE"},{"n":14,"q":"The birth of his child decidedly proved to be an auspicious event in his life","o":["precious","ominous","useless","unforgettable"],"a":1,"e":"Ominous — Auspicious means favourable or promising success; ominous means threatening or suggesting something bad","cat":"Antonyms","t":"AUSPICIOUS"},{"n":15,"q":"The witness corroborated word for word the statement of the victim","o":["accepted","confirmed","denied","repeated"],"a":2,"e":"Denied — Corroborated means confirmed or supported; denied is the opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CORROBORATED"},{"n":16,"q":"It is unwise to sever diplomatic relations with a neighbouring country over small matters","o":["engage","estrange","cut off","twist"],"a":2,"e":"Cut off — Sever means to cut off, break or terminate. Here it means terminating diplomatic relations","cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions: In this section, each item consists of a sentence with a word underlined and is followed by four words or groups of words. Select the word or group of words that is most similar in meaning to the underlined word and indicate your response in the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"SEVER"},{"n":17,"q":"Bad tendencies are to be countered by good ones until all that is evil disappears","o":["opposed","balanced","reduced","bypassed"],"a":0,"e":"Opposed — Countered means opposed or acted against","cat":"Synonyms","t":"COUNTERED"},{"n":18,"q":"The police fired indiscriminately at the crowd, killing many innocent women and children","o":["continuously","without distinguishing","foolishly","rapidly"],"a":1,"e":"Without distinguishing — Indiscriminately means without making distinctions or choosing carefully","cat":"Synonyms","t":"INDISCRIMINATELY"},{"n":19,"q":"Businessmen who lack acumen cannot be expected to be very successful","o":["fairness","sharpness","boldness","righteousness"],"a":1,"e":"Sharpness — Acumen means the ability to make quick and intelligent judgments; mental sharpness","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ACUMEN"},{"n":20,"q":"His candid opinions have won him many friends","o":["kind","courteous","generous","frank"],"a":3,"e":"Frank — Candid means honest, straightforward and frank","cat":"Synonyms","t":"CANDID"},{"n":21,"q":"I disliked him not so much for his meanness BUT FOR his dishonesty","o":["as for","but because","but due to","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"As for — The correct correlative construction is not so much...as... Correct: I disliked him not so much for his meanness as for his dishonesty","cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions: In this section, look at the underlined part of each sentence. Below each sentence are given three possible substitutions for the underlined part. If one of them (a), (b) or (c) is better than the underlined part, indicate your response accordingly. If none improves the sentence, indicate (d) No improvement.","t":"BUT FOR"},{"n":22,"q":"He preferred death RATHER THAN imprisonment","o":["for","to","than","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"To — Prefer X to Y is the standard construction. Correct: He preferred death to imprisonment","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"RATHER THAN"},{"n":23,"q":"They kept the idea SECRETLY","o":["secretive","secret","secretively","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Secret — After keep + object, an adjective is required as object complement. Correct: They kept the idea secret","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"SECRETLY"},{"n":24,"q":"He ought not TO TELL me your secret, but he did","o":["telling","have told","having told","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Have told — For an unfulfilled past obligation, use ought not to have + past participle. Correct: He ought not to have told me your secret","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"TO TELL"},{"n":25,"q":"If I don’t know the meaning of a word, I LOOK IT AFTER in the dictionary","o":["look it out","look it for","look it up","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Look it up — Look up a word means to search for its meaning in a dictionary","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"I LOOK IT AFTER"},{"n":26,"q":"Mr. and Mrs. Rao stay IN THEIR HOME every evening","o":["at home","inside home","within their home","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"At home — Stay at home is the standard idiomatic expression","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"IN THEIR HOME"},{"n":27,"q":"When we saw him last, he RAN to catch a bus","o":["has run","had run","was running","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Was running — The action was in progress when they saw him. Correct: When we saw him last, he was running to catch a bus","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"RAN"},{"n":28,"q":"She CUT A SAD FIGURE in her first performance on the stage","o":["made a sorry figure","cut a sorry face","cut a sorry figure","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Cut a sorry figure — Cut a sorry figure means to make a poor or unimpressive appearance","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"CUT A SAD FIGURE"},{"n":29,"q":"Last evening I went to the optician and bought SPECTACLES","o":["a spectacle","two spectacles","a pair of spectacles","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"A pair of spectacles — Spectacles consists of two connected lenses and is conventionally counted as a pair of spectacles","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"SPECTACLES"},{"n":30,"q":"I would like TO AVAIL a fifteen days’ holiday this summer","o":["to avail of","to avail myself of","to avail myself","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"To avail myself of — Avail oneself of something means to make use of it. Correct: I would like to avail myself of a fifteen days' holiday","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"TO AVAIL"},{"n":31,"q":"I can fly / if I will be / a bird / No error","o":["I can fly","if I will be","a bird","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“if I will be” — In this hypothetical construction, will be is inappropriate. Correct sense: I can fly if I am/were a bird","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each item in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Find the error, or mark (d) No error."},{"n":32,"q":"As soon as / the train arrived / the passengers entered the compartments / No error","o":["As soon as","the train arrived","the passengers entered the compartments","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — As soon as the train arrived, the passengers entered the compartments is grammatically correct","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":33,"q":"Never / I have / seen such a town / No error","o":["Never","I have","seen such a town","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“I have” — When never begins a sentence for emphasis, subject-auxiliary inversion is required. Correct: Never have I seen such a town","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":34,"q":"He goes to his office / by bicycle / every day / No error","o":["He goes to his office","by bicycle","every day","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — He goes to his office by bicycle every day is correct","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":35,"q":"Far from being appreciated / his conduct was discussed / and sharply commented / No error","o":["Far from being appreciated","his conduct was discussed","and sharply commented","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“and sharply commented” — The verb comment normally requires on/upon an object. Correct: his conduct was discussed and sharply commented upon","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":36,"q":"The author had roused himself as he wanted to","o":["get off","meet someone","know the name of the station","keep himself awake"],"a":2,"e":"Know the name of the station — The author explicitly says he roused himself “in order to hear the name of the station.”","cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions: In this section, you have two short passages. Read the passages and answer the items that follow. Select answers based on the contents of the passage and opinion of the author only.","c":"Passage 1: We had just passed Tenali, where I roused myself in order to hear the name of the station. As I was falling asleep again, a violent jolt shot me into the arms of somebody in the seat opposite. The engine with one wheel broken was lying across the track and beside it was the luggage van, likewise, derailed. Groaning, wheezing, gasping, sputtering in its death agony, the engine was like a fallen horse which, snorting, trembling in every limb, its flanks heaving, its chest labouring, seems incapable of making the smallest effort to struggle on to its legs again."},{"n":37,"q":"The engine stopped because","o":["the driver had stopped it","one of the wheels was broken","there was a halting place","there was no fuel"],"a":1,"e":"One of the wheels was broken — The passage states that the engine was lying across the track with one wheel broken","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":38,"q":"The engine is compared to a fallen horse because","o":["it was groaning like a horse","it was lying across the track","it could make no effort to stand upright","it had a broken wheel"],"a":2,"e":"It could make no effort to stand upright — The engine is compared to a fallen horse seemingly incapable of struggling onto its legs again","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":39,"q":"Which of the following is the most appropriate description of the area in which the narrator had been moving?","o":["A steep rocky slope","A peak with a flight of steps","A high peak covered with snow","A long ridge"],"a":2,"e":"A high peak covered with snow — The description of cutting steps, ice-axe use and the snowy summit shows that the climbers were on a high snow-covered peak","cat":"Reading Comprehension","c":"Passage 2: I was beginning to tire a little now. I had been cutting steps continuously for two hours, and Tenzing, too, was moving very slowly. As I chipped steps around still another corner, I wondered rather dully just how long we could keep it up. Our original zest had now quite gone and it was turning more into a grim struggle. I then realised that the ridge ahead, instead of still monotonously rising, now dropped sharply away, and far below I could see the North Col and Rongbuk glacier. I looked upwards to see a narrow ridge running up to a snowy summit. A few more whacks of the ice-axe in the firm snow and we stood on top."},{"n":40,"q":"‘I had been cutting steps continuously for two hours’ suggests that","o":["the climber was unskilled in the art of making steps","he had to cut the steps out of snow before climbing up which was necessarily a slow process","the slowness was caused by the fact that as soon as he cut the steps they were again covered with snow","he had to work suspended from a ledge"],"a":1,"e":"He had to cut the steps out of snow before climbing up, which was necessarily a slow process — Cutting footholds in the snow was necessary for progress and made the ascent slow","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":41,"q":"‘It was turning more into a grim struggle’ would mean","o":["that after the long exertion he was feeling so weak that any further climbing meant a hard struggle with the forces of nature","that Tenzing was making the task more difficult","that strong cold wind was pushing him down from above and that he had to struggle with the forces of nature","that he was in a mood to give up ultimately"],"a":0,"e":"Further climbing meant a hard struggle — After prolonged exertion, their initial enthusiasm had disappeared and climbing had become a grim physical struggle","cat":"Reading Comprehension"},{"n":42,"q":"P: Farm workers Q: spend R: outdoors S: most of their time","o":["P Q R S","P R Q S","P Q S R","R P Q S"],"a":2,"e":"P–Q–S–R Correct: Farm workers spend most of their time outdoors","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence the parts of which have been jumbled and labelled P, Q, R and S. Rearrange the parts and select the proper sequence."},{"n":43,"q":"P: He Q: shuffled the papers R: in a drawer S: together","o":["P Q S R","P Q R S","P S Q R","R S P Q"],"a":0,"e":"P–Q–S–R Correct: He shuffled the papers together in a drawer","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":44,"q":"P: Do you think Q: will R: this soap S: shrink woollen clothes?","o":["P Q R S","P R Q S","Q R P S","Q P R S"],"a":1,"e":"P–R–Q–S Correct: Do you think this soap will shrink woollen clothes?","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":45,"q":"P: We advised Q: the hijackers to surrender R: to the police S: themselves","o":["P Q R S","P R Q S","P Q S R","R P Q S"],"a":2,"e":"P–Q–S–R Correct: We advised the hijackers to surrender themselves to the police","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":46,"q":"P: Sports cars Q: appeal R: to some motorists only S: with noisy exhausts","o":["R Q S P","P S Q R","R S P Q","P Q S R"],"a":1,"e":"P–S–Q–R Correct: Sports cars with noisy exhausts appeal to some motorists only","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":47,"q":"P: He Q: almost R: planned the entire strategy of operation S: single-handed","o":["R S P Q","P R Q S","S Q R P","Q P S R"],"a":1,"e":"P–R–Q–S Correct: He planned the entire strategy of operation almost single-handed","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":48,"q":"P: She has Q: more R: intelligence S: than we suspected her to possess","o":["P Q S R","P R Q S","P S Q R","P Q R S"],"a":3,"e":"P–Q–R–S Correct: She has more intelligence than we suspected her to possess","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":49,"q":"P: They Q: should implant R: in the minds of young children S: sound principles","o":["P Q R S","P Q S R","R P Q S","P R Q S"],"a":1,"e":"P–Q–S–R Correct: They should implant sound principles in the minds of young children","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":50,"q":"P: When I was a student Q: I learnt swimming at the age of 15 R: of class X S: in a government school","o":["P Q R S","P R Q S","Q P R S","Q S P R"],"a":2,"e":"Q–P–R–S — According to the official key. With the fragments as printed, this produces an awkward construction; therefore the official keyed answer (C) is retained rather than silently altering it","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"}]},{"id":"pyq-2016-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2016 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"My friend was / habitual to drive a car / at a very high speed. / No error","o":["My friend was","habitual to drive a car","at a very high speed","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“habitual to drive a car” — Habitual is not followed by to + verb. We say in the habit of driving or accustomed to driving","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each question in this section has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Find the error, or indicate (d) No error."},{"n":2,"q":"Being a day of national mourning / the offices were closed / and the national flag flew half mast. / No error","o":["Being a day of national mourning","the offices were closed","and the national flag flew half mast","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“Being a day of national mourning” — This creates a dangling construction, as if the offices were a day of mourning. Better: It being a day of national mourning, the offices were closed","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":3,"q":"If you are wanting to watch / the birds nesting / you must not let them see you. / No error","o":["If you are wanting to watch","the birds nesting","you must not let them see you","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“If you are wanting to watch” — Want is normally a stative verb and is not used in the continuous here. Correct: If you want to watch","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":4,"q":"He does not listen to / what I say, / although I am advising him for a long time. / No error","o":["He does not listen to","what I say,","although I am advising him for a long time","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“although I am advising him for a long time” — An action continuing for a long period requires the present perfect continuous. Correct: although I have been advising him for a long time","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":5,"q":"It is most essential for us / to reach the station before noon / lest we should miss the train. / No error","o":["It is most essential for us","to reach the station before noon","lest we should miss the train","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“most essential” — Essential is an absolute adjective; most essential is generally considered redundant in such examination grammar. Correct: It is essential","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":6,"q":"I asked him / what had brought / him there. / No error","o":["I asked him","what had brought","him there","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — I asked him what had brought him there is grammatically correct","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":7,"q":"The guards / dare not to harm him. / No error","o":["The guards","dare not to harm him","—","No error"],"a":2,"e":"Official key — The error is in “dare not to harm”: when dare functions as a modal, it takes the bare infinitive. Correct: dare not harm him. The supplied part-label formatting makes the exact segment mapping slightly unclear, so the official C is retained","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":8,"q":"The Principal looks very worried / because students are on strike for the last fifteen days as a protest against / his disciplinary action against some miscreant. / No error","o":["The Principal looks very worried","because students are on strike for the last fifteen days as a protest against","his disciplinary action against some miscreant","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“are on strike for the last fifteen days” — For an action continuing from the past to the present, use have been on strike. Correct: students have been on strike for the last fifteen days","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":9,"q":"The king was embarrassed / to find evidences / against his own queen. / No error","o":["The king was embarrassed","to find evidences","against his own queen","No error"],"a":2,"e":"evidence is normally uncountable","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":10,"q":"As I prefer coffee than tea / my friends always take the trouble / to get me a cup of coffee whenever I visit them. / No error","o":["As I prefer coffee than tea","my friends always take the trouble","to get me a cup of coffee whenever I visit them","No error."],"a":0,"e":"“prefer coffee than tea” — The standard construction is prefer X to Y. Correct: I prefer coffee to tea","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":11,"q":"HOSPITALITY is a virtue for which the natives of the East in general are highly admired","o":["Duty of a doctor","Generosity shown to guests","Cleanliness in hospitals","Kindness"],"a":1,"e":"Generosity shown to guests — Hospitality means friendly and generous treatment of guests or visitors","cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence with an underlined word or words followed by four words or groups of words. Select the word or group of words most similar in meaning to the underlined word.","t":"HOSPITALITY"},{"n":12,"q":"House rent in cities like Mumbai or Delhi has risen to ASTRONOMICAL figures beyond the reach of even high-salaried people","o":["exorbitant","commercial","planetary","illogical"],"a":0,"e":"Exorbitant — Astronomical figuratively means extremely or excessively high; exorbitant is the closest synonym","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ASTRONOMICAL"},{"n":13,"q":"APPROPRIATE technology holds the key to a nation's development","o":["Modern","Suitable","Effective","Growing"],"a":1,"e":"Suitable — Appropriate means suitable or proper for a particular purpose","cat":"Synonyms","t":"APPROPRIATE"},{"n":14,"q":"He was ENAMOURED of his own golden voice","o":["very fond of","concerned with","obsessed with","imbued with"],"a":0,"e":"Very fond of — Enamoured of means filled with love or admiration for; very fond of","cat":"Synonyms","t":"ENAMOURED"},{"n":15,"q":"Some journalists are guilty of indulging in YELLOW JOURNALISM","o":["misrepresentation","vulgarization","sensational reporting","loud gestures"],"a":2,"e":"Sensational reporting — Yellow journalism refers to sensationalized and often exaggerated reporting intended to attract attention","cat":"Synonyms","t":"YELLOW JOURNALISM"},{"n":16,"q":"The culprit was SENTENCED by the Court","o":["acquitted","punished","relieved","pardoned"],"a":0,"e":"Acquitted — Sentenced means judicially punished after conviction; acquitted means declared not guilty","cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words or groups of words. Select the word or group of words most nearly opposite in meaning to the underlined word.","t":"SENTENCED"},{"n":17,"q":"THRIFTY as he is, he can well afford to live within his means","o":["careless","instinctive","sentimental","extravagant"],"a":3,"e":"Extravagant — Thrifty means economical and careful with money; extravagant is the opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"THRIFTY"},{"n":18,"q":"Do not give him a responsible job, he is IMMATURE","o":["thoughtful","cautious","calculating","seasoned"],"a":3,"e":"Seasoned — Immature means inexperienced or undeveloped; seasoned means experienced and mature","cat":"Antonyms","t":"IMMATURE"},{"n":19,"q":"I was prepared to SHOW MY HAND provided he agreed to do the same","o":["to yield","to shake hands","to conceal my plan","to lose my ground"],"a":2,"e":"To conceal my plan — Show my hand means reveal one's intentions or plans; its opposite is to conceal them","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SHOW MY HAND"},{"n":20,"q":"Akbar the great was a SAGACIOUS ruler","o":["haughty","cunning","rude","unwise"],"a":3,"e":"Unwise — Sagacious means wise and possessing good judgment; unwise is its opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SAGACIOUS"},{"n":21,"q":"She has been lying in bed for the last fortnight. I hope she will ______","o":["come out","pull out","pull through","go out"],"a":2,"e":"Pull through — Pull through means recover from a serious illness or difficult situation","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: Each sentence has a blank space and four words. Select the most appropriate word for the blank and indicate your response."},{"n":22,"q":"The path of progress is beset ______ difficulties","o":["with","by","through","along"],"a":0,"e":"With — The standard expression is beset with difficulties, meaning troubled by many difficulties","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":23,"q":"I feel ______ for those who are cruel to their children","o":["sympathy","contempt","admiration","craving"],"a":1,"e":"Contempt — One naturally feels contempt—strong disapproval or lack of respect—for people who are cruel to children","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":24,"q":"Both the parties were keen to have an ______ settlement of the dispute","o":["enviable","inimical","worthy","amicable"],"a":3,"e":"Amicable — An amicable settlement is one reached peacefully and in a friendly manner","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":25,"q":"I have come to know that the two brothers have ______","o":["fallen through","fallen out","fallen for","fallen short"],"a":1,"e":"Fallen out — Fall out means quarrel and cease to be friendly","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":26,"q":"Everybody finds his own work ______ whereas he feels that others have delightful jobs","o":["tedious","fabulous","unprofitable","indecent"],"a":0,"e":"Tedious — Tedious means boring, monotonous or tiresome, contrasting appropriately with delightful jobs","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":27,"q":"The accident took place because of the ______ criminal ______ of the driver","o":["performance","disregard","negligence","slackness"],"a":2,"e":"Negligence — Criminal negligence is the standard expression for serious failure to exercise reasonable care","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":28,"q":"The belief in the ______ of vaccinations is gaining ground","o":["immunity","prevalence","efficacy","workability"],"a":2,"e":"Efficacy — Efficacy means effectiveness or ability to produce the intended result","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":29,"q":"Our new leader is a ______ young man and will take us forward","o":["haughty","intoxicated","fanciful","dynamic"],"a":3,"e":"Dynamic — A dynamic person is energetic, progressive and capable of bringing about change","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":30,"q":"They found a world of ______ between what he said and what he did","o":["chaos","bitterness","difference","hope"],"a":2,"e":"Difference — A world of difference is an idiom meaning a very great difference","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":31,"q":"The statement P: therefore you must listen carefully Q: what the speaker has said R: in order to understand S: will be made just once","o":["S R P Q","S R Q P","R S P Q","S P Q R"],"a":0,"e":"S–R–P–Q — Official key — The official answer is A. The printed fragments appear somewhat awkward; therefore the official sequence is retained rather than altering the key","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence whose parts have been jumbled and labelled P, Q, R and S. Rearrange the parts and select the proper sequence."},{"n":32,"q":"The small boy who P: broke his leg Q: fell down R: and S: climbed the wall","o":["P S R Q","S Q R P","Q S R P","S P R Q"],"a":1,"e":"S–Q–R–P — Correct: The small boy who climbed the wall fell down and broke his leg","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":33,"q":"According to an engineer P: might hit the market next year Q: a newly developed air-cooler system R: if employed in conventional room coolers S: which is based on a principle radically different from","o":["S Q R P","R S Q P","Q S R P","P Q R S"],"a":2,"e":"Q–S–R–P — Correct flow: According to an engineer, a newly developed air-cooler system which is based on a principle radically different from ... if employed in conventional room coolers might hit the market next year","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":34,"q":"The clerk P: on the desk Q: left the money R: in the safe S: which he should have locked up","o":["S R Q P","Q R S P","P Q R S","Q P S R"],"a":3,"e":"Q–P–S–R — Correct: The clerk left the money on the desk which he should have locked up in the safe","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":35,"q":"Hardly had my brother descended from the plane P: when the people Q: waved and cheered R: who had come to receive him S: from the lounge","o":["P Q R S","R S P Q","R P Q S","P R S Q"],"a":3,"e":"P–R–S–Q — Correct: Hardly had my brother descended from the plane when the people who had come to receive him from the lounge waved and cheered","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":36,"q":"The essay P: like that of the human face Q: because its variety is infinite R: is a literary form S: which is not easy to define","o":["P Q R S","P Q S R","R S Q P","R S P Q"],"a":2,"e":"R–S–Q–P — Correct: The essay is a literary form which is not easy to define because its variety is infinite like that of the human face","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":37,"q":"The exhibition committee P: attractive and useful Q: to make exhibition R: making efforts S: has been","o":["Q S R P","S R Q P","Q P S R","S P Q R"],"a":1,"e":"S–R–Q–P — Correct: The exhibition committee has been making efforts to make exhibition attractive and useful","cat":"Ordering of Words / Phrases"},{"n":38,"q":"My old teacher gave many poor students money in spite of he was poor","o":["even though","as if","however","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":0,"e":"Even though — In spite of cannot directly take a finite clause in this form. Correct: My old teacher gave many poor students money even though he was poor","cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions: Look at the underlined part of each sentence. Select the best substitution from (a), (b), or (c). If no substitution improves the sentence, select (d) No Improvement.","t":"IN SPITE OF HE WAS POOR"},{"n":39,"q":"You should to read the newspapers regularly if you want to be well-informed","o":["ought","have to","should be","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":1,"e":"Have to — According to the official key, have to correctly replaces should to. Correct: You have to read the newspapers regularly","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"SHOULD TO"},{"n":40,"q":"If he had listening to me he would not have got lost","o":["listened me","listened to me","listens me","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":1,"e":"Listened to me — The verb listen takes to. The complete conditional should read: If he had listened to me, he would not have got lost","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"HAD LISTENING TO ME"},{"n":41,"q":"He asked for a bank loan also he didn't have sufficient resources","o":["because","even if","requiring","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":0,"e":"Because — According to the official key, because is the intended conjunction connecting the clauses","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"ALSO HE DIDN'T HAVE SUFFICIENT RESOURCES"},{"n":42,"q":"I went to the shop to buy some stationeries","o":["stationery","stationary","stationaries","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":0,"e":"Stationery — Stationery meaning writing materials is uncountable. Correct: to buy some stationery. Stationary means not moving","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"STATIONERIES"},{"n":43,"q":"I have been waiting here for the last two hours","o":["from","since","by","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — For the last two hours is correct because for expresses a duration. Correct: I have been waiting here for the last two hours","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"FOR THE LAST TWO HOURS"},{"n":44,"q":"The table needs to be painted","o":["paint","painting","having paint","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — Needs to be painted is already grammatically correct. (Needs painting is also possible.)","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"TO BE PAINTED"},{"n":45,"q":"It began to rain heavily just when I prepare to go out","o":["prepared","was preparing","have prepared","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":1,"e":"Was preparing — The past continuous describes an action in progress when another action occurred. Correct: It began to rain heavily just when I was preparing to go out","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"PREPARE"},{"n":46,"q":"The couple seemed to love one another very much","o":["every other","each","each other","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":2,"e":"Each other — According to the official key, each other is preferred for two persons: The couple seemed to love each other very much","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"ONE ANOTHER"},{"n":47,"q":"It is high time that we did something","o":["had done","would do","have done","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — After It is high time, the simple past is conventionally used. Correct: It is high time that we did something","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"DID SOMETHING"},{"n":48,"q":"If I were a millionaire, I would have helped the poor","o":["am capable of helping","could have helped","would help","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":2,"e":"Would help — This is a second conditional referring to an unreal present situation: If + past, would + base verb. Correct: If I were a millionaire, I would help the poor","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"WOULD HAVE HELPED THE POOR"},{"n":49,"q":"The training programme was extended for a month","o":["from","until","since","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — Extended for a month correctly indicates the duration of the extension","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"FOR"},{"n":50,"q":"The painting which they were looking at was done by my sister","o":["what","that","whom","NO IMPROVEMENT"],"a":1,"e":"That — According to the official key, that is the preferred substitution: The painting that they were looking at was done by my sister","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"WHICH"}]},{"id":"pyq-2016-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2016 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"When the bus was at full speed, its brakes failed and an accident was ________","o":["inevitable","undeniable","fatal","miserable"],"a":0,"e":"Inevitable — Inevitable means certain to happen and impossible to avoid. If the brakes failed at full speed, an accident was inevitable","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: In this section, each of the following sentences has a blank space and four words given after the sentence. Select whichever word you consider most appropriate for the blank space and indicate your response accordingly."},{"n":2,"q":"To explain his design to his visitors, the architect ________ a simple plan on the blackboard","o":["built","finalised","sketched","arranged"],"a":2,"e":"Sketched — To sketch a plan means to draw it quickly and simply, which fits the context of explaining a design","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":3,"q":"Though Bonsai, a well-known art form, originated in China, it was ________ by the Japanese","o":["cultivated","finished","perfected","enlarged"],"a":2,"e":"Perfected — Perfected means developed or improved to the highest standard. Bonsai originated in China but was perfected by the Japanese","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":4,"q":"He is greatly admired for his ________ behaviour","o":["decrepit","decadent","decorative","decorous"],"a":3,"e":"Decorous — Decorous means polite, proper and socially acceptable. Hence, decorous behaviour","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":5,"q":"Would you mind ________ to the Principal how the trouble started?","o":["remarking","saying","explaining","talking"],"a":2,"e":"Explaining — Would you mind is followed by a gerund (-ing form), and we explain something to someone. Correct: Would you mind explaining to the Principal","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":6,"q":"Vaccination will make people immune ________ certain diseases for a given period","o":["against","to","with","for"],"a":1,"e":"To — The standard construction is immune to a disease. Correct: Vaccination will make people immune to certain diseases","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":7,"q":"The two boys looked so alike that it was impossible to ________ between them","o":["discriminate","discern","distinguish","identify"],"a":2,"e":"Distinguish — Distinguish between means to recognize the difference between two similar things or persons","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":8,"q":"The campers ________ their tents at the base of the mountain","o":["installed","dug","pitched","established"],"a":2,"e":"Pitched — The standard collocation is pitch a tent, meaning to set it up","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":9,"q":"The enemy had captured him and his life was at stake, still he refused to ________ the state secrets","o":["divulge","divert","indulge","invert"],"a":0,"e":"Divulge — Divulge means to reveal secret or confidential information. Hence, divulge the state secrets","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":10,"q":"He handled the machine with DEFT fingers","o":["delicate","sturdy","quick","clumsy"],"a":3,"e":"Clumsy — Deft means skilful and quick in movement; clumsy is its opposite","cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions: Each item consists of an underlined word in a sentence followed by four words. Select the word which is most nearly opposite in meaning to the underlined word.","t":"DEFT"},{"n":11,"q":"I was deeply affected by his URBANE behaviour","o":["rural","rude","irrational","indifferent"],"a":1,"e":"Rude — Urbane means polished, courteous and sophisticated; rude is the opposite in this context","cat":"Antonyms","t":"URBANE"},{"n":12,"q":"His TIMIDITY proved costly","o":["arrogance","boldness","skilfulness","cunning"],"a":1,"e":"Boldness — Timidity means lack of courage or confidence; boldness is its opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"TIMIDITY"},{"n":13,"q":"Arrangements were made to handle the MAMMOTH gathering tactfully","o":["significant","small","unruly","noisy"],"a":1,"e":"Small — Mammoth means extremely large; small is the opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"MAMMOTH"},{"n":14,"q":"He was ENGROSSED in his work when I walked in","o":["occupied","inattentive","engaged","absent"],"a":1,"e":"Inattentive — Engrossed means completely absorbed or attentive; inattentive is the opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ENGROSSED"},{"n":15,"q":"These are the main points of the PRECEDING paragraph","o":["following","previous","first","last"],"a":0,"e":"Following — Preceding means coming before; following means coming after","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PRECEDING"},{"n":16,"q":"He made a SHREWD guess","o":["clever","wild","incorrect","discriminating"],"a":1,"e":"Wild — A shrewd guess is an intelligent, well-judged guess; a wild guess is made without sufficient knowledge or reasoning","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SHREWD"},{"n":17,"q":"He is suffering from a SEVERE cough","o":["violent","mild","bad","continuous"],"a":1,"e":"Mild — Severe means very serious or intense; mild is its opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SEVERE"},{"n":18,"q":"CUMULATIVELY, the effect of these drugs is quite bad","o":["Individually","Obviously","Clearly","Collectively"],"a":0,"e":"Individually — Cumulatively means collectively or taken together; individually is the opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CUMULATIVELY"},{"n":19,"q":"He was CONSPICUOUS because of his colourful shirt","o":["charming","ugly","small","unnoticeable"],"a":3,"e":"Unnoticeable — Conspicuous means easily seen or noticeable; unnoticeable is its opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CONSPICUOUS"},{"n":20,"q":"He hates these CONTINUAL arguments with his friend","o":["repeated","irrational","occasional","regular"],"a":2,"e":"Occasional — Continual means happening repeatedly or frequently; occasional is the opposite","cat":"Antonyms","t":"CONTINUAL"},{"n":21,"q":"She said that she was understanding / his point of view / very well / No error","o":["She said that she was understanding","his point of view","very well","No error"],"a":0,"e":"Official key —, The obvious grammatical problem in the supplied sentence is “was understanding”, since understand is normally a stative verb: she understood his point of view. Thus, the printed question/key appears inconsistent. The official answer A is retained exactly as supplied","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each question has a sentence with three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Find the error, or indicate (d) No error."},{"n":22,"q":"All the houses, having been washed away / by the floods, / the villagers sought shelter in the panchayat office / No error","o":["All the houses, having been washed away","by the floods,","the villagers sought shelter in the panchayat office","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — All the houses having been washed away is an absolute construction, correctly followed by the main clause the villagers sought shelter","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":23,"q":"It is necessary for us to familiarize with / and get used to / the ways of the people among whom we live / No error","o":["It is necessary for us to familiarize with","and get used to","the ways of the people among whom we live","No error"],"a":0,"e":"Official key — The official key marks (A). Standard usage, however, requires the reflexive pronoun after familiarize: familiarize ourselves with","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":24,"q":"The mother asked the child / why did she cry out / in her sleep / No error","o":["The mother asked the child","why did she cry out","in her sleep","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“why did she cry out” — In indirect questions, statement word order is used. Correct: The mother asked the child why she cried out in her sleep","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":25,"q":"I asked him that / why he was angry / but he did not answer / No error","o":["I asked him that","why he was angry","but he did not answer","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“I asked him that” — That is not used before an indirect why-question. Correct: I asked him why he was angry","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":26,"q":"Had I not taken ill; / I would have sent you / my research paper much earlier / No error","o":["Had I not taken ill;","I would have sent you","my research paper much earlier","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“Had I not taken ill” — The official key marks (A). The intended correction is normally Had I not fallen ill... / Had I not become ill","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":27,"q":"The barn owl helps the farmer by destroying rats / which could, if left unchecked, / do a lot of damage to the crops / No error","o":["The barn owl helps the farmer by destroying rats","which could, if left unchecked,","do a lot of damage to the crops","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — The sentence is grammatically acceptable: rats which could, if left unchecked, do a lot of damage","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":28,"q":"He had gone home / three weeks ago and was expected back / yesterday, but he has not come yet / No error","o":["He had gone home","three weeks ago and was expected back","yesterday, but he has not come yet","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“He had gone home three weeks ago” — With a definite past-time expression such as three weeks ago, the simple past is appropriate. Correct: He went home three weeks ago","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":29,"q":"We must sympathize / for others / in their troubles / No error","o":["We must sympathize","for others","in their troubles","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“for others” — The correct preposition is with. Correct: We must sympathize with others in their troubles","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":30,"q":"The scientists in America / are trying for long / to discover the genes responsible for ageing / No error","o":["The scientists in America","are trying for long","to discover the genes responsible for ageing","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“are trying for long” — For an action continuing over a period up to the present, use the present perfect continuous. Correct: The scientists have been trying for long","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":31,"q":"When he heard the rhetorical speech of the leader, he was CARRIED ALONG by his enthusiasm","o":["carried aloft","carried down","carried away","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Carried away — Be carried away means to become excessively excited or influenced by emotion or enthusiasm","cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions: Look at the underlined part of each sentence. Select the best substitution from (a), (b), or (c). If no substitution improves the sentence, select (d) No improvement.","t":"CARRIED ALONG"},{"n":32,"q":"After the heavy rains last week, the water in the lake RAISED ANOTHER TWO FEET","o":["rose another two feet","arisen another two feet","would raise another two feet","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"Rose another two feet — Rise is intransitive; water rises by itself. Correct: The water in the lake rose another two feet","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"RAISED ANOTHER TWO FEET"},{"n":33,"q":"One can live and work in a town without being aware of the daily march of the sun across the sky WITHOUT NEVER seeing the moon and stars","o":["seldom","hardly","ever","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Ever — Without never creates an incorrect double negative. Correct: without ever seeing the moon and stars","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"WITHOUT NEVER"},{"n":34,"q":"Applications of those who are graduates will be considered","o":["whom are graduates","whose are graduates","whom are only graduates","No improvement"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — Those who are graduates is grammatically correct","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"WHO ARE GRADUATES"},{"n":35,"q":"It IS RAINING heavily all through this week","o":["has rained","rains","rained","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"Has rained — According to the official key, (A). The present perfect connects the week's rainfall with the present","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"IS RAINING"},{"n":36,"q":"The sparrow took no NOTICE ABOUT the bread","o":["notice of","notice from","notice to","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"Notice of — The fixed expression is take notice of. Correct: The sparrow took no notice of the bread","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"NOTICE ABOUT"},{"n":37,"q":"As he spoke about his achievements, his HIGH claims amused us","o":["big","tall","long","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Tall — Tall claims means exaggerated or excessive claims","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"HIGH"},{"n":38,"q":"We ought to STAND FOR what is right","o":["stand at","stand on","stand up","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Stand up — The intended expression is stand up for what is right, meaning defend or support what is right. With the printed wording, the full correction would be “stand up for what is right.”","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"STAND FOR"},{"n":39,"q":"He enjoys TO TELL STORIES to children","o":["how to tell stories","telling stories","to narrate stories","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Telling stories — Enjoy is followed by a gerund. Correct: He enjoys telling stories to children","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"TO TELL STORIES"},{"n":40,"q":"In a few minutes’ time, when the clock strikes six, I WOULD BE WAITING here for an hour","o":["shall be waiting on","shall have been waiting","shall wait","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Shall have been waiting — The future perfect continuous describes an action continuing up to a specified future point. Correct: When the clock strikes six, I shall have been waiting here for an hour","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"WOULD BE WAITING"},{"n":41,"q":"Suddenly there was a bright flash, followed by a DEAFENING explosion","o":["dangerous","terrifying","mild","very loud"],"a":3,"e":"Very loud — Deafening means extremely loud, so loud that it seems capable of causing deafness","cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions: Each item consists of an underlined word in a sentence followed by four words. Select the word which is most similar in meaning to the underlined word.","t":"DEAFENING"},{"n":42,"q":"He showed EXEMPLARY courage during the crisis","o":["usual","durable","commendable","some"],"a":2,"e":"Commendable — Exemplary means worthy of imitation, praise or admiration; commendable is closest","cat":"Synonyms","t":"EXEMPLARY"},{"n":43,"q":"When the new teacher entered the classroom, he found the pupils RESTIVE","o":["at rest","idle","quiet","impatient"],"a":3,"e":"Impatient — Restive means restless, impatient or difficult to control","cat":"Synonyms","t":"RESTIVE"},{"n":44,"q":"There is no DEARTH of talent in this country","o":["scarcity","availability","plenty","absence"],"a":0,"e":"Scarcity — Dearth means a shortage or scarcity of something","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DEARTH"},{"n":45,"q":"The servants RETIRED to their quarters","o":["entered","went away","ran away","mobilised"],"a":1,"e":"Went away — Retired here means withdrew or went away to their quarters","cat":"Synonyms","t":"RETIRED"},{"n":46,"q":"The navy gave TACTICAL support to the marines","o":["sensitive","strategic","immediate","expert"],"a":1,"e":"Strategic — Tactical relates to carefully planned actions used to achieve a particular objective; strategic is the closest option","cat":"Synonyms","t":"TACTICAL"},{"n":47,"q":"A genius tends to DEVIATE from the routine way of thinking","o":["dispute","disagree","distinguish","differ"],"a":3,"e":"Differ — Deviate from means to depart from or differ from the usual course or standard","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DEVIATE"},{"n":48,"q":"He was greatly DEBILITATED by an attack of influenza","o":["depressed","weakened","worried","defeated"],"a":1,"e":"Weakened — Debilitated means made weak or feeble","cat":"Synonyms","t":"DEBILITATED"},{"n":49,"q":"His efforts at helping the poor are LAUDABLE","o":["welcome","sincere","good","praiseworthy"],"a":3,"e":"Praiseworthy — Laudable means deserving praise; praiseworthy","cat":"Synonyms","t":"LAUDABLE"},{"n":50,"q":"His conduct brought him REPROACH from all quarters","o":["rebuke","sympathy","indifference","remorse"],"a":0,"e":"Rebuke — Reproach means criticism, blame or rebuke","cat":"Synonyms","t":"REPROACH"}]},{"id":"pyq-2015-ii","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2015 II","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Had she been hungry, she would have devoured the whole lot of it.","o":["Unless she had been hungry","However she had been hungry","As if she had been hungry","No improvement"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — “Had she been hungry, she would have devoured...” is a correct inverted third conditional.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions: Look at the underlined part of each sentence. Below each sentence are three possible substitutions for the underlined part. If one of them is better, select it; if none improves the sentence, select (d) No improvement."},{"n":2,"q":"Until you don’t finish the work, you won’t be given leave.","o":["Until you must finish","Until finishing","Until you finish","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Until you finish — After “until”, the negative “don’t” is not used here. Correct: Until you finish the work, you won’t be given leave.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":3,"q":"The names of the defaulters have been cut off the register.","o":["cut out","struck out","struck off","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Struck off — “Strike a name off a register/list” means remove it officially.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":4,"q":"This is a matter I’d rather not talk about.","o":["of which I’d rather not talk about","I rather not talk about","than I’d rather not talk about","No improvement"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — The sentence is natural and grammatically correct.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":5,"q":"Only when you left I did sleep.","o":["I slept","did I sleep","had I slept","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Did I sleep — When “Only when...” comes first, the main clause requires inversion. Correct: Only when you left did I sleep.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":6,"q":"I will be happy if you will buy me apples.","o":["buy","bought","will be buying","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"Buy — In the first conditional, the if-clause normally takes the simple present.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":7,"q":"After I saved some money, I shall go abroad.","o":["shall save","should have saved","have saved","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Have saved — After “after” referring to a completed future action, the present perfect can be used.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":8,"q":"Can you arrange the car to be ready this evening?","o":["arrange with the car","arrange for the car","arrange that the car","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Arrange for the car — The standard structure is “arrange for something to be done”.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":9,"q":"Hardly had he reached home when the telephone rang.","o":["he had reached","did he reach","he reached","No improvement"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — “Hardly had...when...” is a correct construction.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":10,"q":"He is likely to win the elections by the sweeping majority.","o":["with the sweeping majority","in sweeping majority","by a sweeping majority","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"By a sweeping majority — The indefinite article is required.","cat":"Sentence Improvement"},{"n":11,"q":"His uncle\nP: for success in life,\nQ: always advised his son,\nR: who was a self-made man\nS: to depend on his own efforts","o":["S Q P R","R Q S P","P R S Q","Q P S R"],"a":1,"e":"R–Q–S–P — Answer retained exactly as supplied. Correct arrangement: His uncle, who was a self-made man, always advised his son to depend on his own efforts for success in life.","cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence whose parts have been jumbled and labelled P, Q, R and S. Rearrange the parts and select the proper sequence."},{"n":12,"q":"The doctor\nP: did not like the behavior of the patients\nQ: who was very competent in his profession\nR: when they talked at length\nS: about their problems","o":["R P S Q","S R P Q","Q P R S","P R Q S"],"a":2,"e":"Q–P–R–S — Answer retained exactly as supplied. Correct arrangement: The doctor, who was very competent in his profession, did not like the behavior of the patients when they talked at length about their problems.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":13,"q":"P: from leadership in culture\nQ: in military situations and in face-to-face small groups\nR: leadership has wide range of expressions\nS: to leadership in politics","o":["R S Q P","P Q R S","R P S Q","S Q R P"],"a":2,"e":"R–P–S–Q — Answer retained exactly as supplied.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":14,"q":"He sat\nP: glancing occasionally\nQ: peering through the window\nR: at the figure of the old woman\nS: until he was chilled with the cold","o":["P S R Q","Q R P S","S P R Q","P R S Q"],"a":2,"e":"S–P–R–Q — Answer retained exactly as supplied, as instructed.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":15,"q":"After the earthquake tremors, the TV showed a haggard man\nP: shaking his fist at the sky\nQ: clambering over the ruins\nR: and collapsing with a howl of revolt\nS: of his house and factory","o":["S R Q P","Q S P R","P Q R S","R P S Q"],"a":1,"e":"Q–S–P–R — Correct flow: clambering over the ruins → of his house and factory → shaking his fist at the sky → and collapsing with a howl of revolt.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":16,"q":"Everyone\nP: acknowledges\nQ: when he considers the case calmly\nR: who knows you\nS: that you have been wronged","o":["R S Q P","R P Q S","P Q R S","Q R P S"],"a":1,"e":"R–P–Q–S — Answer retained exactly as supplied.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":17,"q":"Many of his acquaintances avoid him because he is so GARRULOUS.","o":["unreasonable","talkative","quarrelsome","proud"],"a":1,"e":"Talkative — Garrulous means excessively talkative.","cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the word that is nearest in meaning to the underlined word.","t":"GARRULOUS"},{"n":18,"q":"He bore the pain with great FORTITUDE.","o":["resignation","defiance","indifference","forbearance"],"a":3,"e":"Forbearance — Fortitude means courage and endurance in facing pain or adversity; forbearance is the keyed closest option.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"FORTITUDE"},{"n":19,"q":"He gave his TACIT approval to the proposition.","o":["full","loud","clean","implied"],"a":3,"e":"Implied — Tacit means understood or implied without being openly expressed.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"TACIT"},{"n":20,"q":"In spite of hard work, the farmers could not MANAGE yield.","o":["satisfactory","scanty","plenty","normal"],"a":1,"e":"Scanty — Answer retained exactly as supplied in the corrected source.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"MANAGE"},{"n":21,"q":"He was EXHILARATED at the outcome of the election results.","o":["satisfied","surprised","disappointed","overjoyed"],"a":3,"e":"Overjoyed — Exhilarated means extremely happy, excited or elated.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"EXHILARATED"},{"n":22,"q":"He CONCEALED his thoughts very cleverly.","o":["emphasized","expressed","affirmed","revealed"],"a":3,"e":"Revealed — Concealed means hid; revealed means made known.","cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the word that is opposite in meaning to the underlined word.","t":"CONCEALED"},{"n":23,"q":"The proposal was DENOUNCED by one and all.","o":["renounced","recommended","announced","commended"],"a":3,"e":"Commended — Denounced means strongly condemned; commended means praised or approved.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"DENOUNCED"},{"n":24,"q":"She was SKEPTICAL about the safety of the new drug.","o":["doubtful","certain","hopeful","sanguine"],"a":1,"e":"Certain — Skeptical means doubtful; certain is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"SKEPTICAL"},{"n":25,"q":"The answers to the question were COHERENT.","o":["relaxed","loose","consistent","disconnected"],"a":3,"e":"Disconnected — Coherent means logically connected and consistent; disconnected is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"COHERENT"},{"n":26,"q":"It was no ALTRUISTIC motive that prompted him to help her.","o":["selfish","inhuman","brutal","wicked"],"a":0,"e":"Selfish — Altruistic means selfless and concerned for others; selfish is its direct opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"ALTRUISTIC"},{"n":27,"q":"Man has won his dominant position on this planet by his ______ of technology.","o":["command","emphasis","belief","stress"],"a":0,"e":"Command — “Command of technology” means mastery or control over technology.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: Each sentence has a blank space and four words or groups of words. Select the most appropriate option for the blank."},{"n":28,"q":"The day was extremely hot and, in no time, my back was drenched with ______.","o":["prickly heat","perspiration","sores","fatigue"],"a":1,"e":"Perspiration — “Drenched with perspiration” means soaked with sweat.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":29,"q":"The Government is encouraging village upliftment ______ in the country.","o":["programmes","designs","talks","propaganda"],"a":0,"e":"Programmes — “Village upliftment programmes” is the natural expression for organised schemes aimed at rural development.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":30,"q":"Satish was endowed ______ a natural talent for music.","o":["in","by","for","with"],"a":3,"e":"With — The correct expression is “endowed with”.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":31,"q":"Mr. Ghosh is very happy ______ his son’s excellent result.","o":["for seeing","to see","by seeing","see"],"a":1,"e":"To see — The natural construction is “happy to see”.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":32,"q":"Sunlight filtering ______ the stained glass window created a mosaic of colours on the floor.","o":["in","through","at","into"],"a":1,"e":"Through — Light filters through a window.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":33,"q":"Are you feeling doubtful ______ your decision?","o":["about","upon","at","for"],"a":0,"e":"About — The correct collocation is “doubtful about something”.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":34,"q":"I want to study Geology now for I ______ Zoology for the last three years.","o":["am studying","have been studying","had studied","had been studying"],"a":1,"e":"Have been studying — The action began in the past and continues to the present.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":35,"q":"Increase in storage facilities has made it possible to store foods at places ______ of all over the country.","o":["safe","easy","proper","convenient"],"a":3,"e":"Convenient — Answer retained exactly as supplied in the corrected source.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":36,"q":"To say that we were surprised at the cleverness of the child is an understatement; we were really ______.","o":["annoyed","astounded","flattered","confused"],"a":1,"e":"Astounded — Astounded means greatly surprised or amazed.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":37,"q":"In spite of the old woman’s repeated entreaties, he remained ______.","o":["ashamed","docile","indifferent","lethargic"],"a":2,"e":"Indifferent — Indifferent means unconcerned or unmoved.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":38,"q":"There was ______ competition for electoral seats.","o":["diligent","rapid","cut-throat","sparse"],"a":2,"e":"Cut-throat — Cut-throat competition means extremely fierce or ruthless competition.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":39,"q":"The cricket team ______ mainly of State players.","o":["composed","consist","made with","comprises"],"a":3,"e":"Comprises — Comprise means consist of.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":40,"q":"His love for money is the only ______ that drives him to work so hard.","o":["programme","plan","reason","greed"],"a":2,"e":"Reason — His love of money is the only reason that motivates him to work so hard.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":41,"q":"According to ______ sources, the Chief Minister did not consult his Cabinet Members on this issue.","o":["intelligent","simple","reliable","fundamental"],"a":2,"e":"Reliable — “According to reliable sources” is the standard collocation.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":42,"q":"I am fond / in seeing Monday / No error","o":["I am fond","in seeing Monday","—","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“in seeing Monday” — Fond takes the preposition “of”. Correct: I am fond of seeing Monday. The wording is unusual, but the grammatical error is the preposition.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: Each sentence has three underlined parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error."},{"n":43,"q":"I shall leave school / as soon as / I shall find a job / No error","o":["I shall leave school","as soon as","I shall find a job","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“I shall find a job” — In a time clause introduced by “as soon as”, simple present is used for future reference. Correct: as soon as I find a job.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":44,"q":"He is a rich man / he is owning / a number of buildings in Bombay / No error","o":["He is a rich man","he is owning","a number of buildings in Bombay","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“he is owning” — Own is a stative verb and is normally not used in the continuous form. Correct: he owns a number of buildings.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":45,"q":"We were able to make it / in spite of / a bad weather / No error","o":["We were able to make it","in spite of","a bad weather","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“a bad weather” — Weather is uncountable and does not take “a”. Correct: in spite of bad weather.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":46,"q":"Neither of the two letters of credit / were useful / when he needed it / No error","o":["Neither of the two letters of credit","were useful","when he needed it","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“were useful” — Neither is treated as singular in traditional examination grammar. Correct: was useful.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":47,"q":"Little he realized / that he wouldn’t live / to see his grandson married / No error","o":["Little he realized","that he wouldn’t live","to see his grandson married","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“Little he realized” — When “little” meaning not at all begins a sentence, inversion is required. Correct: Little did he realize...","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":48,"q":"A difficult syllabus / is supposed to hurt / juvenile mind / No error","o":["A difficult syllabus","is supposed to hurt","juvenile mind","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“juvenile mind” — A singular countable noun requires an article. Correct: a juvenile mind, or more naturally, juvenile minds.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":49,"q":"The voice of sanity and patriotism / are important to dispel / the prevailing confusion / No error","o":["The voice of sanity and patriotism","are important to dispel","the prevailing confusion","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“are important” — The subject is singular: the voice. Correct: is important.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":50,"q":"You were going to explain / your late arrival / isn’t it? / No error","o":["You were going to explain","your late arrival","isn’t it?","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“isn’t it?” — The question tag must agree with “you were going”. Correct: weren’t you?","cat":"Spotting Errors"}]},{"id":"pyq-2015-i","kind":"pyq","label":"NDA/NA English 2015 I","group":"Previous Years Questions","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Unless you stop to make noise at once / I will have no option but to / bring the matter to the attention of the police / No error","o":["Unless you stop to make noise at once","I will have no option but to","bring the matter to the attention of the police","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“Unless you stop to make noise at once” — Stop to make noise means stop in order to make noise. Here, stop making noise is required.","cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions: A number of sentences are given. Each sentence is underlined in three separate parts labelled (a), (b) and (c). Identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error."},{"n":2,"q":"He couldn’t but help / shed tears at the plight of the villagers / rendered homeless by a devastating cyclone / No error","o":["He couldn’t but help","shed tears at the plight of the villagers","rendered homeless by a devastating cyclone","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“He couldn’t but help” — This is a faulty combination. Use “couldn’t help shedding tears” or “couldn’t but shed tears”.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":3,"q":"Since it was his first election campaign, the candidate was confused / none could clearly understand / either the principles he stood for or the benefits he promised / No error","o":["Since it was his first election campaign, the candidate was confused","none could clearly understand","either the principles he stood for or the benefits he promised","No error"],"a":3,"e":"No error — The sentence is grammatically acceptable.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":4,"q":"It is an established fact that transcendental American poets and philosophers / who lived in the latter half of the nineteenth century / were more influenced by Indian philosophy, in particular by Upanishadic Philosophy / No error","o":["It is an established fact that transcendental American poets and philosophers","who lived in the latter half of the nineteenth century","were more influenced by Indian philosophy, in particular by Upanishadic Philosophy","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“were more influenced by Indian philosophy” — With influenced, the comparative “more” is unnecessary in this context; “were influenced by” is appropriate.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":5,"q":"No sooner did I open the door / when the rain, heavy and stormy, rushed in / making us shiver from head to foot / No error","o":["No sooner did I open the door","when the rain, heavy and stormy, rushed in","making us shiver from head to foot","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“when the rain...” — The correct correlative construction is “No sooner...than”, not “No sooner...when”.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":6,"q":"After opening the door / we entered into the room / next to the kitchen / No error","o":["After opening the door","we entered into the room","next to the kitchen","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“entered into the room” — Enter is transitive here and does not take “into”: entered the room.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":7,"q":"When the dentist came in / my tooth was stopped aching / out of fear that I might lose my tooth / No error","o":["When the dentist came in","my tooth was stopped aching","out of fear that I might lose my tooth","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“was stopped aching” — Incorrect construction. It should be “stopped aching”.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":8,"q":"Emphasis on equality of life ensures / for the health and happiness / of every individual / No error","o":["Emphasis on equality of life ensures","for the health and happiness","of every individual","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“for the health and happiness” — Ensure does not take “for” in this construction: ensures the health and happiness.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":9,"q":"The students were / awaiting for / the arrival of the chief guest / No error","o":["The students were","awaiting for","the arrival of the chief guest","No error"],"a":1,"e":"“awaiting for” — Await is transitive: awaiting the arrival, not awaiting for.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":10,"q":"You will come / to my party tomorrow / isn’t it? / No error","o":["You will come","to my party tomorrow","isn’t it?","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“isn’t it?” — The tag must correspond to “you will”: won’t you?","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":11,"q":"Having read a number of stories / about space travel / his dream now is about to visit the moon / No error","o":["Having read a number of stories","about space travel","his dream now is about to visit the moon","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“his dream now is about to visit” — Correct construction: his dream now is to visit the moon.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":12,"q":"The meeting adjourned abruptly / by the Chairman after / about three hours of deliberation / No error","o":["The meeting adjourned abruptly","by the Chairman after","about three hours of deliberation","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“The meeting adjourned abruptly” — Passive voice is required because the Chairman performed the action: The meeting was adjourned abruptly.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":13,"q":"Not one of the hundreds / of striking workers / were allowed to go near the factory / No error","o":["Not one of the hundreds","of striking workers","were allowed to go near the factory","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“were allowed” — Not one is singular; hence “was allowed”.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":14,"q":"If I had known / this earlier / I will have helped him / No error","o":["If I had known","this earlier","I will have helped him","No error"],"a":2,"e":"“I will have helped him” — In a past unreal conditional: I would have helped him.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":15,"q":"Mr. Smith was accused for murder / but the court found him not guilty / and acquitted him / No error","o":["Mr. Smith was accused for murder","but the court found him not guilty","and acquitted him","No error"],"a":0,"e":"“accused for murder” — The correct preposition is “accused of murder”.","cat":"Spotting Errors"},{"n":16,"q":"Although India is still by far a poor country, it can become rich if its natural and human resources are fully utilised.","o":["few and far between","by and large","by and by","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"By and large — “By and large” means generally/on the whole.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions: Look at the underlined part of each sentence. Select the best substitution from (a), (b) or (c). If none improves the sentence, select (d) No improvement.","t":"BY FAR"},{"n":17,"q":"The more they earn, more they spend.","o":["More they earn, more they spend","More they earn, the more they spend","The more they earn, the more they spend","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"The more they earn, the more they spend — The standard comparative construction is “the more..., the more...”.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"MORE THEY SPEND"},{"n":18,"q":"But in all these cases conversion from one scale to another is easy because scales have well-formulated.","o":["can be well-formulated","are well-formulated","well-formulated","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Are well-formulated — A finite passive verb is needed: because scales are well-formulated.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"HAVE WELL-FORMULATED"},{"n":19,"q":"Five years ago on this date, I am sitting in a small Japanese car, driving across Poland towards Berlin.","o":["was sitting","sat","have been sitting","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"Was sitting — “Five years ago” requires past tense.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"AM SITTING"},{"n":20,"q":"The old man felled some trees in the garden with hardly no effort at all.","o":["hard effort","hardly any effort","a hardly any effort","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Hardly any effort — “Hardly no” creates an incorrect double negative.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"HARDLY NO EFFORT"},{"n":21,"q":"She says she’s already paid me back, but I can’t remember, so I’ll have to take her word.","o":["to take her word true","to take her at her word","to take her word for it","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"To take her word for it — “Take someone’s word for it” means accept what someone says as true.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"TO TAKE HER WORD"},{"n":22,"q":"The workers are hell bent at getting what is due to them.","o":["hell bent on getting","hell bent for getting","hell bent to getting","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"Hell bent on getting — The fixed expression is “hell-bent on + gerund”.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"HELL BENT AT GETTING"},{"n":23,"q":"You are warned against committing the same mistake again.","o":["to commit","for committing","against to commit","No improvement"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — “Warn someone against doing something” is grammatically correct.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"WARNED AGAINST COMMITTING"},{"n":24,"q":"While we would like that all Indian children to go to school, we need to ponder why they do not.","o":["that all the Indian children","if all the children of India","all Indian children","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"All Indian children — Correct construction: would like all Indian children to go to school.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"THAT ALL INDIAN CHILDREN TO GO"},{"n":25,"q":"Due to these reasons we are all in favour of universal compulsory education.","o":["Out of these reasons","For these reasons","By these reasons","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"For these reasons — This is the idiomatic expression meaning because of these reasons.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"DUE TO THESE REASONS"},{"n":26,"q":"When it was feared that the serfs might go too far and gain their freedom from serfdom, the Protestant leaders joined the princes at crushing them.","o":["into crushing","in crushing","without crushing","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"In crushing — The correct expression is “join someone in doing something”.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"JOINED THE PRINCES AT CRUSHING THEM"},{"n":27,"q":"In India, today many of our intellectuals still talk in terms of the French revolution and the Rights of Man, not appreciating that much has happened since then.","o":["much has been happening","much had happened","much might happen","No improvement"],"a":3,"e":"No improvement — “Much has happened since then” correctly uses the present perfect with “since then”.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"MUCH HAS HAPPENED SINCE THEN"},{"n":28,"q":"Taxpayers are to be conscious of their privileges.","o":["have to","need","ought to","No improvement"],"a":2,"e":"Ought to — “Ought to be conscious of their privileges” expresses obligation appropriately.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"ARE TO BE CONSCIOUS"},{"n":29,"q":"I would have waited for you at the station if I knew that you would come.","o":["had known","was knowing","have known","No improvement"],"a":0,"e":"Had known — This is a third conditional: I would have waited...if I had known...","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"KNEW"},{"n":30,"q":"No one could explain how a calm and balanced person like him could penetrate such a mindless act on his friends.","o":["perpetuate","perpetrate","precipitate","No improvement"],"a":1,"e":"Perpetrate — Perpetrate means commit an unlawful, harmful or immoral act. Penetrate is incorrect here.","cat":"Sentence Improvement","t":"PENETRATE"},{"n":31,"q":"S1: At the roadside the driver will be asked to blow through a small glass tube into a plastic bag.\nP: And if the colour change does not reach the line the driver cannot be punished under the new law.\nQ: Inside the tube are chemically treated crystals which change colour if the driver has alcohol on his breath.\nR: But if the colour change does reach the line then the test has proved positive.\nS: If the colour change goes beyond a certain line marked on the tube this indicates that the driver is probably over the specified limit.\nS6: The driver will be asked to go to the police station.","o":["P S Q R","S Q R P","R P S Q","Q S P R"],"a":3,"e":"Q–S–P–R — First the crystals are introduced, then the colour limit is explained, followed by the negative result and positive result.","cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions: Each passage consists of six sentences. S1 and S6 are fixed. The middle four sentences P, Q, R and S are jumbled. Select the proper order for P, Q, R and S."},{"n":32,"q":"S1: Hope springs eternally in the heart of man.\nP: But hope is everlasting.\nQ: Love, friendship and youth perish.\nR: It is nursed by the glorious elements of nature.\nS: Man derives hope from nature in his gallant struggle after some noble ideal.\nS6: This is the central idea of the poem.","o":["Q P R S","S R Q P","R S Q P","Q P S R"],"a":0,"e":"Q–P–R–S — Love, friendship and youth perish contrasts naturally with “But hope is everlasting”, followed by its connection with nature.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":33,"q":"S1: Mr Sherlock Holmes and Doctor Watson were spending a weekend in a University town.\nP: One evening they received a visit from an acquaintance, Mr Hilton Soames.\nQ: On that occasion he was in a state of great agitation.\nR: They were staying in furnished rooms, close to the library.\nS: Mr Soames was a tall, thin man of a nervous and excitable nature.\nS6: It was clear that something very unusual happened.","o":["P R S Q","R P S Q","P Q R S","R P Q S"],"a":1,"e":"R–P–S–Q — First their accommodation is mentioned, then Soames’s visit, his description, and finally his agitated condition.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":34,"q":"S1: The machines that drive modern civilisation derive their power from coal and oil.\nP: But they are not inexhaustible.\nQ: These sources may not be exhausted very soon.\nR: A time may come when some other sources have to be tapped and utilised.\nS: Power may, of course, be obtained in future from forests, water, wind and withered vegetables.\nS6: Nuclear energy may also be effectively used in this respect.","o":["P Q R S","Q P R S","S R Q P","S P Q R"],"a":1,"e":"Q–P–R–S — The present energy sources may not be exhausted soon, but they are finite; therefore other sources may eventually be needed, followed by examples.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":35,"q":"S1: The body can never stop.\nP: To support this endless activity, the body needs all the fuel for action.\nQ: Sometimes it is more active than at other times, but it is always moving.\nR: Even in the deepest sleep we must breathe.\nS: The fuel must come from somewhere.\nS6: It comes from food.","o":["Q P R S","P R Q S","Q R P S","S R Q P"],"a":2,"e":"Q–R–P–S — The body is always active; even during sleep it breathes; therefore it requires fuel, which must come from somewhere.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":36,"q":"S1: American idealism is essentially a belief in the idea of progress.\nP: Therefore, he believes that, because of human effort, the future will be better than the past.\nQ: But if Americans are usually optimistic, they are not wholly unrealistic.\nR: The American tends to view history as a record of human achievement.\nS: They have some common sense practicality.\nS6: This sense they have inherited from the English.","o":["P Q R S","P Q S R","R P Q S","P R Q S"],"a":2,"e":"R–P–Q–S — Americans view history as human achievement, leading to faith in future progress; this optimism is then qualified by practical common sense.","cat":"Ordering"},{"n":37,"q":"Blank 37: Choose the most appropriate word for blank 37.","o":["felt","occurred","happened"],"a":1,"e":"Occurred — The standard expression is “it occurred to him”, meaning he suddenly realised.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions: In the passage, choose the best word from the alternatives given for each numbered blank.","c":"Passage: After having slept for an hour, Bond decided to go out into the city and try to find his contact. After changing his suit, he carefully locked his room and stepped out of the hotel. No one was around. But he had walked only for a few minutes when it suddenly 37. ______ to him that he was being 38. ______ by someone. There was really no 39. ______ for that 40. ______ of footsteps. He was now walking 41. ______ the main street, which was crowded 42. ______ people. He became extremely aware of the danger of people threatening him."},{"n":38,"q":"Blank 38: Choose the most appropriate word for blank 38.","o":["followed","shot","looked"],"a":0,"e":"Followed — He felt that someone was following him.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":39,"q":"Blank 39: Choose the most appropriate word for blank 39.","o":["reason","cause","evidence"],"a":2,"e":"Evidence — There was no real evidence supporting his suspicion.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":40,"q":"Blank 40: Choose the most appropriate word for blank 40.","o":["weight","sound","hurting"],"a":1,"e":"Sound — “Sound of footsteps” is the natural expression.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":41,"q":"Blank 41: Choose the most appropriate word for blank 41.","o":["from","down","over"],"a":1,"e":"Down — One commonly walks down the main street.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":42,"q":"Blank 42: Choose the most appropriate word for blank 42.","o":["with","by","in"],"a":0,"e":"With — The correct collocation is “crowded with people”.","cat":"Fill in the Blanks"},{"n":43,"q":"He was FIRED for negligence on duty.","o":["relieved of his job","scolded","rebuked","attacked"],"a":0,"e":"Relieved of his job — Fired means dismissed or removed from employment.","cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words or groups of words. Select the word or group of words most similar in meaning to the underlined word.","t":"FIRED"},{"n":44,"q":"Democracy is not the standardising of everyone so as to OBLITERATE all peculiarity.","o":["demolish","extinguish","erase","change"],"a":2,"e":"Erase — Obliterate means destroy, remove or erase completely.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"OBLITERATE"},{"n":45,"q":"Divine grace is truly INEFFABLE.","o":["that which cannot be rubbed out","incapable of being understood","that which is too great to be expressed in words","too powerful to be defeated"],"a":2,"e":"That which is too great to be expressed in words — Ineffable means too great or extraordinary to be adequately expressed in words.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"INEFFABLE"},{"n":46,"q":"The convocation address was very EDIFYING.","o":["tedious","in need of editing","instructive","exciting"],"a":2,"e":"Instructive — Edifying means instructive or morally/intellectually improving.","cat":"Synonyms","t":"EDIFYING"},{"n":47,"q":"John is always SHABBILY dressed.","o":["decently","beautifully","extravagantly","scantily"],"a":0,"e":"Decently — Shabbily means poorly or untidily dressed; decently gives the opposite sense.","cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions: Each item consists of a sentence with an underlined word followed by four words. Select the word most nearly opposite in meaning to the underlined word.","t":"SHABBILY"},{"n":48,"q":"The new manager of the Bank is URBANE in his manners.","o":["civilised","slow","rude","foolish"],"a":2,"e":"Rude — Urbane means polished, courteous and refined; rude is its opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"URBANE"},{"n":49,"q":"It is necessary to develop THRIFTY habits to be able to lead a comfortable life.","o":["expensive","extravagant","economical","good"],"a":1,"e":"Extravagant — Thrifty means economical and careful with money; extravagant is the opposite.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"THRIFTY"},{"n":50,"q":"Many people suffer because of PRIDE.","o":["lowliness","submission","humility","obedience"],"a":2,"e":"Humility — Pride is opposed to humility, meaning modesty or lack of excessive self-importance.","cat":"Antonyms","t":"PRIDE"}]},{"id":"sample-01","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 1","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Neither of the explanations offered by the two witnesses / appear to account satisfactorily / for the discrepancy in the records.","o":["Neither of the explanations offered by the two witnesses","appear to account satisfactorily","for the discrepancy in the records.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–10): Spotting Errors Each of the following sentences has been divided into three parts marked (A), (B) and (C). Read each sentence carefully and identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (D) No error.","a":1,"e":"The subject Neither is singular; therefore, appears should be used."},{"n":2,"q":"The Principal, as well as the members of the faculty, / has endorsed the proposal / without any reservation.","o":["The Principal, as well as the members of the faculty,","has endorsed the proposal","without any reservation.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"The main subject is The Principal. The phrase as well as the members of the faculty does not change the number; hence has endorsed is correct."},{"n":3,"q":"Scarcely had the aircraft left the runway / than the pilot reported / an abnormal vibration.","o":["Scarcely had the aircraft left the runway","than the pilot reported","an abnormal vibration.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"The correct correlative construction is Scarcely...when, not Scarcely...than."},{"n":4,"q":"He is one of the few analysts / who has consistently predicted / the consequences of such measures.","o":["He is one of the few analysts","who has consistently predicted","the consequences of such measures.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"The relative pronoun who refers to plural analysts; therefore, have consistently predicted is required."},{"n":5,"q":"It is high time the authorities / take a firm decision / on the long-pending issue.","o":["It is high time the authorities","take a firm decision","on the long-pending issue.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"After It is high time, the simple past is used: took a firm decision."},{"n":6,"q":"The reason why the proposal was rejected / was because it failed to address / the principal concern of the committee.","o":["The reason why the proposal was rejected","was because it failed to address","the principal concern of the committee.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"The correct construction is The reason...was that..., not the reason...was because."},{"n":7,"q":"He denied to have disclosed / any confidential information / to persons outside the organisation.","o":["He denied to have disclosed","any confidential information","to persons outside the organisation.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"Deny is followed by a gerund: denied having disclosed."},{"n":8,"q":"The equipment supplied to the forward posts / were found inadequate / for operations in severe winter conditions.","o":["The equipment supplied to the forward posts","were found inadequate","for operations in severe winter conditions.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Equipment is an uncountable singular noun; therefore, was found inadequate."},{"n":9,"q":"Had the warning been issued earlier, / several casualties might have been avoided / during the evacuation.","o":["Had the warning been issued earlier,","several casualties might have been avoided","during the evacuation.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"The inverted third conditional Had the warning been issued earlier...might have been avoided is correct."},{"n":10,"q":"Each of the candidates / were asked to justify / the assumptions underlying the argument.","o":["Each of the candidates","were asked to justify","the assumptions underlying the argument.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Each takes a singular verb; hence was asked."},{"n":11,"q":"OBDURATE","o":["Compliant","Unyielding","Impartial","Apprehensive"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Each of the following items consists of a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word that is nearest in meaning to the given word.","t":"OBDURATE","a":1,"e":"Obdurate means stubbornly refusing to change one's opinion or course of action."},{"n":12,"q":"LACONIC","o":["Concise","Ambiguous","Persuasive","Elaborate"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"LACONIC","a":0,"e":"Laconic means using very few words."},{"n":13,"q":"EQUIVOCAL","o":["Decisive","Ambiguous","Authentic","Irrelevant"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"EQUIVOCAL","a":1,"e":"Equivocal means deliberately unclear or capable of more than one interpretation."},{"n":14,"q":"ASSIDUOUS","o":["Industrious","Impulsive","Ingenious","Assertive"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ASSIDUOUS","a":0,"e":"Assiduous means hardworking, diligent and persistent."},{"n":15,"q":"PERVASIVE","o":["Temporary","Concealed","Widespread","Superficial"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PERVASIVE","a":2,"e":"Pervasive means spreading widely throughout an area or group."},{"n":16,"q":"PARSIMONIOUS","o":["Frugal","Lavish","Cautious","Economical"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Each of the following items consists of a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word that is opposite in meaning to the given word.","t":"PARSIMONIOUS","a":1,"e":"Parsimonious means excessively unwilling to spend; lavish is the opposite."},{"n":17,"q":"EPHEMERAL","o":["Enduring","Fleeting","Fragile","Intermittent"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"EPHEMERAL","a":0,"e":"Ephemeral means lasting for a very short time."},{"n":18,"q":"TACITURN","o":["Reserved","Garrulous","Reticent","Discreet"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"TACITURN","a":1,"e":"Taciturn means habitually quiet; garrulous means excessively talkative."},{"n":19,"q":"CONCILIATORY","o":["Diplomatic","Accommodating","Antagonistic","Persuasive"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"CONCILIATORY","a":2,"e":"Conciliatory means intended to reduce hostility; antagonistic means hostile or oppositional."},{"n":20,"q":"EXACERBATE","o":["Intensify","Aggravate","Alleviate","Provoke"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"EXACERBATE","a":2,"e":"Exacerbate means make something worse; alleviate means make it less severe."},{"n":21,"q":"The minister’s statement was regarded as THROWING DOWN THE GAUNTLET to his critics.","o":["Avoiding an argument","Issuing an open challenge","Withdrawing from a dispute","Accepting responsibility"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): Idioms and Phrases Each sentence contains an idiom/phrase in capital letters. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning.","t":"THROWING DOWN THE GAUNTLET","a":1,"e":"Throw down the gauntlet means openly challenge someone."},{"n":22,"q":"By ignoring repeated warnings, the company was SAILING CLOSE TO THE WIND.","o":["Acting with admirable courage","Proceeding very cautiously","Taking dangerous risks near permissible limits","Avoiding an unpleasant situation"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"SAILING CLOSE TO THE WIND","a":2,"e":"Sail close to the wind means act dangerously close to legal or acceptable limits."},{"n":23,"q":"His elaborate explanation CUT NO ICE WITH the inquiry committee.","o":["Created a favourable impression","Had no influence upon","Caused considerable confusion","Was deliberately ignored"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"CUT NO ICE WITH","a":1,"e":"Cut no ice with someone means fail to influence or impress that person."},{"n":24,"q":"A careful reader must often READ BETWEEN THE LINES.","o":["Read every sentence repeatedly","Discover an implied meaning","Ignore unnecessary details","Compare different books"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"READ BETWEEN THE LINES","a":1,"e":"Read between the lines means understand the hidden or implied meaning."},{"n":25,"q":"After two successive defeats, the team remained IN THE DOLDRUMS.","o":["Highly optimistic","In a state of inactivity or depression","Completely disorganised","Under severe criticism"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"IN THE DOLDRUMS","a":1,"e":"In the doldrums means in a low, depressed or inactive state."},{"n":26,"q":"The commanding officer insisted that every cadet reports at the ground by 0600 hours.","o":["reported at the ground by 0600 hours","report at the ground by 0600 hours","will report at the ground by 0600 hours","has reported at the ground by 0600 hours"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): In each of the following sentences, a part has been underlined. Select the alternative that best improves the underlined part.","a":1,"e":"After insisted that, the mandative subjunctive takes the base form report."},{"n":27,"q":"Had he informed me earlier, I would be able to assist him.","o":["I could assist him","I would have been able to assist him","I shall have assisted him","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"The condition refers to an unreal past situation; therefore, the main clause requires would have been able."},{"n":28,"q":"The proposal is fraught of serious practical difficulties.","o":["fraught by serious practical difficulties","fraught with serious practical difficulties","fraught from serious practical difficulties","fraught in serious practical difficulties"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"The standard expression is fraught with."},{"n":29,"q":"She is as much capable as any other member of the team.","o":["so capable as","as capable as","more capable as","capable than"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"The correct comparative construction is as + adjective + as."},{"n":30,"q":"The suggestion is worth to consider before taking a final decision.","o":["worthy to consider","worth considering before taking a final decision","worth to be considered","worthy considering before taking a final decision"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"Worth is followed by a gerund: worth considering."},{"n":31,"q":"Implementation of the scheme is contingent ______ the availability of adequate funds.","o":["with","for","upon","at"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate alternative to complete the sentence.","a":2,"e":"The standard expression is contingent upon."},{"n":32,"q":"The spokesperson refrained ______ commenting on matters that were still under investigation.","o":["to","from","for","with"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"The correct construction is refrain from + gerund."},{"n":33,"q":"Had it not been ______ your timely intervention, the negotiations might have collapsed.","o":["of","with","for","through"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"Correct expression: Had it not been for..."},{"n":34,"q":"Lest his remarks ______ misunderstood, he repeated the point in simpler terms.","o":["were","are","be","would be"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"After lest, formal English commonly uses the subjunctive/base form: lest...be misunderstood."},{"n":35,"q":"Not until the final report was released ______ the magnitude of the failure.","o":["we realised","did we realise","we had realised","had we realised"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"When Not until begins a sentence, the main clause requires inversion: did we realise."},{"n":36,"q":"P. because it forces the mind to distinguish\nQ. between what is merely asserted\nR. and what can actually be established\nS. critical reading is intellectually demanding","o":["PQRS","QSRP","SPQR","SRQP"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange them in the correct order to form a meaningful and grammatically correct sentence.","a":2,"e":"Critical reading is intellectually demanding because it forces the mind to distinguish between what is merely asserted and what can actually be established."},{"n":37,"q":"P. only after the patrol had returned\nQ. did the commander realise\nR. how narrowly\nS. the unit had avoided the ambush","o":["PQRS","QPRS","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Only after the patrol had returned did the commander realise how narrowly the unit had avoided the ambush."},{"n":38,"q":"P. the value of a principle\nQ. when adherence to it\nR. is tested most severely\nS. becomes personally inconvenient","o":["PRQS","QSPR","SQPR","RQSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"The value of a principle is tested most severely when adherence to it becomes personally inconvenient."},{"n":39,"q":"P. by replacing blind obedience with understanding\nQ. a well-reasoned question\nR. can strengthen discipline\nS. rather than weaken it","o":["QRSP","QSPR","RQPS","SPQR"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"A well-reasoned question can strengthen discipline rather than weaken it by replacing blind obedience with understanding."},{"n":40,"q":"P. only when the evidence is examined together\nQ. seemingly unrelated details\nR. begin to form\nS. a coherent pattern","o":["QRSP","PQRS","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Only when the evidence is examined together, seemingly unrelated details begin to form a coherent pattern."},{"n":41,"q":"The author’s principal argument is that sound decision-making depends upon","o":["making decisions as rapidly as possible","obtaining complete information before acting","adapting the speed and manner of decision to circumstances","avoiding consultation in difficult situations"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow on the basis of the passage.","c":"In organisations that operate under uncertainty, decisiveness is often praised as though speed itself were evidence of sound judgement. Yet a rapid decision may merely conceal inadequate thought. The opposite error is equally dangerous: endless consultation can become a respectable disguise for indecision. Good judgement lies neither in habitual haste nor in habitual delay. It consists in recognising what kind of situation one faces. Some circumstances demand immediate action on incomplete information; others permit, and indeed require, deliberate examination. Experienced leaders therefore do not follow a single rule about how quickly decisions should be made. They distinguish between information that is merely desirable and information without which action would be irresponsible. They are also prepared to revise a decision when new evidence appears. Such revision is sometimes mistaken for weakness, although an unwillingness to alter one's course in the face of contrary evidence may reveal not firmness but vanity.","a":2,"e":"The passage argues against both habitual haste and habitual delay."},{"n":42,"q":"“A respectable disguise for indecision” suggests that excessive consultation may","o":["make indecision appear reasonable","improve the quality of every decision","prevent disagreement among leaders","result from insufficient authority"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"Excessive consultation may give indecision a respectable appearance."},{"n":43,"q":"According to the passage, experienced leaders distinguish between","o":["useful and confidential information","helpful information and information essential for responsible action","popular and expert opinion","facts that support and contradict them"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"Experienced leaders distinguish desirable information from indispensable information."},{"n":44,"q":"The author regards willingness to revise a decision as","o":["evidence of inconsistency","an admission of incompetence","potentially a sign of sound judgement","a threat to authority"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"Revising a decision in response to new evidence shows responsiveness rather than weakness."},{"n":45,"q":"Which of the following best describes the tone of the passage?","o":["Dogmatic","Analytical","Sarcastic","Sentimental"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"The passage objectively examines different approaches to decision-making."},{"n":46,"q":"The author rejects the view that precision in language is merely","o":["a requirement of legal writing","a matter of excessive concern with minor correctness","an obstacle to informal conversation","a method of improving vocabulary"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow on the basis of the passage.","c":"Precision in language is sometimes dismissed as pedantry, especially when the general intention of a speaker appears obvious. But imprecise language does more than offend grammatical taste; it can blur distinctions that are essential to clear thought. Words do not merely clothe ideas already complete in the mind. Often, the attempt to express an idea accurately forces us to discover whether we have understood it at all. This does not mean that every conversation should resemble a legal document. Language serves many purposes, and informality has its legitimate place. The danger arises when habitual vagueness is mistaken for flexibility or when impressive phrases are allowed to substitute for definite meaning. A person may appear eloquent while saying remarkably little. Conversely, a plain statement, carefully qualified, may carry more intellectual weight than a page of ornamented prose. The discipline of choosing words carefully is therefore not merely linguistic. It is also a discipline of thought.","a":1,"e":"Pedantry here refers to excessive attention to minor correctness."},{"n":47,"q":"According to the passage, attempting to express an idea precisely may","o":["reveal whether the idea itself has been clearly understood","make ordinary conversation unnecessarily formal","reduce the range of meanings available to a speaker","prevent the use of figurative language"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"Precise expression can expose uncertainty or confusion in one's own thinking."},{"n":48,"q":"The author does NOT suggest that","o":["informality has a legitimate function","vague language can conceal vague thinking","every conversation should use highly formal language","apparent eloquence may lack substantial meaning"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"The author explicitly says that informality has a legitimate place."},{"n":49,"q":"The contrast between “a plain statement” and “a page of ornamented prose” is intended to show that","o":["lengthy writing is invariably inferior","simplicity of expression may contain greater intellectual substance","literary language should be avoided","plain language requires little thought"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"A plain but precise statement can convey more thought than elaborate prose."},{"n":50,"q":"The most appropriate title for the passage is","o":["Grammar and Social Etiquette","The Decline of Formal Language","Precision of Language and Clarity of Thought","Why Eloquence Is Misleading"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"This title captures the central relationship developed throughout the passage."}]},{"id":"sample-02","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 2","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"INTRANSIGENT","o":["Impartial","Uncompromising","Inattentive","Indifferent"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Each item in this section consists of a word followed by four alternatives. Select the word that is nearest in meaning to the given word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"INTRANSIGENT","a":1,"e":"Intransigent means unwilling to change one’s views or agree to a compromise."},{"n":2,"q":"ENERVATE","o":["Strengthen","Provoke","Weaken","Restrain"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ENERVATE","a":2,"e":"Enervate means to weaken or deprive of strength and vitality."},{"n":3,"q":"SAGACIOUS","o":["Discerning","Audacious","Suspicious","Obstinate"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"SAGACIOUS","a":0,"e":"Sagacious means wise, perceptive and capable of sound judgement."},{"n":4,"q":"PROLIFIC","o":["Productive","Extravagant","Profound","Precocious"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PROLIFIC","a":0,"e":"Prolific means producing or creating something in large quantities."},{"n":5,"q":"ABSTRUSE","o":["Insignificant","Difficult to understand","Unreliable","Exhaustive"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ABSTRUSE","a":1,"e":"Abstruse means obscure or difficult to comprehend."},{"n":6,"q":"AMELIORATE","o":["Mitigate","Improve","Aggravate","Modify"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–10): Each item in this section consists of a word followed by four alternatives. Select the word that is opposite in meaning to the given word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"AMELIORATE","a":2,"e":"Ameliorate means make something better; aggravate means make it worse."},{"n":7,"q":"CAPRICIOUS","o":["Consistent","Whimsical","Impulsive","Erratic"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"CAPRICIOUS","a":0,"e":"Capricious means unpredictable or given to sudden changes; consistent is the opposite."},{"n":8,"q":"SPORADIC","o":["Occasional","Continuous","Irregular","Scattered"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"SPORADIC","a":1,"e":"Sporadic means occurring irregularly or occasionally; continuous is the opposite."},{"n":9,"q":"IMPETUOUS","o":["Rash","Forceful","Cautious","Resolute"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"IMPETUOUS","a":2,"e":"Impetuous means acting hastily without sufficient thought; cautious is the opposite."},{"n":10,"q":"TACIT","o":["Implicit","Silent","Explicit","Uncertain"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"TACIT","a":2,"e":"Tacit means implied or understood without being openly stated; explicit means clearly stated."},{"n":11,"q":"Since you have raised the objection, you should also suggest an alternative.","o":["Preposition","Adverb","Conjunction","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each of the following sentences has an underlined word. Identify the part of speech to which the underlined word belongs and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":2,"e":"Since introduces the subordinate clause “Since you have raised the objection.”"},{"n":12,"q":"The reconnaissance party reached the post before the main contingent.","o":["Adverb","Preposition","Conjunction","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Before governs the noun phrase “the main contingent.”"},{"n":13,"q":"Once the obstruction was removed, the convoy moved fast.","o":["Adjective","Noun","Adverb","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Fast modifies the verb moved and tells how the convoy moved."},{"n":14,"q":"There is little justification for such an arbitrary conclusion.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Pronoun","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":0,"e":"Little qualifies the noun justification and indicates a small amount."},{"n":15,"q":"What he eventually admitted surprised everyone present.","o":["Adjective","Pronoun","Conjunction","Adverb"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"What means “that which/the thing which” and functions as a fused relative pronoun."},{"n":16,"q":"The quality of the essays submitted by the candidates / were considerably better / than the examiners had expected.","o":["The quality of the essays submitted by the candidates","were considerably better","than the examiners had expected.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–25): Each item in this section consists of a sentence divided into four parts marked (a), (b), (c) and (d). Read each sentence carefully and identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error.","a":1,"e":"The subject is singular quality; hence, was considerably better is correct."},{"n":17,"q":"He would sooner resign from the committee / than to compromise / on a matter of principle.","o":["He would sooner resign from the committee","than to compromise","on a matter of principle.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"After would sooner...than, the bare infinitive is used: than compromise."},{"n":18,"q":"Not only did the proposal fail to reduce expenditure, / but it also created / several unforeseen administrative difficulties.","o":["Not only did the proposal fail to reduce expenditure,","but it also created","several unforeseen administrative difficulties.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"The construction Not only did...but it also... is grammatically correct."},{"n":19,"q":"The chairperson demanded / that every allegation was examined impartially / before the report was finalised.","o":["The chairperson demanded","that every allegation was examined impartially","before the report was finalised.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"After demanded that, the subjunctive is appropriate: that every allegation be examined impartially."},{"n":20,"q":"Each of the recommendations, together with its financial implications, / have been examined carefully / by the competent authority.","o":["Each of the recommendations, together with its financial implications,","have been examined carefully","by the competent authority.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"The subject Each is singular; therefore, has been examined carefully."},{"n":21,"q":"Neither the chairman nor the members of the board / was willing to accept / responsibility for the delay.","o":["Neither the chairman nor the members of the board","was willing to accept","responsibility for the delay.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject members; hence, were willing."},{"n":22,"q":"This is one of the few novels / that deserves to be read / more than once.","o":["This is one of the few novels","that deserves to be read","more than once.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"That refers to the plural noun novels; therefore, that deserve to be read."},{"n":23,"q":"I wish I would have known / about the change in schedule / before leaving for the station.","o":["I wish I would have known","about the change in schedule","before leaving for the station.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"Past regret requires I wish I had known."},{"n":24,"q":"The moment the signal will be given, / the advance party will proceed / towards the designated position.","o":["The moment the signal will be given,","the advance party will proceed","towards the designated position.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"In a future time clause, use the simple present: The moment the signal is given."},{"n":25,"q":"The data collected during the two experiments / indicates that the earlier assumption / may have been incorrect.","o":["The data collected during the two experiments","indicates that the earlier assumption","may have been incorrect.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"In traditional formal/examination usage, data is plural; therefore, indicate is required."},{"n":26,"q":"No sooner had the briefing ended when the officers began to leave the hall.","o":["then the officers began to leave the hall","than the officers began to leave the hall","that the officers began to leave the hall","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): In each of the following sentences, a part has been underlined. Select the alternative that best improves the underlined part. If no improvement is required, select the appropriate option.","a":1,"e":"The correct correlative construction is No sooner...than."},{"n":27,"q":"The proposal is unlikely to be accepted unless it is not substantially revised.","o":["unless it is substantially revised","if it is substantially revised","until it is not substantially revised","unless it will be substantially revised"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"Unless already means “if not”; therefore, another not is unnecessary."},{"n":28,"q":"He is accustomed to work under considerable pressure.","o":["for working under considerable pressure","to working under considerable pressure","with working under considerable pressure","at work under considerable pressure"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"Accustomed to is followed by a noun or gerund: to working."},{"n":29,"q":"Had I knew how serious the situation was, I would have acted differently.","o":["Had I known","If I knew","Had I know","If I would have known"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"The past perfect requires the past participle known, not knew."},{"n":30,"q":"The commanding officer, as well as several members of his staff, were present at the inspection.","o":["have been present at the inspection","was present at the inspection","were being present at the inspection","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"As well as does not make the main subject plural. The commanding officer requires was."},{"n":31,"q":"During the negotiations, the other side repeatedly MOVED THE GOALPOSTS.","o":["Changed the conditions or criteria unfairly","Abandoned the negotiations completely","Accepted a compromise reluctantly","Revealed its real intentions"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each of the following sentences contains an idiom or phrase in capital letters. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning.","t":"MOVED THE GOALPOSTS","a":0,"e":"Move the goalposts means alter the conditions or standards during a process, usually unfairly."},{"n":32,"q":"Although the immediate danger had passed, the commander advised his men to KEEP THEIR POWDER DRY.","o":["Avoid unnecessary expenditure","Remain prepared for possible action","Conceal their intentions from the enemy","Refrain from taking any decision"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"KEEP THEIR POWDER DRY","a":1,"e":"Keep your powder dry means stay ready for possible future action."},{"n":33,"q":"The new administrator decided to CUT THE GORDIAN KNOT rather than allow the dispute to continue indefinitely.","o":["Complicate a simple issue","Solve a difficult problem by bold and decisive action","Avoid taking sides in a dispute","Examine every possible consequence before acting"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"CUT THE GORDIAN KNOT","a":1,"e":"Cut the Gordian knot means solve a complicated problem decisively rather than through prolonged deliberation."},{"n":34,"q":"Their success eventually proved to be a PYRRHIC VICTORY.","o":["A victory achieved unexpectedly","A victory obtained through deception","A victory gained at such great cost that it is almost a defeat","A victory that brings lasting benefits"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"PYRRHIC VICTORY","a":2,"e":"A Pyrrhic victory is a success whose cost nearly outweighs its benefit."},{"n":35,"q":"By announcing the reform before his colleague could do so, he STOLE HIS THUNDER.","o":["Publicly criticised him","Took attention or credit that would otherwise have gone to him","Prevented him from making a mistake","Disclosed his confidential plans"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"STOLE HIS THUNDER","a":1,"e":"Steal someone’s thunder means take credit or attention that properly belongs to another person."},{"n":36,"q":"P. however persuasive it may appear\nQ. an argument cannot be accepted\nR. unless its conclusion follows\nS. from premises that can be defended","o":["PRSQ","QPRS","RSPQ","SQRP"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item in this section consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange the parts in the correct sequence to form a meaningful sentence and select the appropriate option.","a":1,"e":"An argument cannot be accepted, however persuasive it may appear, unless its conclusion follows from premises that can be defended."},{"n":37,"q":"P. only after the evidence had been re-examined\nQ. did the investigators discover\nR. that the apparent contradiction\nS. resulted from a clerical error","o":["PQRS","QRSP","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Only after the evidence had been re-examined did the investigators discover that the apparent contradiction resulted from a clerical error."},{"n":38,"q":"P. in situations demanding judgement\nQ. can be more dangerous\nR. confidence unsupported by competence\nS. than hesitation","o":["RQSP","QRPS","PSRQ","SRQP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Confidence unsupported by competence can be more dangerous than hesitation in situations demanding judgement."},{"n":39,"q":"P. but can instead lead\nQ. the habit of questioning assumptions\nR. to more reliable judgement\nS. does not necessarily produce scepticism","o":["QSPR","SQPR","PRQS","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"The habit of questioning assumptions does not necessarily produce scepticism but can instead lead to more reliable judgement."},{"n":40,"q":"P. may prove significant\nQ. when examined in context\nR. what appears at first\nS. to be a minor inconsistency","o":["SRPQ","RSPQ","PQRS","QRSP ."],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"What appears at first to be a minor inconsistency may prove significant when examined in context."},{"n":41,"q":"The central paradox discussed in the passage is that","o":["experts generally know less than novices","greater knowledge may produce greater awareness of uncertainty","uncertainty always prevents effective action","novices are usually more accurate than experts"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow. Select the most appropriate answer on the basis of the passage only.","c":"Expertise is often associated with certainty. We expect the expert to know, and the hesitant answer may therefore seem less authoritative than the confident one. Yet genuine expertise frequently produces a more refined awareness of uncertainty. The novice, unfamiliar with the exceptions to a general rule, may apply it confidently in every situation. The expert, having encountered cases in which the rule fails, is often more cautious. This does not imply that expertise leads to indecision. Rather, it enables a person to distinguish between uncertainty that matters and uncertainty that does not. A skilled physician, engineer or commander may act decisively while remaining conscious that some assumptions could prove mistaken. What distinguishes such judgement from mere confidence is not the absence of doubt but the capacity to act proportionately despite it. The public, however, does not always reward such intellectual restraint. Categorical statements are easier to communicate and more satisfying to hear than qualified ones. Consequently, those who appear most certain may sometimes receive greater confidence than those whose knowledge gives them good reason to qualify what they say.","a":1,"e":"The passage explains that genuine expertise often makes a person more aware of exceptions and limitations."},{"n":42,"q":"According to the author, the novice often appears more confident because the novice","o":["has greater practical experience","is unaware of exceptions to general rules","understands uncertainty more accurately","is less willing to make decisions"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"A novice may appear more confident because he has not yet encountered cases where the general rule fails."},{"n":43,"q":"Genuine expertise enables a person to","o":["remove uncertainty before taking action","avoid making assumptions altogether","judge which uncertainties are significant","replace cautious judgement with instinct"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"Expertise helps distinguish uncertainty that matters from uncertainty that does not prevent responsible action."},{"n":44,"q":"The phrase “intellectual restraint” most nearly refers to","o":["reluctance to express any opinion","deliberate concealment of knowledge","unwillingness to act without complete certainty","caution in making claims stronger than the evidence permits"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":3,"e":"Intellectual restraint means avoiding unjustified certainty."},{"n":45,"q":"Which inference is most strongly supported by the final paragraph?","o":["Public confidence may sometimes favour certainty of expression over depth of knowledge.","Experts should avoid qualifying their conclusions.","Categorical statements are generally more accurate.","The public distrusts all forms of specialised knowledge."],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"The final paragraph shows that categorical confidence can sometimes be more persuasive to the public than carefully qualified expertise."},{"n":46,"q":"Select the correct Passive Voice of: “They will have completed the inspection before sunset.”","o":["The inspection will complete before sunset.","The inspection will have been completed before sunset.","The inspection would have been completed before sunset.","The inspection has been completed before sunset."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Each of the following questions tests an important aspect of English usage. Select the most appropriate answer and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"Future perfect passive follows will have been + past participle."},{"n":47,"q":"Select the correct Indirect Speech of: The officer said to the cadet, “Why did you leave the post without permission?”","o":["The officer asked the cadet why he had left the post without permission.","The officer asked the cadet why did he leave the post without permission.","The officer told the cadet why he had left the post without permission.","The officer asked why had the cadet left the post without permission."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":0,"e":"In indirect wh-questions, statement word order is used, and did leave changes to had left."},{"n":48,"q":"Choose the appropriate Question Tag: “Hardly anyone understood the implications of the order, ______?”","o":["did they","didn’t they","was he","wasn’t it"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":0,"e":"Hardly anyone has a negative sense, so it takes an affirmative question tag: did they?"},{"n":49,"q":"Choose the correct One-Word Substitution: “A statement that appears to contradict itself but may nevertheless contain a truth.”","o":["Paradox","Fallacy","Parable","Euphemism"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":0,"e":"A paradox is an apparently self-contradictory statement that may nevertheless contain an underlying truth."},{"n":50,"q":"Choose the correct meaning of the phrasal verb in the sentence: “The negotiations BROKE DOWN after both sides refused to compromise.”","o":["were postponed temporarily","failed completely","became confidential","were concluded successfully"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":1,"e":"Break down, when used for negotiations, talks or discussions, means fail or come to an unsuccessful end."}]},{"id":"sample-03","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 3","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"RECALCITRANT","o":["Submissive","Obstinately uncooperative","Indifferent","Impulsive"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Each item in this section consists of a word followed by four alternatives. Select the word that is nearest in meaning to the given word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"RECALCITRANT","a":1,"e":"Recalcitrant means stubbornly resistant to authority, discipline or control."},{"n":2,"q":"CIRCUMSPECT","o":["Cautious","Suspicious","Secretive","Hesitant"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"CIRCUMSPECT","a":0,"e":"Circumspect means careful to consider possible consequences before acting."},{"n":3,"q":"TRENCHANT","o":["Superficial","Incisive","Ambiguous","Elaborate"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"TRENCHANT","a":1,"e":"Trenchant means sharp, forceful and perceptive, especially in expression or criticism."},{"n":4,"q":"ATTENUATE","o":["Intensify","Prolong","Weaken","Conceal"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ATTENUATE","a":2,"e":"Attenuate means to reduce the strength, force or intensity of something."},{"n":5,"q":"PROSAIC","o":["Imaginative","Commonplace","Poetic","Profound"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PROSAIC","a":1,"e":"Prosaic means ordinary, unimaginative or lacking excitement."},{"n":6,"q":"DIFFIDENT","o":["Timid","Confident","Reserved","Hesitant"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–10): Each item in this section consists of a word followed by four alternatives. Select the word that is opposite in meaning to the given word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"DIFFIDENT","a":1,"e":"Diffident means lacking self-confidence; confident is its opposite."},{"n":7,"q":"MITIGATE","o":["Alleviate","Moderate","Exacerbate","Restrain"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"MITIGATE","a":2,"e":"Mitigate means make less severe; exacerbate means make worse."},{"n":8,"q":"SPORADIC","o":["Irregular","Scattered","Continuous","Occasional"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"SPORADIC","a":2,"e":"Sporadic means occurring irregularly or occasionally; continuous is the opposite."},{"n":9,"q":"OPAQUE","o":["Obscure","Dense","Transparent","Dull"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"OPAQUE","a":2,"e":"Opaque means not transparent or difficult to see through."},{"n":10,"q":"MUNIFICENT","o":["Generous","Extravagant","Stingy","Affluent"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"MUNIFICENT","a":2,"e":"Munificent means extremely generous; stingy is the opposite."},{"n":11,"q":"The preliminary evidence appeared to support the hypothesis; ______, the researchers declined to draw a definite conclusion until the larger sample had been examined.","o":["therefore","nevertheless","similarly","moreover"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each of the following sentences has a blank. Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete the sentence and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"It introduces a contrast: the evidence seemed supportive, yet the researchers withheld a definite conclusion."},{"n":12,"q":"The bridge had suffered extensive structural damage; ______, all heavy-vehicle movement across it was suspended.","o":["consequently","conversely","nevertheless","meanwhile"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"Suspension of traffic is the result of the structural damage."},{"n":13,"q":"Several apparently minor lapses can compromise operational security. ______, an unattended document may disclose information that appears insignificant in isolation.","o":["On the contrary","For instance","In conclusion","Otherwise"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The unattended document is given as an example of a seemingly minor security lapse."},{"n":14,"q":"The policy does not prohibit criticism; it merely requires criticism to be supported by evidence. ______, disagreement is permitted, but unsupported allegation is not.","o":["In other words","On the other hand","By contrast","Nevertheless"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"The second statement restates and clarifies the meaning of the first."},{"n":15,"q":"Excessive confidence may cause a person to underestimate risk. ______, excessive caution may prevent timely action even when the evidence justifies it.","o":["Consequently","Conversely","For example","Likewise"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It introduces the opposite danger: excessive caution as against excessive confidence."},{"n":16,"q":"The report must be submitted before the stipulated deadline.","o":["Conjunction","Preposition","Adverb","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): Each of the following sentences has an underlined word. Identify the part of speech to which the underlined word belongs and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"Before governs the noun phrase the stipulated deadline."},{"n":17,"q":"However carefully the plan was prepared, some uncertainty remained.","o":["Conjunction","Adjective","Adverb","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"However modifies carefully and means “no matter how carefully”."},{"n":18,"q":"The possibility that the records had been altered could not be ignored.","o":["Relative pronoun","Conjunction","Adverb","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"That introduces the content clause that the records had been altered."},{"n":19,"q":"He little realised how serious the consequences would be.","o":["Adjective","Pronoun","Adverb","Determiner"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Little modifies the verb realised and means “hardly/not much”."},{"n":20,"q":"What the witness omitted was more significant than what he stated.","o":["Interrogative adjective","Relative pronoun","Conjunction","Adverb"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"What means “that which/the thing which” and introduces a fused relative clause."},{"n":21,"q":"He is one of those officers / who believes that discipline / must be accompanied by understanding.","o":["He is one of those officers","who believes that discipline","must be accompanied by understanding.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–30): Each item in this section consists of a sentence divided into four parts marked (a), (b), (c) and (d). Read each sentence carefully and identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error.","a":1,"e":"Who refers to plural officers; therefore, who believe is required."},{"n":22,"q":"No less than fifty cadets / was selected for the final round / after the preliminary screening.","o":["No less than fifty cadets","was selected for the final round","after the preliminary screening.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"The subject fifty cadets is plural; hence, were selected."},{"n":23,"q":"The committee recommended / that every applicant / submits a separate declaration.","o":["The committee recommended","that every applicant","submits a separate declaration.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":2,"e":"After recommended that, the mandative subjunctive is used: submit a separate declaration."},{"n":24,"q":"Hardly had the patrol entered the valley / when visibility deteriorated sharply / because of the sudden snowfall.","o":["Hardly had the patrol entered the valley","when visibility deteriorated sharply","because of the sudden snowfall.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"Hardly...when is the correct correlative construction."},{"n":25,"q":"Neither the revised instructions nor the earlier circulars / provides a satisfactory explanation / for the apparent contradiction.","o":["Neither the revised instructions nor the earlier circulars","provides a satisfactory explanation","for the apparent contradiction.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer plural subject circulars; therefore, provide is required."},{"n":26,"q":"The Principal objected to / the cadets leaving the premises / without prior permission.","o":["The Principal objected to","the cadets leaving the premises","without prior permission.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"Object to is correctly followed by a gerund-participial construction: objected to the cadets leaving..."},{"n":27,"q":"If he would have examined the documents carefully, / he might have detected / the discrepancy much earlier.","o":["If he would have examined the documents carefully,","he might have detected","the discrepancy much earlier.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"In the third conditional, use the past perfect: If he had examined..."},{"n":28,"q":"The information furnished by the witnesses / were insufficient to establish / the sequence of events conclusively.","o":["The information furnished by the witnesses","were insufficient to establish","the sequence of events conclusively.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Information is an uncountable singular noun; hence, was insufficient."},{"n":29,"q":"It is essential / that every member of the team / is present during the briefing.","o":["It is essential","that every member of the team","is present during the briefing.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":2,"e":"After It is essential that, formal English prefers the subjunctive be present."},{"n":30,"q":"The speaker not only questioned the reliability of the evidence / but also the credibility of those / who had collected it.","o":["The speaker not only questioned the reliability of the evidence","but also the credibility of those","who had collected it.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"Not only...but also correctly coordinates the two objects of questioned: the reliability... and the credibility..."},{"n":31,"q":"I would rather you leave the matter alone for the time being.","o":["you had left the matter alone","you left the matter alone","you will leave the matter alone","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): In each of the following sentences, a part has been underlined. Select the alternative that best improves the underlined part. If no improvement is required, select the appropriate option.","a":1,"e":"After would rather + subject, the past tense expresses a present or future preference."},{"n":32,"q":"The security personnel prevented the visitors to enter the restricted area.","o":["from entering the restricted area","for entering the restricted area","to entering the restricted area","from enter the restricted area"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"Correct construction: prevent someone from doing something."},{"n":33,"q":"By the time the rescue team arrived, the stranded climbers already left the camp.","o":["the stranded climbers have already left the camp","the stranded climbers had already left the camp","the stranded climbers were already leaving the camp","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"The departure occurred before another past event; hence, past perfect is required."},{"n":34,"q":"The revised policy is aimed to reduce unnecessary procedural delays.","o":["at reducing unnecessary procedural delays","for reducing unnecessary procedural delays","to reducing unnecessary procedural delays","towards reduce unnecessary procedural delays"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"Correct construction: aim at + gerund."},{"n":35,"q":"Despite of having been warned repeatedly, he continued to disregard the safety instructions.","o":["In spite having been warned repeatedly","Despite having been warned repeatedly","Although of having been warned repeatedly","Despite he had been warned repeatedly"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"Despite is used directly before a noun or gerund; despite of is incorrect."},{"n":36,"q":"P. although the evidence was circumstantial\nQ. the cumulative effect\nR. of several independent facts\nS. was difficult to dismiss","o":["PQRS","QSPR","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item in this section consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange the parts in the correct sequence to form a meaningful and grammatically correct sentence and select the appropriate option.","a":0,"e":"Although the evidence was circumstantial, the cumulative effect of several independent facts was difficult to dismiss."},{"n":37,"q":"P. only when criticism is examined dispassionately\nQ. can it become\nR. a means of improvement\nS. rather than a cause of resentment","o":["QPRS","PQRS","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Only when criticism is examined dispassionately can it become a means of improvement rather than a cause of resentment."},{"n":38,"q":"P. the apparent simplicity of the rule\nQ. conceals the difficulty\nR. of applying it consistently\nS. to exceptional cases","o":["RSPQ","PQRS","QSPR","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"The apparent simplicity of the rule conceals the difficulty of applying it consistently to exceptional cases."},{"n":39,"q":"P. far from diminishing authority\nQ. admitting an error\nR. may strengthen it\nS. by demonstrating intellectual honesty","o":["QPRS","PQRS","QRSP","RQPS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Admitting an error, far from diminishing authority, may strengthen it by demonstrating intellectual honesty."},{"n":40,"q":"P. what makes misinformation dangerous\nQ. is not merely that it is false\nR. but that it often resembles\nS. information we are already inclined to believe","o":["PRSQ","RSPQ","PQRS","QPRS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":2,"e":"What makes misinformation dangerous is not merely that it is false but that it often resembles information we are already inclined to believe."},{"n":41,"q":"The author’s principal criticism of excessively detailed rules is that they may","o":["make all forms of discipline unnecessary","produce compliance without developing judgement","reduce coordination in routine situations","encourage individuals to ignore safety procedures"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow. Select the most appropriate answer on the basis of the passage only and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","c":"Institutions frequently attempt to prevent mistakes by increasing the number of rules governing individual conduct. The assumption is understandable: if an error arose because someone exercised poor judgement, restricting discretion appears to reduce the possibility of recurrence. Yet rules themselves cannot anticipate every circumstance. An organisation governed by excessively detailed instructions may produce people who become skilled at compliance without becoming capable of judgement. This is not an argument against rules. Some procedures must be standardised, particularly where safety or coordination is involved. The difficulty lies in confusing standardisation with the elimination of discretion. A rule can tell a person what normally ought to be done; judgement becomes necessary when circumstances depart from what is normal. Indeed, the more complex the environment, the less realistic it is to imagine that every future situation can be prescribed in advance. A mature institution therefore does two things simultaneously: it establishes clear boundaries and develops people capable of reasoning within them. Rules without judgement encourage mechanical obedience; judgement without rules may produce inconsistency. Effective discipline depends upon maintaining a productive tension between the two.","a":1,"e":"Excessively detailed rules may make people skilled at following instructions without developing independent judgement."},{"n":42,"q":"According to the passage, judgement becomes particularly necessary when","o":["a person disagrees with every rule","procedures are completely absent","circumstances differ from those normally anticipated","an institution wishes to avoid responsibility"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"The passage states that judgement becomes necessary when circumstances depart from what is normal."},{"n":43,"q":"The phrase “productive tension” refers to","o":["conflict between senior and junior officials","a necessary balance between rules and individual judgement","disagreement caused by inconsistent procedures","pressure created by excessive supervision"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"“Productive tension” refers to maintaining both clear rules and appropriate discretion."},{"n":44,"q":"Which of the following would the author most likely support?","o":["Every possible future situation should be covered by a written rule.","Rules should be replaced entirely by individual discretion.","Organisations should combine clear procedures with training in judgement.","Standardisation is harmful wherever individual responsibility is involved."],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"This directly reflects the author’s idea of establishing boundaries while developing reasoning ability."},{"n":45,"q":"The tone of the passage is best described as","o":["Polemical","Analytical","Nostalgic","Cynical"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"The author objectively examines both the value and limitations of rules rather than arguing emotionally."},{"n":46,"q":"The passage challenges the assumption that memory","o":["becomes stronger through repetition","stores and reproduces experiences without alteration","depends upon later information","is essential to everyday life"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow. Select the most appropriate answer on the basis of the passage only and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","c":"Memory is often treated as though it were a passive storehouse into which experiences are placed and from which they can later be retrieved unchanged. Psychological observation suggests a more complicated process. Remembering is partly reconstructive: when we recall an event, we combine fragments of the original experience with expectations, later information and assumptions about what probably occurred. This helps explain why sincere witnesses may give conflicting accounts without deliberately lying. Confidence in a memory is not necessarily evidence of its accuracy. Indeed, repetition can sometimes increase confidence even when the repeated version has gradually departed from the original event. A story told several times may acquire coherence precisely because inconvenient or uncertain details have been unconsciously removed. The practical lesson is not that memory is worthless. Human life would be impossible without it. Rather, recollection should be treated as evidence that may require corroboration, particularly where important decisions depend upon precise details. Skepticism toward memory should therefore be disciplined rather than absolute.","a":1,"e":"The passage rejects the idea of memory as a passive storehouse that returns experiences unchanged."},{"n":47,"q":"Conflicting accounts by sincere witnesses may occur because","o":["witnesses deliberately conceal relevant facts","recollection is partly reconstructive","most witnesses fail to observe events","confidence invariably reduces accuracy"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"Memories combine original experience with expectations, later information and assumptions, so sincere witnesses may disagree."},{"n":48,"q":"According to the passage, repeated telling of an event may","o":["make the account more coherent while reducing its fidelity to the original event","guarantee that all details become more accurate","eliminate the influence of later information","prevent uncertain details from being forgotten"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"Repetition may increase coherence and confidence even as the memory moves away from what actually occurred."},{"n":49,"q":"The author suggests that memory should","o":["never be admitted as evidence","always be trusted when the witness is confident","be corroborated when precise details are important","be regarded as less useful than written records in every case"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"The author recommends supporting recollection with other evidence when important decisions depend on exact details."},{"n":50,"q":"By “disciplined rather than absolute” skepticism, the author means that we should","o":["reject memory only when two witnesses disagree","distrust all recollection equally","recognise the limitations of memory without treating it as useless","accept memory unless deliberate deception can be proved"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"“Disciplined rather than absolute” skepticism means being cautious about memory without rejecting it altogether ."}]},{"id":"sample-04","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 4","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The committee's TRENCHANT observations exposed several weaknesses in the proposed policy.","o":["conciliatory","incisive","incidental","equivocal"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–10): Each item in this section consists of a sentence with a word/phrase in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word that is nearest in meaning to the given word/phrase and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"TRENCHANT","a":1,"e":"Trenchant means sharp, forceful and effective in expression or criticism."},{"n":2,"q":"Despite the adverse circumstances, the commander remained SANGUINE about the success of the operation.","o":["apprehensive","optimistic","indifferent","incredulous"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"SANGUINE","a":1,"e":"Sanguine means hopeful or confident about the future."},{"n":3,"q":"The force of the criticism was considerably ATTENUATED after the revised figures were released.","o":["intensified","concealed","weakened","justified"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ATTENUATED","a":2,"e":"Attenuate means to reduce the force, strength or intensity of something."},{"n":4,"q":"The negotiators found the opposing delegation remarkably INTRANSIGENT on the principal issue.","o":["uncompromising","impartial","negligent","conciliatory"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"INTRANSIGENT","a":0,"e":"Intransigent means unwilling to change one's views or reach a compromise."},{"n":5,"q":"Her explanation was so PELLUCID that even the most complicated aspects of the issue became intelligible.","o":["concise","lucid","elaborate","ingenious"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PELLUCID","a":1,"e":"Pellucid means extremely clear and easy to understand."},{"n":6,"q":"On assuming public office, he decided to ABJURE all commercial interests that might create a conflict.","o":["disclose","renounce","regulate","acquire"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ABJURE","a":1,"e":"Abjure means formally reject, give up or renounce something."},{"n":7,"q":"The discussion became increasingly DESULTORY, moving from one unrelated issue to another.","o":["systematic","aimless","contentious","repetitive"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"DESULTORY","a":1,"e":"Desultory means lacking a definite plan or moving irregularly from one subject to another."},{"n":8,"q":"The regulations imposed an ONEROUS responsibility upon small institutions.","o":["burdensome","honorary","incidental","temporary"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ONEROUS","a":0,"e":"Onerous means involving a heavy burden, responsibility or obligation."},{"n":9,"q":"The article deals with a RECONDITE aspect of constitutional theory unfamiliar to most readers.","o":["controversial","abstruse","obsolete","practical"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"RECONDITE","a":1,"e":"Recondite means difficult to understand and known only to specialists."},{"n":10,"q":"The authorities attempted to PLACATE the agitated residents by promising an independent inquiry.","o":["intimidate","appease","disperse","deceive"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PLACATE","a":1,"e":"Placate means calm or pacify an angry or dissatisfied person."},{"n":11,"q":"His PRODIGAL expenditure eventually exhausted the resources inherited from his family.","o":["reckless","frugal","lavish","extravagant"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–20): Each item in this section consists of a sentence with a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word that is opposite in meaning to the given word and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"PRODIGAL","a":1,"e":"Prodigal means wastefully extravagant; frugal means economical."},{"n":12,"q":"The witness gave an EQUIVOCAL reply when asked whether he had seen the accused.","o":["ambiguous","hesitant","unambiguous","evasive"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"EQUIVOCAL","a":2,"e":"Equivocal means ambiguous or open to more than one interpretation."},{"n":13,"q":"The prolonged exposure had a DELETERIOUS effect on the equipment.","o":["detrimental","beneficial","cumulative","irreversible"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"DELETERIOUS","a":1,"e":"Deleterious means harmful; beneficial means producing a good effect."},{"n":14,"q":"Even repeated persuasion failed to alter his OBDURATE attitude.","o":["stubborn","yielding","hostile","indifferent"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"OBDURATE","a":1,"e":"Obdurate means stubborn or inflexible; yielding is its opposite."},{"n":15,"q":"The popularity of such fashions is often EPHEMERAL.","o":["transitory","enduring","superficial","recurrent"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"EPHEMERAL","a":1,"e":"Ephemeral means lasting for a very short time."},{"n":16,"q":"He remained MOROSE throughout the journey.","o":["taciturn","cheerful","anxious","resentful"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"MOROSE","a":1,"e":"Morose means gloomy, sullen or ill-tempered."},{"n":17,"q":"The chairman was criticised for his PARSIMONIOUS approach to essential expenditure.","o":["economical","lavish","prudent","frugal"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"PARSIMONIOUS","a":1,"e":"Parsimonious means excessively unwilling to spend money; lavish is the opposite."},{"n":18,"q":"The inquiry was criticised for its CURSORY examination of the evidence.","o":["superficial","hurried","thorough","perfunctory"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"CURSORY","a":2,"e":"Cursory means hurried and superficial; thorough means detailed and complete."},{"n":19,"q":"The community continued to VENERATE the scholar long after his death.","o":["emulate","despise","remember","honour"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"VENERATE","a":1,"e":"Venerate means regard with great respect or reverence; despise is the opposite."},{"n":20,"q":"The region had experienced only SPORADIC rainfall during the previous month.","o":["intermittent","continuous","scanty","unpredictable"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"SPORADIC","a":1,"e":"Sporadic means occurring irregularly or intermittently; continuous is the opposite."},{"n":21,"q":"‘Cut to the chase’","o":["To avoid taking responsibility","To come directly to the important point","To terminate a discussion abruptly","To pursue someone relentlessly"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): Given below are some idioms/phrases followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that best expresses the meaning of the idiom/phrase and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"Cut to the chase means avoid unnecessary details and get to the main point."},{"n":22,"q":"‘Hold water’","o":["To remain calm under pressure","To be logically convincing or valid","To conceal important information","To postpone a decision indefinitely"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","a":1,"e":"An argument that holds water is sound and defensible."},{"n":23,"q":"‘Throw down the gauntlet’","o":["To admit one's defeat","To issue a challenge","To refuse to participate","To settle a dispute amicably"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","a":1,"e":"Throw down the gauntlet means openly challenge someone."},{"n":24,"q":"‘Weather the storm’","o":["To anticipate a difficult situation","To survive a period of difficulty successfully","To avoid an unavoidable crisis","To exaggerate a minor problem"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","a":1,"e":"Weather the storm means endure and overcome a difficult situation."},{"n":25,"q":"‘Run with the hare and hunt with the hounds’","o":["To pursue two objectives efficiently","To support both opposing sides for one's own advantage","To change one's strategy unexpectedly","To cooperate with a stronger opponent"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","a":1,"e":"Run with the hare and hunt with the hounds means try to remain on good terms with two opposing sides."},{"n":26,"q":"The patrol returned after sunset.","o":["Conjunction","Preposition","Adverb","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): Each of the following sentences has an underlined word/phrase. Identify the word class to which the underlined word/phrase belongs and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"After is followed by the noun sunset and functions as a preposition."},{"n":27,"q":"Swimming in turbulent water requires considerable stamina.","o":["Participle","Gerund","Adjective","Adverb"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Swimming functions as the subject of the sentence; therefore, it is a gerund."},{"n":28,"q":"Despite the difficult terrain, the convoy moved steadily throughout the night.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Noun","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Steadily modifies the verb moved."},{"n":29,"q":"The flight was cancelled because of dense fog.","o":["Conjunctival phrase","Noun phrase","Prepositional phrase","Adjectival phrase"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Because of functions as a compound preposition; hence, because of dense fog is a prepositional phrase."},{"n":30,"q":"The officer asked us to follow these instructions meticulously.","o":["Pronoun","Adverb","Determiner","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"These occurs before and determines the noun instructions."},{"n":31,"q":"The committee will not endorse the proposal","o":["unless it is supported by a realistic financial estimate","until it will be supported by a realistic financial estimate","if it would not be supported by a realistic financial estimate","unless it would be supported by a realistic financial estimate"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each item in this section consists of an incomplete sentence followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that best completes the sentence and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":0,"e":"Unless means “if not”; no additional would is required in the conditional clause."},{"n":32,"q":"Had the warning reached us earlier,","o":["we will evacuate the settlement","we could have evacuated the settlement in time","we had evacuated the settlement in time","we would evacuate the settlement yesterday"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"Had the warning reached us earlier is an inverted third conditional requiring could/would have + past participle."},{"n":33,"q":"So intricate was the mechanism","o":["because few technicians understood it","than few technicians could repair it","that few technicians could repair it without assistance","therefore few technicians had repaired it"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"The correct correlative structure is So + adjective + that..."},{"n":34,"q":"It is imperative","o":["that every candidate is present at the briefing","that every candidate be present at the briefing","for every candidate being present at the briefing","that every candidate will be present at the briefing"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"After It is imperative that, the mandative subjunctive be is appropriate."},{"n":35,"q":"He behaves as though","o":["he owns the entire establishment","he would own the entire establishment","he owned the entire establishment","he has owning the entire establishment"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"As though followed by the past tense expresses an unreal or contrary-to-fact situation."},{"n":36,"q":"","o":["No sooner did he entered the room than everyone fell silent.","No sooner had he entered the room than everyone fell silent.","No sooner he had entered the room than everyone fell silent.","No sooner had he entered the room when everyone fell silent."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item in this section consists of four sentences. Select the grammatically correct sentence and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"— Correct structure: No sooner had + subject + past participle + than..."},{"n":37,"q":"","o":["Each of the participants have been given a separate identification number.","Each of the participants has been given a separate identification number.","Each of the participants were given their separate identification number.","Each of the participant has been given a separate identification number."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":1,"e":"— Each is singular and therefore takes has been given."},{"n":38,"q":"","o":["He is senior than all the other officers in the section.","He is senior to all the other officers in the section.","He is more senior to all other officers in the section.","He is senior from all the other officers in the section."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":1,"e":"— The adjective senior is followed by to, not than."},{"n":39,"q":"","o":["I wish I knew about the cancellation yesterday.","I wish I would know about the cancellation yesterday.","I wish I had known about the cancellation yesterday.","I wish I have known about the cancellation yesterday."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"— A regret about a past event requires wish + past perfect: I wish I had known..."},{"n":40,"q":"","o":["The instructor, along with his assistants, were present throughout the exercise.","The instructor, along with his assistants, was present throughout the exercise.","The instructor, along with his assistants, have been present throughout the exercise.","The instructor, along with his assistants, are present throughout the exercise yesterday."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":1,"e":"— The main subject is singular instructor; along with his assistants does not affect subject–verb agreement, so was is correct."},{"n":41,"q":"The number of applicants / have increased considerably / during the last three years","o":["The number of applicants","have increased considerably","during the last three years","despite stricter eligibility conditions."],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Each item in this section consists of a sentence divided into four parts marked (a), (b), (c) and (d). Read each sentence carefully and identify the part containing an error.","a":1,"e":"The subject is The number, which is singular; it should be has increased considerably."},{"n":42,"q":"She is one of the few candidates / who has completed / the demanding course","o":["She is one of the few candidates","who has completed","the demanding course","without seeking additional assistance."],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Who refers to plural candidates; therefore, who have completed is required."},{"n":43,"q":"Neither the revised order / nor the instructions issued earlier / provides any satisfactory guidance","o":["Neither the revised order","nor the instructions issued earlier","provides any satisfactory guidance","on the disputed matter."],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":2,"e":"With neither...nor, the verb agrees with the nearer subject instructions; hence, provide."},{"n":44,"q":"He insisted / to accompany the reconnaissance party / despite repeated warnings","o":["He insisted","to accompany the reconnaissance party","despite repeated warnings","about the hazardous route."],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Insist is not followed by a to-infinitive in this construction. Correct: insisted on accompanying or insisted that he accompany."},{"n":45,"q":"The equipment supplied to these units / are not suitable / for prolonged operation","o":["The equipment supplied to these units","are not suitable","for prolonged operation","under extreme climatic conditions."],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Equipment is an uncountable singular noun; therefore, is not suitable is correct."},{"n":46,"q":"Read the following pairs: Active Voice → Passive Voice I. They made the witness withdraw his earlier statement. → The witness was made to withdraw his earlier statement. II. Someone has stolen the confidential file. → The confidential file has stolen.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–47): Read the following pairs carefully. Identify the pair(s) wherein the transformation has been correctly stated and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":0,"e":"Statement I is correct: after make in the passive, to + infinitive is used: was made to withdraw. Statement II should be The confidential file has been stolen."},{"n":47,"q":"Read the following pairs: Direct Speech → Indirect Speech I. She said, “I have completed the assignment.” → She said that she had completed the assignment. II. The technician said to me, “Do not touch the switch.” → The technician warned me not to touch the switch.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"Statement I correctly changes the present perfect to past perfect. Statement II correctly reports a negative imperative as warned me not to touch."},{"n":48,"q":"Read the following Idioms/Phrases – Meanings: I. Draw a blank — To arrive at a definite conclusion after careful thought. II. Hold your horses — To wait and be patient before acting. III. Leave no stone unturned — To make every possible effort to achieve something.","o":["I only","I and II only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 48–49): Read the following pairs carefully. Select the option that identifies the correctly matched pairs and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":2,"e":"Hold your horses means wait or be patient, and leave no stone unturned means make every possible effort. Draw a blank means fail to obtain an answer or remember something."},{"n":49,"q":"Read the following One-Word Substitutions: I. One who studies or interprets inscriptions — Epigraphist II. A person who walks while asleep — Somnambulist III. Government by the wealthy — Plutocracy","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":3,"e":"An epigraphist studies inscriptions; a somnambulist walks in sleep; plutocracy is government by the wealthy."},{"n":50,"q":"I. Of the two proposals, this is X (better/the better). II. Hardly Y (some/any) of the information supplied by the witness was relevant.","o":["I—better, II—some","I—the better, II—some","I—better, II—any","I—the better, II—any"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Direction (Q. No. 50): Fill in the blanks (X) and (Y) with the appropriate word/phrase given in brackets and select the correct option.","a":3,"e":"When comparing two, the standard expression is the better. After the negative-sense word hardly, any is appropriate."}]},{"id":"sample-05","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 5","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"Each of the recommendations have been examined carefully / by the committee / before inclusion in the final report.","o":["Each of the recommendations have been examined carefully","by the committee","before inclusion in the final report.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Each item in this section consists of a sentence divided into four parts marked (a), (b), (c) and (d). Read each sentence carefully and identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error.","a":0,"e":"— Each is singular; therefore, have been examined should be has been examined."},{"n":2,"q":"The commander, along with two staff officers, / was present at the briefing / when the revised orders were issued.","o":["The commander, along with two staff officers,","was present at the briefing","when the revised orders were issued.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"The subject is singular commander. The phrase along with two staff officers does not affect the number of the verb; was present is correct."},{"n":3,"q":"If the warning had reached us earlier, / we could have prevented / the accident from to occur.","o":["If the warning had reached us earlier,","we could have prevented","the accident from to occur.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":2,"e":"— After prevent...from, use a gerund: prevented the accident from occurring, not from to occur."},{"n":4,"q":"No sooner had the aircraft taken off / when the pilot noticed / an abnormal vibration.","o":["No sooner had the aircraft taken off","when the pilot noticed","an abnormal vibration.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— The correct correlative construction is No sooner...than, not No sooner...when."},{"n":5,"q":"One of the senior officers were asked / to verify the information / before it was circulated.","o":["One of the senior officers were asked","to verify the information","before it was circulated.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"— The subject is One, which is singular; therefore, was asked is required."},{"n":6,"q":"The Captain said to Rohan, “Why did you leave the post without informing me?”","o":["The Captain asked Rohan why he had left the post without informing him.","The Captain asked Rohan why did he leave the post without informing him.","The Captain told Rohan why he had left the post without informing him.","The Captain asked Rohan why had he left the post without informing him."],"cat":"Narration","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–8): Each item consists of a sentence in Direct or Indirect Speech followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that correctly transforms the sentence and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":0,"e":"— In an indirect wh-question, statement word order is used: why he had left. The past simple did leave backshifts to past perfect."},{"n":7,"q":"The doctor said to the patient, “What an extraordinary recovery you have made!”","o":["The doctor told the patient that he had made an extraordinary recovery.","The doctor exclaimed with sorrow that the patient had made an extraordinary recovery.","The doctor exclaimed with admiration that the patient had made an extraordinary recovery.","The doctor asked the patient whether he had made an extraordinary recovery."],"cat":"Narration","a":2,"e":"— The exclamatory sentence expresses admiration; hence, exclaimed with admiration is appropriate."},{"n":8,"q":"The officer ordered the sentry not to allow anyone to enter until he returned.","o":["The officer said to the sentry, “You will not allow anybody to enter until he returns.”","The officer said to the sentry, “Do not allow anyone to enter until I return.”","The officer told the sentry, “Nobody must enter until you return.”","The officer said, “The sentry must not allow anyone to enter until I returned.”"],"cat":"Narration","a":1,"e":"— The negative command not to allow becomes “Do not allow...”, and he returned changes back to I return in Direct Speech."},{"n":9,"q":"","o":["He is one of those officers who never allows personal prejudice to affect his judgement.","He is one of those officers who never allow personal prejudice to affect their judgement.","He is one of those officer who never allow personal prejudice to affect their judgement.","He is among one of those officers who never allows personal prejudice to affect his judgement."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 9–10): Each item consists of four sentences. Select the grammatically correct sentence and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"— In one of those officers who..., who refers to plural officers; hence, who never allow...their judgement."},{"n":10,"q":"","o":["The committee recommended the proposal to be reconsidered immediately.","The committee recommended that the proposal be reconsidered immediately.","The committee recommended that the proposal is reconsidered immediately.","The committee recommended that the proposal will be reconsidered immediately."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":1,"e":"— After recommend that, the mandative subjunctive is appropriate: that the proposal be reconsidered."},{"n":11,"q":"Complement and Compliment","o":["Complement = something that completes or enhances; Compliment = an expression of praise","Complement = praise; Compliment = something that completes","Complement = agreement; Compliment = criticism","Complement = addition to salary; Compliment = formal consent"],"cat":"Confusing Words","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each item consists of a pair of commonly confused words followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that correctly states the meanings of both words.","a":0,"e":"— Complement = something that completes/enhances; compliment = an expression of praise."},{"n":12,"q":"Stationary and Stationery","o":["Stationary = writing materials; Stationery = motionless","Stationary = fixed or not moving; Stationery = writing materials","Stationary = temporary; Stationery = permanent","Stationary = official residence; Stationery = fixed equipment"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":1,"e":"— Stationary = not moving; stationery = writing materials."},{"n":13,"q":"Discreet and Discrete","o":["Discreet = separate and distinct; Discrete = tactful","Discreet = doubtful; Discrete = definite","Discreet = tactful or careful; Discrete = separate and distinct","Discreet = secret; Discrete = continuous"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":2,"e":"— Discreet = tactful/careful; discrete = separate and distinct."},{"n":14,"q":"Eminent and Imminent","o":["Eminent = likely to happen soon; Imminent = distinguished","Eminent = distinguished; Imminent = about to happen","Eminent = permanent; Imminent = temporary","Eminent = obvious; Imminent = uncertain"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":1,"e":"— Eminent = distinguished; imminent = about to happen."},{"n":15,"q":"Allude and Elude","o":["Allude = escape; Elude = refer indirectly","Allude = mention directly; Elude = admit","Allude = refer indirectly; Elude = escape from or avoid","Allude = explain; Elude = conceal"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":2,"e":"— Allude = refer indirectly; elude = escape from or avoid."},{"n":16,"q":"His TERSE response indicated that he had no desire to prolong the discussion.","o":["evasive","brief","hostile","elaborate"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): In each of the following sentences, a word has been given in capital letters. Select the alternative nearest in meaning to the word as used in the sentence.","t":"TERSE","a":1,"e":"Terse means brief and concise, often to the point of abruptness."},{"n":17,"q":"The new procedure may OBVIATE the need for repeated physical verification.","o":["postpone","remove","emphasise","conceal"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"OBVIATE","a":1,"e":"Obviate means remove or prevent the need for something."},{"n":18,"q":"Digital devices have become almost UBIQUITOUS in contemporary workplaces.","o":["expensive","indispensable","omnipresent","sophisticated"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"UBIQUITOUS","a":2,"e":"Ubiquitous means present or found everywhere."},{"n":19,"q":"The examiner was unusually FASTIDIOUS about the accuracy of references and citations.","o":["meticulous","indifferent","hesitant","lenient"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"FASTIDIOUS","a":0,"e":"Fastidious means very attentive to detail and accuracy."},{"n":20,"q":"The announcement did little to ASSUAGE public anxiety.","o":["intensify","alleviate","justify","conceal"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ASSUAGE","a":1,"e":"Assuage means make an unpleasant feeling less intense."},{"n":21,"q":"S1: A claim may sound convincing long before it has been properly examined.\nP: The first task is to identify the assumptions on which the argument rests.\nQ: These assumptions must then be tested against the available evidence.\nR: The evidence itself must also be examined for relevance and reliability.\nS: Only after these stages should the conclusion be accepted or rejected.\nS6: Critical thinking therefore involves a procedure, not merely an attitude of doubt.","o":["QPRS","PSRQ","PQRS","RQSP"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): In each item, S1 and S6 are fixed. The four sentences marked P, Q, R and S are to be arranged between S1 and S6 to form a meaningful and coherent paragraph. Select the correct sequence.","a":2,"e":"First identify assumptions, then test them, examine the evidence, and finally accept or reject the conclusion."},{"n":22,"q":"S1: Organisations often repeat mistakes not because no lessons were learned, but because those lessons were not preserved.\nP: Written records can retain part of this knowledge, especially when they explain why decisions were made.\nQ: Much practical knowledge resides in experienced individuals rather than in manuals.\nR: When such individuals leave, that knowledge may disappear with them.\nS: Serious institutions therefore record both outcomes and the reasoning behind important decisions.\nS6: Institutional memory is strongest when experience is deliberately converted into knowledge that others can use.","o":["QRPS","PQRS","RQSP","QPSR"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Practical knowledge resides in people; it may disappear when they leave; written records preserve it; hence institutions should systematically record it."},{"n":23,"q":"S1: The condition of a river cannot be judged merely by looking at the clarity of its water.\nP: Some pollutants may be colourless, odourless and invisible to casual observation.\nQ: For this reason, scientists also study the organisms living in and around the water.\nR: Changes in these populations may reveal forms of environmental stress that chemical tests alone can miss.\nS: Reliable assessment therefore combines several kinds of evidence.\nS6: Environmental monitoring is consequently a process of interpretation rather than simple observation.","o":["RQPS","PQRS","QSPR","PRSQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Invisible pollutants establish the problem; organisms provide additional evidence; their changes reveal stress; therefore several kinds of evidence must be combined."},{"n":24,"q":"S1: In an emergency, speed is important, but speed without clear priorities can create further confusion.\nP: Yet simply decentralising decisions does not solve the problem.\nQ: Only then can authority be delegated without sacrificing coherence.\nR: Referring every minor decision to a senior authority wastes valuable time.\nS: Subordinates must first understand the commander's priorities and be trained to exercise judgement.\nS6: Effective emergency leadership therefore combines clear intent with prepared autonomy.","o":["RPSQ","SPRQ","QRPS","PRSQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Referring every minor decision upward wastes time; mere decentralisation is insufficient; subordinates need preparation; only then can authority be delegated effectively."},{"n":25,"q":"S1: A statistic can be perfectly accurate and still create a misleading impression.\nP: Its significance depends upon such factors as the denominator, the time period and the basis of comparison.\nQ: A large percentage increase may sound dramatic when the original number was extremely small.\nR: Conversely, a large absolute number may represent only a tiny proportion of a population.\nS: Responsible interpretation therefore requires attention to context as well as arithmetic.\nS6: Numerical literacy is thus partly the ability to ask what a number actually represents.","o":["QPRS","PQRS","PRSQ","RQSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"The significance of statistics depends on context, followed by two contrasting examples, and then the conclusion about responsible interpretation."},{"n":26,"q":"______ the financial implications are concerned, a separate assessment will be submitted next week.","o":["As far as","On account of","In contrast to","By means of"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–35): Each item contains a blank. Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete the sentence.","a":0,"e":"Correct expression: As far as...are concerned."},{"n":27,"q":"______, I met your former instructor at the seminar yesterday.","o":["Consequently","Incidentally","Nevertheless","Accordingly"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It introduces an additional, somewhat unrelated piece of information."},{"n":28,"q":"______ discipline, the academy places equal emphasis on intellectual independence.","o":["In spite of","As a consequence of","Speaking of","In accordance with"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":2,"e":"It introduces the topic of discipline."},{"n":29,"q":"The evidence against the proposal is substantial; ______, its supporters continue to defend it vigorously.","o":["therefore","nevertheless","similarly","henceforth"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It expresses contrast: strong evidence exists, yet supporters continue defending the proposal."},{"n":30,"q":"The inspection revealed serious structural defects; ______, the building was declared unsafe.","o":["conversely","consequently","meanwhile","otherwise"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"Declaring the building unsafe is the consequence of discovering structural defects."},{"n":31,"q":"The policy does not abolish discretion; it defines the limits within which discretion may operate. ______, judgement remains necessary, but it is not unlimited.","o":["For instance","In other words","On the contrary","By comparison"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The second statement restates and clarifies the first."},{"n":32,"q":"______, the proposal involves substantial initial expenditure; however, its long-term savings may justify the cost.","o":["Admittedly","Subsequently","Conversely","Likewise"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"It concedes the disadvantage of high initial expenditure before presenting the counterargument."},{"n":33,"q":"______, a rule that admits too many exceptions soon ceases to function effectively as a rule.","o":["Generally speaking","By the way","Meanwhile","In particular to"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"It introduces a broad general observation."},{"n":34,"q":"The first unit concentrated on speed and mobility. ______, the second was organised primarily for endurance and defensive strength.","o":["Therefore","By contrast","For example","Accordingly"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It contrasts the organisation and purpose of the two units."},{"n":35,"q":"The assumptions underlying the original estimate were incorrect; ______, the conclusion derived from them must be reconsidered.","o":["nevertheless","thus","otherwise","similarly"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The second clause follows logically from the incorrect assumptions."},{"n":36,"q":"CAVEAT","o":["An official commendation","A warning or qualification","A binding agreement","A public accusation"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item consists of a word followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning.","a":1,"e":"A caveat is a warning, caution or condition attached to something."},{"n":37,"q":"ANACHRONISM","o":["A prediction that proves inaccurate","An event recurring at fixed intervals","Something belonging to a period other than the one in which it appears","A record arranged according to date"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"— An anachronism is something placed in or associated with a historical period to which it does not belong."},{"n":38,"q":"EQUANIMITY","o":["Mental calmness and composure","Excessive enthusiasm","Moral superiority","Intellectual uncertainty"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":0,"e":"Equanimity means calmness, especially in difficult circumstances."},{"n":39,"q":"PAUCITY","o":["Excess","Irregularity","Scarcity","Complexity"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"Paucity means a small or insufficient quantity."},{"n":40,"q":"OBFUSCATE","o":["To make something clearer","To make something obscure or confusing","To reject something formally","To examine something closely"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":1,"e":"Obfuscate means deliberately or unintentionally make something difficult to understand."},{"n":41,"q":"P. can be revised without loss of credibility\nQ. a conclusion based on evidence\nR. when stronger evidence\nS. becomes available","o":["PRQS","QPRS","RSPQ","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Each item consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange them in the correct sequence to form a meaningful and grammatically correct sentence.","a":1,"e":"A conclusion based on evidence can be revised without loss of credibility when stronger evidence becomes available."},{"n":42,"q":"P. unless they are translated\nQ. into habits of conduct\nR. principles remain merely abstract\nS. through repeated practice","o":["RPQS","RQPS","PRSQ","QSPR"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Principles remain merely abstract unless they are translated into habits of conduct through repeated practice."},{"n":43,"q":"P. what initially appears to be\nQ. a disagreement about facts\nR. may actually arise from\nS. different definitions of the same term","o":["RSPQ","PQRS","QRSP","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"What initially appears to be a disagreement about facts may actually arise from different definitions of the same term."},{"n":44,"q":"P. by the speed of its response\nQ. the efficiency of an institution\nR. but also by the fairness of its decisions\nS. should not be judged solely","o":["QSPR","SQPR","PRQS","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"The efficiency of an institution should not be judged solely by the speed of its response but also by the fairness of its decisions."},{"n":45,"q":"P. when uncertainty cannot be eliminated\nQ. good judgement consists\nR. not in pretending certainty\nS. but in acting proportionately","o":["RSPQ","QRSP","QSPR","PQRS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Good judgement consists not in pretending certainty but in acting proportionately when uncertainty cannot be eliminated."},{"n":46,"q":"The committee took exception ______ the insinuation that its findings had been politically influenced.","o":["against","to","for","upon"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate alternative to complete the sentence.","a":1,"e":"The standard expression is take exception to something."},{"n":47,"q":"His explanation does not accord ______ the facts established during the inquiry.","o":["to","from","with","upon"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"Correct expression: accord with = agree or correspond with."},{"n":48,"q":"Neither the revised schedule nor the earlier instructions ______ sufficiently clear to avoid confusion.","o":["is","has been","are","was"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"With neither...nor, the verb generally agrees with the nearer subject. Instructions is plural; hence are."},{"n":49,"q":"Lest any misunderstanding ______, the chairperson restated the proposal in precise terms.","o":["arises","arose","arise","would arise"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"After lest, formal English uses the base/subjunctive form: lest any misunderstanding arise."},{"n":50,"q":"Not until the audit had been completed ______ the full extent of the discrepancy.","o":["they discovered","had they discovered","they had discovered","did they discover"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":3,"e":"When Not until begins a sentence, subject–auxiliary inversion is required in the main clause: did they discover."}]},{"id":"sample-06","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 6","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The revised guidelines aim not only to simplify the procedure / but also reducing unnecessary correspondence / between different departments.","o":["The revised guidelines aim not only to simplify the procedure","but also reducing unnecessary correspondence","between different departments.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Each item in this section consists of a sentence divided into four parts marked (a), (b), (c) and (d). Identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error.","a":1,"e":"— Parallel structure is required: not only to simplify... but also to reduce..., not but also reducing."},{"n":2,"q":"He has been responsible for coordinating the exercise / since nearly four years / without any major interruption.","o":["He has been responsible for coordinating the exercise","since nearly four years","without any major interruption.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— A period of time takes for, not since: for nearly four years."},{"n":3,"q":"Every piece of evidence submitted by the prosecution / was examined carefully / before the court reached its conclusion.","o":["Every piece of evidence submitted by the prosecution","was examined carefully","before the court reached its conclusion.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"Every piece of evidence is singular, so was examined is correct."},{"n":4,"q":"I would rather the matter / is discussed after the inspection / than during the inspection itself.","o":["I would rather the matter","is discussed after the inspection","than during the inspection itself.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— After would rather + subject, the past/subjunctive form is used: would rather the matter were discussed."},{"n":5,"q":"Not only the equipment but also the spare parts / was damaged during transit / because of improper packing.","o":["Not only the equipment but also the spare parts","was damaged during transit","because of improper packing.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— With not only...but also, the verb agrees with the nearer subject spare parts; hence were damaged."},{"n":6,"q":"The instructor said to Ravi, “Please check the figures again before you submit the report.”","o":["The instructor requested Ravi to check the figures again before submitting the report.","The instructor told Ravi that please check the figures again before submitting the report.","The instructor asked Ravi that he should checked the figures again.","The instructor requested that Ravi had checked the figures before submission."],"cat":"Narration","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–8): Each item consists of a sentence in Direct or Indirect Speech followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that correctly changes the form of narration.","a":0,"e":"— A polite request with please is correctly reported as requested Ravi to check..."},{"n":7,"q":"The old soldier said, “Alas! I have lost all my comrades.”","o":["The old soldier exclaimed with joy that he had lost all his comrades.","The old soldier exclaimed with sorrow that he had lost all his comrades.","The old soldier said sadly that I have lost all my comrades.","The old soldier regretted that all his comrades will be lost."],"cat":"Narration","a":1,"e":"— Alas! expresses sorrow; therefore, exclaimed with sorrow is appropriate."},{"n":8,"q":"The commander asked the pilot whether he could complete the sortie that day.","o":["The commander said to the pilot, “Could you complete the sortie that day?”","The commander said to the pilot, “Can you complete the sortie today?”","The commander told the pilot, “Will he complete the sortie today?”","The commander said, “Could the pilot complete the sortie yesterday?”"],"cat":"Narration","a":1,"e":"— Whether he could...that day changes back to “Can you...today?” in Direct Speech."},{"n":9,"q":"","o":["Rarely I have seen such careful preparation for an exercise.","Rarely have I seen such careful preparation for an exercise.","Rarely I had seen such careful preparation for an exercise.","Rarely did I have saw such careful preparation for an exercise."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 9–10): Each item consists of four sentences. Select the grammatically correct sentence.","a":1,"e":"— A negative/restrictive adverb such as Rarely at the beginning requires inversion: Rarely have I seen..."},{"n":10,"q":"","o":["Neither of the proposals appear financially viable.","Neither of the proposals are appearing financially viable.","Neither of the proposals appears financially viable.","Neither of the proposal appears financially viable."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"— Neither is singular; hence Neither of the proposals appears..."},{"n":11,"q":"Elicit and Illicit","o":["Elicit = unlawful; Illicit = obtain a response","Elicit = draw out a response; Illicit = unlawful","Elicit = conceal; Illicit = reveal","Elicit = permit; Illicit = prohibit"],"cat":"Confusing Words","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each item consists of a pair of commonly confused words followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that correctly states the meanings of both words.","a":1,"e":"— Elicit = draw out a response; illicit = unlawful."},{"n":12,"q":"Affect and Effect","o":["Affect = usually influence; Effect = result or consequence","Affect = result; Effect = influence only","Affect = approve; Effect = reject","Affect = imitate; Effect = alter"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":0,"e":"— Affect usually means influence; effect as a noun means result or consequence."},{"n":13,"q":"Judicial and Judicious","o":["Judicial = wise; Judicious = connected with courts","Judicial = connected with courts; Judicious = showing sound judgement","Judicial = legal adviser; Judicious = legal decision","Judicial = impartial; Judicious = compulsory"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":1,"e":"— Judicial relates to courts; judicious means showing sound judgement."},{"n":14,"q":"Farther and Further","o":["Farther = additional; Further = physical distance only","Farther = physical distance; Further = additional or more extensive","Farther = earlier; Further = later","Farther and Further always have identical meanings"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":1,"e":"— Farther commonly refers to physical distance; further means additional or more extensive."},{"n":15,"q":"Waive and Wave","o":["Waive = move to and fro; Wave = give up a right","Waive = postpone; Wave = refuse","Waive = voluntarily give up a right; Wave = move the hand or something to and fro","Waive = demand; Wave = surrender formally"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":2,"e":"— Waive = voluntarily give up a right; wave = move to and fro."},{"n":16,"q":"The motives behind his sudden decision remained INSCRUTABLE even to his closest colleagues.","o":["obvious","difficult to understand","admirable","inconsistent"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): In each sentence, a word has been given in capital letters. Select the alternative nearest in meaning to the word as used in the sentence.","t":"INSCRUTABLE","a":1,"e":"Inscrutable means mysterious or impossible to understand fully."},{"n":17,"q":"Her warning proved remarkably PRESCIENT when the predicted shortage occurred a month later.","o":["showing foresight","excessively cautious","deliberately vague","entirely accidental"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PRESCIENT","a":0,"e":"Prescient means having knowledge or awareness of events before they occur."},{"n":18,"q":"Timely maintenance may PRECLUDE the need for expensive repairs later.","o":["postpone","prevent","increase","conceal"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PRECLUDE","a":1,"e":"Preclude means prevent something from happening or make it impossible."},{"n":19,"q":"His PITHY remarks summarised the entire issue in barely two sentences.","o":["concise and forceful","confusing and lengthy","humorous","hesitant"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PITHY","a":0,"e":"Pithy means brief but meaningful and effective."},{"n":20,"q":"The evidence connecting the two incidents remained TENUOUS.","o":["conclusive","weak","elaborate","concealed"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"TENUOUS","a":1,"e":"Tenuous means very weak, slight or insubstantial."},{"n":21,"q":"S1: A contingency plan is useful only if people understand when and how it should be activated.\nP: A plan that is never rehearsed may exist only on paper.\nQ: Exercises reveal ambiguities that routine reading may not expose.\nR: They also show whether responsibilities have been assigned clearly.\nS: Revisions can then be made before an actual emergency occurs.\nS6: Preparedness therefore depends as much on testing plans as on writing them.","o":["QRPS","PRSQ","PQRS","RQSP"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): In each item, S1 and S6 are fixed. Arrange P, Q, R and S between them to form a coherent passage.","a":2,"e":"A plan must first be rehearsed; exercises then reveal ambiguities and responsibilities, after which revisions can be made."},{"n":22,"q":"S1: Good feedback must do more than merely point out that something is wrong.\nP: A practical suggestion can then show how the weakness may be corrected.\nQ: It should first identify the specific feature that needs improvement.\nR: The recipient must also understand why that feature matters.\nS: Such feedback is more likely to guide future performance.\nS6: Effective criticism is therefore diagnostic as well as corrective.","o":["QRPS","PQRS","RQSP","QSPR"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Feedback first identifies the weakness, explains why it matters, suggests correction, and thus guides future performance."},{"n":23,"q":"S1: A rule may remain unchanged while the situation around it changes considerably.\nP: As a result, a procedure once sensible may become inefficient.\nQ: Review does not imply that every established rule should be discarded.\nR: Technological or social developments can alter the conditions in which the rule operates.\nS: Periodic review is therefore necessary even when no obvious failure has occurred.\nS6: It simply ensures that existing rules continue to serve their intended purpose.","o":["RPSQ","PRSQ","QSPR","SRPQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Changed conditions may make a procedure inefficient; therefore review is needed, though review does not mean discarding every rule."},{"n":24,"q":"S1: People often confuse speed of response with quality of response.\nP: In other situations, however, haste may conceal inadequate analysis.\nQ: A quick answer can be useful when delay itself is costly.\nR: The crucial issue is therefore not speed alone but its suitability to the circumstances.\nS: Good judgement requires knowing when to act immediately and when to investigate further.\nS6: Timeliness and thoughtfulness are therefore not necessarily opposites.","o":["PQRS","QPRS","RQPS","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Quick action may sometimes help, but haste can also harm; therefore suitability to circumstances is crucial."},{"n":25,"q":"S1: Averages can conceal important differences within a group.\nP: Two groups may have the same average while differing greatly in distribution.\nQ: Looking only at the average would make these differences invisible.\nR: One may be tightly clustered around the mean, whereas the other may contain extreme values.\nS: Measures of spread therefore provide essential additional information.\nS6: Sound interpretation requires more than a single summary figure.","o":["PRQS","PQRS","RPSQ","QPRS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Groups may share an average but differ in distribution; the example explains this, showing why measures of spread are necessary."},{"n":26,"q":"‘Read the riot act’","o":["To read a legal document aloud","To reprimand someone severely","To encourage open protest","To cancel a punishment"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): Select the alternative that best expresses the meaning of the idiom/phrase.","a":1,"e":"Read the riot act means give someone a severe warning or scolding."},{"n":27,"q":"‘Keep one’s cards close to one’s chest’","o":["To conceal one’s plans or intentions","To behave courageously","To avoid financial risk","To disclose confidential information"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","a":0,"e":"Keep one’s cards close to one’s chest means avoid revealing one’s intentions."},{"n":28,"q":"‘Take the wind out of someone’s sails’","o":["To make someone more determined","To reduce someone’s confidence or advantage","To prevent someone from travelling","To praise someone unexpectedly"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","a":1,"e":"Take the wind out of someone’s sails means deflate confidence or weaken someone’s position."},{"n":29,"q":"‘Throw a spanner in the works’","o":["To repair a difficult situation","To introduce something that disrupts a plan","To work with exceptional efficiency","To reveal a secret arrangement"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","a":1,"e":"Throw a spanner in the works means cause an unexpected obstruction."},{"n":30,"q":"‘Bite the bullet’","o":["To face a difficult situation with courage","To react without thinking","To avoid an unpleasant responsibility","To accept defeat immediately"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","a":0,"e":"Bite the bullet means endure or confront something unpleasant bravely."},{"n":31,"q":"Wait until the signal is given before moving forward.","o":["Preposition","Conjunction","Adverb","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each sentence contains an underlined word. Identify the part of speech of the underlined word as used in the sentence.","a":1,"e":"Until introduces the clause the signal is given."},{"n":32,"q":"The confidential papers were kept inside the cabinet.","o":["Conjunction","Adverb","Preposition","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Inside governs the noun phrase the cabinet."},{"n":33,"q":"The explanation was convincing enough to satisfy the inquiry team.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Pronoun","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Enough modifies the adjective convincing."},{"n":34,"q":"Some were unwilling to comment until the investigation was complete.","o":["Pronoun","Adjective","Adverb","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":0,"e":"Some stands independently for some people/members."},{"n":35,"q":"Even the most experienced members were surprised by the result.","o":["Adjective","Preposition","Adverb","Pronoun"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Even modifies and emphasises the most experienced members."},{"n":36,"q":"The manager suggested that the schedule was revised immediately.","o":["that the schedule be revised immediately","that the schedule is revised immediately","the schedule to revise immediately","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): In each sentence, a part has been underlined. Select the alternative that best improves the underlined portion.","a":0,"e":"— After suggested that, the mandative subjunctive is used: that the schedule be revised."},{"n":37,"q":"Scarcely had the signal been received than the convoy began to move.","o":["when the convoy began to move","then the convoy began to move","that the convoy began to move","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"— The correct correlative is Scarcely...when, not Scarcely...than."},{"n":38,"q":"The rescue team succeeded to complete the evacuation before nightfall.","o":["at complete the evacuation before nightfall","in completing the evacuation before nightfall","for completing the evacuation before nightfall","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"— Correct construction: succeed in + gerund → succeeded in completing."},{"n":39,"q":"The evidence is too weak so as to support such a conclusion.","o":["so weak to support such a conclusion","too weak to support such a conclusion","very weak so that supporting such a conclusion","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"— Correct structure: too + adjective + to-infinitive → too weak to support."},{"n":40,"q":"She would prefer to remain silent than to make an uncertain claim.","o":["remaining silent than making an uncertain claim","to remain silent rather than make an uncertain claim","to remain silent instead making an uncertain claim","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"— The idiomatic construction is prefer to...rather than..."},{"n":41,"q":"The evidence was incomplete; ______, it was sufficient to justify a preliminary inquiry.","o":["nevertheless","therefore","similarly","consequently"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete each sentence.","a":0,"e":"It expresses contrast: the evidence was incomplete, yet sufficient."},{"n":42,"q":"The new arrangement reduces paperwork. ______, it shortens the time required for approval.","o":["Conversely","Moreover","Otherwise","Nevertheless"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It adds a further advantage of the new arrangement."},{"n":43,"q":"The first explanation accounts for the delay; ______, it does not explain why the records disappeared.","o":["accordingly","however","likewise","hence"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It contrasts what the explanation accounts for with what it fails to explain."},{"n":44,"q":"The inspection revealed several safety deficiencies; ______, additional training was ordered.","o":["by contrast","accordingly","meanwhile","nevertheless"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The training was ordered as a consequence of the safety deficiencies."},{"n":45,"q":"Some mistakes result from inadequate training; ______, others arise from excessive confidence.","o":["by contrast","therefore","for instance","consequently"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"It contrasts two different causes of mistakes."},{"n":46,"q":"Read the following Active–Passive Voice pairs: I. They saw him enter the building. → He was seen to enter the building. II. People believe that the bridge is unsafe. → The bridge is believed to be unsafe.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Read each question carefully and select the most appropriate option.","a":2,"e":"He was seen to enter... is the correct passive of a perception verb, and The bridge is believed to be unsafe is also correct."},{"n":47,"q":"Read the following Direct–Indirect Speech pairs: I. He said, “Let us postpone the meeting.” → He suggested that they should postpone the meeting. II. She said to me, “Can you help me?” → She asked me if I could help her.","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"Let us... may be reported with suggested, and a yes/no question correctly changes to asked...if."},{"n":48,"q":"Read the following One-Word Substitutions: I. One who studies birds — Ornithologist II. One who hates mankind — Misanthrope III. A place where bees are kept — Apiary","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":3,"e":"Ornithologist, misanthrope and apiary are all correctly matched."},{"n":49,"q":"Read the following Question Tags: I. Let us begin the discussion, shall we? II. Nobody objected to the proposal, did they? III. I am late, aren’t I?","o":["I only","I and II only","II and III only","I, II and III"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":3,"e":"shall we?, did they?, and the special tag aren’t I? are all correct."},{"n":50,"q":"Fill in the blanks with the correct words: I. There were ______ applicants this year than last year. (fewer/less) II. The inquiry produced ______ evidence than expected. (fewer/less)","o":["I—less, II—fewer","I—fewer, II—fewer","I—less, II—less","I—fewer, II—less"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":3,"e":"Use fewer with countable plural nouns (applicants) and less with uncountable nouns (evidence)."}]},{"id":"sample-07","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 7","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The central argument of the passage is that","o":["numerical measurement should replace subjective judgement","complex performance cannot be evaluated meaningfully","quantitative indicators are useful but should not substitute for informed judgement","institutions perform better when assessed by one measure"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow. Select the most appropriate answer on the basis of the passage only and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","c":"Measurement appears to offer an escape from subjective judgement. Once performance is expressed numerically, comparisons seem straightforward and decisions appear defensible. Yet the apparent objectivity of a number can be misleading. Most things worth measuring—educational quality, administrative efficiency, institutional effectiveness or professional competence—are too complex to be captured by a single indicator. What is measured is usually a proxy for what actually matters. The difficulty increases when people know in advance which indicator will be used to judge them. Behaviour then begins to adapt to the measure. A school evaluated exclusively by examination results may devote increasing attention to activities that raise scores, even when those activities do little to deepen understanding. Similarly, an organisation rewarded for processing cases quickly may reduce the time devoted to difficult cases. The measure ceases merely to describe performance and begins to shape it. None of this makes measurement useless. Without quantitative evidence, judgement may become arbitrary or excessively dependent upon impression. The wiser approach is to use indicators as evidence rather than as substitutes for judgement. Numbers are most informative when their limitations are understood and when they are considered alongside evidence that cannot easily be reduced to numerical form.","a":2,"e":"— The passage argues that quantitative indicators are useful, but they should support rather than replace informed judgement."},{"n":2,"q":"In the passage, a “proxy” is best understood as","o":["an indirect measure used to represent something more complex","an inaccurate calculation deliberately presented as correct","a numerical value derived from unrelated sources","a standard used exclusively for comparison"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"— A proxy is an indirect measure used to represent something more complex that cannot easily be measured directly."},{"n":3,"q":"Why may an indicator begin to distort behaviour?","o":["People may adjust their actions specifically to improve the measured result.","Most indicators are calculated incorrectly.","Institutions generally reject quantitative assessment.","Numerical targets invariably reduce efficiency."],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"— When people know the indicator on which they will be judged, they may alter their behaviour specifically to improve that measured result."},{"n":4,"q":"The author is NOT opposed to measurement because","o":["measurement completely removes human bias","quantitative evidence can prevent judgement from becoming purely impressionistic","every important quality can ultimately be expressed numerically","numerical indicators cannot influence behaviour"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"— The author accepts measurement because quantitative evidence can prevent judgement from becoming arbitrary or based purely on impressions."},{"n":5,"q":"Which one of the following best describes the tone of the passage?","o":["Analytical and cautionary","Sarcastic and dismissive","Celebratory and optimistic","Dogmatic and accusatory"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"The passage objectively analyses both the usefulness and limitations of measurement."},{"n":6,"q":"The engineer said, “Had we tested the valve earlier, we might have detected the defect.”","o":["The engineer said that had they tested the valve earlier, they may detect the defect.","The engineer said that if they had tested the valve earlier, they might have detected the defect.","The engineer said that if they tested the valve earlier, they might detect the defect.","The engineer said that if we had tested the valve earlier, we would detect the defect."],"cat":"Narration","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–8): Each item consists of a sentence in Direct or Indirect Speech followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that correctly changes the form of narration and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"— The inverted conditional Had we tested... becomes if they had tested...; might have detected remains appropriate."},{"n":7,"q":"The officer said to the guard, “Let no visitor enter without written permission.”","o":["The officer advised that no visitor entered without written permission.","The officer ordered the guard not to allow any visitor to enter without written permission.","The officer said that the guard would not allow a visitor without permission.","The officer requested the visitors not to enter without written permission."],"cat":"Narration","a":1,"e":"— The command Let no visitor enter... is correctly reported as ordered the guard not to allow any visitor to enter..."},{"n":8,"q":"Rita asked me whether I had ever considered applying for the scholarship.","o":["Rita said to me, “Did you ever considered applying for the scholarship?”","Rita said to me, “Had you ever considered applying for the scholarship?”","Rita said to me, “Have you ever considered applying for the scholarship?”","Rita asked me, “You have ever considered applying for the scholarship?”"],"cat":"Narration","a":2,"e":"— Whether I had ever considered... changes back to the direct question “Have you ever considered...?”"},{"n":9,"q":"","o":["Rarely I have seen such restraint under intense provocation.","Rarely have I seen such restraint under intense provocation.","Rarely I had seen such restraint under intense provocation.","Rarely did I have seen such restraint under intense provocation."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 9–10): Each item consists of four sentences. Select the grammatically correct sentence and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"— When Rarely begins a sentence, subject–auxiliary inversion is required: Rarely have I seen..."},{"n":10,"q":"","o":["It is essential that every applicant submits the declaration personally.","It is essential that every applicant submitted the declaration personally.","It is essential that every applicant submit the declaration personally.","It is essential that every applicant will submit the declaration personally."],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"— After It is essential that, formal English uses the mandative subjunctive: every applicant submit."},{"n":11,"q":"Adverse and Averse","o":["Adverse = unwilling; Averse = harmful","Adverse = unfavourable; Averse = strongly opposed or unwilling","Adverse = opposite in direction; Averse = beneficial","Adverse = hesitant; Averse = hostile"],"cat":"Confusing Words","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each item consists of a pair of commonly confused words followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that correctly states the meanings of both words.","a":1,"e":"— Adverse = unfavourable; averse = strongly opposed or unwilling."},{"n":12,"q":"Imply and Infer","o":["Imply = deduce; Infer = suggest indirectly","Imply = state openly; Infer = deny","Imply = suggest indirectly; Infer = deduce from evidence","Imply = explain; Infer = assume without evidence"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":2,"e":"— Imply means suggest indirectly; infer means deduce from evidence."},{"n":13,"q":"Ingenious and Ingenuous","o":["Ingenious = clever and inventive; Ingenuous = innocent and candid","Ingenious = innocent; Ingenuous = inventive","Ingenious = dishonest; Ingenuous = intelligent","Ingenious = complex; Ingenuous = artificial"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":0,"e":"— Ingenious = clever/inventive; ingenuous = innocent, frank or candid."},{"n":14,"q":"Prescribe and Proscribe","o":["Prescribe = prohibit; Proscribe = recommend","Prescribe = recommend or order; Proscribe = forbid","Prescribe = describe beforehand; Proscribe = postpone","Prescribe = diagnose; Proscribe = punish"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":1,"e":"— Prescribe = recommend/order; proscribe = prohibit or forbid."},{"n":15,"q":"Climatic and Climactic","o":["Climatic = relating to a climax; Climactic = relating to climate","Climatic = decisive; Climactic = seasonal","Climatic = relating to climate; Climactic = relating to the most exciting or important point","Climatic = weather forecast; Climactic = concluding"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":2,"e":"— Climatic relates to climate; climactic relates to a climax or most important point."},{"n":16,"q":"Excessive secrecy can be INIMICAL to public confidence.","o":["conducive","hostile","irrelevant","subordinate"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): In each of the following sentences, a word has been given in capital letters. Select the alternative nearest in meaning to the word as used in the sentence.","t":"INIMICAL","a":1,"e":"Inimical means harmful, hostile or unfavourable to something."},{"n":17,"q":"A VOCIFEROUS section of the audience demanded that the speaker withdraw his remarks.","o":["loudly insistent","hesitant","indifferent","conciliatory"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"A VOCIFEROUS","a":0,"e":"Vociferous means expressing opinions loudly and forcefully."},{"n":18,"q":"The OSTENSIBLE purpose of the visit was to inspect the new facility.","o":["actual","apparent","concealed","questionable"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"OSTENSIBLE","a":1,"e":"Ostensible means appearing or stated to be true, though not necessarily so."},{"n":19,"q":"The proposed regulation has been held in ABEYANCE pending further consultation.","o":["permanent operation","temporary suspension","judicial review","public discussion"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"ABEYANCE","a":1,"e":"In abeyance means temporarily suspended or inactive."},{"n":20,"q":"Several members DEMURRED at the suggestion that the inquiry should be abandoned.","o":["consented","objected","rejoiced","speculated"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"DEMURRED","a":1,"e":"Demur means raise objections or express reluctance."},{"n":21,"q":"S1: A document does not become reliable historical evidence merely because it is old.\nP: The historian must first ask who produced it and for what purpose.\nQ: Such questions may reveal interests or assumptions that influenced its contents.\nR: Its claims should then be compared with evidence from independent sources.\nS: Agreement among unrelated sources strengthens confidence, while disagreement demands explanation.\nS6: Historical enquiry therefore requires evaluation of evidence rather than simple collection of it.","o":["PQRS","RQPS","QPSR","PRSQ"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): In each item, S1 and S6 are fixed. The four sentences marked P, Q, R and S are to be arranged between S1 and S6 so as to form a meaningful and coherent passage. Select the correct sequence.","a":0,"e":"First examine who produced the document and why; then consider possible bias, compare its claims with other evidence, and assess agreement or disagreement."},{"n":22,"q":"S1: Redundancy in a system may initially appear wasteful.\nP: Under normal conditions, a reserve component may remain almost entirely unused.\nQ: If the primary component fails, however, the reserve may prevent the whole system from collapsing.\nR: The apparent inefficiency therefore purchases resilience against unexpected failure.\nS: This principle explains why critical systems often contain more capacity than routine operation requires.\nS6: Efficiency and resilience are thus not always identical objectives.","o":["QPRS","PQRS","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"The unused reserve appears inefficient; its value emerges when the main component fails, showing that redundancy provides resilience."},{"n":23,"q":"S1: Productive research commonly begins with uncertainty rather than certainty.\nP: A question identifies something that existing knowledge does not adequately explain.\nQ: A tentative hypothesis then offers a possible explanation.\nR: Evidence is gathered to test whether that explanation survives scrutiny.\nS: If it does not, the hypothesis must be modified or abandoned.\nS6: Scientific progress depends as much upon correcting ideas as upon proposing them.","o":["RQPS","PQRS","QSPR","PRSQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Research proceeds logically from a question to a hypothesis, then evidence testing, and finally modification or rejection."},{"n":24,"q":"S1: False information often spreads rapidly in environments where attention is scarce.\nP: Emotionally striking claims are especially likely to be shared before they are verified.\nQ: Repetition then increases familiarity with the claim.\nR: Familiarity may in turn be mistaken for credibility.\nS: Deliberate verification can interrupt this process before familiarity hardens into belief.\nS6: Responsible communication therefore requires restraint as well as speed.","o":["PQRS","QPRS","PRSQ","RQSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Striking claims are shared, repetition creates familiarity, familiarity may become credibility, and verification can interrupt the process."},{"n":25,"q":"S1: Effective delegation requires more than simply transferring authority.\nP: A subordinate must first understand the objective that is to be achieved.\nQ: Within that framework, sufficient discretion must be given to respond to changing circumstances.\nR: Feedback then allows mistakes to be corrected without destroying initiative.\nS: Clear boundaries ensure that discretion remains consistent with the larger purpose.\nS6: Delegation succeeds when autonomy operates within understood limits.","o":["PSQR","PQRS","RQPS","QSRP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"First the objective must be understood; boundaries are then defined, discretion operates within them, and feedback corrects mistakes."},{"n":26,"q":"______ the evidence remains incomplete, the available material is sufficient to justify further investigation.","o":["Although","Therefore","Similarly","Consequently"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–35): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete the sentence.","a":0,"e":"It introduces a contrast between incomplete evidence and sufficient grounds for investigation."},{"n":27,"q":"The first method emphasises speed and flexibility; ______, the second gives priority to accuracy and uniformity.","o":["accordingly","by contrast","moreover","for instance"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It contrasts the priorities of the two methods."},{"n":28,"q":"The team had been repeatedly warned about the deteriorating weather; ______, it continued towards the summit.","o":["consequently","nevertheless","similarly","hence"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The team continued despite repeated warnings."},{"n":29,"q":"Several apparently minor errors can have serious consequences. ______, an incorrect coordinate may divert an entire convoy.","o":["For instance","Otherwise","Conversely","In conclusion"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"The incorrect coordinate is an example of a minor error with serious consequences."},{"n":30,"q":"The proposal would require considerable initial expenditure. ______, the long-term savings may outweigh that cost.","o":["Even so","Therefore","Likewise","Accordingly"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"It introduces a contrasting consideration despite the high initial expenditure."},{"n":31,"q":"______ your request for reassignment, the competent authority will consider it after the present exercise.","o":["With reference to","In consequence of","In contrast with","In spite of"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"This is the appropriate formal expression for referring to a request or earlier communication."},{"n":32,"q":"The estimated demand was revised downward; ______, the production target was reduced.","o":["nevertheless","accordingly","incidentally","meanwhile"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The reduced production target is the logical consequence of the downward revision in demand."},{"n":33,"q":"The arrangement will remain effective ______ all parties continue to observe the agreed conditions.","o":["whereas","provided that","notwithstanding","even though"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It introduces the condition under which the arrangement will remain effective."},{"n":34,"q":"The available evidence does not establish deliberate misconduct; ______, no final accusation should presently be made.","o":["therefore","conversely","meanwhile","likewise"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"Since deliberate misconduct is not established, the logical conclusion is that no final accusation should be made."},{"n":35,"q":"The plan is technically feasible; ______, whether it is financially prudent is another matter.","o":["consequently","however","similarly","hence"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It contrasts technical feasibility with financial prudence."},{"n":36,"q":"ANODYNE","o":["Harmless or soothing","Aggressive and provocative","Difficult to interpret","Morally objectionable"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item consists of a word followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning.","a":0,"e":"Anodyne means unlikely to cause offence or something that soothes pain."},{"n":37,"q":"APOCRYPHAL","o":["Universally accepted as true","Of doubtful authenticity, though widely circulated","Recorded in chronological order","Deliberately insulting"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":1,"e":"Apocryphal describes a story or account of doubtful truth or authenticity."},{"n":38,"q":"QUOTIDIAN","o":["Extraordinary","Occurring every day; commonplace","Existing only temporarily","Secret or confidential"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":1,"e":"Quotidian means daily or ordinary."},{"n":39,"q":"REPROBATE","o":["A morally unprincipled person","A person incapable of making decisions","A person who rejects material comforts","A highly respected scholar"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":0,"e":"A reprobate is a person of immoral or unprincipled character."},{"n":40,"q":"PANACEA","o":["A universal remedy or solution","A temporary compromise","An unavoidable difficulty","A formal prohibition"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":0,"e":"A panacea is something believed to solve all problems or cure all difficulties."},{"n":41,"q":"P. only when alternative explanations\nQ. a causal claim becomes convincing\nR. have been considered and excluded\nS. by the available evidence","o":["PRSQ","QPRS","RSPQ","SQRP"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Each item consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange them in the correct sequence so as to form a meaningful and grammatically correct sentence.","a":1,"e":"A causal claim becomes convincing only when alternative explanations have been considered and excluded by the available evidence."},{"n":42,"q":"P. may conceal important disagreements\nQ. apparent consensus\nR. if key terms\nS. are being used differently","o":["QRSP","QPRS","RQPS","PQSR"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Apparent consensus may conceal important disagreements if key terms are being used differently."},{"n":43,"q":"P. before responding to criticism\nQ. one should first determine\nR. whether it challenges the evidence\nS. or merely the conclusion drawn from it","o":["PQRS","QSPR","RPQS","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Before responding to criticism, one should first determine whether it challenges the evidence or merely the conclusion drawn from it."},{"n":44,"q":"P. reliable judgement depends\nQ. not on eliminating uncertainty\nR. but on proportioning confidence\nS. to the strength of available evidence","o":["QRSP","PQRS","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Reliable judgement depends not on eliminating uncertainty but on proportioning confidence to the strength of available evidence."},{"n":45,"q":"P. what makes a rule effective\nQ. is not its severity alone\nR. but the consistency\nS. with which it is applied","o":["PRQS","PQRS","RQPS","QSPR"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"What makes a rule effective is not its severity alone but the consistency with which it is applied."},{"n":46,"q":"The officer was debarred ______ participating in the selection proceedings because of a conflict of interest.","o":["of","from","against","with"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate alternative to complete the sentence and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"The correct construction is debar someone from doing something."},{"n":47,"q":"The evidence was insufficient to warrant ______ such a serious allegation against the officer.","o":["make","making","to make","made"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"Warrant is followed naturally by a gerund here: warrant making such an allegation."},{"n":48,"q":"Much as I ______ his courage, I cannot approve of the reckless manner in which he acted.","o":["admire","am admiring","admired","have been admire"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":0,"e":"Much as I admire... means “although I admire...”; the simple present is appropriate."},{"n":49,"q":"The proposal would have been accepted but ______ the unexpected escalation in cost.","o":["to","of","for","with"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"But for means “if it had not been for”: but for the unexpected escalation in cost."},{"n":50,"q":"Not one of the witnesses ______ able to identify the individual with certainty.","o":["were","have been","was","are"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"The subject is Not one, which is singular; therefore was able is correct."}]},{"id":"sample-08","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 8","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The chairman made a PROPITIATORY gesture in an attempt to prevent the disagreement from escalating.","o":["conciliatory","provocative","perfunctory","presumptuous"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Each item in this section consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word nearest in meaning to the word in capital letters and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"PROPITIATORY","a":0,"e":"Propitiatory means intended to appease, pacify or win goodwill."},{"n":2,"q":"His analysis was dismissed as JEJUNE because it ignored the complexities of the issue.","o":["profound","superficial","impartial","controversial"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"JEJUNE","a":1,"e":"Jejune means simplistic, shallow or lacking substance."},{"n":3,"q":"She displayed a natural PROCLIVITY for solving intricate mathematical problems.","o":["aversion","inclination","indifference","capacity"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PROCLIVITY","a":1,"e":"Proclivity means a natural tendency or inclination towards something."},{"n":4,"q":"Having lost almost all his savings, the once prosperous merchant became IMPECUNIOUS.","o":["impoverished","parsimonious","extravagant","insolvent-minded"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"IMPECUNIOUS","a":0,"e":"Impecunious means having little or no money."},{"n":5,"q":"The victorious commander was MAGNANIMOUS towards his defeated adversaries.","o":["vindictive","generous","indifferent","submissive"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"MAGNANIMOUS","a":1,"e":"Magnanimous means generous and forgiving, especially towards a defeated rival."},{"n":6,"q":"His ABSTEMIOUS habits surprised those accustomed to extravagant living.","o":["moderate","self-indulgent","restrained","frugal"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–10): Each item in this section consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word opposite in meaning to the word in capital letters and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"ABSTEMIOUS","a":1,"e":"Abstemious means moderate or restrained, especially in eating and drinking; self-indulgent is the opposite."},{"n":7,"q":"The normally RETICENT officer spoke at considerable length during the inquiry.","o":["taciturn","communicative","discreet","diffident"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"RETICENT","a":1,"e":"Reticent means reserved or unwilling to speak freely."},{"n":8,"q":"The material remains MALLEABLE until it is exposed to extremely low temperatures.","o":["flexible","ductile","rigid","pliable"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"MALLEABLE","a":2,"e":"Malleable means flexible or capable of being shaped; rigid is the opposite."},{"n":9,"q":"The spread of such PERNICIOUS ideas could seriously undermine public confidence.","o":["harmful","beneficial","insidious","destructive"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"PERNICIOUS","a":1,"e":"Pernicious means harmful or destructive; beneficial is its opposite."},{"n":10,"q":"Though remarkably ERUDITE, the scholar never displayed intellectual arrogance.","o":["ignorant","scholarly","cultivated","perceptive"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"ERUDITE","a":0,"e":"Erudite means highly learned or scholarly; ignorant is the opposite."},{"n":11,"q":"Nobody was willing to BELL THE CAT when the issue of confronting the corrupt official arose.","o":["disclose a secret","undertake a dangerous task","deceive an opponent","make an accusation publicly"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each of the following sentences contains an idiom/phrase in capital letters. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning.","t":"BELL THE CAT","a":1,"e":"Bell the cat means take responsibility for a difficult or risky action that others avoid."},{"n":12,"q":"Sending an expert in mountaineering to teach basic climbing to that veteran instructor would be like CARRYING COALS TO NEWCASTLE.","o":["attempting the impossible","doing something unnecessary","performing a hazardous task","working without reward"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"CARRYING COALS TO NEWCASTLE","a":1,"e":"Carrying coals to Newcastle means taking something to a place where it is already plentiful."},{"n":13,"q":"The candidates were ON TENTERHOOKS while waiting for the final result.","o":["extremely confident","in anxious suspense","completely exhausted","unwilling to cooperate"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"ON TENTERHOOKS","a":1,"e":"On tenterhooks means being extremely anxious or expectant."},{"n":14,"q":"The commander DREW A LINE IN THE SAND regarding any further violation of safety procedures.","o":["established a firm limit","withdrew an earlier order","concealed his intentions","invited further discussion"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"DREW A LINE IN THE SAND","a":0,"e":"Draw a line in the sand means set a definite boundary beyond which one will not compromise."},{"n":15,"q":"The sudden withdrawal of financial support PUT PAID TO the ambitious expansion plan.","o":["accelerated","brought to an end","financed","temporarily delayed"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"PUT PAID TO","a":1,"e":"Put paid to means stop, destroy or bring something to an end."},{"n":16,"q":"Since the visibility had improved, the aircraft was permitted to take off.","o":["Preposition","Conjunction","Adverb","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): Each of the following sentences has an underlined word. Identify the part of speech to which the underlined word belongs and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"Since introduces the subordinate clause the visibility had improved."},{"n":17,"q":"The sentries waited outside until the inspection was completed.","o":["Adjective","Preposition","Adverb","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":3,"e":"Until introduces the clause the inspection was completed."},{"n":18,"q":"Several objections were raised during the discussion.","o":["Determiner","Pronoun","Adverb","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":0,"e":"Several precedes and determines the noun objections."},{"n":19,"q":"The witness spoke little during the cross-examination.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Pronoun","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Little modifies the verb spoke and means “not much”."},{"n":20,"q":"What the committee ultimately decides will affect every applicant.","o":["Adjective","Relative pronoun","Adverb","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"What means “that which/the thing that” and introduces a fused relative clause."},{"n":21,"q":"______, the new system is expensive to install; nevertheless, it is likely to reduce operating costs substantially.","o":["Granted","Therefore","Likewise","Consequently"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete the sentence.","a":0,"e":"It introduces a concession: the system is expensive, but it may reduce future costs."},{"n":22,"q":"The earlier estimate was based on incomplete data; ______, a fresh assessment was ordered.","o":["nevertheless","accordingly","conversely","meanwhile"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"A fresh assessment was ordered as a consequence of the incomplete data."},{"n":23,"q":"A seemingly trivial oversight may have serious consequences. ______, an incorrect digit in a coordinate can redirect an entire convoy.","o":["On the contrary","For example","Nevertheless","In conclusion"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The incorrect coordinate illustrates the preceding general statement."},{"n":24,"q":"Some argue that strict rules necessarily destroy initiative. ______, clearly defined limits can actually give individuals greater confidence to exercise discretion.","o":["Similarly","On the contrary","Consequently","Meanwhile"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It directly challenges the preceding claim about strict rules destroying initiative."},{"n":25,"q":"The final report will take several weeks to complete. ______, the provisional recommendations may be implemented.","o":["Meanwhile","Conversely","Otherwise","Consequently"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"It indicates what may happen during the period before the final report is completed."},{"n":26,"q":"Were it not for the additional evidence, ______.","o":["the case would have appeared considerably weaker","the case will appear considerably weaker","the case had appeared considerably weaker","the case would appear yesterday considerably weaker"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the alternative that best completes the sentence.","a":0,"e":"Were it not for introduces an unreal condition. Option (A) gives the intended counterfactual result. For a purely past counterfactual, Had it not been for... would be even more conventional."},{"n":27,"q":"So compelling was the evidence ______.","o":["because the committee reopened the investigation","that even the sceptics reconsidered their position","than the committee had reconsidered its findings","therefore no further inquiry was necessary"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"Correct construction: So + adjective + that..."},{"n":28,"q":"It is about time the authorities ______ the obsolete regulations.","o":["revise","revised","will revise","have revise"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"After It is about time + subject, the past tense is used to express that something should already be done."},{"n":29,"q":"No sooner had the final whistle been blown ______ the spectators rushed towards the exits.","o":["when","then","than","that"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":2,"e":"The standard correlative construction is No sooner...than."},{"n":30,"q":"You may proceed with the experiment ______ all the prescribed safety measures are observed.","o":["although","provided that","unless","whereas"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"Provided that means “on condition that”."},{"n":31,"q":"The criteria adopted by the selection board / is considerably more stringent / than those used last year.","o":["The criteria adopted by the selection board","is considerably more stringent","than those used last year.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each item consists of a sentence divided into three parts marked (A), (B) and (C). Identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, choose (D) No error.","a":1,"e":"— Criteria is plural; therefore, are considerably more stringent is required."},{"n":32,"q":"The instructor suggested me / to reconsider my decision / before submitting the final application.","o":["The instructor suggested me","to reconsider my decision","before submitting the final application.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"— Suggest is not normally followed by object + to-infinitive. Correct: The instructor suggested that I reconsider... or suggested reconsidering..."},{"n":33,"q":"Not only the cadets but also the instructor / were surprised by / the sudden alteration in the schedule.","o":["Not only the cadets but also the instructor","were surprised by","the sudden alteration in the schedule.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— With not only...but also, the verb agrees with the nearer subject instructor; therefore, was surprised."},{"n":34,"q":"The officer asked that whether / the equipment had been inspected / before it was dispatched.","o":["The officer asked that whether","the equipment had been inspected","before it was dispatched.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"— Asked that whether is incorrect. Correct: asked whether the equipment had been inspected."},{"n":35,"q":"Each of the reports submitted by the regional offices / has been examined carefully / by the review committee.","o":["Each of the reports submitted by the regional offices","has been examined carefully","by the review committee.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"Each is singular and correctly takes has been examined."},{"n":36,"q":"P. is often more valuable\nQ. an explanation that identifies its limitations\nR. than one\nS. that claims certainty without qualification","o":["QPRS","PQRS","RSPQ","QRPS"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange them in the correct sequence to form a meaningful and grammatically correct sentence.","a":0,"e":"An explanation that identifies its limitations is often more valuable than one that claims certainty without qualification."},{"n":37,"q":"P. only after contradictory evidence appeared\nQ. did the researchers recognise\nR. how heavily their conclusion\nS. depended upon an untested assumption","o":["PQRS","QPRS","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Only after contradictory evidence appeared did the researchers recognise how heavily their conclusion depended upon an untested assumption."},{"n":38,"q":"P. before accepting an apparently obvious explanation\nQ. one should consider\nR. whether another hypothesis\nS. accounts for the same facts equally well","o":["QPRS","PQRS","RSPQ","PSRQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Before accepting an apparently obvious explanation, one should consider whether another hypothesis accounts for the same facts equally well."},{"n":39,"q":"P. cannot compensate\nQ. for a badly defined objective\nR. efficiency in execution\nS. however impressive it may appear","o":["RPQS","RSPQ","PQRS","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Efficiency in execution, however impressive it may appear, cannot compensate for a badly defined objective."},{"n":40,"q":"P. those who revise their views\nQ. in response to stronger evidence\nR. demonstrate intellectual strength\nS. rather than weakness","o":["PRQS","PQRS","QRSP","RSPQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":1,"e":"Those who revise their views in response to stronger evidence demonstrate intellectual strength rather than weakness."},{"n":41,"q":"The chief advantage of historical analogy, according to the passage, is that it can ______.","o":["provide certain solutions to current crises","reveal possibilities that may otherwise be overlooked","eliminate the need for independent investigation","prove that history inevitably repeats itself"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow. Select the most appropriate answer on the basis of the passage only.","c":"Historical analogies are attractive because they make unfamiliar problems appear manageable. A present crisis is compared with an earlier event, and lessons are then drawn from what succeeded or failed in the past. Used carefully, analogy can illuminate possibilities that might otherwise be overlooked. It reminds decision-makers that present circumstances are not entirely unique. The danger begins when resemblance is mistaken for identity. Two situations may share a striking feature while differing in several less visible but more important respects. Political institutions, technology, geography and public expectations may have changed so greatly that a policy successful in one period produces very different consequences in another. An analogy can therefore guide inquiry, but it cannot substitute for inquiry. The wisest use of historical comparison is not to ask, “Which past event is this exactly like?” but rather, “In what respects is this similar, and in what respects is it different?” The second question turns analogy from a source of ready-made answers into a tool for disciplined comparison.","a":1,"e":"The passage explicitly identifies this as an advantage of carefully used historical analogy."},{"n":42,"q":"The principal danger in using historical analogy arises when ______.","o":["differences are examined too carefully","resemblance is treated as complete identity","previous events are poorly documented","decision-makers reject all comparisons"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"The author warns that danger begins when resemblance is mistaken for identity."},{"n":43,"q":"Which one of the following factors does the author mention as capable of limiting the usefulness of a historical analogy?","o":["Changes in technology","Personal memory","Literary interpretation","Numerical measurement"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"Technology is specifically mentioned among the factors that may make two historical situations significantly different."},{"n":44,"q":"The statement “analogy can guide inquiry, but it cannot substitute for inquiry” means that analogy should ______.","o":["replace detailed analysis when time is limited","provide questions rather than automatic answers","be avoided in all serious decision-making","be used only by professional historians"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"Historical analogy can guide investigation, but it cannot replace detailed inquiry."},{"n":45,"q":"The author recommends asking how two situations are both similar and different because this ______.","o":["prevents any conclusion from being reached","converts analogy into a disciplined comparative tool","proves that all historical events are unique","makes factual investigation unnecessary"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"Examining both similarities and differences prevents superficial comparison."},{"n":46,"q":"Read the following Active Voice → Passive Voice pairs: I. People believe him to be exceptionally competent. → He is believed to be exceptionally competent. II. Who prepared this confidential report? → By whom was this confidential report prepared? Identify the correct pair(s).","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Read each question carefully and select the most appropriate option.","a":2,"e":"Both passive transformations are correct: is believed to be and By whom was...prepared?"},{"n":47,"q":"Read the following Direct Speech → Indirect Speech pairs: I. He said, “Alas! I have lost the opportunity.” → He exclaimed with sorrow that he had lost the opportunity. II. She said to me, “May you succeed in your endeavour!” → She wished that I might succeed in my endeavour. Identify the correct pair(s).","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"Alas! is correctly reported through exclaimed with sorrow, and the optative sentence with May... is correctly reported using wished...might."},{"n":48,"q":"Read the following One-Word Substitutions: I. One who compiles dictionaries — Lexicographer II. Official pardon for people convicted of political offences — Amnesty III. Study or collection of coins — Numismatics Identify the correctly matched pair(s).","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":3,"e":"Lexicographer, amnesty and numismatics are all correctly matched."},{"n":49,"q":"Read the following word pairs and meanings: I. Assent — agreement or approval; Ascent — upward movement II. Censor — officially examine/suppress objectionable material; Censure — strong disapproval or criticism Which pair(s) are correctly explained?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"Assent/ascent and censor/censure have all been correctly distinguished."},{"n":50,"q":"Fill in X and Y appropriately: I. Of the three proposed routes, this is by far X (more practical/the most practical). II. Y (Fewer/Less) than twenty candidates qualified for the final stage.","o":["X—more practical, Y—Less","X—the most practical, Y—Less","X—more practical, Y—Fewer","X—the most practical, Y—Fewer"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":3,"e":"With three routes, the superlative the most practical is required; fewer is used with the countable plural noun candidates."}]},{"id":"sample-09","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 9","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The committee rejected the SPURIOUS allegation for want of credible evidence.","o":["convincing","false","serious","unexpected"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Each item in this section consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word nearest in meaning to the word in capital letters and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"SPURIOUS","a":1,"e":"Spurious means false, fake or not genuine."},{"n":2,"q":"The most SALIENT features of the proposal were discussed during the meeting.","o":["controversial","prominent","concealed","insignificant"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"SALIENT","a":1,"e":"Salient means most noticeable, important or prominent."},{"n":3,"q":"His plan to eliminate every form of conflict from society appeared QUIXOTIC.","o":["impractically idealistic","deliberately deceptive","carefully calculated","politically expedient"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"QUIXOTIC","a":0,"e":"Quixotic describes ideas that are highly idealistic but unrealistic or impractical."},{"n":4,"q":"Several MORIBUND industries were revived through technological modernisation.","o":["rapidly expanding","nearing collapse","highly profitable","inadequately regulated"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"MORIBUND","a":1,"e":"Moribund means dying, declining or approaching the end of existence."},{"n":5,"q":"The fresh evidence was sufficient to EXONERATE the officer from the allegation.","o":["accuse","absolve","interrogate","reprimand"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"EXONERATE","a":1,"e":"Exonerate means free someone from blame or an accusation."},{"n":6,"q":"Though naturally GREGARIOUS, he preferred solitude during the examination period.","o":["sociable","reclusive","affable","convivial"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–10): Each item in this section consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word opposite in meaning to the word in capital letters.","t":"GREGARIOUS","a":1,"e":"Gregarious means sociable; reclusive is its opposite."},{"n":7,"q":"His views were considered highly ORTHODOX by the younger members of the committee.","o":["conventional","traditional","unconventional","accepted"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"ORTHODOX","a":2,"e":"Orthodox means conventional or traditionally accepted; unconventional is the opposite."},{"n":8,"q":"A PRUDENT commander never exposes his troops to unnecessary danger.","o":["cautious","reckless","vigilant","judicious"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"A PRUDENT","a":1,"e":"Prudent means wise and cautious; reckless is the opposite."},{"n":9,"q":"Water was ABUNDANT in the region during the monsoon.","o":["plentiful","scarce","adequate","excessive"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"ABUNDANT","a":1,"e":"Abundant means plentiful; scarce means insufficient or rare."},{"n":10,"q":"The medical examination revealed that the growth was BENIGN.","o":["harmless","malignant","curable","painless"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"BENIGN","a":1,"e":"Benign means harmless or non-cancerous; malignant is its opposite."},{"n":11,"q":"Credible and Credulous","o":["Credible = believable; Credulous = too ready to believe","Credible = doubtful; Credulous = trustworthy","Credible = gullible; Credulous = convincing","Credible = honest; Credulous = dishonest"],"cat":"Confusing Words","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each item consists of a pair of commonly confused words followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that correctly explains the meanings of both words.","a":0,"e":"— Credible = believable; credulous = too willing to believe things."},{"n":12,"q":"Emigrate and Immigrate","o":["Emigrate = enter a country; Immigrate = leave a country","Emigrate = leave one’s country to settle elsewhere; Immigrate = enter another country to settle there","Emigrate = travel temporarily; Immigrate = travel permanently","Emigrate = return home; Immigrate = remain abroad"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":1,"e":"— Emigrate = leave one’s country to settle elsewhere; immigrate = enter another country to settle there."},{"n":13,"q":"Persecute and Prosecute","o":["Persecute = institute legal proceedings; Prosecute = harass cruelly","Persecute = harass or oppress; Prosecute = conduct legal proceedings against","Persecute = punish legally; Prosecute = forgive legally","Persecute = investigate; Prosecute = interrogate"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":1,"e":"— Persecute = harass or oppress; prosecute = institute/conduct legal proceedings against someone."},{"n":14,"q":"Defuse and Diffuse","o":["Defuse = spread widely; Diffuse = remove danger","Defuse = make a dangerous situation less tense; Diffuse = spread over a wide area","Defuse = explode; Diffuse = concentrate","Defuse = confuse; Diffuse = clarify"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":1,"e":"— Defuse = remove danger or tension; diffuse = spread over a wide area."},{"n":15,"q":"Canvas and Canvass","o":["Canvas = strong cloth; Canvass = solicit votes or opinions","Canvas = solicit votes; Canvass = strong cloth","Canvas = examine carefully; Canvass = paint","Canvas = campaign; Canvass = conceal"],"cat":"Confusing Words","a":0,"e":"— Canvas = strong cloth; canvass = seek votes, support or opinions."},{"n":16,"q":"The sentry was standing NEAR the entrance when the vehicle arrived.","o":["Adverb","Preposition","Adjective","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): Each of the following sentences contains an underlined word. Identify the part of speech of the underlined word as used in the sentence.","a":1,"e":"Near is followed by the noun phrase the entrance and shows position."},{"n":17,"q":"MUCH remains to be done before the project can be declared complete.","o":["Adjective","Pronoun","Adverb","Determiner"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Much stands independently as the subject of remains."},{"n":18,"q":"The rescue teams worked HARD throughout the night.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Noun","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Hard modifies the verb worked."},{"n":19,"q":"THAT he survived the accident surprised even the doctors.","o":["Demonstrative adjective","Pronoun","Conjunction","Adverb"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"That introduces the noun clause that he survived the accident."},{"n":20,"q":"The THEN Principal strongly opposed the proposed change.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Conjunction","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":0,"e":"In the then Principal, then is used attributively to mean “the person who was Principal at that time.”"},{"n":21,"q":"The officer denied to have disclosed the confidential information.","o":["denied having disclosed the confidential information","denied to disclose the confidential information","denied that disclosing the confidential information","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): In each of the following sentences, a part is incorrect or inappropriate. Select the alternative that best improves the sentence.","a":0,"e":"— Deny is followed by a gerund: denied having disclosed, not denied to have disclosed."},{"n":22,"q":"If I was in your position, I would not ignore such a warning.","o":["If I am in your position","If I were in your position","If I had been in your position","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"— For an unreal present condition, use If I were..."},{"n":23,"q":"The reason for the delay was because the necessary approval had not arrived.","o":["was that the necessary approval had not arrived","was due because the necessary approval had not arrived","is because the necessary approval did not arrive","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"— The correct construction is The reason...was that..., not the reason was because."},{"n":24,"q":"No fewer than five officers has volunteered for the assignment.","o":["have volunteered for the assignment","had volunteers for the assignment","has been volunteering for the assignment","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"— The plural subject five officers requires have volunteered."},{"n":25,"q":"He is accustomed to work under considerable pressure.","o":["for working under considerable pressure","to working under considerable pressure","with working under considerable pressure","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"— Accustomed to is followed by a noun/gerund: accustomed to working."},{"n":26,"q":"After the final decision, the company had effectively BURNED ITS BRIDGES.","o":["destroyed its property","made retreat or reversal difficult","concealed its losses","dismissed its employees"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): Each of the following sentences contains an idiom/phrase in capital letters. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning.","t":"BURNED ITS BRIDGES","a":1,"e":"Burn one’s bridges means take an action that makes returning to an earlier position difficult or impossible."},{"n":27,"q":"The much-publicised programme turned out to be A DAMP SQUIB.","o":["a complete disappointment","a spectacular success","a dangerous undertaking","an expensive mistake"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"A DAMP SQUIB","a":0,"e":"A damp squib means something expected to be impressive but which turns out disappointing."},{"n":28,"q":"After the sudden transfer of several officials, the office was AT SIXES AND SEVENS.","o":["extremely efficient","in a state of confusion","severely understaffed","under investigation"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"AT SIXES AND SEVENS","a":1,"e":"At sixes and sevens means confused or disorganised."},{"n":29,"q":"You should TAKE HIS CLAIMS WITH A PINCH OF SALT.","o":["accept them immediately","treat them with some scepticism","reject them completely","examine them angrily"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"TAKE HIS CLAIMS WITH A PINCH OF SALT","a":1,"e":"Take something with a pinch of salt means not accept it completely as true."},{"n":30,"q":"Unless the new candidate can CUT THE MUSTARD, he will not survive the demanding course.","o":["meet the required standard","avoid responsibility","change the existing rules","outperform everyone"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"CUT THE MUSTARD","a":0,"e":"Cut the mustard means perform satisfactorily or meet expectations."},{"n":31,"q":"The proposal promises considerable long-term savings; ______, the initial expenditure is exceptionally high.","o":["however","therefore","likewise","consequently"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete the sentence.","a":0,"e":"It contrasts long-term savings with high initial expenditure."},{"n":32,"q":"The witness could not remember the exact time of the incident. ______, she clearly remembered the sequence of events.","o":["Consequently","Nevertheless","For instance","Similarly"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"Despite not remembering the exact time, she remembered the sequence clearly."},{"n":33,"q":"The procedure is not intended to remove individual judgement. ______, it provides a framework within which judgement can be exercised.","o":["On the contrary","Consequently","Meanwhile","For example"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"The second sentence directly corrects a possible misunderstanding of the first."},{"n":34,"q":"Some errors arise from ignorance; others, ______, result from excessive confidence.","o":["by contrast","therefore","hence","in conclusion"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"It contrasts two different causes of errors."},{"n":35,"q":"The weather forecast predicted severe thunderstorms; ______, the organisers postponed the outdoor exercise.","o":["otherwise","accordingly","nevertheless","conversely"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"Postponement follows logically from the forecast of severe thunderstorms."},{"n":36,"q":"Neither of the two explanations / appear convincing / in the light of the available evidence.","o":["Neither of the two explanations","appear convincing","in the light of the available evidence.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item consists of a sentence divided into three parts marked (a), (b) and (c). Identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error.","a":1,"e":"— Neither is singular here; appear should be appears."},{"n":37,"q":"The chairman, as well as the other members, / were present at the meeting / when the resolution was adopted.","o":["The chairman, as well as the other members,","were present at the meeting","when the resolution was adopted.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— The main subject is singular chairman; as well as the other members does not change its number. Correct: was present."},{"n":38,"q":"Each of the applicants / have submitted a declaration / regarding previous employment.","o":["Each of the applicants","have submitted a declaration","regarding previous employment.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— Each takes a singular verb; have submitted should be has submitted."},{"n":39,"q":"The committee suggested / that the matter should be reconsidered / before a final decision was taken.","o":["The committee suggested","that the matter should be reconsidered","before a final decision was taken.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"Suggested that the matter should be reconsidered is grammatically acceptable, particularly in British English. Suggested that the matter be reconsidered is also correct."},{"n":40,"q":"I prefer reading a carefully argued report / to watch a lengthy presentation / on the same subject.","o":["I prefer reading a carefully argued report","to watch a lengthy presentation","on the same subject.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— Prefer X to Y requires parallel forms: prefer reading...to watching..., not to watch."},{"n":41,"q":"Blank 41: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["distinguish","divide","differ","discriminate against"],"cat":"Cloze Test","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Read the following passage carefully. Each numbered blank is followed by four alternatives. Select the most appropriate word to fill each blank.","c":"Leadership often requires the ability to (41) ______ between what is merely urgent and what is genuinely important. Issues demanding (42) ______ attention are not necessarily those with the greatest long-term consequences. An effective leader therefore (43) ______ time according to priorities rather than noise. This requires discipline because urgent matters are often more visible and (44) ______. Unless priorities are reviewed periodically, an organisation may become efficient at dealing with minor crises while (45) ______ strategic concerns.","a":0,"e":"The standard construction is distinguish between A and B."},{"n":42,"q":"Blank 42: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["immediate","eventual","reluctant","accidental"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":0,"e":"Urgent matters demand immediate attention."},{"n":43,"q":"Blank 43: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["allocates","dissolves","extracts","postpones"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":0,"e":"A leader allocates time according to priorities."},{"n":44,"q":"Blank 44: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["obsolete","insistent","dormant","remote"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":1,"e":"Urgent matters tend to be conspicuous and demanding or insistent."},{"n":45,"q":"Blank 45: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["preserving","neglecting","resolving","magnifying"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":1,"e":"The contrast is between handling minor crises efficiently and neglecting strategic concerns."},{"n":46,"q":"VIA MEDIA","o":["An extreme course of action","A middle course between two extremes","A method of secret negotiation","A temporary arrangement"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Each item consists of a foreign/Latin expression followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"Via media literally means “middle way”; it refers to a compromise or moderate course between opposing extremes."},{"n":47,"q":"MODUS OPERANDI","o":["A method or manner of operating","A legal obligation","A change in established policy","A temporary suspension"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":0,"e":"Modus operandi means a characteristic method, procedure or way of doing something."},{"n":48,"q":"SINE QUA NON","o":["Something of doubtful value","An unnecessary condition","An essential condition or requirement","A mutually accepted compromise"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":2,"e":"Sine qua non means something absolutely necessary or indispensable."},{"n":49,"q":"PRIMA FACIE","o":["At first sight or on initial appearance","In complete secrecy","Without any legal authority","After detailed examination"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":0,"e":"Prima facie means “at first appearance”; it describes something accepted as true unless disproved by further evidence."},{"n":50,"q":"QUID PRO QUO","o":["A favour or advantage given in return for another","An action taken without preparation","A decision made by majority opinion","A condition that cannot be alteredA"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":0,"e":"Quid pro quo means something given or received in exchange for something else."}]},{"id":"sample-10","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 10","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The officer's PEREMPTORY tone left little room for further discussion.","o":["hesitant","authoritative","apologetic","conciliatory"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Each item in this section consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word nearest in meaning to the word in capital letters and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"PEREMPTORY","a":1,"e":"Peremptory means commanding, decisive and allowing little or no discussion."},{"n":2,"q":"The witness attempted to IMPUGN the credibility of the investigator.","o":["challenge","establish","conceal","exaggerate"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"IMPUGN","a":0,"e":"Impugn means to question or attack the truth, integrity or credibility of something."},{"n":3,"q":"The information had been obtained through SURREPTITIOUS means.","o":["legitimate","secretive","elaborate","accidental"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"SURREPTITIOUS","a":1,"e":"Surreptitious means done secretly, especially to avoid being noticed."},{"n":4,"q":"His EFFRONTERY in questioning the integrity of the entire committee surprised everyone.","o":["timidity","audacity","resentment","ignorance"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"EFFRONTERY","a":1,"e":"Effrontery means shameless boldness or excessive audacity."},{"n":5,"q":"The witness continued to PREVARICATE whenever he was asked a direct question.","o":["speak evasively","exaggerate deliberately","confess openly","remain completely silent"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"PREVARICATE","a":0,"e":"Prevaricate means to avoid telling the truth by speaking evasively."},{"n":6,"q":"The problem initially appeared INTRACTABLE.","o":["complex","manageable","persistent","insoluble"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–10): Each item consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word opposite in meaning to the word in capital letters.","t":"INTRACTABLE","a":1,"e":"Intractable means difficult or impossible to manage; manageable is its opposite."},{"n":7,"q":"The officer was SCRUPULOUS in maintaining the financial records.","o":["conscientious","negligent","meticulous","cautious"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"SCRUPULOUS","a":1,"e":"Scrupulous means extremely careful and conscientious; negligent is the opposite."},{"n":8,"q":"His DEFERENTIAL manner towards his seniors was noticed by everyone.","o":["respectful","courteous","insolent","restrained"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"DEFERENTIAL","a":2,"e":"Deferential means respectfully submissive; insolent is its opposite."},{"n":9,"q":"The instructions were EXPLICIT and left little possibility of misunderstanding.","o":["vague","precise","elaborate","emphatic"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"EXPLICIT","a":0,"e":"Explicit means clearly and directly stated; vague is the opposite."},{"n":10,"q":"The chairman remained PLACID despite the heated exchange.","o":["calm","agitated","composed","restrained"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"PLACID","a":1,"e":"Placid means calm and peaceful; agitated is the opposite."},{"n":11,"q":"A person who abandons or renounces his religious faith is called an ______.","o":["apostate","ascetic","agnostic","amateur"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each item consists of a group of words followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that can be substituted for the given group of words.","a":0,"e":"An apostate is one who renounces a religious faith or belief."},{"n":12,"q":"A person who attacks established beliefs or cherished traditions is an ______.","o":["optimist","iconoclast","opportunist","introvert"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","a":1,"e":"An iconoclast attacks or challenges established beliefs, traditions or institutions."},{"n":13,"q":"A person who deliberately sets fire to property is called an ______.","o":["anarchist","arsonist","artisan","antagonist"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","a":1,"e":"An arsonist deliberately sets fire to property."},{"n":14,"q":"Something that cannot be corrected or reformed is described as ______.","o":["incredible","incorrigible","invincible","inevitable"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","a":1,"e":"Incorrigible means incapable of being corrected or reformed."},{"n":15,"q":"A person who deliberately avoids the company of other people and lives alone is a ______.","o":["recluse","renegade","novice","pilgrim"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","a":0,"e":"A recluse deliberately lives in seclusion and avoids the company of others."},{"n":16,"q":"ENOUGH has already been said on the subject.","o":["Adjective","Pronoun","Adverb","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): In each of the following sentences, a word has been underlined. Identify the part of speech of the underlined word as used in the sentence.","a":1,"e":"Enough stands independently and functions as the subject of has been said."},{"n":17,"q":"The aircraft took off AFTER the weather had improved considerably.","o":["Preposition","Adverb","Conjunction","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"After introduces the clause the weather had improved considerably."},{"n":18,"q":"WALKING along the narrow ridge, the patrol noticed fresh footprints.","o":["Gerund","Present participle","Noun","Adverb"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Walking begins a participial phrase describing the patrol."},{"n":19,"q":"He is BUt a beginner and should not be judged too harshly.","o":["Conjunction","Preposition","Adverb","Pronoun"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"But means only in this sentence and therefore functions as an adverb."},{"n":20,"q":"The rescue party arrived LATE because of the landslide.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Preposition","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Late modifies the verb arrived."},{"n":21,"q":"The first proposal would reduce expenditure; ______, it might considerably weaken operational capability.","o":["however","therefore","similarly","moreover"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete the sentence.","a":0,"e":"It introduces a contrast between reduced expenditure and weakened capability."},{"n":22,"q":"Several witnesses gave mutually contradictory accounts. ______, the investigators sought independent documentary evidence.","o":["Nevertheless","Consequently","Incidentally","Conversely"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"Seeking independent evidence was a consequence of the contradictory accounts."},{"n":23,"q":"The regulations apply not merely to permanent employees but also to contractual staff. ______, nobody performing the specified duties is exempt.","o":["On the contrary","In other words","By comparison","Meanwhile"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"It restates the meaning of the preceding sentence."},{"n":24,"q":"The machine performed satisfactorily under normal conditions. ______, its reliability under extreme temperatures remains uncertain.","o":["Hence","Similarly","Even so","Accordingly"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":2,"e":"It introduces a reservation despite satisfactory performance under normal conditions."},{"n":25,"q":"______ the question of accountability, responsibility ultimately rests with the officer who authorised the action.","o":["Speaking of","Despite","Whereas","Consequently"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"It introduces the topic of accountability."},{"n":26,"q":"The commander would rather the patrol returns before darkness.","o":["the patrol returned before darkness","the patrol will return before darkness","the patrol had return before darkness","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): In each of the following sentences, a part is incorrect or inappropriate. Select the alternative that best improves the sentence.","a":0,"e":"— After would rather + subject, the past tense is used: would rather the patrol returned."},{"n":27,"q":"Scarcely had the inspection commenced than a serious defect was detected.","o":["when a serious defect was detected","then a serious defect was detected","that a serious defect was detected","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"— The correct correlative construction is Scarcely...when, not Scarcely...than."},{"n":28,"q":"The officer is fully capable to handle such a complicated situation.","o":["for handling such a complicated situation","of handling such a complicated situation","to handling such a complicated situation","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"— The correct construction is capable of + gerund: capable of handling."},{"n":29,"q":"Despite he was repeatedly warned, he entered the restricted area.","o":["Despite of repeated warnings","Despite being repeatedly warned","Although of being warned","In spite he was repeatedly warned"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"— Despite is followed by a noun or gerund phrase: Despite being repeatedly warned."},{"n":30,"q":"Had it not been his timely intervention, the situation might have deteriorated further.","o":["Had it not been for his timely intervention","Had it not being his timely intervention","If it had not for his timely intervention","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"— The correct expression is Had it not been for..."},{"n":31,"q":"More than one candidate / have complained about / the ambiguity in the instructions.","o":["More than one candidate","have complained about","the ambiguity in the instructions.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each item consists of a sentence divided into three parts marked (a), (b) and (c). Identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error.","a":1,"e":"— More than one normally takes a singular verb. Have complained should be has complained."},{"n":32,"q":"The committee comprises of / five senior officers drawn / from different branches of the service.","o":["The committee comprises of","five senior officers drawn","from different branches of the service.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"— Comprise does not take of in this construction. Correct: The committee comprises five senior officers."},{"n":33,"q":"He has been serving / at this institution since / more than twelve years.","o":["He has been serving","at this institution since","more than twelve years.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— Since is incorrect with a period of duration. It should be for more than twelve years."},{"n":34,"q":"It is high time / the authorities take effective measures / to prevent further deterioration.","o":["It is high time","the authorities take effective measures","to prevent further deterioration.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— After It is high time, the past tense is normally used: the authorities took effective measures."},{"n":35,"q":"I look forward / to meet the members of the delegation / during their forthcoming visit.","o":["I look forward","to meet the members of the delegation","during their forthcoming visit.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"— Look forward to is followed by a gerund: look forward to meeting."},{"n":36,"q":"P. a sensible policy must remain flexible\nQ. without becoming so vague\nR. that it ceases to guide action\nS. enough to accommodate exceptional circumstances","o":["PSQR","SPQR","PQRS","QSPR"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange them in the correct sequence to form a meaningful and grammatically correct sentence.","a":0,"e":"A sensible policy must remain flexible enough to accommodate exceptional circumstances without becoming so vague that it ceases to guide action."},{"n":37,"q":"P. becomes particularly dangerous\nQ. when it is repeated often enough\nR. misinformation that initially seems implausible\nS. to acquire the familiarity of truth","o":["RPQS","RQSP","PQRS","QRPS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Misinformation that initially seems implausible becomes particularly dangerous when it is repeated often enough to acquire the familiarity of truth."},{"n":38,"q":"P. depends not merely on possessing information\nQ. but on knowing\nR. sound judgement\nS. which information deserves greater weight","o":["RPQS","RSPQ","PQRS","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Sound judgement depends not merely on possessing information but on knowing which information deserves greater weight."},{"n":39,"q":"P. unless people are encouraged\nQ. procedures alone cannot guarantee improvement\nR. to identify their weaknesses\nS. and suggest better alternatives","o":["QPRS","PQRS","RSPQ","QRPS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Procedures alone cannot guarantee improvement unless people are encouraged to identify their weaknesses and suggest better alternatives."},{"n":40,"q":"P. may initially appear convincing\nQ. an explanation based on coincidence\nR. but becomes weaker\nS. as independent evidence accumulates","o":["QPRS","PQRS","RSPQ","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"An explanation based on coincidence may initially appear convincing but becomes weaker as independent evidence accumulates."},{"n":41,"q":"Blank 41: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["examined","exhausted","persuaded","imposed"],"cat":"Cloze Test","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Read the following passage carefully. Each numbered blank is followed by four alternatives. Select the most appropriate word to fill each blank.","c":"Good decisions are rarely the product of information alone. Evidence must first be (41) ______, weighed and interpreted. A fact that appears decisive in isolation may become less significant when viewed in (42) ______. Skilled decision-makers therefore resist the temptation to reach conclusions too (43) ______. They remain prepared to revise an opinion when fresh evidence (44) ______. Such flexibility does not indicate weakness; rather, it reflects intellectual (45) ______.","a":0,"e":"Evidence must first be examined, then weighed and interpreted."},{"n":42,"q":"Blank 42: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["isolation","context","proportion","contrast"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":1,"e":"A fact may have a different significance when viewed in context."},{"n":43,"q":"Blank 43: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["reluctantly","hastily","cautiously","precisely"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":1,"e":"Skilled decision-makers avoid reaching conclusions too quickly or hastily."},{"n":44,"q":"Blank 44: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["emerges","retreats","declines","escapes"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":0,"e":"Fresh evidence emerges when it becomes available or comes to light."},{"n":45,"q":"Blank 45: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["vanity","honesty","obstinacy","indifference"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":1,"e":"Willingness to revise an opinion in the light of new evidence reflects intellectual honesty."},{"n":46,"q":"AD HOC","o":["For a particular purpose","Without legal authority","For an indefinite period","By mutual consent"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Each item consists of a foreign/Latin expression followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":0,"e":"Ad hoc means created or arranged for a specific purpose or situation."},{"n":47,"q":"STATUS QUO","o":["A sudden change in circumstances","The existing state of affairs","A temporary compromise","A final legal decision"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":1,"e":"Status quo means the present or existing condition of things."},{"n":48,"q":"BONA FIDE","o":["Genuine and made in good faith","Secret and unauthorised","Doubtful or disputed","Temporary and conditional"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":0,"e":"Bona fide means genuine, authentic or made sincerely and in good faith."},{"n":49,"q":"DE FACTO","o":["According to law","Existing in fact or practice","By common agreement","Contrary to established practice"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":1,"e":"De facto means existing in reality or practice, even if not formally established by law."},{"n":50,"q":"CAVEAT EMPTOR","o":["Let the buyer beware","Let justice be done","Let the seller decide","Let the matter remain undecided"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":0,"e":"Caveat emptor means that the buyer is responsible for checking the quality or suitability of goods before purchase."}]},{"id":"sample-11","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 11","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The minister appeared remarkably INSOUCIANT despite the mounting criticism.","o":["unconcerned","apprehensive","meticulous","resentful"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Each item in this section consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word nearest in meaning to the word in capital letters and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"INSOUCIANT","a":0,"e":"Insouciant means showing little concern or anxiety; hence, unconcerned."},{"n":2,"q":"The historian dismissed the account as largely APOCRYPHAL.","o":["authoritative","doubtful","exhaustive","contemporary"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"APOCRYPHAL","a":1,"e":"Apocryphal refers to something of doubtful authenticity or truth."},{"n":3,"q":"The cadet’s CONTUMACIOUS behaviour eventually invited disciplinary action.","o":["obedient","insubordinate","hesitant","ingenuous"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"CONTUMACIOUS","a":1,"e":"Contumacious means stubbornly disobedient or rebellious against authority."},{"n":4,"q":"The singer’s MELLIFLUOUS voice held the audience spellbound.","o":["melodious","strident","feeble","monotonous"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"MELLIFLUOUS","a":0,"e":"Mellifluous means pleasantly smooth and musical in sound."},{"n":5,"q":"The editorial proceeded to EXCORIATE the administration for its repeated lapses.","o":["defend","criticise severely","imitate","reassure"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"EXCORIATE","a":1,"e":"Excoriate means to condemn or criticise someone very harshly."},{"n":6,"q":"His PUSILLANIMOUS response disappointed those who expected firm leadership.","o":["timid","courageous","cautious","reluctant"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–10): Each item consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word opposite in meaning to the word in capital letters.","t":"PUSILLANIMOUS","a":1,"e":"Pusillanimous means cowardly or timid; courageous is its opposite."},{"n":7,"q":"The witness gave a PROLIX account of an otherwise simple incident.","o":["elaborate","concise","repetitive","digressive"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"PROLIX","a":1,"e":"Prolix means unnecessarily lengthy or wordy; concise is the opposite."},{"n":8,"q":"The investigator was remarkably PERSPICACIOUS in detecting inconsistencies in the testimony.","o":["perceptive","obtuse","vigilant","judicious"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"PERSPICACIOUS","a":1,"e":"Perspicacious means perceptive and quick to understand; obtuse means slow to understand."},{"n":9,"q":"The conciliator found the aggrieved party surprisingly PLACABLE.","o":["forgiving","implacable","receptive","amenable"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"PLACABLE","a":1,"e":"Placable means capable of being calmed or appeased; implacable is its opposite."},{"n":10,"q":"The officer maintained a TEMPERATE approach even during intense provocation.","o":["restrained","excessive","moderate","balanced"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"TEMPERATE","a":1,"e":"Temperate means moderate or restrained; excessive expresses the opposite idea."},{"n":11,"q":"A person who studies the origin and history of words is called an ______.","o":["entomologist","etymologist","ethnologist","epistemologist"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each item consists of a group of words followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that can be substituted for the given group of words.","a":1,"e":"An etymologist studies the origin, history and development of words."},{"n":12,"q":"A person who collects and studies postage stamps is called a ______.","o":["philatelist","numismatist","archivist","antiquarian"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","a":0,"e":"A philatelist collects and studies postage stamps."},{"n":13,"q":"A person recovering from an illness is called a ______.","o":["recluse","convalescent","invalid","itinerant"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","a":1,"e":"A convalescent is a person recovering from illness or medical treatment."},{"n":14,"q":"Government by the wealthy is known as ______.","o":["oligarchy","plutocracy","theocracy","bureaucracy"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","a":1,"e":"Plutocracy means government or control by the wealthy."},{"n":15,"q":"A speech delivered without previous preparation is called ______.","o":["obituary","extempore","soliloquy","panegyric"],"cat":"One-Word Substitution","a":1,"e":"An extempore speech is delivered without prior preparation."},{"n":16,"q":"Since you insist on accompanying us, you must follow the safety instructions.","o":["Preposition","Conjunction","Adverb","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): In each of the following sentences, a word has been underlined. Identify the part of speech of the underlined word as used in the sentence.","a":1,"e":"Since introduces the subordinate clause “you insist on accompanying us”; hence, it is a conjunction."},{"n":17,"q":"The helicopter landed near the temporary medical camp.","o":["Adverb","Adjective","Preposition","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Near precedes the noun phrase “the temporary medical camp”; therefore, it is a preposition."},{"n":18,"q":"Little was known about the operation outside headquarters.","o":["Adjective","Pronoun","Adverb","Determiner"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Little stands independently as the subject of “was known” and functions as a pronoun."},{"n":19,"q":"The river was flowing unusually fast after the cloudburst.","o":["Adjective","Noun","Adverb","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Fast modifies the verb “was flowing” and tells how the river was flowing."},{"n":20,"q":"The sentry looked down from the watchtower.","o":["Adverb","Preposition","Adjective","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":0,"e":"Down modifies “looked” and indicates direction."},{"n":21,"q":"The evidence was largely circumstantial; ______, the court considered it insufficient for conviction.","o":["accordingly","likewise","incidentally","meanwhile"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete the sentence.","a":0,"e":"The second statement is a consequence of the first; accordingly means “therefore.”"},{"n":22,"q":"The original estimate appeared realistic. ______, several unforeseen expenses emerged during implementation.","o":["Furthermore","Nonetheless","Therefore","Similarly"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The unforeseen expenses contrast with the apparently realistic estimate; nonetheless expresses this contrast."},{"n":23,"q":"The proposal does not abolish the existing system. ______, it seeks only to modify certain procedures.","o":["More precisely","Consequently","Otherwise","In addition"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"The second sentence clarifies exactly what the proposal seeks to do."},{"n":24,"q":"The new equipment is lighter and more durable. ______, it requires considerably less maintenance.","o":["Conversely","Furthermore","Nevertheless","Instead"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The sentence adds another advantage of the new equipment; furthermore is appropriate."},{"n":25,"q":"The first method relies heavily on human judgement. ______, the second depends almost entirely on automated analysis.","o":["Conversely","Therefore","Likewise","Moreover"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"The two methods are contrasted with each other; conversely expresses the contrast."},{"n":26,"q":"No sooner had the convoy crossed the bridge when the road behind it collapsed.","o":["than the road behind it collapsed","then the road behind it collapsed","that the road behind it collapsed","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): In each of the following sentences, a part is incorrect or inappropriate. Select the alternative that best improves the sentence.","a":0,"e":"The correct correlative construction is No sooner...than."},{"n":27,"q":"The committee recommended that the proposal was reconsidered before implementation.","o":["that the proposal be reconsidered","that the proposal is reconsidered","the proposal to be reconsidering","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"After recommend, the subjunctive be reconsidered is appropriate."},{"n":28,"q":"The official denied to have disclosed the confidential note.","o":["to disclose","having disclosed","for disclosing","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":1,"e":"Deny is normally followed by a gerund: deny doing/having done something."},{"n":29,"q":"The new procedure is aimed to reduce unnecessary delays in processing applications.","o":["at reducing unnecessary delays","for reduce unnecessary delays","to reducing unnecessary delays","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"The correct expression is be aimed at + gerund."},{"n":30,"q":"If the warning had reached us earlier, we could prevent the accident.","o":["could have prevented the accident","could prevent an accident","had prevented the accident","No improvement"],"cat":"Sentence Improvement","a":0,"e":"The condition refers to an unreal past situation; therefore, the third conditional requires could have prevented."},{"n":31,"q":"The commander, together with two liaison officers, / were present at the briefing / before the delegation arrived.","o":["The commander, together with two liaison officers,","were present at the briefing","before the delegation arrived.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each item consists of a sentence divided into three parts marked (a), (b) and (c). Identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, select (d) No error.","a":1,"e":"The subject is singular commander; the intervening phrase does not affect agreement. Use was present."},{"n":32,"q":"The new regulations prevent officers / from disclosing classified material / without proper authorisation.","o":["The new regulations prevent officers","from disclosing classified material","without proper authorisation.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"Prevent someone from doing something is the correct construction, and the sentence is grammatically correct."},{"n":33,"q":"She has scarcely finished / the operational briefing / when the lights suddenly went out.","o":["She has scarcely finished","the operational briefing","when the lights suddenly went out.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":0,"e":"For two completed past events, the correct construction is She had scarcely finished...when..."},{"n":34,"q":"The inquiry revealed that / neither of the two witnesses / were willing to testify.","o":["The inquiry revealed that","neither of the two witnesses","were willing to testify.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":2,"e":"Neither of the two witnesses takes a singular verb here: was willing."},{"n":35,"q":"The young officer is accustomed / to work under severe pressure / during prolonged operations.","o":["The young officer is accustomed","to work under severe pressure","during prolonged operations.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Accustomed to is followed by a noun or gerund: accustomed to working."},{"n":36,"q":"P. a sensible policy must remain flexible\nQ. without becoming so vague\nR. that it ceases to guide action\nS. enough to accommodate exceptional circumstances","o":["PSQR","SPQR","PQRS","QSPR"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange them in the correct sequence to form a meaningful and grammatically correct sentence.","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: A sensible policy must remain flexible enough to accommodate exceptional circumstances without becoming so vague that it ceases to guide action."},{"n":37,"q":"P. becomes particularly dangerous\nQ. when it is repeated often enough\nR. misinformation that initially seems implausible\nS. to acquire the familiarity of truth","o":["RPQS","RQSP","PQRS","QRPS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: Misinformation that initially seems implausible becomes particularly dangerous when it is repeated often enough to acquire the familiarity of truth."},{"n":38,"q":"P. depends not merely on possessing information\nQ. but on knowing\nR. sound judgement\nS. which information deserves greater weight","o":["RPQS","RSPQ","PQRS","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: Sound judgement depends not merely on possessing information but on knowing which information deserves greater weight."},{"n":39,"q":"P. unless people are encouraged\nQ. procedures alone cannot guarantee improvement\nR. to identify their weaknesses\nS. and suggest better alternatives","o":["QPRS","PQRS","RSPQ","QRPS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: Procedures alone cannot guarantee improvement unless people are encouraged to identify their weaknesses and suggest better alternatives."},{"n":40,"q":"P. may initially appear convincing\nQ. an explanation based on coincidence\nR. but becomes weaker\nS. as independent evidence accumulates","o":["QPRS","PQRS","RSPQ","QRSP"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: An explanation based on coincidence may initially appear convincing but becomes weaker as independent evidence accumulates."},{"n":41,"q":"Blank 41: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["tested","concealed","imposed","evaded"],"cat":"Cloze Test","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Read the following passage carefully. Each numbered blank is followed by four alternatives. Select the most appropriate word to fill each blank.","c":"Reliable analysis demands more than collecting facts. Each claim must first be (41) ______ against the available evidence, and the evidence itself must be assessed for (42) ______. Analysts who become excessively attached to an early hypothesis may interpret later information (43) ______. Sound reasoning therefore requires a willingness to suspend judgement when the evidence remains (44) ______. Such restraint is not indecision; rather, it is a sign of intellectual (45) ______.","a":0,"e":"A claim should be tested against evidence to determine whether it is valid."},{"n":42,"q":"Blank 42: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["credibility","abundance","conformity","familiarity"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":0,"e":"Evidence must be assessed for its credibility, that is, whether it is reliable and believable."},{"n":43,"q":"Blank 43: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["selectively","randomly","generously","mechanically"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":0,"e":"Attachment to an existing hypothesis may cause a person to interpret later evidence selectively."},{"n":44,"q":"Blank 44: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["conclusive","inconclusive","redundant","unanimous"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":1,"e":"Judgement should be suspended when evidence does not permit a definite conclusion; such evidence is inconclusive."},{"n":45,"q":"Blank 45: Select the most appropriate word.","o":["discipline","vanity","rigidity","complacency"],"cat":"Cloze Test","a":0,"e":"Restraining oneself from premature conclusions demonstrates intellectual discipline."},{"n":46,"q":"EX OFFICIO","o":["By virtue of one’s office","Outside official authority","After leaving office","By special permission"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Each item consists of a foreign/Latin expression followed by four alternatives. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":0,"e":"Ex officio means holding a position or performing a function by virtue of one’s office."},{"n":47,"q":"MUTATIS MUTANDIS","o":["Without making any alteration","With the necessary changes having been made","Contrary to established custom","By unanimous agreement"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":1,"e":"Mutatis mutandis means applying something after making the necessary alterations."},{"n":48,"q":"INTER ALIA","o":["Between two parties","Among other things","In equal proportion","In private"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":1,"e":"Inter alia means among other things."},{"n":49,"q":"PER SE","o":["For a particular purpose","In itself","By legal authority","For the time being"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":1,"e":"Per se means by itself or in itself."},{"n":50,"q":"ULTRA VIRES","o":["Within legal authority","Beyond one’s legal power or authority","In accordance with precedent","By mutual consent"],"cat":"Foreign Expressions","a":1,"e":"Ultra vires describes an act performed beyond the legal powers or authority of a person or body."}]},{"id":"sample-12","kind":"sample","label":"NDA/NA English Sample Paper 12","group":"Online Sample Papers","questions":[{"n":1,"q":"The proposed restriction was considered INIMICAL to the interests of small businesses.","o":["hostile","conducive","irrelevant","incidental"],"cat":"Synonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 1–5): Each item in this section consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word nearest in meaning to the word in capital letters and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"INIMICAL","a":0,"e":"Inimical means harmful, hostile or unfavourable to something."},{"n":2,"q":"His LUGUBRIOUS account of the expedition created an atmosphere of gloom.","o":["humorous","mournful","exaggerated","confusing"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"LUGUBRIOUS","a":1,"e":"Lugubrious means excessively sad, gloomy or mournful."},{"n":3,"q":"The committee found the report unnecessarily TURGID in style.","o":["lucid","pompous","concise","informal"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"TURGID","a":1,"e":"Turgid describes language or style that is excessively elaborate, inflated or pompous."},{"n":4,"q":"The negotiator attempted to MOLLIFY the aggrieved employees before the meeting began.","o":["appease","provoke","dismiss","interrogate"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"MOLLIFY","a":0,"e":"Mollify means to calm, soothe or reduce someone’s anger."},{"n":5,"q":"The lecturer’s monotonous delivery had a SOPORIFIC effect on the audience.","o":["stimulating","sleep-inducing","disturbing","persuasive"],"cat":"Synonyms","t":"SOPORIFIC","a":1,"e":"Soporific means tending to induce sleep or drowsiness."},{"n":6,"q":"His OBSEQUIOUS behaviour towards senior officials irritated his colleagues.","o":["servile","submissive","assertive","deferential"],"cat":"Antonyms","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 6–10): Each item in this section consists of a sentence containing a word in capital letters followed by four alternatives. Select the word opposite in meaning to the word in capital letters and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","t":"OBSEQUIOUS","a":2,"e":"Obsequious means excessively submissive or servile; assertive is its opposite."},{"n":7,"q":"The political situation remained highly VOLATILE throughout the negotiations.","o":["unstable","unpredictable","stable","turbulent"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"VOLATILE","a":2,"e":"Volatile means unstable or liable to change suddenly; stable is the opposite."},{"n":8,"q":"The inquiry revealed that the officer’s conduct was ABERRANT rather than typical.","o":["abnormal","customary","irregular","deviant"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"ABERRANT","a":1,"e":"Aberrant means departing from the normal or usual; customary means usual or conventional."},{"n":9,"q":"Despite repeated failures, she remained TENACIOUS in pursuing the objective.","o":["persistent","determined","irresolute","resolute"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"TENACIOUS","a":2,"e":"Tenacious means persistent and determined; irresolute means uncertain or lacking determination."},{"n":10,"q":"The expedition demanded an AUSTERE lifestyle for several months.","o":["rigorous","luxurious","frugal","simple"],"cat":"Antonyms","t":"AUSTERE","a":1,"e":"Austere means simple, strict and without luxury; luxurious is its opposite."},{"n":11,"q":"After several unsuccessful attempts, the team finally decided to THROW IN THE TOWEL.","o":["admit defeat","change its strategy","conceal its failure","postpone the decision"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 11–15): Each of the following sentences contains an idiom/phrase in capital letters. Select the alternative that best expresses its meaning.","t":"THROW IN THE TOWEL","a":0,"e":"Throw in the towel means to give up or acknowledge defeat."},{"n":12,"q":"The negotiator decided to CALL HIS BLUFF when the rival threatened to withdraw from the agreement.","o":["expose his secret","challenge him to prove his threat","flatter him deliberately","accept his conditions"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"CALL HIS BLUFF","a":1,"e":"Call someone’s bluff means to challenge a person to prove that a threat or claim is genuine."},{"n":13,"q":"His repeated excuses CUT NO ICE with the disciplinary committee.","o":["caused considerable confusion","had no influence","created sympathy","ended the dispute"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"CUT NO ICE","a":1,"e":"Cut no ice means to have no effect or influence on someone."},{"n":14,"q":"The organisers JUMPED THE GUN by announcing the results before verification was complete.","o":["acted prematurely","avoided responsibility","acted courageously","deliberately misled everyone"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"JUMPED THE GUN","a":0,"e":"Jump the gun means to act before the proper or appropriate time."},{"n":15,"q":"The search party LEFT NO STONE UNTURNED in its effort to locate the missing climbers.","o":["searched only the obvious places","made every possible effort","abandoned the search temporarily","relied entirely on local help"],"cat":"Idioms & Phrases","t":"LEFT NO STONE UNTURNED","a":1,"e":"Leave no stone unturned means to make every possible effort to achieve something."},{"n":16,"q":"Before the convoy departed, every vehicle was inspected thoroughly.","o":["Preposition","Conjunction","Adverb","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 16–20): Each of the following sentences has an underlined word. Identify the part of speech to which the underlined word belongs and mark your response on the Answer Sheet accordingly.","a":1,"e":"Before introduces the subordinate clause “the convoy departed”; hence, it functions as a conjunction."},{"n":17,"q":"The messenger hurried across the courtyard.","o":["Adverb","Conjunction","Preposition","Adjective"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Across precedes the noun phrase “the courtyard” and functions as a preposition."},{"n":18,"q":"Many were absent from the morning parade.","o":["Determiner","Pronoun","Adjective","Adverb"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Many stands independently in place of a noun and is therefore a pronoun."},{"n":19,"q":"The rescue team arrived early despite the difficult terrain.","o":["Adjective","Adverb","Noun","Preposition"],"cat":"Word Class","a":1,"e":"Early modifies the verb “arrived” and tells when the team arrived."},{"n":20,"q":"Either route will take the patrol to the observation post.","o":["Pronoun","Adverb","Determiner","Conjunction"],"cat":"Word Class","a":2,"e":"Either comes before the noun “route” and determines it."},{"n":21,"q":"______, the proposed reform will cause some initial inconvenience; its long-term benefits, however, may outweigh the difficulties.","o":["Admittedly","Consequently","Likewise","Incidentally"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 21–25): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the most appropriate discourse marker/expression to complete the sentence.","a":0,"e":"Admittedly introduces a point that is accepted before presenting a contrasting consideration."},{"n":22,"q":"The navigation system malfunctioned repeatedly. ______, the mission was postponed until repairs were completed.","o":["In contrast","For that reason","In addition","By comparison"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"The postponement is the result of the malfunction; hence, for that reason is appropriate."},{"n":23,"q":"Several safeguards have already been introduced. ______, every critical decision now requires independent review.","o":["More importantly","On the contrary","Otherwise","Nevertheless"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"The second statement adds a more significant safeguard to those already mentioned."},{"n":24,"q":"The findings appear convincing at first glance. ______, the sample used in the study is too small to justify a definite conclusion.","o":["Similarly","That said","As a result","For instance"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":1,"e":"That said introduces a qualification or contrast to the preceding statement."},{"n":25,"q":"The two proposals appear almost identical on paper. ______, they would operate quite differently once implemented.","o":["In practice","In addition","For example","Accordingly"],"cat":"Discourse Markers","a":0,"e":"The sentence contrasts how the proposals appear on paper with how they would actually operate."},{"n":26,"q":"Had the reserve unit not arrived in time, ______.","o":["the position might have been lost","the position may be lost","the position had been lost","the position would lose"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 26–30): Each of the following sentences contains a blank. Select the alternative that best completes the sentence.","a":0,"e":"The sentence expresses an unreal past condition; might have been lost correctly states the possible past result."},{"n":27,"q":"Not until the final report was released ______.","o":["the extent of the damage became clear","did the extent of the damage become clear","had the extent of the damage became clear","the damage did become clearly"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"After an initial negative expression such as Not until, subject–auxiliary inversion is required."},{"n":28,"q":"I would rather you ______ the matter until the inquiry is over.","o":["do not discuss","did not discuss","will not discuss","have not discussed"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"Would rather + subject is followed by the simple past when referring to another person’s present or future action."},{"n":29,"q":"The proposal can be approved only if ______.","o":["all relevant departments consent to it","all relevant departments will consent to it","all relevant departments had consented it","every departments consent to it"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":0,"e":"In a conditional clause introduced by if, the simple present is normally used for a future condition."},{"n":30,"q":"Lest any confidential material ______, all electronic devices were collected before the briefing.","o":["is disclosed","be disclosed","was disclosed","will be disclosed"],"cat":"Fill in the Blanks","a":1,"e":"Lest is commonly followed by the subjunctive/base form: lest...be disclosed."},{"n":31,"q":"Neither the commanding officer nor the junior officers / was willing to accept / responsibility for the procedural lapse.","o":["Neither the commanding officer nor the junior officers","was willing to accept","responsibility for the procedural lapse.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 31–35): Each item consists of a sentence divided into three parts marked (A), (B) and (C). Identify the part containing an error. If there is no error, choose (D) No error.","a":1,"e":"With neither...nor, the verb generally agrees with the nearer subject. Since junior officers is plural, were willing is required."},{"n":32,"q":"He is one of the officers / who has consistently performed well / under difficult operational conditions.","o":["He is one of the officers","who has consistently performed well","under difficult operational conditions.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Who refers to the plural noun officers; therefore, who have consistently performed well is correct."},{"n":33,"q":"The number of complaints received this month / have increased significantly / compared with the previous month.","o":["The number of complaints received this month","have increased significantly","compared with the previous month.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"The subject is The number, which is singular; hence, has increased is required."},{"n":34,"q":"The newly appointed officer / is superior than his predecessor / in administrative experience.","o":["The newly appointed officer","is superior than his predecessor","in administrative experience.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":1,"e":"Superior takes the preposition to, not than: superior to his predecessor."},{"n":35,"q":"Hardly had the meeting begun / when the chairman received / an urgent message from headquarters.","o":["Hardly had the meeting begun","when the chairman received","an urgent message from headquarters.","No error"],"cat":"Spotting Errors","a":3,"e":"Hardly had...when is the correct correlative construction, and the sentence is grammatically correct."},{"n":36,"q":"P. without losing sight of its central objective\nQ. to respond to changing circumstances\nR. a sound policy must remain flexible enough\nS. while retaining internal consistency","o":["RQPS","RPQS","QRSP","RSPQ"],"cat":"Ordering","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 36–40): Each item consists of four parts marked P, Q, R and S. Arrange them in the correct sequence to form a meaningful and grammatically correct sentence.","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: A sound policy must remain flexible enough to respond to changing circumstances without losing sight of its central objective while retaining internal consistency."},{"n":37,"q":"P. when evidence contradicts his original assumptions\nQ. a researcher shows intellectual honesty\nR. by revising a preferred explanation\nS. rather than defending it indefinitely","o":["QRPS","QPRS","RQSP","PQRS"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: A researcher shows intellectual honesty by revising a preferred explanation when evidence contradicts his original assumptions rather than defending it indefinitely."},{"n":38,"q":"P. not because they lack information\nQ. many organisations make poor decisions\nR. but because they fail\nS. to distinguish relevant evidence from noise","o":["QPRS","PQRS","QRSP","RSPQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: Many organisations make poor decisions not because they lack information but because they fail to distinguish relevant evidence from noise."},{"n":39,"q":"P. only after the emergency drill\nQ. did the team realise\nR. how vulnerable the communication system was\nS. had been completed","o":["PSQR","PQRS","SQRP","PRSQ"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: Only after the emergency drill had been completed did the team realise how vulnerable the communication system was."},{"n":40,"q":"P. the apparent simplicity of a solution\nQ. should never prevent us\nR. from examining\nS. its hidden assumptions","o":["PQRS","QRSP","PRSQ","QPSR"],"cat":"Ordering","a":0,"e":"Correct sentence: The apparent simplicity of a solution should never prevent us from examining its hidden assumptions."},{"n":41,"q":"According to the passage, numerical indicators are attractive mainly because they are ______.","o":["impossible to manipulate","objective-looking and easy to compare","always accurate","capable of replacing judgement"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 41–45): Read the following passage carefully and answer the questions that follow. Select the most appropriate answer on the basis of the passage only.","c":"Modern organisations increasingly depend on numerical indicators to evaluate performance. Numbers appear objective, easy to compare and convenient for setting targets. Yet a measure is useful only when it accurately represents the quality it is intended to assess. Once an indicator becomes a target in itself, people may begin improving the number rather than the underlying performance. For example, if a department is judged solely by how quickly it resolves complaints, employees may close cases rapidly without addressing their causes. The recorded performance improves, but the actual service may deteriorate. The problem lies not in measurement itself but in confusing an indicator with the complex reality it represents. Good evaluation therefore combines quantitative measures with judgement, context and periodic review. Metrics should inform decisions, not replace thought. A useful indicator is one that helps people ask better questions rather than encouraging them to stop asking questions altogether.","a":1,"e":"The passage states that numbers appear objective, are easy to compare and are convenient for setting targets."},{"n":42,"q":"What problem may arise when an indicator becomes a target in itself?","o":["Employees may improve the measured number without improving actual performance","Organisations may stop collecting numerical information","Targets become impossible to understand","Employees may refuse to work towards any objective"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":0,"e":"The passage warns that people may focus on improving the indicator rather than the underlying performance."},{"n":43,"q":"The example of complaint resolution shows that ______.","o":["faster performance always improves service","numerical targets are inherently useless","recorded success may conceal poor actual performance","complaints should never be measured"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"The complaint-resolution example shows that better numerical results can coexist with deteriorating actual service."},{"n":44,"q":"According to the author, effective evaluation should combine numerical measures with ______.","o":["intuition alone","judgement, context and periodic review","stricter targets only","fewer performance indicators"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":1,"e":"The passage explicitly recommends combining quantitative measures with these three elements."},{"n":45,"q":"The central idea of the passage is that metrics should ______.","o":["replace human judgement whenever possible","be treated as exact representations of reality","support thoughtful evaluation rather than substitute for it","be abandoned because they can be misused"],"cat":"Reading Comprehension","a":2,"e":"The central argument is that metrics should inform judgement, not replace thinking."},{"n":46,"q":"Read the following Active Voice → Passive Voice pairs: I. Someone has stolen my identity card. → My identity card has been stolen. II. They will look into the matter. → The matter will be looked into by them. Identify the correct pair(s).","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","d":"Directions (Q. Nos. 46–50): Read each question carefully and select the most appropriate option.","a":2,"e":"Both passive constructions are correct: has been stolen and will be looked into accurately preserve the meaning of the active sentences."},{"n":47,"q":"Read the following Direct Speech → Indirect Speech pairs: I. Rina said, “I completed the report yesterday.” → Rina said that she had completed the report the previous day. II. The officer said to the cadets, “Do not leave the ground.” → The officer ordered the cadets not to leave the ground. Identify the correct pair(s).","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"In I, yesterday correctly changes to the previous day and the tense backshifts. In II, the negative command correctly becomes ordered...not to leave."},{"n":48,"q":"Read the following One-Word Substitutions: I. One who walks in sleep — Somnambulist II. One who is unable to pay his debts — Insolvent III. Study of inscriptions — Epigraphy Identify the correctly matched pair(s).","o":["I and II only","II and III only","I and III only","I, II and III"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":3,"e":"A somnambulist walks in sleep; an insolvent cannot pay debts; epigraphy is the study of inscriptions."},{"n":49,"q":"Read the following word pairs and meanings: I. Adapt — modify to suit a new purpose; Adopt — take up or accept II. Imply — suggest indirectly; Infer — arrive at a conclusion from evidence Which pair(s) are correctly explained?","o":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"Adapt means modify to suit a purpose, while adopt means accept or take up. Imply means suggest indirectly, while infer means draw a conclusion."},{"n":50,"q":"Fill in X and Y appropriately: I. Between the two proposals, the first is X (better/the best). II. There is Y (little/few) justification for delaying the decision.","o":["X—better, Y—few","X—the best, Y—few","X—better, Y—little","X—the best, Y—little"],"cat":"Grammar & Usage","a":2,"e":"Between two alternatives, the comparative better is used. Justification is uncountable, so little is correct."}]}];
